BANKS["PSY-ATT"] = {
  label: "Attachment",
  color: "#be185d",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"ATT-01",stem:"Bowlby's theory of attachment proposes that attachment is best explained by:",
      options:{A:"Classical conditioning — infants attach to whoever feeds them.",B:"An innate, evolutionary drive",C:"Operant conditioning — attachment forms through reinforcement of crying.",D:"Social learning — infants imitate the attachment behaviours they observe."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ATT-01",
      scaffold:"Bowlby's monotropic theory: attachment is innate (evolutionary — keeps the infant near a protective caregiver), monotropy (one primary attachment figure), critical period (0–2.5 years), and the internal working model (template for future relationships). This is NOT conditioning — Bowlby explicitly rejected the cupboard love explanation.",
      reforge:{stem:"Lorenz's (1935) imprinting study with geese supports Bowlby's theory because:",options:{A:"It shows geese attach to whoever feeds them.",B:"It demonstrates an innate, time-limited attachment mechanism — geese imprinted on the first moving object they saw, suggesting biological programming rather than learning.",C:"Geese and humans have identical attachment systems.",D:"It proves the internal working model."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"ATT-02",stem:"Ainsworth's Strange Situation (1969) classified infants into attachment types based on:",
      options:{A:"Their IQ scores at 6 months.",B:"The number of hours they spent with their caregiver per week.",C:"Their behaviour during separation from and reunion with their caregiver",D:"Brain scans taken during attachment-related scenarios."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-ATT-02",
      scaffold:"The Strange Situation is a controlled observation: 8 episodes including separation and reunion with caregiver, and introduction of a stranger. Key behaviours: separation anxiety (distress when caregiver leaves), stranger anxiety, reunion behaviour (how they respond when caregiver returns), and use of caregiver as safe base. These behaviours determine the attachment type.",
      reforge:{stem:"In the Strange Situation, which behaviour is MOST important for classifying attachment type?",options:{A:"How much the infant cries when the stranger enters.",B:"Reunion behaviour — how the infant responds when the caregiver returns, since this reveals the underlying working model of the relationship.",C:"The infant's age in months.",D:"Whether the infant smiles at the stranger."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"ATT-03",stem:"A student describes a Type B (securely attached) infant as 'one who doesn't cry at all when the caregiver leaves.' Why is this wrong?",
      options:{A:"Securely attached infants show no distress at all during separation.",B:"Secure infants always cry uncontrollably throughout the Strange Situation.",C:"The Strange Situation does not actually measure separation responses.",D:"Securely attached infants show moderate separation anxiety but are easily comforted on reunion."},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-ATT-03",
      scaffold:"Three types: Type A (insecure-avoidant) — little distress on separation, ignores/avoids caregiver on reunion. Type B (secure) — moderate distress, easily soothed on reunion, uses caregiver as safe base. Type C (insecure-resistant/ambivalent) — high distress, difficult to soothe on reunion, clingy and angry. Students often think 'secure = no distress' which is wrong.",
      reforge:{stem:"An infant shows intense distress when the caregiver leaves but is not comforted on reunion — pushing the caregiver away while still upset. This is most consistent with:",options:{A:"Type B — secure attachment.",B:"Type C — insecure-resistant (ambivalent) attachment, characterised by high distress and inconsistent reunion behaviour.",C:"Type A — insecure-avoidant attachment.",D:"No attachment has formed."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"ATT-04",stem:"Which of the following is a valid evaluation of the Strange Situation?",
      options:{A:"The procedure lacks ecological validity",B:"It has no limitations — it is perfectly valid.",C:"It is too time-consuming to be a useful research tool.",D:"It only works with infants over 3 years old."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-ATT-04",
      scaffold:"Strange Situation evaluations: Strength — standardised, replicable, clear behavioural categories, high inter-rater reliability. Limitations — artificial (lab-based), may cause distress (ethics), categories may be culturally biased (e.g. German infants often classified as avoidant but this may reflect cultural norms of independence), doesn't capture all attachment types (Main and Solomon added Type D — disorganised).",
      reforge:{stem:"Cross-cultural research by van IJzendoorn and Kroonenberg (1988) found that Type B (secure) attachment was most common across all cultures, but proportions of A and C varied. This suggests:",options:{A:"The Strange Situation is entirely invalid for cross-cultural research.",B:"Secure attachment appears to be universal, but cultural norms influence the expression of insecure attachment types — cautioning against applying Western classification criteria globally.",C:"German infants are more securely attached than American infants.",D:"Attachment type is entirely determined by genes."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"ATT-05",stem:"Bowlby's concept of the 'internal working model' refers to:",
      options:{A:"A brain scan showing activation patterns linked to attachment behaviour.",B:"A cognitive framework from early attachment that shapes expectations of future relationships.",C:"The infant's physical model of their caregiver's facial expressions.",D:"A standardised diagram used to classify the three main attachment types."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ATT-05",
      scaffold:"Internal working model: the mental representation of self, caregiver, and relationships formed in infancy. It acts as a template — a securely attached infant develops positive expectations of others (trustworthy, responsive) and self (worthy of love). Bowlby argued this template influences all later relationships, including romantic ones (continuity hypothesis). Evaluation: hard to test directly; may be deterministic.",
      reforge:{stem:"Research suggests that securely attached infants tend to have better peer relationships and romantic partnerships in adulthood. This supports Bowlby's idea of:",options:{A:"Monotropy — one primary attachment is most important.",B:"The continuity hypothesis — the internal working model formed in infancy acts as a template for all future relationships, with secure early attachment predicting positive later outcomes.",C:"The critical period — all development must occur before age 2.",D:"Imprinting — adult relationships mirror the first attachment."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"ATT-06",stem:"Bowlby's maternal deprivation hypothesis claims that separation from the primary caregiver in early childhood causes:",
      options:{A:"Temporary distress with no lasting effects.",B:"Only cognitive damage, not emotional damage.",C:"Permanent, irreversible intellectual and emotional damage",D:"Damage only if the child is under 6 months old."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-ATT-06",
      scaffold:"Maternal deprivation hypothesis: prolonged separation/loss during the critical period (0–2.5 years) causes irreversible damage including low IQ, delinquency, and affectionless psychopathy (inability to form deep relationships, lack of guilt). Evidence: Bowlby's 44 Thieves study — 86% of affectionless thieves had experienced early separation. Criticism: Rutter distinguished deprivation from privation; effects may be reversible with good substitute care (Czech twins case).",
      reforge:{stem:"Rutter (1981) criticised Bowlby's maternal deprivation hypothesis by arguing that:",options:{A:"Early separation has no effect on development.",B:"Bowlby confused deprivation (loss of an existing bond) with privation (never forming a bond) — the effects of privation are far more severe, and even deprivation effects can be reversed with good quality substitute care.",C:"Only fathers can cause maternal deprivation.",D:"The critical period extends to age 10."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"ATT-07",stem:"A student answers a question about Bowlby by only describing his theory without any evaluation. On a 16-mark essay, what is the likely issue?",
      options:{A:"No issue — description is all that's needed.",B:"The student will lose marks for being too detailed.",C:"16-mark essays only require knowledge, not evaluation.",D:"The student scores AO1 marks only (up to 6) but loses all AO3 evaluation marks (up to 10)"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-TECH-02",
      scaffold:"AQA 16-mark essays: AO1 (knowledge/description) = up to 6 marks. AO3 (evaluation/analysis) = up to 10 marks. Evaluation means: using studies to support or contradict the theory, identifying methodological limitations, considering alternative explanations, and reaching a conclusion. Description-only answers are capped at the lowest band regardless of quality.",
      reforge:{stem:"To access the top band in a 16-mark AQA Psychology essay, a student must:",options:{A:"Write more than 4 pages.",B:"Demonstrate thorough and effective evaluation — using evidence to sustain an argument, showing understanding of limitations, and reaching a reasoned conclusion — not just accurate description.",C:"Include a diagram.",D:"Refer to at least 10 studies."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"ATT-08",stem:"Which of the following correctly distinguishes Harlow's (1958) rhesus monkey study from Bowlby's theory?",
      options:{A:"Harlow showed infants prefer contact comfort over food, directly challenging behaviourist learning theory.",B:"Harlow and Bowlby reached the same conclusions about the basis of attachment.",C:"Harlow demonstrated that food provision is the foundation of all attachment bonds.",D:"Bowlby's theory applies to animals; Harlow's applies specifically to humans."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-ATT-08",
      scaffold:"Harlow: infant monkeys ran to the cloth mother when frightened (contact comfort), not the wire mother who provided milk. This disproves cupboard love (food-based attachment). BUT the monkeys raised with surrogates were socially damaged — aggressive, poor mothers themselves. So contact comfort alone is insufficient. Supports Bowlby's point that attachment quality matters, not just physical presence. Ethical issue: caused lasting harm to the monkeys.",
      reforge:{stem:"Harlow's study supports Bowlby's monotropic theory because:",options:{A:"Both show that feeding is the basis of attachment.",B:"Both emphasise that a sensitive, responsive caregiver relationship — not just physical proximity or food — is essential for healthy development.",C:"Both were conducted with human infants.",D:"Both argue that fathers are unimportant."},correct:"B"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["SOC-MET"] = {
  label: "Methods in Context",
  color: "#7f1d1d",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"MET-01",stem:"A sociologist wants to investigate bullying in schools. She decides to conduct covert participant observation. Which of the following is the MOST significant ethical problem?",
      options:{A:"The sample will be too small to draw any valid conclusions from.",B:"Participants cannot give informed consent — they are unaware they are being studied.",C:"The data collected will lack validity because researchers are present.",D:"Schools will almost always refuse access to covert researchers on site."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MET-01",
      scaffold:"Covert observation = researcher conceals their identity. Key ethical issue: no informed consent, no right to withdraw. Participants cannot choose to opt out. BSA ethics guidelines require informed consent unless deception is justified by the research value. In educational settings this is especially sensitive given child protection concerns. Overt observation removes this problem but risks the Hawthorne effect (changing behaviour when watched).",
      reforge:{stem:"A researcher uses overt observation in a classroom. Students know they are being watched. The main methodological weakness is:",options:{A:"It is unethical — consent was not sought.",B:"The Hawthorne effect — students may change their behaviour because they know they are being observed, reducing the validity of the data.",C:"The sample is not representative.",D:"It produces quantitative data rather than qualitative."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"MET-02",stem:"Why do interpretivists prefer qualitative methods such as unstructured interviews when studying pupils' experience of school?",
      options:{A:"They produce more reliable data than questionnaires or structured interviews.",B:"They are cheaper and quicker to conduct than other sociological methods.",C:"Qualitative methods capture the meanings and interpretations behind behaviour.",D:"Schools require qualitative methods by law for all educational research."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-MET-02",
      scaffold:"Interpretivism: social reality is constructed through meaning — to understand behaviour, you must understand how participants interpret their world. Unstructured interviews allow participants to speak freely, generate rich qualitative data, and let unexpected themes emerge. Trade-off: low reliability (hard to replicate), low representativeness (small samples). Positivists prefer structured methods for reliability and generalisability.",
      reforge:{stem:"A positivist sociologist would criticise unstructured interviews for lacking:",options:{A:"Validity — they don't capture true meanings.",B:"Reliability — the interview varies each time and cannot be standardised or replicated, making it difficult to check results or generalise.",C:"Ethics — participants share too much.",D:"Depth — they are too superficial."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"MET-03",stem:"Official statistics on exclusions from schools are used by some sociologists. Which is a valid limitation of using these as a data source?",
      options:{A:"They are not published regularly enough.",B:"They only include data from state schools.",C:"They cannot be used to identify trends over time.",D:"They are a social construction"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-MET-03",
      scaffold:"Official statistics: strengths — large scale, longitudinal, free, allow trend analysis. Weaknesses — social construction (reflect decisions of gatekeepers e.g. police, teachers), not all incidents recorded, definitions change over time, may reflect institutional racism/classism rather than reality. Marxists argue they serve ruling-class interests; feminists note they may undercount crimes against women.",
      reforge:{stem:"Interactionists argue that official crime statistics tell us more about:",options:{A:"The actual levels of crime in society.",B:"The labelling practices of agencies like the police — who gets stopped, charged, and convicted — rather than objective patterns of criminal behaviour.",C:"The effectiveness of the criminal justice system.",D:"Victims' experiences."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"MET-04",stem:"A researcher conducts a questionnaire survey of 2,000 students about their attitudes to school. A strength of this method is:",
      options:{A:"It is highly reliable and representative",B:"It produces valid data about students' true feelings.",C:"Students always answer questionnaires honestly.",D:"It allows the researcher to probe unexpected responses."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-MET-04",
      scaffold:"Questionnaire strengths: large samples → representativeness → generalisability; standardised questions → reliability; quick and cheap; no interviewer bias; respondents may be more honest (especially on sensitive topics) with no researcher present. Weaknesses: low validity (fixed questions, no depth), response bias (social desirability), low response rates, assumes fixed meanings.",
      reforge:{stem:"A sociologist studying teachers' racist attitudes would be LEAST likely to use a questionnaire because:",options:{A:"Questionnaires cannot reach large samples.",B:"Teachers are unlikely to honestly disclose prejudiced attitudes on a questionnaire — the social desirability effect would reduce validity, and qualitative methods would better uncover hidden meanings.",C:"Questionnaires are too expensive.",D:"Positivists forbid questionnaire use."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"MET-05",stem:"What is meant by 'triangulation' in sociological research?",
      options:{A:"Using three different samples in one study.",B:"Using more than one research method or data source to cross-check findings",C:"A statistical technique for analysing data.",D:"Conducting research in three different schools."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MET-05",
      scaffold:"Triangulation: using multiple methods to cross-reference findings. E.g. combining questionnaires (quantitative breadth) with interviews (qualitative depth) and official statistics. If findings converge, confidence in validity increases. Used by realists who argue both breadth and depth are needed. Drawback: time-consuming, expensive, may produce contradictory data that is hard to reconcile.",
      reforge:{stem:"A researcher uses questionnaires AND in-depth interviews in the same study on student experience. This is an example of:",options:{A:"Positivist methodology.",B:"Triangulation — combining methods to test whether findings are consistent and to produce a richer, more valid picture of the phenomenon.",C:"A case study.",D:"Longitudinal research."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"MET-06",stem:"Identify one practical problem with conducting sociological research in schools. (2 marks)",
      type:"short_answer",
      model_answer:"Researchers require permission from multiple gatekeepers — headteachers, parents, and sometimes local authorities — before accessing pupils (knowledge). This can be time-consuming and may result in refused access, restricting which schools and pupils can be studied and affecting the representativeness of the sample (analysis).",
      tag:"MC-MET-06",
      scaffold:"Practical issues in school research: access (need permission from headteachers, parents); disruption to lessons; child protection requirements (DBS checks); pupils may behave differently with a researcher present; timetabling restrictions limit observation time. These are distinct from ethical or theoretical issues — identify which type the question asks about."
    },
    {
      id:"MET-07",stem:"A student answers a methods question by writing only about whether a method is 'reliable or valid' without applying it to the specific research context. What mark-scheme issue does this create?",
      options:{A:"Nothing — reliability and validity are the only criteria examiners look for.",B:"A generic answer with no AO2 application to the specific research context.",C:"It shows strong AO1 knowledge, which is the only skill being assessed in methods questions.",D:"The student should have discussed ethics instead of reliability and validity."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-TECH-01",
      scaffold:"AQA Sociology methods questions: AO1 (knowledge of method) + AO2 (application to context) + AO3 (evaluation). Examiners specifically penalise generic answers that could apply to any study. A top answer names the method, explains its relevance to the SPECIFIC setting (schools, prisons, etc.), and evaluates using context-specific examples — e.g. why pupil power dynamics affect interview validity.",
      reforge:{stem:"'Evaluate the use of interviews to study the experience of exclusion from school.' A top-mark answer would:",options:{A:"List all advantages and disadvantages of interviews in general.",B:"Explain how unstructured interviews allow excluded pupils to express their experiences freely (AO1), apply this specifically to the sensitivity of exclusion as a topic (AO2), and evaluate by considering whether pupils would feel comfortable sharing with an adult researcher (AO3).",C:"Compare interviews to observation without mentioning schools.",D:"Describe the history of the interview as a research method."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"MET-08",stem:"Which research method would MOST likely be used by a feminist sociologist studying gender inequality in classroom interactions?",
      options:{A:"Large-scale structured questionnaire.",B:"Official statistics on school exclusions.",C:"Content analysis of government education policy.",D:"Covert or overt observation"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-MET-08",
      scaffold:"Feminist methodology: prefers qualitative methods (interviews, observation) to give voice to women's experiences, values subjectivity and reflexivity, suspicious of positivist 'objectivity' as a male construct. In school settings: observation captures gender dynamics in real time (e.g. teacher attention, classroom talk); interviews let girls express their own interpretations rather than answering researcher-set categories.",
      reforge:{stem:"A Marxist sociologist studying the hidden curriculum would most likely use:",options:{A:"A structured questionnaire with closed questions.",B:"Ethnographic methods — sustained participant observation and interviews — to uncover the implicit messages about class, hierarchy, and conformity that schools transmit beneath the surface of the formal curriculum.",C:"Official statistics on exam results.",D:"Longitudinal surveys of income."},correct:"B"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["ECON-1.1"] = {
  label: "Supply & Demand",
  color: "#1e3a5f",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"SD-01",stem:"A student says: 'When the price of coffee rises, demand falls.' What is the more precise economic statement?",
      options:{A:"When the price of coffee rises, the quantity demanded falls",B:"The statement is correct as written.",C:"Demand rises when price rises due to the Veblen effect.",D:"Price has no effect on demand."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-SD-01",
      scaffold:"'Demand' = the whole relationship between price and quantity (the curve). 'Quantity demanded' = a specific amount at a specific price (a point on the curve). A price change causes a movement ALONG the curve (change in Qd). A shift of the whole demand curve is caused by non-price factors: income, tastes, prices of related goods, expectations, population. This distinction is tested constantly.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which of the following would shift the demand curve for electric cars to the RIGHT?",options:{A:"A fall in the price of electric cars.",B:"A rise in consumers' incomes (electric cars are a normal good) and government subsidies making them cheaper to buy.",C:"A rise in the price of petrol (petrol cars are a substitute).",D:"Both B and C would shift the demand curve right."},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"SD-02",stem:"A rise in wages for baristas increases the cost of producing coffee. On a supply and demand diagram, this causes:",
      options:{A:"The demand curve shifts left as consumers reduce quantity demanded.",B:"The supply curve shifts left — higher production costs mean less supplied at every price.",C:"A movement along the existing supply curve, not a shift.",D:"Both supply and demand curves shift left simultaneously."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-SD-02",
      scaffold:"Supply shifts: LEFTWARD when costs rise (wages, raw materials, taxes, energy) or regulations increase. RIGHTWARD when costs fall (technology improvements, subsidies, cheaper inputs). Demand curve is unaffected by production costs — it reflects consumer willingness to pay, not firm costs. A movement along supply = price changes; a shift = non-price factors change.",
      reforge:{stem:"A government subsidy is given to UK solar panel manufacturers. What happens to the supply curve for solar panels?",options:{A:"Shifts left — subsidies reduce firm profits.",B:"Shifts right — subsidies lower production costs, so firms are willing to supply more at every price.",C:"No change — subsidies only affect demand.",D:"The demand curve shifts right."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"SD-03",stem:"At the market equilibrium price for wheat, which of the following is true?",
      options:{A:"Quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied.",B:"Quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded.",C:"Quantity demanded equals quantity supplied",D:"Price is at its maximum possible level."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-SD-03",
      scaffold:"Equilibrium = where demand and supply curves intersect. At this price: Qd = Qs, no surplus (excess supply) and no shortage (excess demand). Above equilibrium price: surplus → price falls back to equilibrium. Below equilibrium price: shortage → price rises back to equilibrium. The price mechanism automatically moves markets toward equilibrium.",
      reforge:{stem:"A government sets a maximum price (price ceiling) for rent BELOW the equilibrium market rent. What results?",options:{A:"A surplus of housing — too many houses are supplied.",B:"A shortage of housing — at the lower price, quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied, as landlords supply fewer properties and more people seek them.",C:"No effect — the market adjusts automatically.",D:"Equilibrium is re-established immediately."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"SD-04",stem:"The price elasticity of demand (PED) for insulin is estimated at −0.1. This means:",
      options:{A:"Demand is price elastic — a 1% rise in price causes a 10% fall in demand.",B:"Demand rises when price rises.",C:"Demand is price inelastic",D:"There is no relationship between price and demand for insulin."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-SD-04",
      scaffold:"PED = % change in Qd / % change in price. Inelastic: |PED| < 1 (quantity barely responds to price). Elastic: |PED| > 1 (quantity responds strongly). Factors affecting PED: necessity vs luxury, availability of substitutes, proportion of income spent, time period. Insulin = necessity + no close substitutes = very inelastic. Revenue implication: price rise → higher revenue for inelastic goods.",
      reforge:{stem:"A firm raises the price of its product by 10% and total revenue rises. This suggests:",options:{A:"Demand is price elastic — quantity fell proportionally more than price rose.",B:"Demand is price inelastic — quantity fell proportionally less than price rose, so the price increase more than offset the fall in quantity sold.",C:"Supply is perfectly inelastic.",D:"The good is an inferior good."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"SD-05",stem:"Butter and margarine are substitute goods. If the price of butter rises significantly, what happens in the market for margarine?",
      options:{A:"Demand for margarine falls — consumers have less money after buying butter.",B:"Supply of margarine falls — butter manufacturers cut back.",C:"The margarine market is unaffected.",D:"Demand for margarine rises"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-SD-05",
      scaffold:"Substitutes: goods that can be used in place of each other (butter/margarine, Coke/Pepsi, bus/train). A rise in the price of Good A → consumers switch to Good B → demand for B increases (rightward shift). Complements work oppositely: goods consumed together (cars and petrol) — rise in price of cars → demand for petrol falls. Cross-price elasticity of demand (XED) distinguishes: positive XED = substitutes, negative XED = complements.",
      reforge:{stem:"Petrol and cars are complement goods. If the government imposes a large tax on petrol, raising its price significantly, what happens to the demand for cars?",options:{A:"Demand for cars rises — drivers buy more efficient cars.",B:"Demand for cars falls — as petrol becomes more expensive, the total cost of car ownership rises, reducing the demand for cars (complement effect).",C:"Demand for cars is unaffected — they are different markets.",D:"Supply of cars falls."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"SD-06",stem:"A student correctly draws a supply and demand diagram but labels the vertical axis 'price' and the horizontal axis 'quantity'. Why might this be penalised in an Edexcel Economics exam?",
      options:{A:"Price (£ or P) on the vertical axis and quantity (Q) per time period on the horizontal.",B:"The labels are already correct — no change needed.",C:"The axes should be reversed: quantity on vertical, price on horizontal.",D:"Economics diagrams do not require labelled axes in exam answers."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-TECH-04",
      scaffold:"Edexcel marks scheme requires: vertical axis = Price (P or £), horizontal axis = Quantity (Q) per period (per week/month/year). Also: both curves must be labelled (D and S or D1/D2 for shifts), equilibrium must be marked (P* and Q* or Pe and Qe), arrows showing direction of shift. Missing any of these is flagged in examiner reports as a common error worth losing easy marks.",
      reforge:{stem:"A student draws a supply curve shifting right but forgets to label the new curve. What does the examiner report say about this?",options:{A:"It doesn't matter — the shift is visible.",B:"Unlabelled curves are a common error — the new curve must be labelled S2 (or S1) and the new equilibrium price and quantity marked with dotted lines. Without labels, it is unclear what the diagram shows.",C:"Only demand curves need labels.",D:"Arrows replace the need for labels."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"SD-07",stem:"In a free market, a sudden frost destroys 30% of the UK's strawberry crop. Trace the effect through to the new equilibrium.",
      type:"short_answer",
      model_answer:"The frost reduces the supply of strawberries — the supply curve shifts left (fewer strawberries available at every price) (K). At the original price, quantity supplied now falls short of quantity demanded — a shortage exists. This excess demand puts upward pressure on price (An). Price rises until a new equilibrium is reached where quantity demanded has fallen (movement along demand curve) and quantity supplied has recovered to meet it — at a higher price and lower quantity than before (An).",
      tag:"MC-SD-02",
      scaffold:"Chain: supply shock (frost) → supply shifts left → shortage at old price → price rises → Qd falls + Qs rises → new equilibrium at higher P, lower Q. Draw: original D and S, S shifts left to S2, new equilibrium P2 (higher) and Q2 (lower). Mark with dotted lines."
    },
    {
      id:"SD-08",stem:"Price elasticity of supply (PES) for new housing is very low in the short run. Which factor MOST explains this?",
      options:{A:"Low demand for housing.",B:"Supply of housing is constrained by planning permission, construction time",C:"Builders choose not to respond to higher prices.",D:"The government controls all house building."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-SD-08",
      scaffold:"PES factors: time period (supply more elastic in long run), spare capacity (elastic if factories idle), ease of storage (storable goods more elastic), factor mobility (can resources switch to this industry?). Housing: long production time, planning constraints, land scarcity → very low PES (inelastic). Implication: a rise in house prices leads to little new supply in the short run, mainly just higher prices — relevant to UK housing crisis.",
      reforge:{stem:"PES for oil extracted from the North Sea is low because:",options:{A:"There is too much oil — supply is very elastic.",B:"Extraction requires large fixed capital investment and infrastructure that cannot be rapidly scaled up in response to price rises — making supply inelastic in the short run.",C:"Oil prices are controlled by the government.",D:"North Sea oil is of poor quality."},correct:"B"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["GEO-TEC"] = {
  label: "Tectonic Hazards",
  color: "#0f766e",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"TEC-01",spec:"TECH",stem:"At a destructive plate boundary where oceanic meets continental crust, the oceanic plate always subducts because:",
      options:{A:"It moves faster and momentum carries it downward.",B:"Continental plates are anchored by mountain ranges and cannot move.",C:"It is denser due to its basaltic composition, so it sinks beneath the less dense granitic continental crust.",D:"Ocean water pressure forces the oceanic plate beneath the land."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-TEC-01",
      scaffold:"Oceanic crust is basaltic — denser than the granitic continental crust. At convergent boundaries, the denser plate always subducts. This generates: deep ocean trenches (downward pull), earthquakes (friction), and composite volcanoes as the plate melts and magma rises. Example: Nazca plate subducting under South American plate to form the Andes.",
      reforge:{stem:"The Mariana Trench (deepest point on Earth) formed at a destructive boundary because:",options:{A:"Millions of years of erosion deepened the ocean floor at that location.",B:"The subducting Pacific plate drags the ocean floor downward as it descends into the mantle.",C:"Surrounding plates were pushed upward by volcanic activity, making the trench appear deeper.",D:"Underwater volcanic eruptions displaced sediment, creating the depression."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"TEC-02",spec:"TECH",stem:"The 2010 Haiti earthquake killed ~230,000 people; the 2011 Christchurch earthquake (smaller magnitude) killed 185. The PRIMARY explanation for this difference is:",
      options:{A:"Haiti is located directly on a plate boundary; New Zealand is further away.",B:"The Christchurch earthquake was focused much deeper, reducing surface shaking.",C:"New Zealand received international aid within hours while Haiti waited days.",D:"Haiti's poverty and weak building standards meant more damage and slower rescue."},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-TEC-02",
      scaffold:"Physical factors set the hazard size. Human factors determine the disaster. Haiti: informal housing, no enforced building codes, weak governance, overwhelmed hospitals. Christchurch: earthquake-resistant buildings, well-trained emergency services, strong economy. Smaller NZ earthquake, fraction of the deaths. Vulnerability, not magnitude, determines impact.",
      reforge:{stem:"A magnitude 8 earthquake strikes both a high-income and a low-income country simultaneously. Which statement best predicts outcomes?",options:{A:"Both countries suffer similar casualties since the earthquake magnitude is identical.",B:"The low-income country likely suffers far greater loss of life due to weaker buildings, slower emergency response, and higher density of informal settlements.",C:"The high-income country suffers more because its denser urban infrastructure creates more falling debris.",D:"Casualties are determined entirely by population density, not development level."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"TEC-03",spec:"TECH",stem:"Composite volcanoes at subduction zones produce explosive eruptions primarily because:",
      options:{A:"Silica-rich magma from melting oceanic crust has high viscosity",B:"They are taller, giving accumulated lava more gravitational energy.",C:"They erupt more frequently than other volcano types, preventing pressure release.",D:"Seawater enters the magma chamber and converts instantly to high-pressure steam."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-TEC-03",
      scaffold:"Key chain: subducting oceanic crust melts → silica-rich magma → high viscosity → gases cannot escape → pressure builds → explosive eruption. Shield volcanoes (hot spots/constructive) produce basaltic, low-silica magma — low viscosity, gases escape easily, gentle effusive eruptions. The magma's silica content, controlled by what type of crust melts, determines the eruption style.",
      reforge:{stem:"Hawaiian volcanoes erupt frequently but rarely cause deaths. The primary reason is:",options:{A:"Hawaii has evacuated all residents from the island of Hawaii permanently.",B:"Low-viscosity basaltic lava flows slowly and predictably, giving residents adequate time to evacuate safely.",C:"Hawaiian eruptions are detected by satellite months in advance.",D:"The Hawaiian government maintains lava diversion walls that redirect all flows to the ocean."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"TEC-04",spec:"TECH",stem:"A student claims earthquakes only occur at destructive plate boundaries. Which evidence best contradicts this?",options:{A:"Iceland experiences earthquakes yet has no subduction zone, proving they only occur at destructive boundaries.",B:"The 2010 Haiti earthquake occurred at a conservative boundary — plates sliding horizontally with no subduction.",C:"Deep-focus earthquakes occur beneath the Mid-Atlantic Ridge at constructive boundaries.",D:"The 2021 Icelandic eruptions and earthquakes occurred at the same location, conflating the two hazard types."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-TEC-04",
      scaffold:"Earthquakes occur at all three boundary types. Destructive: subduction friction, major earthquakes + volcanoes. Constructive: plates pulling apart, usually minor earthquakes + volcanoes. Conservative: plates sliding horizontally, major SHALLOW earthquakes, NO volcanoes. Haiti 2010 = Enriquillo-Plantain Garden fault, a conservative boundary. The San Andreas (California) is also conservative.",
      reforge:{stem:"Conservative plate boundaries produce earthquakes but not volcanoes because:",options:{A:"The plates at conservative boundaries move too slowly to generate sufficient frictional heat.",B:"Plates slide past each other horizontally with no subduction and no gap for magma to rise, so only earthquakes are produced.",C:"Conservative boundaries exclusively occur beneath deep ocean water, preventing volcanic surface eruptions.",D:"The magma generated at conservative boundaries is too viscous to reach the surface."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"TEC-05",spec:"TECH",stem:"Evaluate whether responses to tectonic hazards matter more than the physical characteristics of the hazard. (9 marks)",
      type:"extended_answer",
      model_answer_outline:{
        AO1:"Physical characteristics: magnitude, depth, boundary type. Responses: early warning, building design, emergency services, evacuation.",
        AO2:"Japan 2011: magnitude 9.0, early warning gave 90 seconds; earthquake-resistant buildings survived; deaths mainly from tsunami. Haiti vs Christchurch: smaller NZ quake, fraction of the deaths due to superior response capacity.",
        AO3:"However, extreme events can overwhelm even well-prepared societies. The 2011 tsunami exceeded designed wall heights. Physical characteristics set the ceiling. Conclusion: responses matter more for moderate events; physical factors dominate at extremes. Development mediates both."},
      tag:["MC-TEC-02","MC-TECH-02"],
      scaffold:"9-mark answers need: knowledge of both factors, at least two case studies with specific data, and a clear judgement about which matters more and under what conditions."
    },
    {
      id:"TEC-06",spec:"TECH",stem:"Which term describes the point on Earth's surface directly above where an earthquake originates underground?",
      options:{A:"Focus",B:"Fault line",C:"Epicentre",D:"Seismic zone"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-TEC-06",
      scaffold:"Focus (hypocentre) = the origin point underground where the earthquake actually starts. Epicentre = the point on the surface directly above the focus. Shallow earthquakes (focus under 70km) cause more damage because less energy is absorbed before reaching the surface. Examiners consistently flag students confusing these two terms.",
      reforge:{stem:"Two earthquakes have the same magnitude. Earthquake A has a focus at 10km depth; Earthquake B at 300km depth. Which causes more surface damage?",options:{A:"Earthquake B, since it releases energy over a longer period.",B:"Earthquake A, because the shallow focus means energy reaches the surface with minimal absorption, causing more intense shaking.",C:"Both cause identical surface damage since they have equal magnitude.",D:"Earthquake B, since deeper earthquakes radiate energy outward more evenly."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"TEC-07",spec:"TECH",stem:"Iceland is exceptionally volcanically active despite lying on a constructive boundary. The additional factor explaining this is:",
      options:{A:"Iceland is composed of unusually thin crust that allows magma to breach easily.",B:"Iceland's ice sheets create pressure on the crust that squeezes magma upward.",C:"All constructive boundaries produce this level of activity; Iceland is typical, not exceptional.",D:"Iceland also sits above a mantle hot spot, producing additional magma independently of the plate boundary movement."},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-TEC-07",
      scaffold:"Iceland = constructive boundary (Eurasian + North American diverging) PLUS a hot spot. The combination creates exceptional activity. Eruptions produce low-viscosity basaltic lava. The 2010 Eyjafjallajokull eruption disrupted European airspace via ash cloud — a useful example of economic impact without significant direct casualties.",
      reforge:{stem:"Compared to subduction zone volcanoes, eruptions at constructive boundaries rarely cause fatalities. The main reason is:",options:{A:"Constructive boundaries are always far offshore, beyond where populations live.",B:"Low-viscosity basaltic lava flows slowly, giving time for evacuation, unlike the explosive pyroclastic flows of composite volcanoes.",C:"Eruptions at constructive boundaries are detected months in advance by monitoring equipment.",D:"Governments permanently exclude populations from all areas near constructive boundaries."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"TEC-08",spec:"TECH",stem:"Japan's 2011 Tohoku earthquake killed ~16,000 people despite advanced preparation. This MOST effectively illustrates which principle?",
      options:{A:"Even well-prepared countries cannot fully mitigate extreme tectonic events",B:"Earthquake preparation in Japan has failed and needs complete redesign.",C:"Nuclear power stations should never be constructed near tectonic plate boundaries.",D:"The magnitude 9.0 earthquake was uniquely powerful and therefore impossible to prepare for."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-TEC-02",
      scaffold:"Japan's earthquake preparation DID work — buildings survived, early warning fired. Deaths came from the tsunami, which at 13m overtopped walls built for 5-6m events based on historical data. The lesson: hazard management must plan for worst-case scenarios, not just historical averages. This is the key limitation of purely reactive hazard planning.",
      reforge:{stem:"The key lesson from the 2011 Tohoku tsunami walls being overtopped is:",options:{A:"Tsunami walls are an ineffective technology and should be replaced with other measures.",B:"Hazard management must plan for worst-case scenarios, not historical averages, since underestimating potential magnitude creates critical vulnerability.",C:"All coastal communities in Japan should be permanently relocated to inland areas.",D:"International coordination is the most important factor in reducing tsunami casualties."},correct:"B"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-GEO-HAZ"] = {
  label: "Hazardous Earth",
  color: "#0f766e",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-01",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"At a destructive (subduction) plate boundary, what typically happens?",
      options:{A:"Two plates move apart, allowing magma to rise and form new crust",B:"Two continental plates collide and neither sinks, forming fold mountains but no volcanoes",C:"Two plates slide past each other with no vertical movement, causing earthquakes but rarely volcanoes",D:"The denser oceanic plate sinks beneath the lighter continental plate, causing earthquakes and volcanic activity"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-TEC-01",
      scaffold:"Destructive = one plate (denser, oceanic) subducts beneath the other — the friction and melting causes both earthquakes and volcanoes. B describes a conservative boundary, C a constructive boundary, D a collision boundary.",
      reforge:{stem:"At a conservative (transform) plate boundary, why do volcanoes rarely form even though earthquakes do?",options:{A:"Plates slide past each other with no subduction and no gap for magma to rise, so there's no route for magma to reach the surface",B:"Conservative boundaries only exist under the ocean",C:"Volcanoes only form at collision boundaries",D:"The plates move too slowly to generate any heat"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-02",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"The 2010 Haiti earthquake (magnitude 7.0) occurred on which type of plate boundary?",
      options:{A:"Conservative",B:"Collision",C:"Destructive",D:"Constructive"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-TEC-04",
      scaffold:"Conservative (transform) boundary — shallow focus (~13km) meant intense shaking at the surface despite the \"only\" 7.0 magnitude. Destructive boundaries involve subduction (like Japan's); constructive boundaries produce new crust; collision boundaries crumple two continental plates into mountains.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why did Haiti's shallow-focus earthquake cause such intense surface shaking despite its moderate 7.0 magnitude?",options:{A:"The depth of focus has no effect on surface shaking",B:"A shallow focus means less energy is absorbed by rock before reaching the surface, intensifying the shaking",C:"Shallow earthquakes are always more powerful in magnitude",D:"Haiti has unusually dense rock that amplifies all seismic waves"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-03",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"Japan's 2011 Tohoku earthquake (magnitude 9.0) killed roughly 18,000 people. Haiti's 2010 earthquake (magnitude 7.0) — far less powerful — killed over 100,000. Which best explains this?",
      options:{A:"Earthquakes near the Caribbean are always more dangerous than those in the Pacific",B:"Development level shapes vulnerability — magnitude alone doesn't determine impact.",C:"Haiti's earthquake released more energy than Japan's",D:"Japan has fewer people living near the coast"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-TEC-02",
      scaffold:"Development level shapes vulnerability: building codes, early warning systems, and emergency response — the exact contrast this pairing is designed to teach. Magnitude is a direct, logarithmic measure of energy release, so B is factually wrong; Japan actually has one of the world's highest coastal population densities.",
      reforge:{stem:"A magnitude 8 earthquake strikes both a high-income and a low-income country simultaneously. Which statement best predicts outcomes?",options:{A:"Both countries suffer similar casualties since the magnitude is identical",B:"The low-income country likely suffers far greater loss of life due to weaker buildings, slower emergency response, and less effective warning systems",C:"The high-income country suffers more because its infrastructure is more complex",D:"Casualties are determined entirely by population density, not development level"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-04",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"Mount Pinatubo's 1991 eruption (VEI 6) occurred on which plate boundary, and what was a major global effect?",
      options:{A:"A conservative boundary; the eruption had no measurable global effect",B:"A destructive boundary; the eruption caused a mega-tsunami across the Pacific",C:"A constructive boundary; the eruption caused global warming",D:"A destructive boundary; sulfur dioxide caused global temperatures to fall by 0.5°C."},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-HAZ-01",
      scaffold:"VEI 6, destructive boundary, and a global cooling effect — one eruption, measurable worldwide impact. Pinatubo sits on a destructive (subduction) boundary, not constructive or conservative, and large sulfur dioxide eruptions cause cooling (blocking sunlight), not warming.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why did Mount Pinatubo's 1991 eruption cause global temperatures to fall rather than rise?",options:{A:"Volcanic eruptions always cause long-term warming",B:"Pinatubo's location near the equator has no effect on climate",C:"The huge volume of sulfur dioxide released formed a haze that reflected sunlight back into space",D:"The ash cloud trapped heat close to the Earth's surface"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-05",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"Iceland's Eyjafjallajökull eruption in 2010 caused huge economic disruption but almost no deaths. Why?",
      options:{A:"Its ash cloud grounded European flights for days, costing over $1 billion.",B:"It occurred on a destructive boundary similar to Pinatubo",C:"The eruption was extremely explosive (VEI 6+) and destroyed nearby towns",D:"Iceland had no volcanic monitoring in place"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-HAZ-02",
      scaffold:"Constructive boundary, low death toll, huge economic cost — over 100,000 flights cancelled. Impact isn't only measured in lives lost. Eyjafjallajökull's 2010 eruption was far smaller than Pinatubo's VEI 6 and didn't destroy nearby settlements; Iceland also has extensive volcanic monitoring.",
      reforge:{stem:"A volcanic eruption causes almost no deaths but costs an industry over $1 billion. What does this show about measuring hazard impact?",options:{A:"An eruption with no deaths cannot be considered a hazard",B:"Impact can be economic and social, not just measured by death toll",C:"Low death tolls mean an eruption wasn't newsworthy",D:"Economic impact only matters for earthquakes, not volcanoes"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-06",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"A country builds earthquake-resistant buildings and holds regular evacuation drills. Which part of hazard management is this?",
      options:{A:"Monitoring",B:"Protection and planning",C:"Prediction",D:"Modifying the hazard itself"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MGT-01",
      scaffold:"Monitor, predict, protect, plan — four different strategies. Buildings and drills sit under protection and planning. Prediction means forecasting when/where a hazard will strike; monitoring means tracking real-time hazard data; humans can't modify an earthquake or eruption itself.",
      reforge:{stem:"Seismologists use satellite data to track ground deformation near a volcano before it erupts. Which part of hazard management is this?",options:{A:"Protection — reducing the vulnerability of buildings",B:"Planning — preparing an evacuation response",C:"Modification — changing the hazard itself",D:"Monitoring — tracking real-time hazard data to detect warning signs"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-07",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"Hurricane Katrina made landfall as a Category 3 storm, yet caused catastrophic flooding in New Orleans. What was the main direct cause of the flooding?",
      options:{A:"A secondary earthquake",B:"Katrina made direct landfall as a Category 5 storm",C:"Extremely high rainfall alone, unrelated to storm surge or levees",D:"Levees and flood walls protecting the city failed under the storm surge, flooding roughly 80% of New Orleans"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-HAZ-03",
      scaffold:"Levee failure under storm surge, not wind alone — New Orleans sits below sea level, making flood defences the critical factor. Katrina peaked at Category 5 over open water but weakened to a strong Category 3 by landfall; hurricanes don't trigger earthquakes.",
      reforge:{stem:"New Orleans sits largely below sea level. Why does this make storm surge, rather than wind speed, the greater flooding risk during a hurricane?",options:{A:"Wind speed always causes more flooding than storm surge",B:"Being below sea level has no effect on flood risk",C:"Seawater pushed inland by the surge has nowhere to drain and can overwhelm flood defences protecting low-lying land",D:"Storm surge only affects cities above sea level"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-08",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"Typhoon Haiyan (2013) had some of the highest landfall wind speeds ever recorded, yet its roughly 6,300 deaths were caused mainly by:",
      options:{A:"The storm surge overwhelmed Tacloban.",B:"Cold temperatures following the storm",C:"A secondary earthquake",D:"Wind damage to buildings alone"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-HAZ-04",
      scaffold:"Storm surge, not wind, was the deadliest element — the same lesson as Katrina, in a lower-income setting with a much higher death toll. Typhoons don't trigger earthquakes, and tropical storms are warm-weather systems, so cold temperatures weren't a factor.",
      reforge:{stem:"Two tropical storms have similar wind speeds, but one causes far more deaths. Storm surge height is the key difference. What does this tell us about tropical storm hazards?",options:{A:"Storm surge only matters for storms in the Pacific",B:"Storm surge, not wind speed, is often the deadliest element of a tropical storm, especially in low-lying coastal areas",C:"Wind speed is always the best predictor of deaths",D:"Death toll is unrelated to any physical characteristic of the storm"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-FB-01",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the explanation of a destructive plate boundary.",
      template:"At a destructive boundary, the ___ oceanic plate is subducted beneath the ___ continental plate. Friction and melting at the subduction zone cause both ___ and volcanic activity.",
      blanks:["denser","lighter","earthquakes"],
      bank:["denser","lighter","earthquakes","thinner","heavier","tsunamis"],
      tag:"MC-TEC-01",
      scaffold:"Destructive boundaries: the denser oceanic plate sinks beneath the lighter continental plate. The friction and melting this causes produces both earthquakes and volcanic activity — not just one or the other."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-FB-02",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about the 2010 Haiti earthquake.",
      template:"Haiti's 2010 earthquake occurred on a ___ boundary, where the Caribbean and North American plates slide past each other. Its ___ focus meant the shaking at the surface was especially intense.",
      blanks:["conservative","shallow"],
      bank:["conservative","shallow","destructive","deep"],
      tag:"MC-TEC-04",
      scaffold:"Haiti sits on a conservative (transform) boundary. Because the earthquake's focus was shallow, less energy was absorbed by rock before reaching the surface, intensifying the shaking despite a 'moderate' 7.0 magnitude."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-FB-03",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the comparison between the Tohoku and Haiti earthquakes.",
      template:"Japan's Tohoku earthquake was far more ___ than Haiti's, yet caused far fewer deaths, because Japan's greater ___ funded earthquake-resistant construction and an effective early warning system.",
      blanks:["powerful","wealth"],
      bank:["powerful","wealth","weak","population"],
      tag:"MC-TEC-02",
      scaffold:"Magnitude is a direct measure of energy release, so Tohoku (9.0) was far more powerful than Haiti (7.0). But wealth and development level — building codes, warning systems, emergency response — determine vulnerability, which is why Haiti's death toll was so much higher."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-FB-04",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about Mount Pinatubo's 1991 eruption.",
      template:"Mount Pinatubo erupted on a ___ boundary. The huge volume of ___ dioxide released formed a haze that reflected sunlight, causing global temperatures to ___ by about 0.5°C.",
      blanks:["destructive","sulfur","fall"],
      bank:["destructive","sulfur","fall","constructive","carbon","rise"],
      tag:"MC-HAZ-01",
      scaffold:"Pinatubo sits on a destructive boundary. Large sulfur dioxide eruptions form a haze that reflects sunlight back into space, causing global cooling rather than warming."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-FB-05",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about Eyjafjallajökull's 2010 eruption.",
      template:"Eyjafjallajökull sits on a ___ boundary and caused almost no deaths, but its fine ash cloud grounded European flights for days — showing that hazard impact can be ___, not just measured in lives lost.",
      blanks:["constructive","economic"],
      bank:["constructive","economic","destructive","social"],
      tag:"MC-HAZ-02",
      scaffold:"Iceland sits on a constructive boundary, producing relatively low-explosivity activity. Despite almost no deaths, the eruption cost the airline industry over $1 billion — proof that impact isn't only measured in deaths."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-FB-06",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about hazard management strategies.",
      template:"Building earthquake-resistant structures and running evacuation drills are examples of ___ and planning, while using satellite data to track ground deformation before an eruption is an example of ___.",
      blanks:["protection","monitoring"],
      bank:["protection","monitoring","prediction","modification"],
      tag:"MC-MGT-01",
      scaffold:"Hazard management has four strands: monitor, predict, protect, plan. Buildings and drills reduce vulnerability and prepare a response (protection/planning); tracking real-time data like ground deformation is monitoring, not prediction."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-FB-07",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about Hurricane Katrina's flooding.",
      template:"Katrina made landfall as 'only' a Category 3 storm, but New Orleans' ___ failed under the storm surge. Because the city sits below sea level, ___ rather than wind speed was the greater flooding risk.",
      blanks:["levees","storm surge"],
      bank:["levees","storm surge","bridges","wind"],
      tag:"MC-HAZ-03",
      scaffold:"New Orleans' levees and flood walls failed under the storm surge, flooding roughly 80% of the city. Because the city sits below sea level, seawater pushed inland by the surge had nowhere to drain — making storm surge, not wind, the critical factor."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-FB-08",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about Typhoon Haiyan's death toll.",
      template:"Despite record landfall wind speeds, most of Typhoon Haiyan's roughly 6,300 deaths were caused by the ___, a wall of seawater around 7.5 metres high that overwhelmed low-lying areas like ___.",
      blanks:["storm surge","Tacloban"],
      bank:["storm surge","Tacloban","wind","Manila"],
      tag:"MC-HAZ-04",
      scaffold:"As with Katrina, storm surge — not wind — was the deadliest element. A roughly 7.5m wall of seawater overwhelmed low-lying coastal areas like Tacloban, in a lower-income setting with a much higher death toll than Katrina."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-09",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"Which is the correct definition of the asthenosphere?",
      options:{A:"The molten layer of iron and nickel that surrounds the inner core",B:"The semi-molten layer at the top of the mantle which flows due to convection currents, moving the solid lithosphere above",C:"The thicker, less dense crust that makes up the continents",D:"The solid rock that lies on top of the mantle, broken up into tectonic plates"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-HAZ-KT-01",
      scaffold:"Layer order from the surface down: crust → lithosphere (crust + rigid upper mantle) → asthenosphere (semi-molten, flows via convection, carries the plates) → lower mantle → outer core (molten) → inner core (solid). B describes the lithosphere, C the outer core, D continental crust specifically.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which layer lies directly beneath the lithosphere and allows tectonic plates to move?",options:{A:"The asthenosphere, because it is semi-molten and flows via convection currents",B:"The inner core, because it is solid iron and nickel",C:"The continental crust, because it is thick and less dense",D:"The outer core, because it surrounds the inner core"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-10",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"A student mixes up 'lithosphere' and 'asthenosphere'. What is the key difference?",
      options:{A:"They are two names for exactly the same layer",B:"The lithosphere lies beneath the asthenosphere",C:"The lithosphere is the solid rock (crust plus rigid upper mantle) that sits on top of the semi-molten, flowing asthenosphere beneath it",D:"The asthenosphere is solid and the lithosphere is semi-molten"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-HAZ-KT-02",
      scaffold:"Lithosphere = solid, broken into tectonic plates. Asthenosphere = semi-molten, flows due to convection currents, and it's this flow that drags the lithospheric plates above it — the mechanism behind plate movement.",
      reforge:{stem:"A student says 'the lithosphere flows and carries the asthenosphere along with it.' What is wrong with this statement?",options:{A:"Neither layer is capable of movement",B:"The statement is completely correct",C:"The lithosphere is semi-molten, not the asthenosphere",D:"It has the relationship backwards — the semi-molten asthenosphere flows and drags the rigid lithosphere above it, not the other way round"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-11",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"What is subduction?",
      options:{A:"The rise in sea level caused by a tropical cyclone pushing water onto the shore",B:"A process where two plates slide past each other with no vertical movement",C:"A process at a destructive plate boundary where one plate is pushed below another and forced to sink into the asthenosphere",D:"A process where magma rises through the middle of a plate, away from any boundary"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-HAZ-KT-03",
      scaffold:"Subduction only happens at destructive (convergent) boundaries, and always to the denser oceanic plate. B describes a conservative boundary, C describes a hotspot, D describes a storm surge — a good example of how exam distractors often swap in a different key term's definition.",
      reforge:{stem:"Subduction always involves which type of crust sinking beneath the other at a destructive boundary?",options:{A:"Continental crust always sinks beneath oceanic crust",B:"Both plates sink together at equal rates",C:"Subduction can occur at a conservative boundary",D:"The denser oceanic crust sinks beneath the less dense continental (or another oceanic) crust"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-12",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"How does a hotspot differ from a normal volcanic plate boundary?",
      options:{A:"A hotspot is an area where unusually hot magma breaks through the middle of a plate, away from any plate boundary, whereas most volcanoes form at destructive or constructive boundaries",B:"A hotspot is another name for a destructive plate boundary",C:"A hotspot only occurs at conservative boundaries",D:"A hotspot is where two plates move apart"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-HAZ-KT-04",
      scaffold:"Hawaii is the classic hotspot example — it sits in the middle of the Pacific Plate, nowhere near a boundary. This is why hotspot volcanism catches students out: they expect all volcanoes to sit on boundaries.",
      reforge:{stem:"Hawaii's volcanoes sit in the middle of the Pacific Plate, far from any plate boundary. What best explains this?",options:{A:"A destructive plate boundary running directly under Hawaii",B:"A hotspot — unusually hot magma breaks through the middle of the plate and rises to the surface",C:"A conservative plate boundary running directly under Hawaii",D:"Hawaii cannot have volcanoes since it is mid-plate"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-13",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"What distinguishes primary effects of a hazard from secondary effects?",
      options:{A:"There is no real difference — the terms are interchangeable",B:"Primary effects only apply to earthquakes; secondary effects only apply to volcanoes",C:"Primary effects are caused directly by the hazard itself; secondary effects are a knock-on result of the primary effects",D:"Primary effects happen years after the hazard; secondary effects happen immediately"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-HAZ-KT-05",
      scaffold:"Example: an earthquake collapsing buildings is a primary effect; a fire caused by a ruptured gas main afterwards is a secondary effect. Don't confuse this pairing with immediate/long-term responses, which is about timing of the response, not the type of effect.",
      reforge:{stem:"A collapsed building is a primary effect of an earthquake. Which of these is a SECONDARY effect of the same earthquake?",options:{A:"The initial collapse of the building",B:"Cracks appearing in walls the moment the earthquake strikes",C:"The ground shaking itself",D:"A fire caused by a ruptured gas main after the shaking stops"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-14",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"A student writes 'immediate responses happen straight after a hazard, long-term responses happen much later' but can't give an example of either. Which pair correctly illustrates the distinction?",
      options:{A:"Immediate responses are always carried out by NGOs, long-term responses always by governments",B:"Immediate: search and rescue teams pulling survivors from rubble in the days after an earthquake. Long-term: rebuilding homes to a higher building code over the following years",C:"Immediate: rebuilding infrastructure over several years. Long-term: emergency shelters provided within hours",D:"Both immediate and long-term responses happen in the same week"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-HAZ-KT-06",
      scaffold:"Immediate responses = hours to about a week (search and rescue, emergency shelter, first aid). Long-term responses = weeks to years afterwards (rebuilding, improved building codes, economic recovery). B has the timeframes swapped.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which pair correctly matches an example to immediate and long-term responses after a hurricane?",options:{A:"Immediate: emergency shelters set up within days. Long-term: rebuilding sea defences to a higher standard over the following years",B:"Both examples describe long-term responses",C:"Immediate: rebuilding sea defences over several years. Long-term: emergency shelters within days",D:"Both examples describe immediate responses"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-15",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"Which statement correctly defines hazard risk?",
      options:{A:"A hazard caused by processes on the land, as opposed to the atmosphere",B:"The magnitude of a hazard on the Richter scale",C:"The total economic cost of a hazard once it has occurred",D:"The probability that a natural hazard will negatively affect a population"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-HAZ-KT-07",
      scaffold:"Hazard risk is about probability of harm, not just the physical size of the event — it depends on hazard, exposure and vulnerability together. D is actually the definition of a geological hazard, a different term entirely.",
      reforge:{stem:"Two hazards have the same magnitude, but one occurs in a densely populated, poorly-prepared area. What best describes the difference in hazard risk?",options:{A:"Hazard risk is higher in the densely populated, poorly-prepared area, since risk depends on probability of harm to a population, not physical magnitude alone",B:"Hazard risk is identical in both cases since magnitude is identical",C:"Hazard risk only depends on the Richter scale reading",D:"Hazard risk cannot differ between two hazards of the same magnitude"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-16",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"What is the correct definition of the Inter-Tropical Convergence Zone (ITCZ)?",
      options:{A:"The point where the Hadley and Ferrel cells meet at the poles",B:"A belt at 30° north and south where air permanently sinks and no rain falls",C:"An area surrounding the equator where global winds converge, causing an area of low pressure with rainy conditions",D:"A high-pressure belt found only over the Sahara Desert"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-HAZ-KT-08",
      scaffold:"The ITCZ sits roughly over the equator, where the Hadley cells of each hemisphere converge — rising warm, moist air creates low pressure and heavy rainfall, and it's the ITCZ's seasonal shift north and south that drives many tropical wet/dry seasons.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which statement correctly explains why the ITCZ brings heavy rainfall near the equator?",options:{A:"Cold, dry air sinks at the equator, creating high pressure and no rainfall",B:"Warm, moist air converges and rises there, creating an area of low pressure and rainy conditions",C:"The ITCZ is a permanently fixed feature that never shifts throughout the year",D:"The ITCZ only exists over oceans, never over land"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-17",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"A Hadley cell and a Ferrel cell are both part of atmospheric circulation. What is the key difference between them?",
      options:{A:"A Ferrel cell is found only over oceans, a Hadley cell only over land",B:"They are two names for the same atmospheric cell",C:"A Hadley cell only exists in the Southern Hemisphere, a Ferrel cell only in the Northern Hemisphere",D:"In a Hadley cell, air rises at the equator and sinks at 30°; in a Ferrel cell, air rises at 60° and sinks at around 30°, alongside air arriving from the equator"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-HAZ-KT-09",
      scaffold:"Three-cell model, low to high latitude: Hadley cell (0°–30°) → Ferrel cell (30°–60°) → Polar cell (60°–90°). Each cell's rising-air edge produces low pressure and rain; each sinking-air edge produces high pressure and dry conditions — this is why deserts cluster around 30° north and south.",
      reforge:{stem:"In the three-cell model of atmospheric circulation, where does air sink to create the world's major hot desert belts?",options:{A:"At 60° north and south, where the Ferrel and Polar cells meet",B:"At the equator, where the ITCZ is located",C:"Around 30° north and south, where air from the Hadley and Ferrel cells both sink",D:"Only at the poles, at 90° north and south"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-18",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"A tropical storm forms in the Atlantic and moves towards the Caribbean. A very similar storm forms in the western Pacific and moves towards the Philippines. What are these two storms called respectively?",
      options:{A:"Hurricane; typhoon",B:"Cyclone; hurricane",C:"Typhoon; hurricane",D:"Hurricane; cyclone"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-HAZ-KT-10",
      scaffold:"Same phenomenon (tropical cyclone), different regional names: hurricane (USA, Latin America, Caribbean), typhoon (India, Japan, Philippines), cyclone (Oceania, Madagascar). Examiners sometimes ask you to name the correct regional term for a given location.",
      reforge:{stem:"A storm forms in the western Pacific and moves towards Japan and the Philippines. What is this storm correctly called?",options:{A:"A hurricane",B:"A typhoon",C:"A cyclone",D:"A tornado"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-19",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"Inside a tropical cyclone, what is the difference between the eye and the eyewall?",
      options:{A:"The eyewall is at the centre of the storm; the eye surrounds it",B:"The eye has the strongest winds; the eyewall is calm",C:"They are the same feature, just named differently by different countries",D:"The eye is an area of extremely low pressure and calm conditions at the centre; the eyewall surrounding it has the most intense winds and heaviest rain"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-HAZ-KT-11",
      scaffold:"Counter-intuitive but commonly tested: the very centre (the eye) is the calmest part of the storm, while the ring immediately around it (the eyewall) is the most violent and destructive part.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which statement best describes conditions inside the eye of a tropical cyclone?",options:{A:"High pressure and freezing temperatures",B:"Extremely low pressure and calm conditions, in contrast to the violent winds of the surrounding eyewall",C:"The strongest winds and heaviest rainfall in the entire storm",D:"Identical wind speed and rainfall to the eyewall"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-20",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"What is the difference between the greenhouse effect and the enhanced greenhouse effect?",
      options:{A:"The greenhouse effect is the natural process that warms the Earth's surface; the enhanced greenhouse effect is extra warming caused by increased greenhouse gas emissions from human activity",B:"They are identical processes with different names used in different countries",C:"The greenhouse effect is caused by humans; the enhanced greenhouse effect is entirely natural",D:"The enhanced greenhouse effect only happens at the poles"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-HAZ-KT-12",
      scaffold:"Without any greenhouse effect, Earth would be too cold for life — it's natural and essential. The 'enhanced' version is the problem: burning fossil fuels adds extra greenhouse gases, trapping more energy and raising global temperatures faster than natural systems can adjust to.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why is the enhanced greenhouse effect considered a problem, when the natural greenhouse effect is essential for life on Earth?",options:{A:"The natural greenhouse effect no longer exists",B:"Both effects are identical and equally beneficial",C:"Human activity has added extra greenhouse gases, trapping more energy and warming the Earth's surface faster than natural systems can adjust to",D:"The enhanced greenhouse effect is entirely natural and unrelated to human activity"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-21",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"Which of these is a correct source of evidence for past climate change, and what does it show?",
      options:{A:"Storm surge records — a measure of past tropical cyclone wind speed only",B:"Ice cores — cylinders of ice extracted from ice sheets or glaciers, analysed for trapped air bubbles and dust to reconstruct past environmental conditions",C:"The Richter scale — a logarithmic measure of past earthquake magnitudes",D:"The Coriolis Effect — the effect of Earth's rotation on wind, unrelated to past climate"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-HAZ-KT-13",
      scaffold:"Ice cores and tree rings are the two named proxy evidence sources in this glossary. Ice cores trap air bubbles (past atmospheric CO2) and dust layers; tree rings record annual growth conditions (wider rings = warmer/wetter years). B, C and D are unrelated tectonic or atmospheric terms used as distractors.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why are ice cores considered a reliable source of evidence for past climate change?",options:{A:"They measure earthquake magnitude, not climate",B:"They can only be extracted from tropical rainforests",C:"They only show evidence from the last ten years",D:"They trap air bubbles and dust from the time the ice formed, allowing scientists to analyse past atmospheric composition and conditions"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-22",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"Milankovitch Cycles is an umbrella term. Which three processes does it cover?",
      options:{A:"Eccentricity (the shape of Earth's orbit), obliquity (axial tilt), and precession (the wobble of Earth's axis)",B:"Subduction, convection currents, and plate boundaries",C:"Primary effects, secondary effects, and long-term responses",D:"The Hadley, Ferrel and Polar cells"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-HAZ-KT-14",
      scaffold:"These three astronomical cycles change how much solar energy reaches Earth and where, over tens of thousands of years, and are a key natural cause of long-term climate change — distinct from the human-driven enhanced greenhouse effect.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which of the following is one of the three named Milankovitch Cycles?",options:{A:"Subduction — a plate sinking beneath another plate",B:"The ITCZ — the zone where global winds converge",C:"Obliquity — the changing tilt of the Earth's axis, between roughly 21.5° and 24.5°",D:"The Coriolis Effect — the effect of Earth's rotation on wind"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-23",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"A student says earthquakes at conservative boundaries are 'less dangerous' because no volcanoes form there. Why is this wrong?",
      options:{A:"Conservative boundaries cannot produce earthquakes",B:"Conservative boundaries only occur under oceans",C:"Volcanoes are the only serious tectonic hazard",D:"Earthquake risk depends on magnitude, depth, population density and preparedness, not whether volcanoes are present"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-TEC-08",
      scaffold:"Conservative boundaries can produce very damaging shallow earthquakes because plates lock, stress builds, then is released suddenly. Hazard risk is not just the physical event: exposure, vulnerability, building quality and preparedness control the scale of disaster.",
      reforge:{stem:"A magnitude 7 earthquake hits a densely populated city with weak building codes on a conservative boundary. Which statement is most accurate?",options:{A:"It cannot be serious because there is no volcano",B:"It will only create lava flows",C:"It is automatically safer than any constructive boundary",D:"It may be highly destructive because shallow shaking, exposure and vulnerability can combine to produce high risk"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-24",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"Why do tropical cyclones usually weaken quickly after moving over land?",
      options:{A:"They lose their supply of warm ocean water and gain more friction over land",B:"The Coriolis effect stops completely over land",C:"Air pressure rises because mountains create earthquakes",D:"They turn into anticyclones as soon as they cross the coast"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-HAZ-15",
      scaffold:"Tropical cyclones are powered by heat and moisture from warm seas, normally above about 27°C. Once over land, that energy source is cut off and surface friction disrupts the storm's circulation, so wind speeds usually fall.",
      reforge:{stem:"A tropical cyclone crosses a coastline and moves inland. Which change is most likely after several hours?",options:{A:"It weakens because warm ocean moisture is cut off and friction increases",B:"It strengthens because land is warmer than the sea",C:"It becomes a tectonic hazard",D:"It stops rotating because Earth no longer spins beneath it"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-25",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"A student writes: 'Monitoring means predicting the exact date of an earthquake.' What is the correction?",
      options:{A:"Monitoring only means rebuilding after a disaster",B:"Monitoring collects evidence such as seismic activity or gas emissions to assess risk, but exact earthquake prediction is not currently possible",C:"Monitoring guarantees that all hazards can be stopped",D:"Monitoring is the same as evacuation"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MGT-02",
      scaffold:"Hazard monitoring reduces risk by tracking warning signs and informing preparation, but it does not remove uncertainty. Volcanic eruptions can sometimes be forecast from signs such as gas emissions and ground deformation; earthquakes cannot be predicted to an exact date and time.",
      reforge:{stem:"Scientists detect increasing sulphur dioxide emissions and ground swelling around a volcano. What does this monitoring allow them to do?",options:{A:"Cancel the eruption",B:"Assess rising risk and warn authorities, while accepting uncertainty remains",C:"Predict the exact minute of an earthquake",D:"Prove the volcano is extinct"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-HAZ-26",spec:"GCSE-HAZ",stem:"Which statement best explains why climate change can increase tropical cyclone hazard risk?",
      options:{A:"Climate change creates new plate boundaries",B:"Tropical cyclones only form in cold polar seas",C:"Warmer oceans can provide more energy and moisture, increasing the potential intensity and rainfall of storms",D:"Climate change prevents storm surges from forming"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-HAZ-16",
      scaffold:"Tropical cyclones need warm ocean water. Climate change does not mean every storm becomes stronger, but warmer seas and a warmer atmosphere can increase rainfall intensity and the potential for very intense storms, while sea-level rise worsens storm-surge flooding.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why might a similar tropical cyclone cause worse coastal flooding in a warmer future climate?",options:{A:"Earthquakes become more common in the eyewall",B:"The Coriolis effect disappears",C:"Sea level rise can make storm surge start from a higher baseline, and warmer air can hold more moisture",D:"Storms no longer need warm ocean water"},correct:"C"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-GEO-UKLAND"] = {
  label: "UK's Evolving Physical Landscape",
  color: "#0f766e",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-01",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"On the Holderness Coast, waves attack a section of soft boulder clay cliff by compressing air into cracks until the rock shatters. What process is this?",
      options:{A:"Abrasion — waves hurling sand and pebbles at the cliff, sandpapering it away",B:"Hydraulic action",C:"Solution — soluble rock dissolving in seawater",D:"Attrition — rocks wearing each other down as they collide"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-COAST-01",
      scaffold:"Hydraulic action is the sheer force of water and compressed air breaking rock apart — no rock-on-rock contact needed, which is why it's so effective on Holderness's soft, unconsolidated boulder clay. Abrasion needs the waves to be carrying material to throw at the cliff; attrition happens between loose rock fragments, not to the cliff face itself; solution only affects soluble rock like limestone or chalk, not boulder clay.",
      reforge:{stem:"Further along the Holderness coast, waves carrying sand and shingle scrape against the cliff face, wearing it away like sandpaper. What process is this?",options:{A:"Solution — rock dissolving",B:"Attrition — pebbles wearing each other down",C:"Abrasion — material carried by waves grinding against the cliff",D:"Hydraulic action — compressed air shattering rock"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-02",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"A headland and bay coastline forms where alternating bands of resistant rock (like chalk) and less resistant rock (like clay) meet the sea. What causes the headlands to form?",
      options:{A:"Differential erosion — the less resistant rock is eroded faster, leaving the resistant rock standing out as a headland",B:"Headlands are always made of the same rock type as the bays next to them",C:"Longshore drift deposits sand only in front of resistant rock",D:"The resistant rock is eroded faster, leaving low-lying bays behind"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-COAST-02",
      scaffold:"Differential erosion: softer rock (clay) erodes quickly, carving out a bay, while harder rock (chalk or limestone) resists erosion and juts out as a headland. It's the rate of erosion, not the rock forming faster, that creates the shape — and headlands and bays are made of different rock types by definition.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why do headlands, once formed, typically erode more slowly than the bays beside them?",options:{A:"Bays receive no wave energy at all",B:"Headlands are protected from waves entirely",C:"Headlands and bays erode at exactly the same rate",D:"They're made of more resistant rock, which withstands erosion better than the softer rock of the bay"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-03",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"A student describes a headland's erosional sequence as: 'crack, then arch, then stack, then stump.' What has been missed out?",
      options:{A:"A spit, which forms between the crack and the arch",B:"A stack always forms before the cave",C:"A cave — hydraulic action and abrasion widen a crack into a cave before it erodes through to form an arch",D:"Nothing — the sequence is complete as stated"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-COAST-03",
      scaffold:"The correct sequence is crack → cave → arch → stack → stump. Waves widen a weakness into a cave; continued erosion breaks through the headland to form an arch; the arch collapses, isolating a stack; the stack erodes down to a stump. A spit is a depositional feature, not part of this erosional sequence.",
      reforge:{stem:"An arch's roof eventually collapses because it can no longer support its own weight, isolating a tall column of rock from the headland. What landform has just been created?",options:{A:"A cave",B:"A stump",C:"A spit",D:"A stack"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-04",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"Waves approach a beach at an angle, but the backwash always returns straight down the beach under gravity. What landform does this longshore drift process build over time at a river mouth or bend in the coastline?",
      options:{A:"A stack",B:"A spit",C:"A headland",D:"A wave-cut platform"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-COAST-04",
      scaffold:"Longshore drift moves material along the coast in a zigzag (swash at an angle, backwash straight down the beach). Where the coastline changes direction — like a river mouth — the material keeps being deposited in the same direction, building out a spit, sometimes with a curved/hooked end where wave refraction bends it. Headlands and stacks are erosional features; a wave-cut platform is the flat rock left behind at the base of an eroding cliff.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why does a spit often develop a hooked or curved end?",options:{A:"A change in the dominant wind/wave direction curves the end of the spit inward",B:"River currents always create a 90-degree bend",C:"Spits are always straight and never curve",D:"The hook is caused by erosion, not deposition"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-05",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"At Mappleton on the Holderness Coast, rock groynes and a rock revetment were built to protect the village and the coast road. A student says: 'This solved erosion for the whole Holderness coast.' Why is this wrong?",
      options:{A:"Holderness has no erosion problem elsewhere, so protecting one village was unnecessary",B:"Groynes starve beaches further along the coast of sediment, so erosion speeds up nearby.",C:"Hard engineering has no effect on longshore drift",D:"Groynes and revetments stop all erosion everywhere on Holderness"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-COAST-05",
      scaffold:"Groynes trap sediment moving by longshore drift to protect one stretch of coast — but that starves beaches further along, which lose their natural buffer and erode faster. This is exactly why Holderness (one of Europe's fastest-eroding coastlines, ~2m/year on average) still has undefended sections like Cowden losing land rapidly despite Mappleton's defences.",
      reforge:{stem:"A village protects its coastline with groynes. A neighbouring, undefended village further down the coast reports erosion rates increasing afterwards. What's the most likely explanation?",options:{A:"The groynes trapped sediment that would otherwise have travelled by longshore drift to replenish the neighbouring beach",B:"Groynes increase wave energy along the whole coastline",C:"Hard engineering always reduces erosion everywhere, with no local trade-offs",D:"The two villages' erosion rates are unrelated to each other"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-06",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"A river's long profile runs from source to mouth. A student says: 'Erosion is the dominant process everywhere along a river.' Why is this wrong?",
      options:{A:"A river's energy stays exactly the same from source to mouth",B:"Deposition dominates everywhere along a river",C:"Erosion and deposition never both occur on the same river",D:"Vertical erosion dominates upstream; deposition dominates downstream."},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-RIV-01",
      scaffold:"A river's energy and gradient are highest near the source, favouring vertical erosion (cutting down, forming V-shaped valleys/waterfalls). Further downstream, gradient flattens, energy falls, and the river deposits the sediment it's carrying, building features like floodplains. It's a gradual shift, not an on/off switch, and both processes can occur at once in different parts of the channel.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why do meanders (bends) typically develop in a river's middle to lower course rather than close to the source?",options:{A:"Meanders form randomly with no link to gradient or energy",B:"Meanders only form where a river is at its steepest",C:"Lower gradient and energy there allow lateral (sideways) erosion and deposition to dominate over the vertical erosion seen upstream",D:"Rivers only erode in their upper course and never downstream"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-07",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"On the outside bend of a meander, the river is deep and erosion dominates; on the inside bend, the river is shallow and deposition dominates. A student says this is just random. What actually causes it?",
      options:{A:"Helicoidal flow — a corkscrew current that erodes the outside bend, deposits on the inside.",B:"Meanders have no consistent pattern of erosion or deposition",C:"Erosion only ever happens on the inside of a bend",D:"The river bed is naturally deeper on the outside bend with no link to current or flow"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-RIV-02",
      scaffold:"Helicoidal (corkscrew) flow explains the asymmetry: faster current and greater erosive power hits the outside bend (undercutting it into a river cliff), while slower-moving water on the inside bend deposits sediment as a gently sloping slip-off slope. Over time this exaggerates the bend until it can be cut off, forming an oxbow lake.",
      reforge:{stem:"Over many years, a meander's neck becomes so narrow that the river cuts straight across it during a flood, leaving the old bend isolated. What landform has just formed?",options:{A:"A spit",B:"A floodplain",C:"A waterfall",D:"An oxbow lake"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-08",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"In July 2007, Tewkesbury (at the confluence of the Severn and Avon) flooded severely, cutting the town off and flooding thousands of homes. A student says this was caused purely by 'too much rain.' What else made it worse?",
      options:{A:"Rainfall was the only factor and location made no difference",B:"Saturated ground and its river confluence location meant floodwater overwhelmed the town.",C:"Tewkesbury has no rivers nearby, so flooding must have come from the sea",D:"The floods were caused entirely by a dam failure upstream"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-RIV-03",
      scaffold:"2007 was one of the wettest summers on record — but Tewkesbury's flood risk is compounded by its confluence location, where the Severn and Avon's floodwaters combine, and by antecedent (prior) rainfall leaving the ground saturated with nowhere for new rainfall to infiltrate, forcing more of it into rapid surface runoff.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why does a settlement located at the confluence of two rivers face a greater flood risk than one on a single river of similar size?",options:{A:"Two rivers meeting always cancel out each other's flood risk",B:"Confluences always have lower rainfall than other locations",C:"Floodwater from both rivers can arrive and combine at the same point, increasing the total discharge the settlement must cope with",D:"Confluence location has no bearing on flood risk"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-27",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"A stretch of coast has a wide beach at low tide but a narrow beach after a winter storm. Which explanation best fits this change?",
      options:{A:"Storm waves can move beach sediment offshore, narrowing the beach temporarily",B:"The beach must have changed from a depositional landform into an erosional landform",C:"Longshore drift stops whenever wave energy increases during a storm",D:"Beach width is controlled only by the geology of the cliff behind it, not by wave conditions"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-COAST-06",
      scaffold:"High-energy storm waves have greater erosive power and can move sand and shingle offshore into bars or along the coast. Some sediment may return during calmer conditions, so a narrow post-storm beach does not necessarily mean permanent cliff retreat.",
      reforge:{stem:"What is the most likely effect of calmer, constructive waves after a storm has removed beach material?",options:{A:"They increase cliff undercutting by concentrating all energy at the cliff base",B:"They can move sediment up the beach and rebuild a wider berm through deposition",C:"They dissolve all beach sediment before it can be moved",D:"They stop sediment movement because swash is absent"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-28",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"Why can urbanisation increase flood risk during heavy rainfall?",
      options:{A:"Urban surfaces always absorb more rainfall than woodland soils",B:"Concrete and tarmac reduce infiltration, sending water rapidly into drains",C:"Buildings lower river discharge by storing all rainfall permanently",D:"Urban areas prevent precipitation from reaching the drainage basin during storms"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-RIV-04",
      scaffold:"Impermeable surfaces reduce infiltration and interception. Water reaches drains and the river quickly, shortening lag time and increasing peak discharge. The exact response also depends on rainfall intensity, basin size and drainage design.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which change would most likely lengthen a drainage basin's lag time?",options:{A:"Replacing grass with more tarmac",B:"Straightening every tributary channel",C:"Adding woodland and permeable surfaces that intercept and infiltrate rainfall",D:"Removing wetlands from the floodplain"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-29",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"A floodplain is useful for farming but can be risky for new housing. Which planning response best manages this conflict?",
      options:{A:"Build all new homes on the lowest part of the floodplain",B:"Use floodplain zoning to restrict vulnerable development and keep higher-risk land for suitable uses",C:"Remove every riverbank so water can spread into urban areas",D:"Allow development wherever land prices are lowest"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-RIV-05",
      scaffold:"Floodplain zoning uses flood-risk maps to guide land use. Highly vulnerable development is restricted in areas with frequent or severe flooding, while parks, grazing or playing fields may be more appropriate. It reduces exposure rather than pretending the hazard can be eliminated.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why might a playing field be considered more suitable than a hospital on a high-risk floodplain?",options:{A:"A playing field is less vulnerable to disruption and can tolerate occasional flooding better",B:"Hospitals never need road access during floods",C:"Playing fields prevent all floodwater from entering the river",D:"Hospitals are always built below sea level"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-30",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"Which combination best explains why a river may have greater discharge downstream?",
      options:{A:"The channel becomes narrower and rainfall disappears",B:"Tributaries add water, while the basin collects runoff from a larger area",C:"The river loses all groundwater as it approaches the mouth",D:"Discharge rises only because bedload becomes smaller"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-RIV-06",
      scaffold:"Downstream, tributaries join the main channel and the river drains an increasingly large catchment, so total discharge generally increases. Local abstraction, evaporation or infiltration can interrupt the pattern, so this is a general trend rather than an unbreakable rule.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which observation would be evidence that a downstream discharge trend has been interrupted by human activity?",options:{A:"A tributary joins the river",B:"The catchment becomes larger",C:"A reservoir abstracts water so discharge is lower just downstream of the dam",D:"The river reaches a wider floodplain"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-FB-01",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about coastal erosion processes on Holderness.",
      template:"___ ___ is the sheer force of water (and compressed air) breaking rock apart, while ___ is material carried by waves grinding against the cliff like sandpaper.",
      blanks:["Hydraulic","action","abrasion"],
      bank:["Hydraulic","action","abrasion","attrition","solution","weathering"],
      tag:"MC-COAST-01",
      scaffold:"Hydraulic action is water and compressed air shattering rock with no rock-on-rock contact needed. Abrasion is different — it needs the waves to be carrying sand/pebbles to throw at the cliff, sandpapering it away."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-FB-02",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about headland and bay formation.",
      template:"Headlands and bays form through ___ erosion: ___ resistant rock like clay erodes quickly to form a bay, while more ___ rock like chalk juts out as a headland.",
      blanks:["differential","less","resistant"],
      bank:["differential","less","resistant","uniform","more","soluble"],
      tag:"MC-COAST-02",
      scaffold:"Differential erosion means different rock types erode at different rates when exposed to the same wave energy — the softer, less resistant rock (clay) is worn back into a bay while the harder, more resistant rock (chalk) survives as a headland."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-FB-03",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the erosional sequence that forms a stack.",
      template:"The sequence runs: crack → ___ → ___ → stack → ___.",
      blanks:["cave","arch","stump"],
      bank:["cave","arch","stump","spit","platform","bay"],
      tag:"MC-COAST-03",
      scaffold:"Crack → cave → arch → stack → stump. Hydraulic action and abrasion widen a crack into a cave; continued erosion breaks through to form an arch; the arch's roof collapses, isolating a stack; the stack erodes down to a stump."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-FB-04",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about spit formation.",
      template:"___ ___ moves material along the coast in a zigzag pattern. Where the coastline changes direction, such as at a river mouth, material builds out into a ___.",
      blanks:["Longshore","drift","spit"],
      bank:["Longshore","drift","spit","stack","headland","platform"],
      tag:"MC-COAST-04",
      scaffold:"Longshore drift: swash carries material up the beach at an angle (driven by prevailing wind), backwash drags it straight back down under gravity — the net zigzag movement builds a spit wherever the coastline suddenly changes direction."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-FB-05",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about hard engineering at Mappleton on the Holderness Coast.",
      template:"Rock groynes at Mappleton trap sediment moved by longshore drift, protecting the village — but this ___ beaches further along the coast of sediment, meaning ___ actually speeds up in those undefended areas.",
      blanks:["starves","erosion"],
      bank:["starves","erosion","supplies","deposition"],
      tag:"MC-COAST-05",
      scaffold:"Groynes trap sediment travelling by longshore drift to protect one stretch of coast, but that starves beaches further down the coast of the sediment they'd otherwise receive — those undefended sections, like Cowden, then erode faster than before."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-FB-06",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about a river's long profile.",
      template:"In the upper course, high energy and steep gradient favour ___ erosion. Further downstream, gradient and energy fall, and ___ becomes dominant as the river drops its sediment.",
      blanks:["vertical","deposition"],
      bank:["vertical","deposition","lateral","erosion"],
      tag:"MC-RIV-01",
      scaffold:"Vertical erosion (cutting downwards, forming steep V-shaped valleys and waterfalls) dominates where gradient and energy are highest, near the source. As the river flows towards its mouth, falling energy means it increasingly deposits sediment instead of eroding."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-FB-07",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about meander formation.",
      template:"___ flow is a corkscrew-like current that erodes the ___ bend of a meander, undercutting it into a river cliff, while depositing sediment as a slip-off slope on the ___ bend.",
      blanks:["Helicoidal","outside","inside"],
      bank:["Helicoidal","outside","inside","vertical","upper","lower"],
      tag:"MC-RIV-02",
      scaffold:"Helicoidal (corkscrew) flow moves faster on the outside of a bend, eroding it into a steep river cliff, then spirals across the channel to deposit sediment more slowly on the inside bend as a gently sloping slip-off slope — the asymmetry that drives a meander to grow."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-FB-08",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about the July 2007 Tewkesbury floods.",
      template:"Tewkesbury sits at the ___ of the Severn and Avon, so floodwater from both rivers arrived together. The ground was already ___ from a very wet preceding month, increasing surface ___.",
      blanks:["confluence","saturated","runoff"],
      bank:["confluence","saturated","runoff","source","dry","infiltration"],
      tag:"MC-RIV-03",
      scaffold:"Tewkesbury's confluence location meant floodwater from both the Severn and Avon combined at the same point. Ground already saturated from a very wet preceding month couldn't absorb (infiltrate) new rainfall, forcing more of it into rapid surface runoff and worsening the flood."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-09",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"A student mixes up abrasion and attrition. What is the correct distinction?",
      options:{A:"Abrasion is where loose material sandpapers the walls and floor of a river, cliff or glacier; attrition is where rocks carried by the water bang against each other, chipping away to become smaller and smoother",B:"Abrasion only happens in rivers, attrition only happens at the coast",C:"Attrition wears away the cliff face directly; abrasion only affects the rocks being transported",D:"Abrasion and attrition are two names for the same erosional process"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-UKLAND-KT-01",
      scaffold:"Both are erosion processes but act on different things: abrasion is material scraping against the landform itself (the cliff or riverbed), while attrition is transported rocks scraping against each other, reducing their own size and making them rounder — this is why beach pebbles are smoother than upstream river rocks.",
      reforge:{stem:"Sea spray flings loose sand and shingle at a cliff face, gradually wearing it away. Which erosion process is this?",options:{A:"Attrition — rocks wear each other down as they collide",B:"Hydraulic action — air compressed into cracks breaks the rock apart",C:"Solution — acid in the water dissolves the rock",D:"Abrasion — loose material sandpapers the cliff surface"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-10",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"What is hydraulic action, and where does it occur?",
      options:{A:"The dissolving of chemical compounds in a rock by weak acid in rainwater",B:"The pressure of air compressed into cracks in a rock face, which weakens and breaks the rock apart — it's a coastal erosion process most powerful during storms",C:"The rolling of large boulders along a riverbed",D:"The transportation of sediment along a beach by the prevailing wind"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-UKLAND-KT-02",
      scaffold:"Hydraulic action is purely a mechanical, force-based process — air trapped in cracks is compressed by incoming waves and then releases suddenly, widening the crack. It's distinct from corrasion (material flung at the cliff) and solution (chemical dissolving), which are the other coastal erosion processes.",
      reforge:{stem:"During a storm, waves compress air into cracks in a cliff face, and the sudden release widens the crack. Which process is this?",options:{A:"Attrition",B:"Longshore drift",C:"Hydraulic action",D:"Traction"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-11",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"A student can't tell freeze-thaw weathering apart from mechanical erosion like hydraulic action. What is the key difference?",
      options:{A:"Freeze-thaw only happens at the coast; hydraulic action only happens on mountains",B:"Freeze-thaw is a form of weathering — it breaks rock apart in place using water freezing and expanding in cracks — while hydraulic action is a form of erosion, which involves the rock being worn away and removed by a moving force like waves",C:"They are identical processes with different names",D:"Freeze-thaw requires no water at all"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-UKLAND-KT-03",
      scaffold:"Weathering breaks rock down where it is, with no transport involved (freeze-thaw, biological, chemical weathering are all sub-aerial — they happen 'in the open air'). Erosion involves a moving agent (water, wind, ice) wearing away and carrying material off. Freeze-thaw often weakens a cliff first, making it more vulnerable to erosion afterwards.",
      reforge:{stem:"A cliff face is weakened over winter by water repeatedly freezing and expanding in its cracks, before waves finally erode the loosened rock in spring. Which two processes does this describe, in order?",options:{A:"Only erosion is involved — no weathering takes place",B:"Erosion first, followed by freeze-thaw weathering",C:"Only weathering is involved — no erosion takes place",D:"Freeze-thaw weathering first, followed by erosion"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-12",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"A coastline has bands of resistant and less resistant rock running perpendicular (at a right angle) to the shore. What type of coast is this, and what landform does it typically produce?",
      options:{A:"A discordant coast, which always prevents any erosion from occurring",B:"A concordant coast, where rock bands run perpendicular to the shore",C:"A discordant coast — differential erosion of the alternating rock bands produces a sequence of headlands (resistant rock) and bays (less resistant rock)",D:"A concordant coast, which produces a straight, unbroken cliff line with no headlands or bays"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-UKLAND-KT-04",
      scaffold:"Discordant = perpendicular bands = headland-and-bay coastline (e.g. Swanage). Concordant = parallel bands = a smoother, straighter coastline because the same resistant rock band runs continuously along the front, protecting what's behind it (e.g. Lulworth Cove's cove formed where the outer resistant band was breached).",
      reforge:{stem:"A coastline has bands of the same resistant rock running parallel to the shore, protecting the land behind it. What type of coast is this, and what does it typically produce?",options:{A:"A concordant coast, which tends to produce a smoother, straighter coastline",B:"A discordant coast, which always produces headlands and bays",C:"A discordant coast, which produces a smooth, unbroken cliff line",D:"A concordant coast, which always prevents any coastal landforms from forming"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-13",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"What is the difference between hard engineering and soft engineering as approaches to coastal management?",
      options:{A:"Hard engineering only protects rivers; soft engineering only protects coasts",B:"Hard engineering uses artificial concrete structures (e.g. sea walls, groynes, rock armour) to halt coastal recession; soft engineering uses natural materials and sustainable approaches (e.g. beach nourishment, managed retreat) to work with natural processes",C:"They are the same strategy carried out at different times of year",D:"Soft engineering always costs more than hard engineering"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-UKLAND-KT-05",
      scaffold:"Hard engineering gives strong, immediate protection but is expensive, unsightly and can increase erosion elsewhere along the coast (starving beaches downdrift of sediment). Soft engineering works with natural sediment processes and is often cheaper long-term but offers less immediate certainty.",
      reforge:{stem:"A coastal town chooses beach nourishment and managed retreat instead of a sea wall. What type of approach is this?",options:{A:"Neither approach counts as coastal management",B:"This combination is not possible under coastal management strategies",C:"Hard engineering — using artificial concrete structures",D:"Soft engineering — using natural materials and sustainable approaches"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-14",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"How does a groyne reduce coastal erosion?",
      options:{A:"It is a low wall built perpendicular to the shore that traps sediment moved by longshore drift, building up a wider beach that absorbs wave energy before it reaches the cliff",B:"It only works by dissolving the salt in incoming waves",C:"It removes sediment from the beach to lower the risk of flooding",D:"It is a wall built parallel to the shore that redirects wave energy away from the cliff"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-UKLAND-KT-06",
      scaffold:"Don't confuse a groyne (perpendicular to the shore, traps sediment moving along the beach) with a sea wall (parallel to the shore, deflects wave energy directly). A wider trapped beach means waves lose energy over a longer distance before reaching the cliff base.",
      reforge:{stem:"A sea wall is built parallel to the shore rather than perpendicular like a groyne. How does it protect the coast?",options:{A:"It traps sediment moved by longshore drift, exactly like a groyne",B:"It has no effect on wave energy",C:"It redirects the energy of incoming waves away from the cliff or town, rather than trapping sediment like a groyne does",D:"It only works by removing sand from the beach entirely"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-15",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"In a river, what is the difference between traction, saltation and suspension as methods of transportation?",
      options:{A:"Suspension is the rolling of boulders; traction is the floating of fine sediment",B:"Traction rolls large boulders along the bed; saltation bounces smaller sediment along the bed; suspension carries light material floating within the water itself",C:"All three terms describe exactly the same process of transportation",D:"Traction only occurs in the upper course; saltation and suspension only occur in the lower course"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-UKLAND-KT-07",
      scaffold:"The four transportation processes scale with sediment size and river energy: traction (largest, rolled along the bed), saltation (medium, bounced along the bed), suspension (small, carried within the flow), and solution (dissolved chemicals, carried invisibly in the water).",
      reforge:{stem:"Large boulders are too heavy to be carried by the river and instead roll along the riverbed. Which transportation process is this?",options:{A:"Saltation",B:"Solution",C:"Traction",D:"Suspension"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-16",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"What is a river's floodplain, and why is floodplain zoning used to manage it?",
      options:{A:"Floodplain zoning is a hard-engineering technique used exclusively at the coast",B:"A floodplain is another name for a river's drainage basin",C:"A floodplain is a landform found only in the upper course, where the river erodes rather than floods",D:"A floodplain is the low-lying, wide valley floor in the lower course that acts as overspill when the channel is full; floodplain zoning controls what can be built there to reduce the risk to people and property from flooding"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-UKLAND-KT-08",
      scaffold:"Floodplains naturally flood — that's their function, spreading excess discharge over a wide area and reducing flood height downstream. Floodplain zoning is a management response: restricting housing and infrastructure on the areas most likely to flood, rather than trying to stop the river flooding at all.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why does a floodplain naturally flood, and why is this actually useful?",options:{A:"It is the low-lying valley floor that acts as overspill when the river channel is full, spreading excess water over a wide area and reducing flood height downstream",B:"Floodplains never flood under any circumstances",C:"Floodplains exist purely to be built on with housing",D:"Floodplains are found only in the upper course of a river"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-17",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"What does a storm hydrograph show, and what is the 'lag time'?",
      options:{A:"A storm hydrograph only measures rainfall, not discharge",B:"A storm hydrograph shows how a river's discharge changes over a short period, such as during and after a storm; the lag time is the delay between peak rainfall and peak discharge",C:"A storm hydrograph shows a river's long profile from source to mouth",D:"The lag time is the total volume of water in a drainage basin"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-UKLAND-KT-09",
      scaffold:"A short lag time (steep rising limb) means a 'flashy' hydrograph — water reaches the channel quickly, raising flood risk. This happens where infiltration is low (impermeable rock, urbanised or saturated ground) so more rainfall becomes rapid surface runoff instead of soaking in.",
      reforge:{stem:"A river has a long lag time and a gently rising limb on its storm hydrograph. What does this suggest about the drainage basin?",options:{A:"The drainage basin is heavily urbanised with impermeable surfaces everywhere",B:"The basin has just experienced a period of saturation with no infiltration at all",C:"Lag time has no relationship to infiltration or flood risk",D:"High infiltration (e.g. permeable rock, vegetated ground) is allowing rainfall to soak in rather than quickly becoming surface runoff, lowering flood risk"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKLAND-18",spec:"GCSE-UKLAND",stem:"A student defines a permeable rock as 'a type of igneous rock.' Why is this definition wrong?",
      options:{A:"Permeability only applies to rivers, never to rock",B:"Permeable means the same thing as metamorphic",C:"Permeability describes whether water can pass through a rock (permeable) or not (impermeable) — it is a property of the rock, not a rock type, and both igneous and sedimentary rocks can be permeable or impermeable depending on their structure",D:"The definition is correct — only igneous rocks can be permeable"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-UKLAND-KT-10",
      scaffold:"Permeable/impermeable is a property that cuts across the three rock types (igneous, sedimentary, metamorphic). Chalk (sedimentary) is permeable; clay (sedimentary) is often impermeable — so students shouldn't assume rock type alone tells you permeability.",
      reforge:{stem:"Chalk is a sedimentary rock that allows water to pass through it easily. What property does this describe?",options:{A:"Chalk is permeable",B:"Permeability only applies to igneous rock",C:"Chalk is metamorphic",D:"Chalk is impermeable"},correct:"A"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["ECON-1.1"].questions.find(function(q){return q.id==="SD-01";}).correct="D";

BANKS["GCSE-GEO-ENQUIRY"] = {
  label: "Geographical Enquiry & Fieldwork Skills",
  color: "#0f766e",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENQ-01",spec:"GCSE-ENQ",stem:"A student's fieldwork hypothesis reads: 'The river is different at different points.' Why is this a poor hypothesis?",
      options:{A:"Hypotheses are not allowed in GCSE fieldwork",B:"It is not directional or measurable — a good hypothesis states the expected direction of change, such as 'width increases with distance downstream'",C:"It should instead be written as a question",D:"It is too short to be a hypothesis"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENQ-01",
      scaffold:"A hypothesis must be clear, directional and measurable — a statement that can be tested and shown to be true or false, e.g. 'River discharge increases with distance from the source' or 'Environmental quality increases with distance from the new road.' 'The river is different' names no variable and no direction, so it can never be tested. Aims and hypotheses are often built from an established theory or model, such as Bradshaw's model for rivers.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which of these is correctly written as a testable geographical hypothesis?",options:{A:"Environmental quality is a useful thing to measure",B:"The city centre and the edge of the city are not the same",C:"Some parts of the urban area are nicer than others",D:"Environmental quality improves with increasing distance from the CBD"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENQ-02",spec:"GCSE-ENQ",stem:"Why might a fieldwork enquiry use a null hypothesis alongside its main hypothesis?",
      options:{A:"It removes bias — the null hypothesis states the opposite, so data is not selectively interpreted just to prove the original statement",B:"It replaces the need for a conclusion",C:"It is only used when the equipment fails",D:"It guarantees the results will be reliable"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-ENQ-02",
      scaffold:"A null hypothesis is the opposite statement to the hypothesis, e.g. hypothesis: 'Environmental quality improves with distance from the CBD'; null hypothesis: 'Environmental quality does not improve with distance from the CBD.' Testing both prevents a student from unconsciously ignoring evidence that doesn't fit — if the hypothesis can't be proved, the null hypothesis must be accepted instead.",
      reforge:{stem:"A student only tests their hypothesis and ignores the null hypothesis. What is the risk?",options:{A:"The sample size automatically becomes too small",B:"The fieldwork becomes illegal under exam board rules",C:"They may unconsciously interpret ambiguous data to support their hypothesis, introducing bias into the conclusion",D:"There is no risk — null hypotheses are optional decoration"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENQ-03",spec:"GCSE-ENQ",stem:"Which is an example of PRIMARY data in a fieldwork enquiry?",
      options:{A:"Census population data downloaded from the ONS website",B:"An old newspaper article about a flood event",C:"River width and depth measurements taken by the student on the day of fieldwork",D:"Historical weather records from the Met Office"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-ENQ-03",
      scaffold:"Primary data is collected first-hand by the student: questionnaires, river measurements, photographs, interviews. Secondary data is collected by someone else and reused: census data, weather records, old photographs, maps, newspaper articles. Primary data is reliable and specific to the enquiry but time-consuming; secondary data is quick and free but not specific to the enquiry and its quality can't be controlled.",
      reforge:{stem:"What is the main limitation of relying on secondary data in a fieldwork enquiry?",options:{A:"It cannot be presented on a graph",B:"It is illegal to use in GCSE coursework",C:"It is always more expensive than primary data",D:"It is not specific to the enquiry, may be out of date, and the student has no control over how it was collected"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENQ-04",spec:"GCSE-ENQ",stem:"A geography class wants to sample environmental quality at sites every 200 metres along a transect. Which sampling method are they using?",
      options:{A:"Random sampling — sites selected using random number generation",B:"Systematic sampling — sites selected at regular intervals",C:"Stratified sampling — sites selected to represent subsets of the population",D:"Opportunistic sampling — sites selected based on whatever is accessible"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENQ-04",
      scaffold:"Systematic = regular intervals (e.g. every 200m or every tenth person) — quick and covers the whole area, but not every site has an equal chance of selection, which can introduce bias. Random = numbered grid squares chosen via random number generator — least biased, but may miss whole areas or land on inaccessible sites. Stratified = proportional to known subsets, e.g. if 10% of the population is over 65, 10% of questionnaire respondents should be too.",
      reforge:{stem:"A researcher wants their questionnaire sample to include the same proportion of over-65s as the wider population. Which sampling method are they using?",options:{A:"Stratified sampling",B:"Opportunistic sampling",C:"Random sampling",D:"Systematic sampling"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENQ-05",spec:"GCSE-ENQ",stem:"Why might a student use opportunistic sampling instead of their planned sampling strategy?",
      options:{A:"It removes the need for a risk assessment",B:"Opportunistic sampling always gives more accurate results",C:"Access to some sites may be limited on the day, so the nearest accessible point to the planned sample site is used instead",D:"It is required by the exam board for all fieldwork"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-ENQ-05",
      scaffold:"Opportunistic sampling is a practical compromise, not a preferred method — used when the planned site (from systematic, random or stratified sampling) cannot be reached, e.g. private land, dangerous terrain, restricted access. The rule is to stay as close as possible to the originally selected site so the sample remains as representative as it can be.",
      reforge:{stem:"A river sample site selected by random sampling turns out to be on private land with no public access. What is the best response?",options:{A:"Trespass to reach the exact site",B:"Select a completely different river",C:"Abandon the enquiry entirely",D:"Use an opportunistic approach — select the nearest accessible point to the original site"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENQ-06",spec:"GCSE-ENQ",stem:"A student conducts an environmental quality survey on their own using a bipolar scale from -3 to +3. What is the main weakness of this method?",
      options:{A:"Bipolar scales cannot be used for environmental quality",B:"It is subjective — the score depends on one person's judgement, which can be reduced by using groups and taking the mode score",C:"It produces qualitative data that cannot be compared between sites",D:"It requires expensive specialist equipment"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENQ-06",
      scaffold:"Environmental Quality Surveys (EQS) use a sliding or bipolar scale (e.g. -3 to +3) to judge features like litter, graffiti, and green space — they produce quantitative data, but the score reflects one person's opinion, making them subjective. This is reduced by completing the survey in small groups to reach a consensus, or by combining scores from multiple students and using the mode.",
      reforge:{stem:"How can the subjectivity of an environmental quality survey best be reduced?",options:{A:"Complete it in small groups to reach a consensus, or take the mode score from several students' independent surveys",B:"Use a wider bipolar scale, e.g. -10 to +10",C:"Replace the survey with a single photograph",D:"Only survey sites during the day"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENQ-07",spec:"GCSE-ENQ",stem:"A student wants to show the relationship between river width and discharge collected at eight sample sites. Which graph is most appropriate?",
      options:{A:"A choropleth map, since it shows spatial variation by shading",B:"A rose diagram, since it uses compass direction",C:"A scattergraph, since it shows the relationship between two variables and allows a best-fit line to be added",D:"A pie chart, since it shows proportions of a whole"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-ENQ-07",
      scaffold:"Scattergraphs plot two variables against each other, with points left unconnected — a best-fit line reveals the relationship, and outliers/anomalies stand out clearly. Pie charts show proportions of a whole; rose diagrams plot directional data like wind or noise; choropleth maps show spatial variation using shading bands. Match graph type to the type of comparison being made — that's the recurring exam skill.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which limitation applies specifically to scattergraphs?",options:{A:"They can only show the relationship between two sets of data, and individual points cannot easily be labelled",B:"They can only be used with qualitative data",C:"They cannot show anomalies or outliers",D:"They must always show a perfect positive correlation"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENQ-08",spec:"GCSE-ENQ",stem:"A choropleth map is used to show environmental quality scores across zones of a town. What is its main limitation?",
      options:{A:"It cannot display more than two categories of data",B:"It only works for numerical data with negative values",C:"It cannot be drawn using GIS software",D:"It creates an impression of an abrupt change at zone boundaries, when in reality quality often changes gradually"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-ENQ-08",
      scaffold:"Choropleth maps shade areas according to a value range using one colour in different intensities — good for showing a clear overall spatial pattern, but the sharp boundary between two shades implies a sudden change that doesn't exist on the ground, and variation within a single shaded zone is hidden. Proportional symbol maps avoid this by placing a scaled symbol at each exact point instead.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why might a proportional symbols map be preferred over a choropleth map for showing traffic counts at exact sample sites?",options:{A:"It can display continuous surfaces better than any other method",B:"It shows data specific to the exact location without implying false boundaries between zones",C:"It is quicker to construct than a choropleth map",D:"It removes the need for a scale or key"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENQ-09",spec:"GCSE-ENQ",stem:"In a data set of environmental quality scores: 2, 3, 3, 4, 5, 5, 5, 8, what is the mode?",
      options:{A:"5",B:"8",C:"4",D:"3"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-ENQ-09",
      scaffold:"Mode = the value that occurs most often. Here 5 appears three times, more than any other value. Mean = sum ÷ count = 35 ÷ 8 = 4.375. Median = middle value when ranked = average of the 4th and 5th values = (4+5)/2 = 4.5. Range = highest − lowest = 8 − 2 = 6. Learn to calculate all three measures of central tendency plus range, since exam questions often give a small raw data set and ask for one specific measure.",
      reforge:{stem:"Using the same data set (2, 3, 3, 4, 5, 5, 5, 8), what is the range?",options:{A:"3",B:"8",C:"5",D:"6"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENQ-10",spec:"GCSE-ENQ",stem:"A student finds one river velocity reading is far lower than all the others recorded nearby with similar gradients. What should they do with this anomaly?",
      options:{A:"Replace it with the mean of the other readings without comment",B:"Identify it, describe how it differs from the pattern, and try to explain the possible cause rather than deleting it",C:"Automatically delete it from the data set before graphing",D:"Ignore it since anomalies never affect conclusions"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENQ-10",
      scaffold:"Anomalies are results that don't fit the expected pattern or trend. Good practice is to identify them, describe how they differ, and suggest an explanation — equipment fault, human error in reading/recording, an unusual local feature (e.g. an obstruction affecting flow), or a genuine but unexpected geographical process. Simply deleting inconvenient data undermines the validity of the whole enquiry.",
      reforge:{stem:"A river velocity anomaly is later found to be caused by a fallen branch obstructing flow at that exact spot. How should this be treated in the conclusion?",options:{A:"As evidence that all velocity data is unreliable",B:"As proof that the hypothesis is entirely wrong",C:"As an explained anomaly caused by a local, temporary obstruction rather than evidence against the general trend",D:"As data that should never have been collected"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENQ-11",spec:"GCSE-ENQ",stem:"Which statement best describes what an 'evaluation' of a fieldwork enquiry should include?",
      options:{A:"Limitations of data collection methods, how reliable the conclusions were, and how the enquiry could be improved or extended",B:"A simple restatement of the results without any comment on accuracy",C:"A description of what the weather was like on the day of the fieldwork",D:"A list of all the equipment used with no comment on its effectiveness"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-ENQ-11",
      scaffold:"Evaluation ≠ conclusion. Conclusion returns to the hypothesis and states whether it was supported. Evaluation critically examines the enquiry itself: were sample sites accessible, was the sample size big enough, was the time available enough, were the questions/methods appropriate, was there human or equipment error, did the weather or unforeseen issues (roadworks, unusual river flow) affect results — and what would you change if repeating it.",
      reforge:{stem:"A student writes: 'My hypothesis was supported because velocity increased downstream.' Is this a conclusion or an evaluation?",options:{A:"Neither — it is a restatement of the aim",B:"Both, since conclusions and evaluations are the same thing",C:"An evaluation — it discusses the reliability of the methods used",D:"A conclusion — it returns to the hypothesis and states whether the evidence supports it"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENQ-12",spec:"GCSE-ENQ",stem:"A student used a float to measure river velocity but suggests a flow meter would have been better. Why?",
      options:{A:"Flow meters remove the need for repeat readings",B:"A flow meter gives a more direct, precise reading of velocity rather than relying on timing an object with a stopwatch, which introduces human reaction-time error",C:"A float can only be used in still water",D:"A float always overestimates velocity"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENQ-12",
      scaffold:"This is a standard 'suggest an improvement' exam answer. Using a float: measure a set distance (e.g. 10m), time how long the float takes with a stopwatch, repeat three times per position for a mean. Human reaction time in starting/stopping the stopwatch introduces error. A flow meter placed at least 3cm below the surface gives a direct velocity reading, removing that source of error — though it is more expensive and needs training to use correctly.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which improvement to a river fieldwork enquiry would most directly increase the reliability of the conclusions?",options:{A:"Removing the health and safety risk assessment",B:"Only measuring the river at a single site",C:"Increasing the sample size and taking repeat readings at each site to calculate a more robust mean",D:"Reducing the number of sites"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENQ-FB-01",spec:"GCSE-ENQ",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about hypotheses.",
      template:"A good hypothesis must be clear, ___ and ___, so that it can be tested against the data collected. A ___ hypothesis is the opposite statement, used to reduce bias.",
      blanks:["directional","measurable","null"],
      bank:["directional","measurable","null","random","vague","secondary"],
      tag:"MC-ENQ-01",
      scaffold:"'The river is different' fails all three tests. 'Discharge increases with distance downstream' passes: it names a direction (increases) and a measurable variable (discharge). Testing the null hypothesis alongside it guards against selectively interpreting ambiguous results."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENQ-FB-02",spec:"GCSE-ENQ",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about data types.",
      template:"Data collected first-hand by the student is called ___ data, while data collected by someone else and reused, such as census figures, is called ___ data.",
      blanks:["primary","secondary"],
      bank:["primary","secondary","qualitative","quantitative","random","stratified"],
      tag:"MC-ENQ-03",
      scaffold:"Primary: questionnaires, river measurements, photographs taken by the student. Secondary: census data, Met Office weather records, old maps and newspaper articles. Primary is specific and controlled but time-consuming; secondary is quick and free but not tailored to the enquiry."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENQ-FB-03",spec:"GCSE-ENQ",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about sampling methods.",
      template:"___ sampling selects sites at regular intervals, ___ sampling gives every site an equal chance of selection, and ___ sampling ensures subsets of the population are represented proportionally.",
      blanks:["Systematic","random","stratified"],
      bank:["Systematic","random","stratified","opportunistic","biased","secondary"],
      tag:"MC-ENQ-04",
      scaffold:"Systematic = fixed intervals, quick, covers the whole area, but not fully unbiased. Random = numbered grid squares via random number generator, least biased overall. Stratified = proportional representation of known subsets, e.g. age groups matching the census."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENQ-FB-04",spec:"GCSE-ENQ",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about measures of central tendency.",
      template:"The ___ is calculated by adding all values and dividing by the number of values. The ___ is the middle value when data is ranked in order. The ___ is the most frequently occurring value.",
      blanks:["mean","median","mode"],
      bank:["mean","median","mode","range","interquartile","anomaly"],
      tag:"MC-ENQ-09",
      scaffold:"Mean = total ÷ count. Median = middle value once ranked (average the two middle values if there is an even number of data points). Mode = most frequent value. Range = highest − lowest — a measure of dispersion (spread), not central tendency."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENQ-FB-05",spec:"GCSE-ENQ",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about fieldwork evaluation.",
      template:"Evaluation should identify problems with the data collection ___, suggest ___ that could have been made, and assess how ___ the conclusions were.",
      blanks:["methods","improvements","reliable"],
      bank:["methods","improvements","reliable","hypotheses","graphs","aims"],
      tag:"MC-ENQ-11",
      scaffold:"Evaluation covers: accessibility of sites, sample size, duration of data collection, appropriateness of methods/questions, equipment issues, human error, and unforeseen problems on the day. It then suggests concrete improvements — larger sample, more measurements, better equipment, repeat visits — before judging overall reliability."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENQ-18",spec:"GCSE-ENQ",stem:"A student surveys only people entering a shopping centre to investigate residents' views of urban regeneration. What is the main sampling problem?",
      options:{A:"The sample may over-represent shoppers and exclude non-users, creating bias",B:"Shopping-centre surveys always produce too many numerical answers",C:"A questionnaire cannot investigate people's views",D:"The sample is automatically stratified because everyone enters through the same doors at similar times"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-ENQ-12",
      scaffold:"Sampling at one shopping centre is convenient, but it may exclude non-shoppers, older residents and people who avoid the area. The results may not represent the target population. Sampling several locations and times would improve coverage.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which change would most improve the representativeness of this urban survey?",options:{A:"Ask only the first five people who pass the entrance",B:"Collect responses at several locations and times, with a planned sample of different resident groups",C:"Remove all questions about regeneration",D:"Use one respondent's answer as the community average"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENQ-19",spec:"GCSE-ENQ",stem:"A student records traffic counts at 08:00 on one weekday and concludes that the road is always congested. Which evaluation is strongest?",
      options:{A:"The conclusion is fully reliable because 08:00 is a precise time",B:"Traffic counts cannot be used in geographical enquiry",C:"One time and one day may not represent daily or weekly variation; repeated counts would improve reliability",D:"The student should remove all numerical data from the investigation"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-ENQ-13",
      scaffold:"A single time-point can capture a morning peak but cannot establish that congestion is constant. Traffic varies by day, weather, holidays, incidents and school terms. Repeating counts at different times and on more than one day would test whether the pattern is typical.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which evidence would best test whether a morning traffic peak is typical?",options:{A:"Repeat counts at the same time on several weekdays and compare them with off-peak readings",B:"Count vehicles once at midnight",C:"Use a photograph of the road with no time recorded",D:"Ask one driver whether the road is usually busy"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENQ-20",spec:"GCSE-ENQ",stem:"A choropleth map shades areas according to average income. Which design choice is most important for interpreting it fairly?",
      options:{A:"Use as many decorative colours as possible",B:"Choose sensible class intervals and include a clear key so shade differences represent meaningful income ranges",C:"Leave out the map title because the pattern should speak for itself",D:"Use one identical shade for every area to avoid bias"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENQ-14",
      scaffold:"A choropleth's pattern depends heavily on its classification. Sensible, clearly labelled class intervals and a readable key allow the reader to interpret differences. Arbitrary intervals can exaggerate or hide spatial patterns.",
      reforge:{stem:"What could happen if a map uses very wide income classes for some values and very narrow classes for others without explanation?",options:{A:"The map may exaggerate some differences and conceal others, making the spatial pattern misleading",B:"The map becomes automatically more accurate because it has fewer labels",C:"Class intervals have no effect on a choropleth pattern",D:"Every area must appear in a separate colour"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENQ-21",spec:"GCSE-ENQ",stem:"A fieldwork group finds that two variables have a strong correlation. What conclusion is scientifically safest?",
      options:{A:"The first variable definitely caused the second",B:"The variables are unrelated because correlation is never useful",C:"The pattern suggests a relationship, but other factors and the possibility of coincidence must be considered",D:"The data can be accepted without checking sample size or anomalies"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-ENQ-15",
      scaffold:"Correlation shows that two variables change together; it does not by itself prove causation. A good conclusion describes the relationship, considers anomalies and sample size, and identifies other possible explanations.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which additional step would make a correlation-based conclusion more convincing?",options:{A:"Check sample size, identify anomalies and compare the relationship with a plausible geographical explanation",B:"Delete results that do not fit the expected pattern",C:"Change the axis labels until the correlation looks stronger",D:"Treat the correlation as proof without considering other variables"},correct:"A"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-GEO-RIVERFIELD"] = {
  label: "River Fieldwork — River Darent",
  color: "#0f766e",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"GCSE-RVF-01",spec:"GCSE-RVF",stem:"A class investigates the River Darent at three sites: Westerham (near the source), Park Farm, and Lullingstone Roman Villa (further downstream). Which hypothesis links this to Bradshaw's model?",
      options:{A:"Channel width decreases with distance downstream",B:"Channel width and discharge increase with distance downstream from Westerham to Lullingstone Roman Villa",C:"Discharge is unrelated to distance from the source",D:"Velocity is identical at all three sites"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-RVF-01",
      scaffold:"Bradshaw's model predicts that as a river moves from source to mouth, width, depth, velocity, discharge and load size generally increase, while gradient and bed material size decrease. Moving from Westerham (upper course, closer to source) through Park Farm to Lullingstone (further downstream), the expected pattern is increasing width and discharge — this is the standard hypothesis for a downstream-change enquiry on the Darent.",
      reforge:{stem:"According to Bradshaw's model, which variable should DECREASE between Westerham and Lullingstone Roman Villa?",options:{A:"Cross-sectional area",B:"Discharge",C:"Channel gradient and the size of bed material",D:"Channel width"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-RVF-02",spec:"GCSE-RVF",stem:"At each River Darent site, students measure width using a tape measure. What is the correct technique?",
      options:{A:"Measure only the deepest channel, ignoring the banks",B:"Measure the widest point of the entire valley, including the floodplain",C:"Estimate the width visually without any equipment",D:"Measure from where the dry bank meets the water on one side to where it meets the water on the opposite side, keeping the tape taut and clear of the water"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-RVF-02",
      scaffold:"Width is measured bank-to-bank at the point where dry land meets water. The tape must be held taut and must not touch the water's surface, since a sagging or wet tape would distort the reading and reduce reliability. This is one of the two starting measurements (with depth) for almost every river enquiry.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why must the tape measure used for river width be kept taut and clear of the water surface?",options:{A:"A sagging or wet tape would give an inaccurate width reading, reducing the reliability of the data",B:"It prevents the tape from being swept downstream",C:"It is a legal requirement under health and safety law",D:"It has no effect on accuracy, only on speed"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-RVF-03",spec:"GCSE-RVF",stem:"When measuring river depth across the channel at Park Farm, why should the metre rule be held with its flat side facing the banks (i.e. edge-on to the flow)?",
      options:{A:"It makes the rule easier to read from the riverbank",B:"It prevents the rule from being seen by fish",C:"It has no effect on measurement accuracy",D:"It reduces the rule's impact on the water level at that point, giving a more accurate depth reading"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-RVF-03",
      scaffold:"Placing the metre rule flat-side against the flow (rather than broad-side, which would dam the water slightly and raise the level around it) reduces its impact on the water height, giving a more accurate depth reading. Depth is measured at regular intervals across the width to build a full cross-sectional picture and calculate mean depth for the discharge formula.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why is depth measured at several regular points across the river's width, rather than just once in the centre?",options:{A:"It is required only for rivers wider than 5 metres",B:"It removes the need to measure width separately",C:"A single central reading would not capture how depth varies across the channel, and a mean depth is needed to calculate discharge accurately",D:"Depth never varies across a channel, so multiple readings check for equipment error only"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-RVF-04",spec:"GCSE-RVF",stem:"At a Darent site, students measure velocity using a float over a 10m stretch, timing it with a stopwatch at the left bank, centre, and right bank. Why take readings at three positions across the width?",
      options:{A:"Velocity varies across the channel — it is typically fastest in the centre/thalweg and slower near the banks due to friction",B:"Three positions are needed to calculate river width",C:"It is only done to save time compared to using a flow meter",D:"Velocity is identical everywhere in a channel so this simply checks for errors"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-RVF-04",
      scaffold:"Friction with the bed and banks slows water near the edges, so velocity is generally highest in the centre/thalweg and lowest near the banks. Taking three readings (left, centre, right) and repeating each three times allows a representative mean velocity to be calculated — a single reading anywhere would misrepresent the whole cross-section.",
      reforge:{stem:"A group's float times at the left bank, centre and right bank of a Darent site are 22s, 14s, 21s over 10m. What does this pattern suggest?",options:{A:"The float was defective, since velocity should be identical everywhere",B:"Velocity is highest in the centre and lower near both banks, consistent with reduced friction away from the channel edges",C:"The measurements must be discarded as anomalies",D:"The river is flowing upstream at the banks"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-RVF-05",spec:"GCSE-RVF",stem:"A group calculates cross-sectional area as 1.8 m² and mean velocity as 0.4 m/s at a Darent site. What is the discharge?",
      options:{A:"7.2 m³/s",B:"2.2 m³/s",C:"1.4 m³/s",D:"0.72 m³/s"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-RVF-05",
      scaffold:"Discharge (m³/s) = cross-sectional area (m²) × velocity (m/s). Cross-sectional area = width × mean depth. Here: 1.8 × 0.4 = 0.72 m³/s. Always show both formulas and the substitution in an exam answer — method marks are available even if the final figure is wrong.",
      reforge:{stem:"A river site has a width of 3.5m and mean depth of 0.6m. What is the cross-sectional area?",options:{A:"4.1 m²",B:"0.6 m²",C:"2.1 m²",D:"1.05 m²"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-RVF-06",spec:"GCSE-RVF",stem:"Students measure the river's gradient at Westerham using a clinometer and two ranging poles set 10m apart. Why is gradient a useful measurement to link to Bradshaw's model?",
      options:{A:"Gradient typically decreases downstream, so comparing it across the three Darent sites tests a core prediction of the model",B:"Gradient can only be measured at the river's source",C:"Gradient has no relationship to any other river variable",D:"Gradient always stays the same throughout a river's course"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-RVF-06",
      scaffold:"Bradshaw's model predicts gradient decreases from source to mouth as the river erodes vertically less and laterally more. On the Darent, comparing gradient at Westerham (steeper, upper course) against Lullingstone (gentler, lower course) directly tests this. Method: place ranging poles 0m and 10m apart, use the clinometer to measure the angle/height difference between them.",
      reforge:{stem:"If the Darent's gradient is much steeper at Westerham than at Lullingstone Roman Villa, what would Bradshaw's model predict about velocity?",options:{A:"Velocity and gradient have no relationship in Bradshaw's model",B:"Despite the steeper gradient at Westerham, discharge and channel efficiency downstream mean velocity is often similar or even higher at Lullingstone",C:"Velocity can only be measured where gradient is zero",D:"Velocity must always be highest wherever gradient is steepest"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-RVF-07",spec:"GCSE-RVF",stem:"Which of these is a river-specific health and safety risk that should appear in a risk assessment for fieldwork on the Darent?",
      options:{A:"Risk of sunburn only, since rivers have no other hazards",B:"Weil's disease from contact with contaminated water, managed by washing hands and covering open wounds",C:"There are no additional risks compared to classroom-based work",D:"Risk of altitude sickness"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-RVF-07",
      scaffold:"River-specific risks: slippery rocks (sturdy footwear with grip), Weil's disease from contaminated water (wash hands, cover cuts, use anti-bacterial gel), weather (check forecast, sun protection), working in an unfamiliar place (work in groups, carry maps/phones, hi-vis). A good risk assessment names the specific hazard, who it affects, and precisely how it is managed — not just 'be careful'.",
      reforge:{stem:"A risk assessment states: 'Hazard: slippery rocks. Risk: injury from slipping.' What is missing to complete it properly?",options:{A:"How the risk is managed — e.g. wearing sturdy, appropriate footwear with a good grip",B:"A description of what the rocks look like",C:"The exact GPS coordinates of every rock",D:"Nothing — hazard and risk are sufficient on their own"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-RVF-08",spec:"GCSE-RVF",stem:"A student wants to display the change in the Darent's channel shape from Westerham to Lullingstone. Which graph type is most appropriate?",
      options:{A:"A pie chart showing proportions of the channel",B:"A choropleth map",C:"A line graph plotting the river cross-section, since the joined points reveal the channel's shape",D:"A rose diagram, since it uses compass direction"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-RVF-08",
      scaffold:"A river cross-section is a special case of a line graph: although depth readings are not strictly continuous data, joining the plotted points shows the shape of the channel bed clearly — useful for comparing a narrow, deep upper-course channel (Westerham) against a wider, shallower or more uniform lower-course channel (Lullingstone).",
      reforge:{stem:"Why might a scattergraph be more useful than a bar graph for showing the Darent's width against discharge across all sample sites?",options:{A:"A scattergraph is always easier to draw than a bar graph",B:"Bar graphs cannot use numerical data at all",C:"A scattergraph removes the need to record discharge separately",D:"A scattergraph reveals the relationship (correlation) between the two continuous variables, which a bar graph comparing discrete categories cannot show"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-RVF-09",spec:"GCSE-RVF",stem:"At one Darent site, velocity is unexpectedly much lower than at neighbouring sites with a similar gradient. What is the best way to treat this in the analysis?",
      options:{A:"Delete the reading so it doesn't affect the graph",B:"Assume the equipment is always correct and rewrite the hypothesis",C:"Ignore it since anomalies do not need to be discussed",D:"Identify it as an anomaly and suggest a possible cause, such as an obstruction, vegetation, or a measurement/equipment error"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-RVF-09",
      scaffold:"Anomalies should be named and explained, not hidden. Possible causes at a river site: debris or vegetation obstructing flow, a meander or pool slowing the current, human error in float timing, or a reading taken in an atypical spot. This is a very common 4-mark 'describe and explain an anomaly' exam question.",
      reforge:{stem:"A 4-mark exam question shows a graph of the Darent's velocity with one clear anomaly. What must a full-marks answer include?",options:{A:"A description of how the anomalous point differs from the general trend, plus a plausible explanation for why it occurred",B:"Only the numerical value of the anomaly",C:"A statement that the whole enquiry is invalid",D:"A new hypothesis unrelated to the anomaly"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-RVF-10",spec:"GCSE-RVF",stem:"After completing the River Darent enquiry, a student writes an evaluation. Which statement is a genuine evaluation point rather than a conclusion?",
      options:{A:"'Bradshaw's model predicts width should increase downstream'",B:"'Our results matched the pattern predicted by Bradshaw's model'",C:"'Our float method may have underestimated true velocity due to reaction-time errors in starting and stopping the stopwatch'",D:"'Discharge increased from Westerham to Lullingstone, supporting our hypothesis'"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-RVF-10",
      scaffold:"B, C and D all restate findings or theory — that's conclusion territory. A critically examines the method itself and its limitation, which is what evaluation requires: was the equipment/method appropriate, how reliable is the data, what would you change next time (e.g. use a flow meter instead of a float to remove reaction-time error).",
      reforge:{stem:"Which of these is the strongest evaluation point about sample size on the Darent enquiry?",options:{A:"Sample size has no effect on the reliability of a conclusion",B:"Only measuring at three sites limits how confidently the trend can be generalised across the river's whole course — more sites between Westerham and Lullingstone would strengthen the conclusion",C:"Three sites is always enough for any river enquiry",D:"The number of sites only matters for urban fieldwork, not river fieldwork"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-RVF-FB-01",spec:"GCSE-RVF",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about measuring river discharge.",
      template:"Discharge is calculated using the formula: cross-sectional area × ___. Cross-sectional area itself is calculated as ___ × mean ___.",
      blanks:["velocity","width","depth"],
      bank:["velocity","width","depth","gradient","time","length"],
      tag:"MC-RVF-05",
      scaffold:"Two-step calculation: (1) cross-sectional area (m²) = width (m) × mean depth (m); (2) discharge (m³/s) = cross-sectional area (m²) × velocity (m/s). Learn this chain — it's a guaranteed calculation question on river fieldwork."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-RVF-FB-02",spec:"GCSE-RVF",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about Bradshaw's model applied to the River Darent.",
      template:"Moving downstream from Westerham to Lullingstone Roman Villa, Bradshaw's model predicts that channel width and ___ will increase, while ___ and the size of ___ material will decrease.",
      blanks:["discharge","gradient","bed"],
      bank:["discharge","gradient","bed","velocity","depth","load"],
      tag:"MC-RVF-01",
      scaffold:"Bradshaw's model: width, depth, velocity, discharge and load quantity increase downstream; gradient, bed material size (load calibre) and friction decrease downstream. Using named sites (Westerham → Lullingstone) to test this model is exactly what an exam expects for a place-specific fieldwork answer."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-RVF-FB-03",spec:"GCSE-RVF",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about river velocity measurement technique.",
      template:"Using a float, students measure a set distance such as ___ metres, time how long the float takes with a ___, and repeat at each position three times to calculate a ___.",
      blanks:["10","stopwatch","mean"],
      bank:["10","stopwatch","mean","clinometer","median","5"],
      tag:"MC-RVF-04",
      scaffold:"Standard float method: measure a known distance (commonly 10m), time the float's travel with a stopwatch, and repeat three times at each of the three cross-channel positions (left, centre, right) to calculate a reliable mean velocity for that site."
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-GEO-URBFIELD"] = {
  label: "Urban Fieldwork — Stratford, London",
  color: "#0f766e",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"GCSE-URF-01",spec:"GCSE-URF",stem:"A class investigates change across Old Stratford, Carpenters Estate, East Village and the Olympic Park. Which is the most appropriate hypothesis for this enquiry?",
      options:{A:"Environmental quality improves with proximity to the regenerated Olympic Park compared with the older Carpenters Estate and Old Stratford",B:"Environmental quality is identical across all areas of Stratford",C:"Population density has no relationship to housing type in Stratford",D:"Traffic levels cannot be measured in Stratford"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-URF-01",
      scaffold:"A good urban hypothesis names a clear, testable, directional relationship. Stratford is ideal for this because it contains contrasting zones within walking distance: Old Stratford and Carpenters Estate (older, less-regenerated housing) versus East Village and the Olympic Park (post-2012 regeneration). A hypothesis like 'environmental quality improves with proximity to the regenerated Olympic Park' can be tested directly using an EQS at each location.",
      reforge:{stem:"What would be an appropriate null hypothesis for the Stratford investigation above?",options:{A:"The Olympic Park is not located in Stratford",B:"Stratford has no housing at all",C:"Environmental quality does not improve with proximity to the regenerated Olympic Park",D:"Environmental quality always improves everywhere in London"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URF-02",spec:"GCSE-URF",stem:"Why is Stratford, London a strong location for an urban change enquiry linked to regeneration theory?",
      options:{A:"It has no variation in building age or land use",B:"It has no public transport links, making data collection easier",C:"It is entirely rural, so it cannot be used for an urban enquiry",D:"It contains sharply contrasting land use within a small area — older estates like Carpenters alongside post-Olympic regeneration such as East Village and the Olympic Park"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-URF-02",
      scaffold:"Regeneration is investment to reverse the decline of a run-down area. Stratford is unusually compressed for fieldwork purposes: Carpenters Estate (1960s social housing, some now vacant pending redevelopment) sits close to East Village (former 2012 Olympic Athletes' Village, now private and rented housing) and the Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park (redeveloped green and leisure space). This lets students compare pre- and post-regeneration environments on a single day's fieldwork.",
      reforge:{stem:"A student annotates a photograph of Carpenters Estate: 'older, low-rise blocks with visible signs of disrepair, contrasting with the new East Village development nearby.' What analytical skill is this annotation demonstrating?",options:{A:"Secondary data analysis — since photographs cannot be primary data",B:"Qualitative analysis — using an annotation to add explanatory meaning to a photograph, not just a label",C:"Statistical analysis — since it refers to a measurable value",D:"Quantitative analysis — calculating a numerical value from the photograph"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URF-03",spec:"GCSE-URF",stem:"Students conduct an Environmental Quality Survey at Old Stratford, Carpenters Estate, East Village and the Olympic Park. Which feature would NOT typically appear on an EQS scoring sheet?",
      options:{A:"River discharge in cubic metres per second",B:"Amount of green space and vegetation",C:"Amount of litter",D:"Condition of pavements"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-URF-03",
      scaffold:"EQS scores urban features on a bipolar scale, typically -2 to +2: pavements, buildings, litter, traffic, green space, graffiti. River discharge is a river fieldwork measurement, not an urban one — a classic exam distractor testing whether students can tell urban and river methods apart. Scoring each feature at all four Stratford locations allows a direct site-to-site comparison.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why might an EQS score for 'green space' be notably higher at the Olympic Park than at Carpenters Estate?",options:{A:"Green space cannot be scored using an EQS",B:"Carpenters Estate has more parks than the Olympic Park",C:"The Olympic Park was purpose-built with extensive parkland and landscaping as part of the 2012 regeneration, unlike the older, more built-up estate",D:"EQS scores are unrelated to actual land use"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URF-04",spec:"GCSE-URF",stem:"A student conducts a pedestrian count at East Village and the Olympic Park at 9am, 11am, 1pm and 3pm using a tally chart. What is the main purpose of repeating the count at different times?",
      options:{A:"It replaces the need for a risk assessment",B:"Pedestrian flow varies through the day, so multiple time slots give a fuller, more representative picture than a single snapshot",C:"It has no purpose — one count at any time is equally valid",D:"It removes the need to visit more than one location"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-URF-04",
      scaffold:"Footfall changes with time of day — a leisure destination like the Olympic Park may peak differently to a commuter route near East Village. A single count only captures one moment, which may not be typical. Repeating counts at set times (9am, 11am, 1pm, 3pm) captures this variation and produces more reliable, comparable data across sites.",
      reforge:{stem:"A pedestrian count at the Olympic Park is much higher on a Saturday afternoon than a count taken at Carpenters Estate on a Tuesday morning. Why is this comparison methodologically weak?",options:{A:"The Olympic Park is not a valid fieldwork location",B:"Pedestrian counts cannot be compared between any two sites",C:"Tally charts cannot be used for pedestrian data",D:"The day and time were not controlled between sites, so the difference may reflect timing rather than a genuine difference between locations"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URF-05",spec:"GCSE-URF",stem:"Students use a questionnaire to ask residents near Carpenters Estate: 'The regeneration of the Olympic Park area has improved this neighbourhood.' Respondents choose strongly agree, agree, disagree or strongly disagree. What type of question is this?",
      options:{A:"A closed numerical question",B:"An open question with unlimited possible answers",C:"A statement question using a scale to gauge opinion",D:"A secondary data source"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-URF-05",
      scaffold:"Question types: closed (single word/number/limited list, e.g. 'How long have you lived here?'), statement/scale (agree–disagree scale, as here), open (any answer, e.g. 'What are your views on the regeneration?'). Statement questions produce data that is easy to quantify and compare, while open questions produce richer but harder-to-compare qualitative data.",
      reforge:{stem:"'What are your views on the redevelopment of Carpenters Estate?' is an example of which question type, and what is its main limitation for data analysis?",options:{A:"A secondary data question, since it is not answered by the respondent",B:"An open question — responses are richer but harder to quantify and compare systematically than closed or scale questions",C:"A statement question — responses are given as a numerical score",D:"A closed question — responses are limited to yes or no"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URF-06",spec:"GCSE-URF",stem:"Which of these is a risk specifically worth including in a risk assessment for fieldwork around Stratford's Carpenters Estate and East Village?",
      options:{A:"Risk of tidal flooding, since Stratford is on the coast",B:"Risk of encountering livestock",C:"Risk of altitude sickness from elevation change",D:"Traffic and road-crossing risk, managed by wearing hi-vis and following safe crossing procedures"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-URF-06",
      scaffold:"Urban fieldwork risks differ from river/rural ones: traffic and road crossings, getting lost in an unfamiliar area, contact with strangers during questionnaires, and general pollution exposure are the relevant hazards for a built-up site like Stratford. Livestock, tides and altitude sickness are risks associated with rural, coastal or upland fieldwork instead — another common distractor pattern in exam questions.",
      reforge:{stem:"A student is completing questionnaires with the public near Stratford station. What precaution addresses the 'contact with strangers' risk most directly?",options:{A:"Planning questions carefully in advance and working in groups, withdrawing politely if someone becomes annoyed or offended",B:"Avoiding all human contact during the enquiry",C:"Completing questionnaires only inside a locked room",D:"Skipping the questionnaire method entirely"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URF-07",spec:"GCSE-URF",stem:"A student wants to compare EQS scores for four Stratford sites (Old Stratford, Carpenters Estate, East Village, Olympic Park) on one chart. Which graph type is most appropriate?",
      options:{A:"A bar graph, since it clearly compares a single score across separate categories/sites",B:"A triangular graph, since there are four categories",C:"A scattergraph, since there is only one variable being measured",D:"A choropleth map, since EQS data cannot be mapped"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-URF-07",
      scaffold:"Bar graphs are ideal for comparing a single measured value (total EQS score) across distinct categories (the four named sites) — simple to construct and interpret, though they don't show relationships or explain causes. A scattergraph needs two variables; a triangular graph needs three percentage components; EQS scores actually can be mapped as proportional symbols, but a bar graph is the more direct comparison tool here.",
      reforge:{stem:"If a student instead wanted to test whether EQS score is related to distance from the Olympic Park across many small sites, which graph would be more appropriate than a bar graph?",options:{A:"A pie chart, showing the proportion of each site's score",B:"A scattergraph, plotting EQS score against distance from the Olympic Park to reveal any correlation",C:"A rose diagram",D:"A population pyramid"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URF-08",spec:"GCSE-URF",stem:"A student's Stratford enquiry finds Carpenters Estate has a surprisingly HIGH environmental quality score despite being the oldest housing studied. How should this anomaly be treated?",
      options:{A:"Assume the survey method is entirely invalid and abandon the enquiry",B:"Ignore it, since anomalies are not relevant to urban enquiries",C:"Investigate and explain a plausible cause, such as recent partial refurbishment, low traffic on the survey day, or subjectivity in the scoring",D:"Delete the result so the graph shows a clean trend"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-URF-08",
      scaffold:"Treat unexpected urban results the same way as any anomaly: describe it, then suggest a specific, plausible cause tied to the actual location — e.g. a particular street within Carpenters Estate may have been recently maintained, the survey may have been completed by only one student (subjectivity), or the day's weather/traffic was unusually light. Specific, place-based explanations score far better than generic ones.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which explanation for the Carpenters Estate anomaly above would be considered a strong, well-reasoned answer?",options:{A:"The result should simply be relabelled as correct without explanation",B:"Carpenters Estate must not really exist in Stratford",C:"Anomalies in urban data are always caused by equipment failure",D:"The EQS was completed by only one student that day, introducing subjective bias that could be reduced next time by using group consensus scoring"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URF-09",spec:"GCSE-URF",stem:"A student evaluating their Stratford fieldwork writes: 'We only visited each site once, on a dry Tuesday afternoon in term time.' What is this evaluation point highlighting?",
      options:{A:"An irrelevant detail with no bearing on data quality",B:"A strength that guarantees the enquiry is fully reliable",C:"A limitation of the enquiry's reliability — results may not represent the sites under different weather, days or times, so repeating fieldwork under varied conditions would strengthen the conclusions",D:"A statement that belongs in the aims, not the evaluation"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-URF-09",
      scaffold:"This is a genuine, well-targeted evaluation point: single-visit fieldwork captures only one snapshot in time. Time of day, day of the week, weather and season can all affect footfall, traffic and even perceived environmental quality. A strong evaluation names the specific limitation and proposes a specific fix — e.g. repeat visits at a weekend, in different weather, or at a different time of year.",
      reforge:{stem:"How could the Stratford urban enquiry's reliability be most directly improved?",options:{A:"Reducing the number of questionnaire respondents",B:"Repeating data collection at different times of day and on different days of the week to check whether the pattern holds consistently",C:"Only visiting the Olympic Park and ignoring the other three sites",D:"Removing the environmental quality survey entirely"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URF-10",spec:"GCSE-URF",stem:"Which theory or concept would most appropriately underpin a Stratford fieldwork enquiry into land use change since the 2012 Olympics?",
      options:{A:"Bradshaw's model, since Stratford is a river source",B:"Rostow's stages of economic growth, since it applies only to national economies",C:"The demographic transition model, since it explains only population change over time",D:"Urban regeneration and the sustainability of redevelopment, since Stratford was transformed as part of the London 2012 Olympic legacy"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-URF-10",
      scaffold:"Every fieldwork enquiry needs an identifiable underpinning theory or concept. For an urban enquiry like Stratford, the relevant frameworks are urban land use change and sustainability of regeneration — directly relevant given the area's transformation from industrial/1960s estate land into the Olympic Park, East Village and ongoing Carpenters Estate redevelopment plans. Bradshaw's model is for rivers; Rostow's and the DTM apply at a national/international scale, not to a single urban regeneration site.",
      reforge:{stem:"A student writes: 'Sustainability of urban regeneration' as their underpinning concept for Stratford. Which finding would directly relate to this concept?",options:{A:"Whether East Village's new housing includes affordable homes and green infrastructure that will remain viable and beneficial to residents long-term",B:"The exact wind direction recorded on the day of fieldwork",C:"The distance between Old Stratford and Park Farm",D:"The number of respondents who refused a questionnaire"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URF-FB-01",spec:"GCSE-URF",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about Environmental Quality Surveys in Stratford.",
      template:"An EQS typically scores features such as pavements, buildings, litter, traffic and ___ space on a ___ scale, and the resulting data is ___.",
      blanks:["green","bipolar","quantitative"],
      bank:["green","bipolar","quantitative","river","open","qualitative"],
      tag:"MC-URF-03",
      scaffold:"EQS features: pavements, buildings, litter, traffic, green space, graffiti — each scored on a bipolar scale (e.g. -2 to +2). Despite being based on subjective judgement, the output is quantitative (numerical) data, which is why it can be compared and graphed across sites like Old Stratford, Carpenters Estate, East Village and the Olympic Park."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URF-FB-02",spec:"GCSE-URF",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about Stratford's urban regeneration.",
      template:"Stratford's ___ Estate represents older, pre-regeneration housing, while ___ Village was built as the Athletes' Village for the 2012 Olympics and the Queen Elizabeth Olympic ___ is the redeveloped green and leisure space.",
      blanks:["Carpenters","East","Park"],
      bank:["Carpenters","East","Park","Old","West","Village"],
      tag:"MC-URF-02",
      scaffold:"The four named Stratford fieldwork locations: Old Stratford (established area), Carpenters Estate (1960s social housing, partly awaiting redevelopment), East Village (former Athletes' Village, now housing), Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park (redeveloped green/leisure space). This contrast makes Stratford ideal for testing a regeneration-based hypothesis."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URF-FB-03",spec:"GCSE-URF",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about question types in urban questionnaires.",
      template:"A ___ question limits answers to a single word or number, an ___ question allows any response, and a ___ question asks respondents to rate agreement on a scale.",
      blanks:["closed","open","statement"],
      bank:["closed","open","statement","random","stratified","secondary"],
      tag:"MC-URF-05",
      scaffold:"Closed: 'How long have you lived here?' Open: 'What are your views on the regeneration?' Statement: 'The regeneration has improved this neighbourhood — strongly agree to strongly disagree.' Closed and statement questions are quick to analyse and compare; open questions give richer detail but are harder to quantify."
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-GEO-UKHUMAN"] = {
  label: "UK's Evolving Human Landscape",
  color: "#0f766e",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-01",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"London's Docklands, once derelict after the Port of London's container trade collapsed, was transformed into Canary Wharf's offices, flats, and the Jubilee Line Extension. A student says this just means 'new buildings were put up.' What term better describes this process?",
      options:{A:"Suburbanisation — outward growth of housing at the city edge",B:"Deindustrialisation — the decline of manufacturing industry",C:"Counter-urbanisation — people moving out of cities into the countryside",D:"Urban regeneration — long-term investment to reverse economic and environmental decline of a run-down area"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-URB-01",
      scaffold:"Urban regeneration is a planned, long-term attempt to reverse decline in a run-down urban area — improving the economy, environment, and social conditions, not just erecting buildings. London's Docklands fell derelict as container shipping moved to deep-water ports like Tilbury, then the London Docklands Development Corporation drove investment into Canary Wharf's offices, new housing, and transport links (DLR, Jubilee Line Extension). Counter-urbanisation and suburbanisation describe population movement out of cities; deindustrialisation is the decline itself, not the response to it.",
      reforge:{stem:"Bristol's Harbourside was redeveloped from disused docks into housing, museums, and leisure space, bringing jobs and investment back to the area. What is this an example of?",options:{A:"Urban sprawl",B:"Rural depopulation",C:"Urban regeneration",D:"Counter-urbanisation"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-02",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"Through the 1960s-80s, London's docks and East End manufacturing lost huge numbers of jobs as container shipping and cheaper overseas production took over. What is this process called, and what did it cause before regeneration?",
      options:{A:"Deindustrialisation — the decline of manufacturing/traditional industry, causing unemployment, dereliction, and urban decline",B:"Counter-urbanisation — people leaving cities for rural areas",C:"Immigration — international migrants arriving in cities",D:"Reindustrialisation — the growth of new manufacturing industry"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-URB-02",
      scaffold:"Deindustrialisation is the long-term decline of manufacturing/traditional industry, driven by cheaper overseas labour, automation, and changing trade patterns (here, larger container ships needing deep-water ports further downriver). It left London's Docklands derelict and unemployed for years — exactly the problem the Docklands regeneration scheme was designed to fix.",
      reforge:{stem:"A former coal-mining town in South Yorkshire lost its main employer in the 1980s-90s as UK coal mining declined sharply. What is the general term for this loss of traditional industry?",options:{A:"Counter-urbanisation",B:"Deindustrialisation",C:"Regeneration",D:"Urbanisation"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-03",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"London's population includes large numbers of people born outside the UK, drawn by job opportunities, universities, and existing migrant communities. A student says international migration only affects London's population size. What else does it change?",
      options:{A:"Nothing else — migration only ever changes population totals",B:"Its social and cultural character — creating ethnically diverse, multicultural areas with a wide range of shops, food, religions and languages",C:"It makes London's population entirely homogeneous",D:"It only affects the age structure, not culture"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-URB-03",
      scaffold:"International migration is a major driver of London's dynamic character: it doesn't just add to the population count, it reshapes the city's ethnic and cultural make-up, creating diverse neighbourhoods (e.g. Brick Lane's Bangladeshi community, Southall's Punjabi community) with distinct shops, places of worship, and cuisines — part of what makes London a 'world city'.",
      reforge:{stem:"Areas of London such as Brick Lane have a strong South Asian character, with specific cuisines, shops, and mosques reflecting the heritage of migrant communities who settled there. This is best explained as an effect of what process?",options:{A:"Deindustrialisation",B:"Rural depopulation",C:"International migration shaping local culture and identity",D:"Counter-urbanisation"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-04",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"Cornwall has seen house prices rise sharply as retirees and second-home buyers move in, while young local people are increasingly priced out and some villages feel 'empty' outside the summer season. A student says rural areas never change. What's actually happening here?",
      options:{A:"Deindustrialisation — the decline of manufacturing",B:"Urban regeneration — the renewal of derelict city-centre areas",C:"Urbanisation — the growth of the whole country's urban population",D:"Rural landscape change — counter-urbanisation and second-home ownership are altering Cornwall's population, housing market, and communities"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-URB-04",
      scaffold:"Rural landscapes change too, not just cities: counter-urbanisation (people moving from cities to attractive rural/coastal areas, often to retire) combined with high demand for second homes has pushed Cornish house prices far above what local wages can support, squeezing out younger residents and, in some villages, leaving houses empty for much of the year — a major and controversial issue in Cornwall's 'second homes crisis'.",
      reforge:{stem:"A coastal village in Cornwall sees average house prices rise sharply, its primary school threatened with closure due to falling pupil numbers, and a large share of homes standing empty outside peak summer weeks. Which process best explains this pattern?",options:{A:"Counter-urbanisation and second-home ownership pricing out local residents",B:"Deindustrialisation",C:"Urban sprawl into the green belt",D:"International migration"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-05",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"The Eden Project was built inside a disused china clay pit near St Austell, Cornwall, using giant biomes to house plants from around the world and attract over a million visitors a year. A student says this is 'just a tourist attraction.' What wider planning goal does it demonstrate?",
      options:{A:"Urban sprawl — the unplanned outward spread of a city",B:"Sustainable rural regeneration — turning a derelict industrial site into a new source of jobs and income with a strong environmental focus",C:"Deindustrialisation — reducing manufacturing industry",D:"Counter-urbanisation — people moving from cities to the countryside"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-URB-05",
      scaffold:"The Eden Project reclaimed a derelict, environmentally scarred china clay pit and turned it into a major visitor attraction, creating thousands of local jobs and diversifying Cornwall's economy away from its declining primary industries — while its design emphasises renewable energy, recycled materials and environmental education. It's a rural regeneration project, not simply a tourist site.",
      reforge:{stem:"A disused quarry is transformed into an eco-attraction with renewable energy, education centres on biodiversity, and hundreds of new local jobs in a struggling rural economy. What does this best represent?",options:{A:"Urban sprawl",B:"Deindustrialisation",C:"Sustainable rural regeneration of a former industrial site",D:"Counter-urbanisation"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-06",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"Cornwall's tin mining and fishing industries have declined sharply since the mid-20th century, while tourism now dominates the economy. A student says this makes Cornwall's economy more secure than before. Why is this often untrue?",
      options:{A:"Tourism has had no effect on Cornwall's economy at all",B:"Tourism jobs are guaranteed to be permanent, well-paid, and available all year round",C:"Tin mining and fishing have grown rather than declined in Cornwall",D:"Tourism is highly seasonal, so many jobs are low-paid and only available for part of the year, leaving Cornwall economically vulnerable outside peak season"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-URB-06",
      scaffold:"Cornwall's shift from primary industries (tin mining, fishing) to a tourism-dominated economy hasn't simply replaced one stable industry with another: tourism is strongly seasonal, so many jobs are part-time, low-paid, and concentrated in summer, leaving many Cornish workers facing unemployment or underemployment for much of the year and the wider economy vulnerable to downturns in visitor numbers.",
      reforge:{stem:"A rural economy that once depended on fishing now relies mainly on summer tourism. Local workers report struggling to find paid work between October and March each year. What does this reveal about economies that shift heavily towards tourism?",options:{A:"They can become vulnerable to seasonal unemployment and underemployment outside peak visitor months",B:"The decline of fishing has no bearing on the wider local economy",C:"Tourism-based economies are always more stable than industries like fishing",D:"Seasonal employment patterns don't apply to tourism-dependent economies"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-FB-01",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about London's Docklands.",
      template:"___ ___ is long-term investment to reverse the economic and environmental decline of a run-down area, as seen at London's Docklands where derelict wharves became the offices and flats of ___.",
      blanks:["Urban","regeneration","Canary Wharf"],
      bank:["Urban","regeneration","Canary Wharf","sprawl","counter-urbanisation","green belt"],
      tag:"MC-URB-01",
      scaffold:"Urban regeneration reverses decline through planned, long-term investment — London's derelict Docklands were transformed into the offices, housing and transport links of Canary Wharf."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-FB-02",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about deindustrialisation.",
      template:"___ is the long-term decline of traditional industry, caused by factors such as cheaper overseas competition and changing technology, which left London's Docklands ___ and unemployed before ___ schemes were introduced.",
      blanks:["Deindustrialisation","derelict","regeneration"],
      bank:["Deindustrialisation","derelict","regeneration","urbanisation","booming","migration"],
      tag:"MC-URB-02",
      scaffold:"Deindustrialisation — docks and factories closing as container shipping moved elsewhere and manufacturing shifted overseas — left London's Docklands derelict and unemployed, creating the need for regeneration."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-FB-03",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about migration and London's character.",
      template:"International ___ has shaped London's social and cultural character, creating ethnically ___ areas such as Brick Lane, with distinctive shops, food and places of ___.",
      blanks:["migration","diverse","worship"],
      bank:["migration","diverse","worship","homogeneous","regeneration","sprawl"],
      tag:"MC-URB-03",
      scaffold:"International migration doesn't just change population size — it reshapes culture and identity, creating diverse, multicultural neighbourhoods like Brick Lane with their own shops, cuisines and religious buildings."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-FB-04",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about rural change in Cornwall.",
      template:"___ ___ and rising demand for ___ homes have pushed up house prices across Cornwall, pricing out many young local residents.",
      blanks:["Counter","urbanisation","second"],
      bank:["Counter","urbanisation","second","de","industrialisation","first"],
      tag:"MC-URB-04",
      scaffold:"Counter-urbanisation — people, often retirees, moving from cities into rural/coastal Cornwall — combined with high demand for second homes has changed Cornwall's housing market, raising prices well beyond what local wages can support."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-FB-05",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about the Eden Project.",
      template:"The Eden Project reclaimed a disused china clay ___ near St Austell, creating a ___ rural regeneration scheme that brought thousands of ___ jobs to the area.",
      blanks:["pit","sustainable","local"],
      bank:["pit","sustainable","local","quarry","unsustainable","national"],
      tag:"MC-URB-05",
      scaffold:"The Eden Project turned a derelict, scarred china clay pit into a major eco-attraction, diversifying Cornwall's economy with thousands of local jobs while keeping a strong environmental focus — a model of sustainable rural regeneration."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-FB-06",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about Cornwall's economy.",
      template:"As tin mining and fishing declined, Cornwall's economy shifted towards ___, but this industry is highly ___, leaving many workers facing ___ for much of the year.",
      blanks:["tourism","seasonal","unemployment"],
      bank:["tourism","seasonal","unemployment","manufacturing","stable","overemployment"],
      tag:"MC-URB-06",
      scaffold:"Cornwall's shift from primary industries to tourism hasn't guaranteed economic security: tourism is strongly seasonal, so many jobs are part-time and concentrated in summer, leaving workers facing unemployment or underemployment for much of the rest of the year."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-07",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"A student mixes up immigration and migration. What is the correct distinction?",
      options:{A:"Immigration is always temporary, migration is always permanent",B:"Immigration and migration are two words for exactly the same process",C:"Migration only refers to movement between countries; immigration only refers to movement within a country",D:"Immigration is the permanent movement of people to live in a country different from their home country; migration (in this context) is the permanent movement of people to live elsewhere within the same country"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-UKHUMAN-KT-01",
      scaffold:"International movement = immigration (into a country) or emigration (out of a country). Internal movement = migration (e.g. London to Cornwall). Net migration then measures the balance of immigration minus emigration for a country.",
      reforge:{stem:"A UK resident moves permanently from Manchester to Cornwall. Which term correctly describes this movement?",options:{A:"Immigration, since it crosses an international border",B:"Migration, since it is movement within the same country",C:"Net migration, since it only measures one person",D:"Emigration, since it leaves the UK entirely"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-08",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"What is net migration?",
      options:{A:"The difference between the number of migrants moving into a country and the number moving out of it",B:"The total number of people who have ever migrated into a country",C:"The percentage of a country's population that was born abroad",D:"A measure of how many people move within a country each year"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-UKHUMAN-KT-02",
      scaffold:"Net migration is a balance figure: (immigration) minus (emigration). Positive net migration means more people are arriving than leaving; this is one of the key drivers of the UK's growing and increasingly multicultural population alongside natural increase.",
      reforge:{stem:"A country records 50,000 people arriving to live there and 30,000 people leaving in the same year. What is the net migration figure?",options:{A:"-20,000 — more people left than arrived",B:"80,000 — the total of both figures added together",C:"+20,000 — more people arrived than left",D:"Net migration cannot be calculated from this information"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-09",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"How does a Trans-National Company (TNC) differ from ordinary Foreign Direct Investment (FDI)?",
      options:{A:"A TNC is a company that operates in countries other than where its headquarters is based; FDI is the investment itself — when a business or person in one country invests in a business located in another — which a TNC typically carries out as it expands abroad",B:"A TNC and FDI are identical terms describing the same thing",C:"A TNC only operates within its home country",D:"FDI can only be carried out by governments, never by companies"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-UKHUMAN-KT-03",
      scaffold:"A TNC is the organisation (e.g. a car manufacturer with factories on several continents); FDI is the financial mechanism it uses to set up or invest in those overseas operations. TNC investment is a major driver of globalisation.",
      reforge:{stem:"A UK-based supermarket chain opens and operates stores across several other countries. What type of organisation does this make it, and what did it likely use to fund this expansion abroad?",options:{A:"An NGO, funded entirely through charitable donations",B:"An enterprise zone, funded through privatisation",C:"A trade bloc, funded through government grants",D:"A Trans-National Company (TNC), funded through Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) in each new country"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-10",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"What does the 'shrinking world' effect describe, and what causes it?",
      options:{A:"The declining global population",B:"The feeling of the world becoming smaller and more easily accessible, caused by improvements in transport and communication technology that make travel and contact faster and cheaper",C:"A measurable reduction in the Earth's physical land area",D:"The process of a country's economy shrinking due to deindustrialisation"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-UKHUMAN-KT-04",
      scaffold:"'Shrinking world' is about perceived, not physical, distance — jet travel, container shipping and instant digital communication mean places that once took weeks to reach are now hours away, which is a major driver behind globalisation and increased migration and trade.",
      reforge:{stem:"Cheaper long-haul flights and instant global communication mean a business call can now be made across the world in seconds rather than weeks by letter. What geographical concept does this illustrate?",options:{A:"Segregation — groups of people becoming isolated from wider society",B:"Urbanisation — more people living in cities",C:"The shrinking world — the world feeling smaller and more accessible due to improved transport and communication",D:"Deindustrialisation — the decline of manufacturing"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-11",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"A student defines an enterprise zone as 'any area with a lot of businesses.' How should this be corrected?",
      options:{A:"An enterprise zone only applies to farming and agriculture",B:"The student's definition is already correct",C:"An enterprise zone is a region specifically set up by the government to encourage businesses to move in, typically offering lower tax rates or newly constructed offices as an incentive",D:"An enterprise zone is a region where all business activity is banned"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-UKHUMAN-KT-05",
      scaffold:"Enterprise zones are a deliberate government regeneration tool, not just a description of where businesses happen to cluster — they're used to attract investment into areas that have lost industry, such as former docklands or coalfield towns.",
      reforge:{stem:"A former industrial area is designated by the government with lower tax rates and new offices to attract businesses back. What is this designation called?",options:{A:"An enterprise zone",B:"A trade bloc",C:"A rural-urban continuum",D:"A multicultural event"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-12",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"Which correctly orders the four types of industry from raw materials to finished service, and gives an accurate example of each?",
      options:{A:"Quaternary → Tertiary → Secondary → Primary, with the examples reversed",B:"Tertiary industry refers to raw material extraction such as mining",C:"Primary and secondary industry both refer to manufacturing, just at different scales",D:"Primary (farmers extracting raw materials) → Secondary (factory workers manufacturing goods) → Tertiary (teachers providing a service) → Quaternary (architects working in design and technology)"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-UKHUMAN-KT-06",
      scaffold:"Primary = extracting raw materials (farming, mining). Secondary = manufacturing (turning raw materials into products). Tertiary = providing a service to people (teaching, retail). Quaternary = knowledge/technology-based work (research, design, IT). The UK's economy has shifted heavily from primary/secondary towards tertiary/quaternary over the last century — this is a key idea across the whole topic.",
      reforge:{stem:"A product designer working on new technology, and a factory worker assembling a product, belong to which two industry sectors respectively?",options:{A:"Secondary and quaternary",B:"Quaternary and secondary",C:"Tertiary and primary",D:"Primary and tertiary"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-13",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"What is privatisation, and how does it differ from a government grant?",
      options:{A:"Privatisation means a government takes over a private company",B:"Privatisation is when essential services are taken over by a private company from the government; a grant is money given by the government to a business or organisation, so the two involve opposite directions of control or funding",C:"A grant always leads to privatisation of the organisation receiving it",D:"Privatisation and a grant are the same government policy"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-UKHUMAN-KT-07",
      scaffold:"Privatisation shifts ownership of a service from public to private hands (e.g. rail, water). A grant is government money flowing the other way, into a business or organisation, often to support development goals such as research or regeneration — don't confuse the direction of each.",
      reforge:{stem:"A government sells off a previously state-run rail service to a private company. What is this process called?",options:{A:"Enterprise zoning",B:"A grant",C:"Nationalisation",D:"Privatisation"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-14",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"What is the rural-urban continuum, and what is it used to show?",
      options:{A:"An imaginary line drawn from the centre of an urban city to the centre of a rural area, used to show how living conditions and population characteristics change gradually between the two",B:"The boundary that legally separates urban and rural land use",C:"A physical road connecting a city centre to the nearest rural village",D:"A measure of a country's total population density"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-UKHUMAN-KT-08",
      scaffold:"The continuum is a conceptual tool, not a real line — it helps geographers describe how characteristics like population density, house prices, age structure and services change gradually, rather than switching abruptly, as you move from city centre to countryside.",
      reforge:{stem:"Population density, house prices and access to services tend to change gradually rather than suddenly as you travel from a city centre out into the surrounding countryside. What concept does this describe?",options:{A:"A fixed legal boundary between rural and urban land",B:"Economic inequality",C:"The rural-urban continuum",D:"Net migration"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-15",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"A student says 'segregation and multiculturalism mean the same thing.' Why is this wrong?",
      options:{A:"Segregation only refers to religious groups, multiculturalism only to ethnic groups",B:"The student is correct — they are interchangeable terms",C:"Segregation is where a group is isolated from the rest of society, either by choice or exclusion; multiculturalism/multicultural events celebrate cultures and religions from different backgrounds mixing and being shared within a community — the two describe near-opposite social outcomes",D:"Multiculturalism means a population has only one ethnic group"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-UKHUMAN-KT-09",
      scaffold:"Segregation describes separation and isolation between groups; multicultural events (like Chinese New Year or Notting Hill Carnival) represent integration and shared celebration of diverse cultures within the same community — exam answers about a place's cultural character often need you to distinguish evidence of one from the other.",
      reforge:{stem:"A neighbourhood hosts an annual festival celebrating a culture originally from another country, drawing residents of many backgrounds together. What does this best represent, in contrast to segregation?",options:{A:"A multicultural event — celebrating cultural mixing, the near-opposite of isolation between groups",B:"Hate crime — since it focuses on one culture's traditions",C:"Segregation — since a specific culture is being highlighted",D:"Net migration — since the event involves migrants"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-16",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"What does the Commonwealth refer to, and how is it linked to UK migration patterns?",
      options:{A:"A trade bloc that only the UK and USA belong to",B:"An alternative name for the European Union",C:"A term describing only the UK's domestic regions",D:"A group of countries that were colonised by Britain as part of the British Empire — historical Commonwealth ties (language, institutional links) have shaped patterns of migration to the UK from countries such as India, Jamaica and Nigeria"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-UKHUMAN-KT-10",
      scaffold:"The Commonwealth's shared colonial history explains why post-war UK immigration (e.g. the Windrush generation from the Caribbean) often came from former colonies — historical ties, language and existing community networks make migration between these countries more likely.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why do patterns of UK immigration often link back to former British colonies?",options:{A:"The Commonwealth only includes European countries",B:"Shared history through the Commonwealth — including language and existing community ties — has made migration from these countries to the UK more likely",C:"The Commonwealth is unrelated to migration patterns",D:"Commonwealth countries are legally barred from migrating to the UK"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-17",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"Why did the Jubilee Line Extension support regeneration in London's Docklands?",
      options:{A:"It improved access to jobs and investment, making the area more attractive to businesses and residents",B:"It moved every Docklands resident into the countryside",C:"It removed the need for any new housing or services",D:"It caused container shipping to return to the old docks"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-UKHUMAN-11",
      scaffold:"Better transport can create a multiplier effect: it connects residents to employment, attracts firms and encourages further investment in housing and services. The Jubilee Line Extension was one part of Docklands regeneration, alongside the DLR, Canary Wharf development and environmental improvement.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which outcome would provide evidence of a transport-led multiplier effect in Docklands?",options:{A:"A new station opens but no businesses, jobs or residents follow",B:"Improved access encourages offices to locate nearby, creating jobs and demand for shops and services",C:"All transport investment reduces land values",D:"The railway operates without connecting to any other part of London"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-18",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"Why can tourism make Cornwall's economy vulnerable despite creating jobs?",
      options:{A:"Tourism guarantees stable employment in every season",B:"Tourism removes the need for transport and internet links",C:"Seasonal demand can produce insecure work and a downturn can reduce visitors and local income",D:"Tourism has no relationship with house prices or land use"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-UKHUMAN-12",
      scaffold:"Tourism diversifies Cornwall beyond declining mining and fishing, but it can create dependence on seasonal and discretionary spending. Jobs may be temporary, homes may become second residences, and bad weather or recession can reduce demand.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which policy would best reduce Cornwall's dependence on seasonal tourism?",options:{A:"Develop year-round sectors such as renewable energy, digital work and education alongside tourism",B:"Convert all local housing into holiday lets",C:"Close transport links outside the summer season",D:"Reduce the range of employment available to local residents"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-19",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"How can rural-urban migration affect the population structure of both places?",
      options:{A:"It always increases the proportion of working-age people in rural areas",B:"It can remove younger adults from rural areas while increasing the working-age population of towns and cities",C:"It changes population size but never age structure",D:"It causes identical population changes in origin and destination areas"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-UKHUMAN-13",
      scaffold:"Migrants are often young adults, so origin areas may experience ageing and a smaller working-age population, while destinations gain workers and consumers. Migration can reshape both population size and age structure.",
      reforge:{stem:"A rural district loses many young adults to cities. Which consequence is most plausible if the pattern continues?",options:{A:"A larger elderly share and pressure on services such as healthcare and transport",B:"A guaranteed increase in local school enrolment",C:"A younger workforce without any out-migration",D:"No demographic change because only population totals matter"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-UKHUMAN-20",spec:"GCSE-UKHUMAN",stem:"What is one likely consequence when house prices rise quickly in a popular rural area?",
      options:{A:"Local workers always become more able to buy homes",B:"Second homes and retirement demand can reduce the supply of affordable housing for younger residents",C:"Rural services automatically expand because prices rise",D:"Population pressure disappears as villages become cheaper"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-UKHUMAN-14",
      scaffold:"Counter-urbanisation, retirement migration and second-home ownership can increase demand and prices. Existing owners may gain, but local young adults and lower-income workers can be priced out, while seasonal occupancy can weaken year-round demand for services.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which finding would best support the claim that rising second-home ownership is affecting a rural community?",options:{A:"A higher proportion of homes are empty outside the holiday season while local rents and prices rise",B:"Every village shop reports longer opening hours all year",C:"More local young adults can afford to buy their first home",D:"House prices fall as visitor numbers increase"},correct:"A"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-GEO-URB"] = {
  label: "Challenges of an Urbanising World",
  color: "#0f766e",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"GCSE-URB-01",spec:"GCSE-URB",stem:"Mumbai has grown from around 3 million people in 1950 to over 20 million in its metropolitan area today, making it a megacity. A student says this growth is purely from births in the city itself. What else is driving it?",
      options:{A:"Mumbai's growth is caused entirely by counter-urbanisation",B:"International migration from other countries is the only cause",C:"Nothing else — natural increase alone explains all megacity growth",D:"Rural-urban migration — people moving from the countryside to Mumbai in search of jobs and better opportunities"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-MUM-01",
      scaffold:"Megacity growth (a city with over 10 million people) is driven by both natural increase (births minus deaths) AND rural-urban migration. In Mumbai, and most rapidly urbanising cities in lower and middle income countries, migration is often the dominant factor — people are pulled by the promise of jobs, education and services, and pushed from rural areas by poverty, drought, or lack of opportunity.",
      reforge:{stem:"A rapidly growing city in a lower income country sees its population double in 15 years, with most of the growth made up of young adults arriving from rural provinces rather than being born in the city. What best explains this?",options:{A:"Counter-urbanisation",B:"Rural-urban migration driven by push and pull factors",C:"International migration only",D:"Natural increase from a falling death rate alone"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URB-02",spec:"GCSE-URB",stem:"Migrants moving to Mumbai are often 'pushed' from rural areas by poverty and drought, and 'pulled' by the promise of jobs and services. A student says push and pull factors always come true for migrants. Why might this be wrong for many arriving in Mumbai?",
      options:{A:"Many migrants cannot find formal jobs or affordable housing, ending up in informal settlements like Dharavi rather than achieving the opportunities that pulled them there",B:"Every migrant to Mumbai finds a well-paid formal job immediately",C:"Mumbai has no housing shortage, so all migrants are easily housed",D:"Push and pull factors don't apply to internal (rural-urban) migration"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-MUM-02",
      scaffold:"Push factors (rural poverty, drought, lack of land/jobs) and pull factors (perceived jobs, education, healthcare) explain why people move, but the reality often falls short: Mumbai's formal job market and housing supply can't absorb the scale of migration, so many migrants find only informal work and end up in slums like Dharavi rather than the opportunities they expected.",
      reforge:{stem:"A migrant moves to a megacity expecting factory work and a proper house, but instead finds only casual, informal work and has to build a shelter from scrap materials in an unplanned settlement. What does this show about push-pull migration theory?",options:{A:"Informal settlements only exist because of natural disasters",B:"Push and pull factors guarantee a better outcome for every migrant",C:"The pull factors that attracted the migrant don't always match the reality once they arrive",D:"This migrant must have moved for climate reasons only"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URB-03",spec:"GCSE-URB",stem:"Dharavi, one of Asia's largest slums, sits in the middle of Mumbai and is home to around 1 million people in roughly 2.1 km². A student calls it 'just an area of poverty with nothing going on.' Why is this an oversimplification?",
      options:{A:"Dharavi is a wealthy, formally planned district of Mumbai",B:"Dharavi has no economic activity of any kind",C:"Everyone in Dharavi is unemployed",D:"Dharavi has a huge, thriving informal economy — recycling, leather goods, pottery and textiles — generating an estimated $1 billion a year, alongside serious challenges like overcrowding and poor sanitation"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-MUM-03",
      scaffold:"Dharavi is often stereotyped as pure deprivation, but it's also a major centre of small-scale, informal industry (recycling, leather, pottery, garments) that contributes hugely to Mumbai's economy — while still facing real challenges: extreme population density, poor sanitation, limited access to clean water, and insecure land tenure. Both things are true at once.",
      reforge:{stem:"A visitor to Dharavi is surprised to find thousands of small workshops recycling plastic and producing leather goods for export, generating significant income, despite the area's poor infrastructure and sanitation. What does this suggest about informal settlements?",options:{A:"They can combine severe deprivation with genuine, significant economic activity",B:"Poor infrastructure always means no economic activity exists",C:"Informal settlements never have any economic value",D:"Informal settlements are identical to formally planned industrial zones"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URB-04",spec:"GCSE-URB",stem:"Rapid urban growth in Mumbai has caused challenges including traffic congestion, air and water pollution, and a severe housing shortage. A student says these problems only affect informal settlements like Dharavi. Why is this wrong?",
      options:{A:"Traffic congestion in Mumbai only occurs inside Dharavi",B:"Air pollution in Mumbai comes only from informal settlements",C:"Congestion, pollution and strain on infrastructure affect the whole city, including wealthier formal areas, because the scale of growth has outpaced planned infrastructure everywhere",D:"Wealthy areas of Mumbai are completely unaffected by any city-wide infrastructure problems"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-MUM-04",
      scaffold:"Rapid, often unplanned urban growth strains city-wide systems, not just informal areas: Mumbai's roads, public transport, water supply, and waste management all face pressure from a population that has grown far faster than formal infrastructure could keep pace with, so congestion and pollution affect richer and poorer districts alike, even if the severity of housing/sanitation problems is worse in slums.",
      reforge:{stem:"A rapidly growing city's commute times triple over two decades, and its air quality index regularly reaches 'hazardous' levels city-wide, not just in its poorest districts. What does this suggest about the nature of these urban challenges?",options:{A:"Only slum residents ever experience traffic congestion",B:"They stem from city-wide infrastructure struggling to keep pace with growth, not just conditions in informal settlements",C:"Rich areas of a city are structurally immune to congestion",D:"Air pollution in cities is always confined to informal settlements"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URB-05",spec:"GCSE-URB",stem:"The Dharavi Redevelopment Project proposes demolishing much of the settlement and rehousing residents in high-rise flats, partly funded by selling some of the reclaimed land to private developers. A student says all Dharavi residents support this. Why is this a key controversy?",
      options:{A:"Redevelopment schemes never affect where people can run a business from",B:"Every single resident of Dharavi is fully in favour of demolition with no concerns",C:"Many residents and business owners fear losing their livelihoods (informal workshops don't fit easily into high-rise flats), affordable community networks, and central location, so the scheme is contested rather than universally welcomed",D:"The scheme has no effect on informal businesses operating in Dharavi"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-MUM-05",
      scaffold:"Top-down redevelopment schemes like this one are controversial: while they promise improved housing, critics point out that high-rise flats don't easily accommodate ground-floor workshops that Dharavi's informal economy depends on, some residents may not qualify for rehousing (needing proof of long-term residency), and community/social networks built up over decades can be broken up — so support is far from unanimous.",
      reforge:{stem:"A city government plans to demolish an informal settlement and move residents into apartment blocks, funding the project by selling part of the site to developers. Local workshop owners protest that flats have no space for their ground-floor businesses. What does this illustrate about top-down slum redevelopment schemes?",options:{A:"They can conflict with the informal economic activities that residents depend on, making them controversial rather than universally welcomed",B:"Selling land to developers has no bearing on how a scheme is received",C:"Workshop owners in informal settlements never object to redevelopment",D:"Redevelopment always benefits every resident equally with no downsides"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URB-06",spec:"GCSE-URB",stem:"Sites and services schemes provide migrants with a legal plot of land, basic infrastructure (water, electricity, sanitation), and let residents build their own homes gradually. A student says this is identical to the Dharavi Redevelopment Project's approach. What's the key difference?",
      options:{A:"There is no meaningful difference between the two approaches",B:"Sites and services is a bottom-up, low-cost approach that works with residents' own building efforts, rather than a top-down demolition-and-rebuild scheme funded by land sales",C:"Sites and services schemes provide no infrastructure at all",D:"Both schemes demolish existing homes and force residents into high-rise flats"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MUM-06",
      scaffold:"Sites and services is a bottom-up strategy: it gives residents secure land tenure and basic services, then lets them build and improve homes themselves over time at low cost — contrasting with top-down schemes like Dharavi's redevelopment plan, which demolish existing settlements and replace them with formally built high-rise housing, often disrupting existing communities and informal economies.",
      reforge:{stem:"A city scheme gives informal settlement residents legal ownership of a small plot plus a water connection and basic drainage, then leaves residents to build and upgrade their own housing over several years. What type of urban management strategy is this?",options:{A:"Counter-urbanisation policy",B:"A top-down demolition and high-rise redevelopment scheme",C:"A green belt policy",D:"A bottom-up sites and services scheme"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URB-FB-01",spec:"GCSE-URB",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about Mumbai's growth.",
      template:"Mumbai's growth into a ___ (over 10 million people) is driven by both natural increase and ___-___ migration, as people move from rural areas in search of jobs.",
      blanks:["megacity","rural","urban"],
      bank:["megacity","rural","urban","counter","international","city"],
      tag:"MC-MUM-01",
      scaffold:"A megacity has over 10 million people. Mumbai's growth combines natural increase with large-scale rural-urban migration, as people are pushed from the countryside and pulled by the promise of jobs and services in the city."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URB-FB-02",spec:"GCSE-URB",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about push and pull factors.",
      template:"Migrants to Mumbai are ___ from rural areas by poverty and drought, and ___ by the promise of jobs — but many end up in ___ work and informal settlements rather than the opportunities they expected.",
      blanks:["pushed","pulled","informal"],
      bank:["pushed","pulled","informal","formal","counter-urbanised","regenerated"],
      tag:"MC-MUM-02",
      scaffold:"Push factors (rural poverty, drought) and pull factors (perceived jobs and services) drive migration, but the reality often falls short — many migrants find only informal, insecure work rather than the formal jobs they hoped for."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URB-FB-03",spec:"GCSE-URB",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about Dharavi's economy.",
      template:"Dharavi has a large ___ economy — including ___, leather goods and textiles — generating an estimated $1 billion a year, despite facing serious challenges like overcrowding and poor ___.",
      blanks:["informal","recycling","sanitation"],
      bank:["informal","recycling","sanitation","formal","tourism","transport"],
      tag:"MC-MUM-03",
      scaffold:"Dharavi combines a thriving informal economy (recycling, leather, textiles, pottery) worth an estimated $1 billion a year with real challenges — extreme density and poor sanitation — both true at once."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URB-FB-04",spec:"GCSE-URB",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about urban challenges in Mumbai.",
      template:"Rapid urban growth strains ___-wide infrastructure in Mumbai, causing traffic ___ and pollution that affect wealthier formal areas as well as ___ settlements.",
      blanks:["city","congestion","informal"],
      bank:["city","congestion","informal","rural","regeneration","sustainable"],
      tag:"MC-MUM-04",
      scaffold:"Mumbai's rapid growth has outpaced formal infrastructure city-wide, so congestion and pollution affect richer and poorer districts alike, even though housing and sanitation problems are worse in informal settlements."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URB-FB-05",spec:"GCSE-URB",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about the Dharavi Redevelopment Project.",
      template:"The Dharavi Redevelopment Project proposes ___ much of the settlement and rehousing residents in ___-rise flats, but is controversial because ___ workshops don't easily fit into flats.",
      blanks:["demolishing","high","informal"],
      bank:["demolishing","high","informal","building","low","formal"],
      tag:"MC-MUM-05",
      scaffold:"The scheme's top-down demolition-and-rebuild approach is controversial partly because ground-floor informal workshops, central to Dharavi's economy, don't fit easily into high-rise flats — a major source of resident and business opposition."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URB-FB-06",spec:"GCSE-URB",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about sites and services schemes.",
      template:"Sites and services is a ___-up approach that gives residents a legal plot and basic ___ (water, electricity, sanitation), letting them build their own homes ___ over time.",
      blanks:["bottom","infrastructure","gradually"],
      bank:["bottom","infrastructure","gradually","top","instantly","tourism"],
      tag:"MC-MUM-06",
      scaffold:"Sites and services schemes work bottom-up: secure land tenure plus basic infrastructure, with residents building and upgrading housing themselves over time — a contrast to top-down demolition-and-rebuild schemes."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URB-07",spec:"GCSE-URB",stem:"Mumbai is described as having both formal and informal employment. Which example best illustrates informal employment?",
      options:{A:"A government-employed railway engineer with a contract and pension",B:"A street vendor or small recycling worker without secure contract, regulation or reliable pay",C:"A university professor employed by the state",D:"A bank manager in a registered office"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MUM-07",
      scaffold:"Informal employment is common in rapidly urbanising cities: work is often small-scale, unregistered, insecure and outside tax or labour protections. It can provide vital income, but workers are vulnerable because rights, safety and pay are not guaranteed.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why is Dharavi's informal economy important despite its problems?",options:{A:"It provides no employment at all",B:"It is run entirely by foreign governments",C:"It only employs tourists",D:"It creates jobs and income through workshops, recycling and small businesses, but often without secure protection or formal regulation"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URB-08",spec:"GCSE-URB",stem:"A student says Mumbai's traffic congestion is only an environmental problem. Why is this incomplete?",
      options:{A:"Congestion has environmental, economic and social effects, including air pollution, lost work time and reduced quality of life",B:"Congestion is beneficial because it always increases income",C:"Congestion only affects rural areas",D:"Congestion has no link to air pollution"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-MUM-08",
      scaffold:"Urban problems are multi-dimensional. Traffic congestion increases emissions and air pollution, but it also wastes time, raises transport costs, delays deliveries and can reduce quality of life for residents and commuters.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which answer best evaluates a new metro line in Mumbai?",options:{A:"It only matters if it makes the city look modern",B:"It cannot affect employment access",C:"It may reduce congestion and emissions while improving access to jobs, but benefits depend on cost, coverage and affordability",D:"It will solve all urban problems immediately"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URB-09",spec:"GCSE-URB",stem:"Why can rapid urbanisation lead to water and sanitation problems in Mumbai?",
      options:{A:"Cities do not need water infrastructure",B:"Population growth can outpace the construction of piped water, sewers and waste systems",C:"Sanitation problems only happen when birth rates fall",D:"Informal settlements always receive infrastructure before formal housing"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MUM-09",
      scaffold:"Rapid urbanisation becomes a challenge when infrastructure cannot keep up with population growth. Informal settlements may lack reliable piped water, toilets and drainage, raising health risks and increasing pressure on already stretched city services.",
      reforge:{stem:"A settlement grows quickly before sewers and piped water are installed. Which consequence is most likely?",options:{A:"Improved public health automatically",B:"Higher risk of waterborne disease and pressure on shared facilities",C:"Immediate disappearance of informal work",D:"No change because infrastructure is unrelated to population"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-URB-10",spec:"GCSE-URB",stem:"Which criticism of top-down urban redevelopment is strongest in the Dharavi case?",
      options:{A:"It always gives every resident a larger house and better-paid job",B:"It is identical to self-help housing",C:"It can ignore residents' livelihoods and community networks if decisions are made mainly by government and developers",D:"It never changes land values"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-MUM-10",
      scaffold:"Top-down redevelopment can improve housing and infrastructure, but in Dharavi the controversy is whether high-rise rehousing protects the dense networks of small workshops, street-level businesses and community support that make the settlement function.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why might some Dharavi residents oppose high-rise rehousing even if flats have better sanitation?",options:{A:"They may lose street-level workshop space, customer access and community networks that support their livelihoods",B:"They prefer having no legal security",C:"They are unaffected by any redevelopment",D:"High-rise flats are always cheaper to run than informal homes"},correct:"A"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-GEO-DEV"] = {
  label: "Development Dynamics",
  color: "#0f766e",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-01",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"Why do geographers use the Human Development Index (HDI) rather than GDP per capita alone to measure development?",
      options:{A:"HDI is measured every year whereas GDP is measured every ten years",B:"GDP per capita cannot be measured in developing countries",C:"HDI is easier to calculate than GDP per capita",D:"HDI combines life expectancy, education and income, so it captures social as well as economic development"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-DEV-01",
      scaffold:"Development has many strands — economic, social, demographic, political, environmental — so a single economic indicator gives a partial picture. HDI scores between 0 and 1 using life expectancy (health), education level/literacy (education) and GNI per capita (wealth). A country can have high GDP from oil exports but low life expectancy and literacy; HDI exposes that, GDP per capita hides it.",
      reforge:{stem:"A country has a high GDP per capita but a low HDI score. What is the most likely explanation?",options:{A:"HDI cannot be applied to wealthy countries",B:"The country has miscalculated its GDP",C:"Wealth is concentrated in a narrow sector or a small elite, so it has not translated into health and education for the population",D:"The country must have a very small population"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-02",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"A student writes: 'GDP per capita tells us the quality of life of people in a country.' Why is this a weak claim?",
      options:{A:"It is an average, so it hides inequality — many people may earn far below it, and it says nothing about health, education or what the money is spent on",B:"Quality of life can only be measured by life expectancy",C:"GDP per capita measures income, and income is irrelevant to quality of life",D:"GDP per capita is never published for developing countries"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-DEV-02",
      scaffold:"This is a classic 2-mark exam question. GDP per capita = total output ÷ population. Problems: (1) it's a mean, so extreme wealth at the top drags it up while most live below it; (2) it measures production, not wellbeing — spending on weapons counts the same as spending on hospitals; (3) GDP even rises after a disaster because of rebuilding. Use it alongside social indicators, not instead of them.",
      reforge:{stem:"Following a major earthquake, a country's GDP rises the next year because of reconstruction spending. What does this show about GDP as a development measure?",options:{A:"GDP should be replaced entirely by birth rate",B:"GDP measures economic activity regardless of whether it improves people's lives, so a rise can mask a fall in wellbeing",C:"Earthquakes are good for development",D:"GDP is always calculated incorrectly after disasters"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-03",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"Which set of characteristics best describes an emerging country?",
      options:{A:"No industry, no trade links and a declining population",B:"Very high human development, a dominant service sector and an ageing population",C:"Rapid economic growth based on industrial development, rising incomes and high or medium human development",D:"Low human development, subsistence agriculture and very low incomes"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-DEV-03",
      scaffold:"Edexcel B uses three categories: developing (low human development — poor quality of life, inadequate services), emerging (high/medium human development — rapid industrial-led growth, rising incomes, reasonable standard of living for most), and developed (very high human development — modern industries, high incomes). India, China and Brazil are the standard emerging examples. B describes a developing country; C describes a developed one.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which of the following would be the strongest single piece of evidence that a country has moved from 'developing' to 'emerging'?",options:{A:"A sustained shift in employment and GDP from agriculture towards manufacturing and services, alongside rising GNI per capita",B:"Joining the United Nations",C:"A rise in the number of tourists visiting",D:"An increase in total population"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-04",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"Which of the following is a HISTORICAL cause of global inequality?",
      options:{A:"Landlocked countries being cut off from seaborne trade",B:"Tropical cyclones repeatedly destroying infrastructure",C:"Corrupt governments failing to invest in healthcare",D:"Colonialism — richer nations took political control of other countries and exploited them economically"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-DEV-04",
      scaffold:"Exams often ask you to sort causes into categories. Environmental: climate/hazards, disease, topography, landlocked position. Historical: colonialism and neo-colonialism, trade patterns. Political/economic: governance, corruption, international relations, resource security. Social: healthcare, education, demography, poverty. B is environmental, C is environmental, D is political — only A is historical.",
      reforge:{stem:"What distinguishes neo-colonialism from colonialism?",options:{A:"Neo-colonialism only affects countries that were never colonised",B:"Neo-colonialism uses indirect 'soft power' — trade terms, aid conditions, IGOs and TNC dominance — rather than the direct political and military control of colonialism",C:"They are two words for exactly the same process",D:"Neo-colonialism happened first, before colonialism"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-05",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"Why does a landlocked location tend to slow a country's economic development?",
      options:{A:"Being landlocked means a country has no natural resources",B:"It is cut off from seaborne trade routes, so exporting and importing costs more and depends on neighbouring countries",C:"Landlocked countries always have desert climates",D:"Landlocked countries cannot join international organisations"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-DEV-05",
      scaffold:"Most world trade moves by sea because container shipping is cheap and bulk. A landlocked country (e.g. Chad) must move goods overland through neighbours first, adding cost, delay and political risk — which discourages TNC investment. Note this is a physical/environmental cause that interacts with political ones: relations with the neighbour controlling your route matter enormously.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which combination of physical factors would most severely limit a developing country's ability to grow economically?",options:{A:"Landlocked position, mountainous terrain and a climate that supports disease vectors such as malaria",B:"A long coastline and a temperate climate",C:"Flat, fertile land close to a navigable river",D:"Large deposits of minerals near a major port"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-06",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"Rostow's modernisation theory argues that countries develop by:",
      options:{A:"Remaining permanently dependent on wealthier core countries",B:"Relying entirely on aid from non-governmental organisations",C:"Passing through five linear stages from traditional society to high mass consumption, driven by investment and industrialisation",D:"Reducing trade with other countries to protect local industry"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-DEV-06",
      scaffold:"Rostow (1960): Stage 1 traditional society (subsistence farming, bartering) → Stage 2 pre-conditions for take-off (surpluses traded, infrastructure, shift to manufacturing) → Stage 3 take-off (industrial and regional growth, investment) → Stage 4 drive to maturity (technology, diversification) → Stage 5 high mass consumption (consumer society, dominant service sector). Learn the stage names in order — they're commonly asked directly.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which is the strongest criticism of Rostow's modernisation theory?",options:{A:"It says nothing about industrialisation",B:"It was based on a study of European countries, all of which failed to develop",C:"It has too many stages to be useful",D:"It assumes all countries start from the same position and ignores how colonialism and debt repayments can block a country from ever reaching take-off"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-07",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"Frank's dependency theory explains global inequality by arguing that:",
      options:{A:"Peripheral countries sell low-value raw materials to core countries, which process them into high-value goods and grow richer — keeping the periphery dependent",B:"All countries pass through the same five stages of growth",C:"Poverty is caused entirely by physical geography",D:"Development depends only on the size of a country's population"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-DEV-07",
      scaffold:"Frank developed dependency theory in opposition to Rostow. Core (Europe, North America) vs periphery (much of Africa, Latin America). The periphery exports coffee beans, cocoa, sugar and minerals cheaply; the core adds value through processing and sells manufactured goods back. Wealth therefore flows to the core. It's a structural explanation — poverty isn't a starting point you grow out of, it's actively produced by the relationship.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which piece of evidence most challenges Frank's dependency theory?",options:{A:"Some countries in the periphery have large populations",B:"Former peripheral countries such as China, Brazil and India have achieved rapid economic growth and are now emerging economies",C:"Some core countries have experienced recessions",D:"Coffee prices change from year to year"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-08",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"Which of these is a TOP-DOWN development strategy?",
      options:{A:"An NGO training local farmers in improved growing techniques",B:"Micro-hydro schemes built and maintained by villagers in rural Nepal",C:"A microfinance loan enabling a woman to start a small business",D:"A hydroelectric power dam funded by a World Bank loan"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-DEV-08",
      scaffold:"Top-down: large-scale, expensive, high technology, decided by governments/IGOs (IMF, World Bank), aimed at economic growth — dams, ports, airports, motorways. Bottom-up: small-scale, local, low-cost, often NGO-led, aimed at social development — wells, clinics, schools, farmer training, intermediate technology, microfinance, Fairtrade. B, C and D are all bottom-up.",
      reforge:{stem:"A government builds a large HEP dam with an IGO loan. Which criticism is most specific to top-down strategies?",options:{A:"The project is too small to make any difference",B:"It only helps women",C:"Local people may not benefit at all, and a lack of local education and training makes the high-tech scheme costly to maintain",D:"It cannot be funded because IGOs never lend money"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-09",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"What is the main advantage of intermediate technology as a development strategy?",
      options:{A:"It generates the highest possible GDP growth",B:"It is appropriate to local needs, affordable, and can be built and maintained by the community itself",C:"It replaces the need for any local labour",D:"It requires large loans from the IMF"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-DEV-09",
      scaffold:"Intermediate (appropriate) technology is matched to the skills, wealth and needs of a local community. The Nepal micro-hydro example: villagers build and maintain it, and the electricity lets children study at night and families use phones and the internet. Because there's no dependence on outside engineers or expensive spares, it's sustainable — which is exactly the failure point of many top-down projects.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why might a bottom-up NGO project be criticised despite meeting local needs effectively?",options:{A:"It cannot improve health or education",B:"Local people are unable to use the technology",C:"Governments can come to rely on NGOs instead of developing their own national systems to support their people",D:"It always costs more than a large dam"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-10",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"How does Fairtrade aim to reduce the development gap?",
      options:{A:"It removes all tariffs between wealthy countries",B:"It provides emergency food aid after disasters",C:"It cancels the debts of the world's poorest countries",D:"It sets trade standards guaranteeing producers a fairer price, with part of the price invested back into community development projects"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-DEV-10",
      scaffold:"Fairtrade is a bottom-up trade-based strategy: minimum prices, environmentally sustainable farming, and a social premium reinvested locally. The Gumutindo Coffee Cooperative in eastern Uganda is the standard example — over 90% of small coffee farmers joined, and milling the coffee before roasting adds value locally rather than exporting raw beans, which raises farmer income. Note how that directly counters Frank's core–periphery problem.",
      reforge:{stem:"Fairtrade cooperatives often process crops locally before export. Why does this matter for development?",options:{A:"Processing adds value within the producing country instead of the profit going to core countries, so more income stays local",B:"It allows the country to stop trading altogether",C:"Processing reduces the weight of the crop so it is cheaper to ship",D:"It guarantees the country will reach Rostow's stage 5"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-11",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"In 2006 the IMF cancelled the debts of 19 of the world's poorest countries. What was the intended development benefit?",
      options:{A:"Money previously spent on debt interest could be redirected into infrastructure, industry and services",B:"It removed the need for those countries to trade",C:"The countries could immediately join the G20",D:"It guaranteed those countries would become developed within ten years"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-DEV-11",
      scaffold:"Many developing countries borrowed heavily in the 1970s–80s and then could not repay because of high interest rates — debt servicing swallowed money that should have funded development. Debt relief frees that money: Uganda used savings to provide safe water for over 2 million people. Evaluation point: the benefit depends on governance — a corrupt government may retain the money rather than invest it.",
      reforge:{stem:"What is the key limitation of debt relief as a development strategy?",options:{A:"No country has ever been granted debt relief",B:"The freed-up money only helps if the government is transparent and invests it — corrupt governments may keep it",C:"Debt relief can only be given to developed countries",D:"Debt relief always increases a country's debt"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-12",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"Why do TNCs typically locate manufacturing and data-processing operations in emerging countries?",
      options:{A:"TNCs are legally required to locate outside developed countries",B:"Emerging countries have the highest wages in the world",C:"Lower labour costs, large workforces, and government incentives such as special economic zones with reduced taxes and lighter regulation",D:"Emerging countries have the strictest environmental regulations"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-DEV-12",
      scaffold:"Pull factors for TNCs: cheap, often skilled and English-speaking labour; large domestic markets; special economic zones (SEZs) and export processing zones (EPZs) with low or no tax; restricted union rights and low minimum wages; limited environmental and health-and-safety regulation, which cuts costs. Note that the last two are simultaneously the reason TNC investment is criticised.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which is the strongest argument that TNC investment does NOT fully benefit an emerging country?",options:{A:"TNCs are not allowed to pay taxes",B:"TNCs never create any jobs at all",C:"TNC factories always close within one year",D:"Higher-level management and technical jobs usually stay in the TNC's home country, leaving the host as producer and resource extractor, and the TNC can relocate whenever it becomes more profitable to do so"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-13",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"What is the 'positive multiplier effect' in development?",
      options:{A:"Aid money is divided equally between all regions of a country",B:"New investment creates jobs and incomes, which raises spending and tax revenue, funding further investment and attracting more companies",C:"Population grows faster than the economy can support",D:"A country's debt increases each year through compound interest"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-DEV-13",
      scaffold:"The multiplier is the engine behind the 'cycle of wealth'. TNC builds a factory → jobs and wages → workers spend locally → other businesses grow and co-locate → government collects more tax → invests in education, healthcare and infrastructure → the workforce becomes more skilled and attractive to further investment. It's the reason exam answers about development should chain consequences, not just list them.",
      reforge:{stem:"A 3-mark question asks you to explain how outsourcing affects economic development. Which answer would score all three marks?",options:{A:"TNCs invest in infrastructure (1), which encourages other companies to co-locate (1), creating further job opportunities (1)",B:"Outsourcing is bad for developed countries because they lose jobs.",C:"Outsourcing creates jobs.",D:"Outsourcing is when work is sent to another country. It is common in India. Many people work in call centres."},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-14",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"According to the demographic transition model (DTM), which stage is characterised by a rapidly FALLING birth rate while death rate continues to fall more slowly?",
      options:{A:"Stage 2",B:"Stage 1",C:"Stage 3",D:"Stage 5"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-DEV-14",
      scaffold:"Stage 1: high BR, high DR, low total population. Stage 2: DR falls (better diet, healthcare, clean water) while BR stays high — population rises rapidly. Stage 3: BR falls rapidly (contraception, family planning, lower infant mortality, cost of children) while DR falls slowly — growth slows. Stage 4: both low and fluctuating. Stage 5: DR exceeds BR, population declines. Emerging countries such as India and Nigeria sit in stage 3.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why does the birth rate fall sharply in stage 3 of the DTM?",options:{A:"Governments make it illegal to have more than one child",B:"The death rate rises so quickly that fewer adults survive to have children",C:"People emigrate, so fewer babies are born abroad",D:"Falling infant mortality means parents no longer need many children for some to survive, alongside better contraception, family planning and education and employment for women"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-15",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"A population pyramid has a very narrow base and a wide top. What does this indicate?",
      options:{A:"A youthful population with a high birth rate — DTM stage 2",B:"An ageing population with a low birth rate and high life expectancy — typically DTM stage 5",C:"A country with equal numbers in every age group",D:"A country experiencing rapid population growth"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-DEV-15",
      scaffold:"Pyramid shapes: concave with a very wide base = stage 2 (Niger, youthful, high BR and DR). Pyramid = stage 3 (Nepal). Column = stage 4 (USA). Pentagon with narrow base = stage 5 (Japan, ageing). When interpreting, check four things: young dependents (base width), working population (middle), old dependents (top), and any male/female imbalance — the UAE's male bulge at 25–39 reflects labour migration.",
      reforge:{stem:"What are the main implications for a government of an ageing population?",options:{A:"Higher pension and social care costs and pressure on healthcare, while a shrinking workforce reduces tax revenue",B:"An increase in the birth rate",C:"Rapidly rising unemployment among young people",D:"A need to build many more primary schools"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-16",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"Calculate the dependency ratio for a country with 30 million young dependents, 10 million old dependents and 80 million people of working age.",
      options:{A:"40",B:"125",C:"25",D:"50"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-DEV-16",
      scaffold:"Dependency ratio = (young dependents + old dependents) ÷ working population × 100. Here: (30 + 10) ÷ 80 × 100 = 40 ÷ 80 × 100 = 50. This means 50 dependents for every 100 workers. A high ratio means a heavier burden on the working population through taxation. Show the formula in your working — marks are available for method even if the arithmetic slips.",
      reforge:{stem:"A country's dependency ratio rises from 48 to 67 over thirty years while its birth rate falls. What is the most likely cause?",options:{A:"A sudden rise in the number of children",B:"An increase in the working population",C:"An ageing population — the growth is in old dependents, not young ones",D:"Large-scale immigration of working-age adults"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-FB-01",spec:"GCSE-DEV",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about categories of development.",
      template:"A ___ country has low human development, while an ___ country is experiencing rapid growth based on industrial development. The difference in wealth between countries is called the development ___.",
      blanks:["developing","emerging","gap"],
      bank:["developing","emerging","gap","developed","cycle","ratio"],
      tag:"MC-DEV-03",
      scaffold:"The three Edexcel B categories are developing (low human development), emerging (high/medium human development, rapid industrial growth) and developed (very high human development). The difference between them is the development gap."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-FB-02",spec:"GCSE-DEV",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about the Human Development Index.",
      template:"HDI combines life ___, education level and ___ per capita to give a score between 0 and ___.",
      blanks:["expectancy","GNI","1"],
      bank:["expectancy","GNI","1","100","mortality","literacy"],
      tag:"MC-DEV-01",
      scaffold:"HDI runs from 0 (least developed) to 1 (most developed) and combines a health measure (life expectancy), an education measure (literacy and years of schooling) and a wealth measure (GNI per capita). Because it is composite, it is harder to mislead with than any single indicator."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-FB-03",spec:"GCSE-DEV",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about Rostow's model.",
      template:"Rostow's model has five stages, beginning with ___ society and ending with high mass ___. The third stage, when rapid industrialisation occurs, is called ___-off.",
      blanks:["traditional","consumption","take"],
      bank:["traditional","consumption","take","modern","production","drive"],
      tag:"MC-DEV-06",
      scaffold:"The five stages in order: traditional society, pre-conditions for take-off, take-off, drive to maturity, high mass consumption. Take-off (stage 3) is the pivotal one — countries that cannot raise the capital to reach it, often because of debt repayments, remain stuck."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-FB-04",spec:"GCSE-DEV",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about Frank's dependency theory.",
      template:"In Frank's model, the ___ produces and sells low-value ___ materials to the ___, which processes them into high-value goods.",
      blanks:["periphery","raw","core"],
      bank:["periphery","raw","core","centre","finished","margin"],
      tag:"MC-DEV-07",
      scaffold:"Frank's core–periphery model: the periphery exports cheap raw materials (coffee, cocoa, sugar, minerals); the core adds value by processing them and sells manufactured goods back at high prices. Wealth accumulates in the core, keeping the periphery dependent."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-FB-05",spec:"GCSE-DEV",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about development strategies.",
      template:"___-down strategies are usually large, expensive projects funded by IGOs such as the World Bank, while ___-up strategies are small-scale community projects often run by ___.",
      blanks:["Top","bottom","NGOs"],
      bank:["Top","bottom","NGOs","Bottom","top","TNCs"],
      tag:"MC-DEV-08",
      scaffold:"Top-down: dams, ports, motorways — IGO or government funded, economic focus, high technology. Bottom-up: wells, clinics, farmer training, microfinance — NGO-led, social focus, appropriate technology. Watch the capital letter on the first blank."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-FB-06",spec:"GCSE-DEV",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about the demographic transition model.",
      template:"In stage ___ of the DTM the death rate falls while the birth rate stays high, so population rises rapidly. In stage ___ the death rate exceeds the birth rate and population ___.",
      blanks:["2","5","declines"],
      bank:["2","5","declines","3","4","rises"],
      tag:"MC-DEV-14",
      scaffold:"Stage 2 is the rapid-growth stage: better healthcare, diet and clean water cut the death rate but attitudes to family size have not yet changed. Stage 5 is decline: birth rate falls below death rate, as in Japan and South Korea."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-FB-07",spec:"GCSE-DEV",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about the dependency ratio.",
      template:"Dependency ratio = (young dependents + ___ dependents) ÷ ___ population × ___.",
      blanks:["old","working","100"],
      bank:["old","working","100","total","retired","1000"],
      tag:"MC-DEV-16",
      scaffold:"Learn this formula exactly — it is a guaranteed calculation mark. A high ratio means each worker supports more dependents through taxation. Always show the substitution before the answer."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-FB-08",spec:"GCSE-DEV",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about causes of global inequality.",
      template:"___ is where a country takes political control of another and exploits it economically. Today, rich countries use 'soft power' through trade, aid and TNCs instead — this is called ___-colonialism.",
      blanks:["Colonialism","neo"],
      bank:["Colonialism","neo","Globalisation","post","Dependency","anti"],
      tag:"MC-DEV-04",
      scaffold:"Colonialism = direct (hard power) control: military and political rule. Neo-colonialism = indirect (soft power) control: favourable trade terms, aid conditions, IGO influence, TNC brand dominance. Examiners specifically warn against confusing the two."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-17",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"What is the Brandt Line?",
      options:{A:"The boundary between the world's tectonic plates",B:"A line that divides developed and developing countries on a world map",C:"A line that shows the route taken by Frank's Dependency Model",D:"A line joining every country with a Human Development Index above 0.9"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-DEV-KT-01",
      scaffold:"The Brandt Line runs roughly at 30°N, dipping to include Australia and New Zealand in the 'rich north'. It's a simplification — some countries north of the line (e.g. parts of the former USSR) are less developed than some south of it (e.g. Australia) — which is a common evaluation point in exam answers.",
      reforge:{stem:"A map divides the world roughly along 30°N into a wealthier 'north' and poorer 'south'. What is this line called, and what is its main limitation?",options:{A:"The Brandt Line has no known limitations",B:"The equator — it divides the world exactly into developed and developing halves",C:"The Brandt Line — it oversimplifies development, since some countries north of the line are less developed than some south of it",D:"Rostow's line — it perfectly predicts every country's development stage"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-18",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"A student confuses a 'top-down' and a 'bottom-up' development project. What is the correct distinction?",
      options:{A:"A top-down project is a large-scale scheme run by a government or TNC, where benefits should trickle down to the poor; a bottom-up project is a small scheme run by an NGO or charity that aims to help the poorest families directly",B:"A top-down project is always smaller than a bottom-up project",C:"A bottom-up project is always funded by a national government",D:"They are two names for exactly the same type of scheme"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-DEV-KT-02",
      scaffold:"Top-down = large-scale, government/TNC-led, trickle-down benefits (e.g. a dam project). Bottom-up = small-scale, NGO/charity-led, targets the poorest directly (e.g. a well-building scheme). Exams often ask you to evaluate which is more effective — bottom-up reaches the poorest more reliably but on a smaller scale.",
      reforge:{stem:"An NGO runs a small well-building scheme aimed directly at the poorest families in a village. What type of development project is this?",options:{A:"A government-run national infrastructure scheme",B:"A top-down project",C:"A TNC-led scheme with trickle-down benefits",D:"A bottom-up project"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-19",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"What is the key difference between capitalism and communism as economic systems?",
      options:{A:"Communism is always run by private companies; capitalism is always run by the state",B:"Capitalism and communism are the same system with different names",C:"Capitalism is run by private companies for profit, on the belief that a richer country becomes more developed; communism holds that goods and production should be owned communally, often by the state, so everyone has access to what they need",D:"Capitalism only exists in developing countries"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-DEV-KT-03",
      scaffold:"This links to why some countries develop differently — a capitalist system relies on private investment and market growth to raise living standards, while a communist system aims for equality through state or communal ownership rather than private profit.",
      reforge:{stem:"Under a communist economic system, how are goods and the means of production typically owned?",options:{A:"Entirely by private companies seeking profit",B:"By no one — production is banned under communism",C:"Exclusively by international TNCs",D:"Communally, often by the state, so that everyone has access to what they need"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-20",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"Which of the following correctly defines GDP per capita, and how does it differ from plain GDP?",
      options:{A:"GDP per capita only measures spending by the government, not private companies",B:"GDP per capita is the value of all produce and spending in a country divided by its population, giving an average figure per person; GDP alone is just the total value with no adjustment for population size",C:"GDP per capita and GDP are identical measures",D:"GDP per capita is always higher than total GDP"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-DEV-KT-04",
      scaffold:"Dividing by population is what makes GDP per capita useful for comparing wealth between countries of very different sizes — a huge total GDP (like India's) can still come with a low GDP per capita if the population is large.",
      reforge:{stem:"Country X has the same total GDP as Country Y, but twice the population. How would their GDP per capita compare?",options:{A:"Country X's GDP per capita would be roughly half of Country Y's, since the same wealth is divided among twice as many people",B:"Their GDP per capita would be identical",C:"GDP per capita is unaffected by population size",D:"Country X's GDP per capita would be double Country Y's"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-21",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"What does an indicator of development actually measure?",
      options:{A:"The exact location of the Brandt Line",B:"A way of measuring development that focuses on social, economic or environmental progress — e.g. life expectancy, GDP per capita or pollution levels",C:"The five stages of Rostow's model",D:"Only the total population of a country"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-DEV-KT-05",
      scaffold:"An 'indicator' is the general category — any single measure used as a proxy for development. Social indicators (literacy rate, life expectancy), economic indicators (GDP per capita), and environmental indicators (pollution levels) are all types of indicator; HDI is a composite that combines several of them.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which of these is an environmental indicator of development, as opposed to a social or economic one?",options:{A:"Literacy rate",B:"Life expectancy",C:"Pollution levels",D:"GDP per capita"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-22",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"A student defines infant mortality rate as 'how many babies are born each year.' How should this be corrected?",
      options:{A:"The student's definition is correct as written",B:"Infant mortality rate is a measure of literacy, not health",C:"Infant mortality rate measures deaths of people over 65",D:"Infant mortality rate is the fraction of children under 5 years old who die, compared to the total number of births — it measures deaths, not births"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-DEV-KT-06",
      scaffold:"Infant mortality rate is a widely used social/health indicator — a high rate usually points to poor healthcare access, sanitation and nutrition, so it's commonly paired with life expectancy as evidence of a country's level of development.",
      reforge:{stem:"A country has a high birth rate but a low infant mortality rate has recently been achieved through better healthcare. What does the infant mortality figure specifically measure?",options:{A:"The proportion of children under 5 who die, compared to the total number of births — a measure of child health and healthcare access",B:"The average age of first-time parents",C:"The literacy rate of new mothers",D:"The total number of babies born in a year"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-23",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"What distinguishes economic inequality from social inequality?",
      options:{A:"Economic inequality is the difference in wealth between the richest 10% and poorest 10% of a population; social inequality is the difference in quality of life between the highest- and lowest-income households",B:"Social inequality is always measured using GDP per capita",C:"Economic inequality only applies between countries, never within one",D:"They are identical measures with different names"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-DEV-KT-07",
      scaffold:"Economic inequality is purely about income/wealth distribution. Social inequality is broader — it includes quality-of-life gaps such as access to healthcare, education and services, not just money.",
      reforge:{stem:"A country has low economic inequality (a small gap between richest and poorest 10%) but high social inequality. What does this suggest?",options:{A:"This combination is impossible",B:"Social inequality only measures income, exactly like economic inequality",C:"Economic and social inequality always rise and fall together",D:"Income may be relatively evenly distributed, but quality of life still varies significantly between the highest- and lowest-income households, e.g. in access to healthcare or education"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-24",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"Which correctly defines informal work, and why is it common in lower income countries?",
      options:{A:"A synonym for a top-down development project",B:"Self-employed or temporary work that provides a small wage with limited health and safety regulations — it's common where formal jobs are scarce and there is little regulation of employment",C:"Work carried out exclusively by NGOs",D:"Work that is always highly paid and closely regulated by government inspectors"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-DEV-KT-08",
      scaffold:"Informal work (street vending, casual labour) is common in squatter settlements and rapidly urbanising cities — it provides income where formal employment is limited, but offers no job security, minimum wage or safety protection.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why might informal work be common in a rapidly urbanising city with limited formal employment?",options:{A:"Informal work guarantees a higher income than formal employment",B:"Informal work is always better regulated than formal work",C:"It offers a source of income (e.g. street vending, casual labour) where formal jobs are scarce, even though it lacks job security, a minimum wage or safety regulation",D:"Informal work only exists in wealthy developed countries"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-25",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"What is a squatter settlement?",
      options:{A:"A large-scale government housing scheme built to a high safety standard",B:"A cluster of temporary housing which migrants or low-income families live in, often built on unfavourable land",C:"An area where only wealthy families live",D:"A rural farming settlement with no urban connection"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-DEV-KT-09",
      scaffold:"Squatter settlements form where rural-urban migrants can't afford formal housing — they're often built on steep slopes, floodplains or waste ground precisely because that land is unwanted and unregulated, which increases residents' exposure to hazards.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why are squatter settlements often built on steep slopes, floodplains or other unfavourable land?",options:{A:"Squatter settlements are only found in rural areas, never in cities",B:"Governments deliberately build squatter settlements as planned developments",C:"Migrants and low-income families cannot afford formal housing, and this unwanted, unregulated land is the only space available to them",D:"This land is always the safest and most desirable in a city"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-26",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"A student says 'the poverty line is the same figure in every country.' Why is this wrong?",
      options:{A:"The poverty line is the minimum income a person needs to live adequately, and what counts as 'adequate' — cost of food, housing, services — varies between countries, so the line is set differently in each context",B:"The poverty line is a fixed international figure that never changes",C:"The poverty line measures literacy, not income",D:"The poverty line only applies to developed countries"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-DEV-KT-10",
      scaffold:"Poverty lines can be set nationally (relative to a country's own cost of living) or internationally (e.g. the World Bank's extreme poverty line), which is why comparing poverty rates between countries needs care over which measure is being used.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why can't a single global poverty line be applied fairly to every country?",options:{A:"Poverty is impossible to measure in any country",B:"Every country has exactly the same cost of living",C:"Poverty lines are only ever set by international organisations, never nationally",D:"The cost of living, including food, housing and services, varies between countries, so what counts as 'enough income to live adequately' differs from place to place"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-27",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"What is governance, and why does it matter for development?",
      options:{A:"The physical shape and relief of a country's land",B:"Another term for GDP per capita",C:"A measure of a country's total rainfall",D:"The management of a place or group of people — weak or corrupt governance can block investment and misdirect resources away from development"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-DEV-KT-11",
      scaffold:"Governance covers how decisions are made and enforced — good governance (transparent institutions, low corruption) tends to attract investment and direct aid effectively, while poor governance (see: corruption) can trap a country in low development despite aid or resources.",
      reforge:{stem:"A country has valuable natural resources and receives significant international aid, yet remains poorly developed. Which factor is most likely to explain this?",options:{A:"The country must have no indicators of development at all",B:"Natural resources always guarantee high development",C:"Weak or corrupt governance, which can misdirect resources and aid away from development projects",D:"International aid always guarantees improved governance"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-28",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"How does corruption act as a barrier to development?",
      options:{A:"Corruption involves governments being dishonest or stealing money for financial gain or to stay in power, which diverts funds away from healthcare, education and infrastructure",B:"Corruption always increases a country's GDP per capita",C:"Corruption is a physical, not a political, cause of inequality",D:"Corruption only affects developed countries"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-DEV-KT-12",
      scaffold:"Corruption sits in the political/economic category of causes of inequality alongside trade barriers and debt — money that should fund public services (schools, hospitals, roads) is instead diverted for private or political gain, slowing development even where a country has decent resources.",
      reforge:{stem:"How does corruption most directly slow a country's development, even where resources or aid are available?",options:{A:"Corruption always increases a country's GDP per capita immediately",B:"Funds intended for healthcare, education or infrastructure are diverted for private or political gain instead of reaching public services",C:"Corruption only affects a country's topography",D:"Corruption has no measurable effect on development"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-29",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"What is topography, and how can it act as a barrier to development?",
      options:{A:"A synonym for climate change",B:"The shape and relief of the land (e.g. mountainous, low-lying, flat) — difficult topography such as steep mountains can make building infrastructure and transport links expensive and slow",C:"A measure of a country's literacy rate",D:"The formal name for a country's government system"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-DEV-KT-13",
      scaffold:"Topography is a physical/environmental cause of inequality, alongside climate and being landlocked. Mountainous terrain (e.g. much of Nepal or Bolivia) raises the cost of roads, railways and electricity grids, which can isolate communities from trade and services.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why might a mountainous, landlocked country find it harder to attract TNC investment than a coastal country with flat terrain?",options:{A:"Topography has no effect on a country's ability to trade or attract investment",B:"Mountainous countries always have better transport links than coastal ones",C:"Difficult topography and lack of sea access raise the cost of building infrastructure and transporting goods to trade, making investment less attractive",D:"TNCs only ever invest in landlocked countries"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-30",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"A student defines a 'Non-Governmental Organisation (NGO)' as 'a private company that makes money for shareholders.' What's wrong with this definition?",
      options:{A:"An NGO operates separately from government but does not aim to make a profit — examples include charities and universities, unlike a private company which exists to generate profit",B:"NGOs are always run directly by national governments",C:"The definition is correct — NGOs are simply another type of private company",D:"NGOs only operate in developed countries"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-DEV-KT-14",
      scaffold:"The defining features of an NGO are independence from government and a non-profit purpose — this is why NGOs are typically behind bottom-up development projects, which prioritise reaching the poorest rather than generating a financial return.",
      reforge:{stem:"An organisation operating in a country has no government affiliation and does not aim to make a profit. What type of organisation is this most likely to be?",options:{A:"A private, profit-driven company",B:"A national government department",C:"A TNC seeking to maximise shareholder profit",D:"An NGO (Non-Governmental Organisation), such as a charity"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-31",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"Why is using a single development indicator risky when comparing countries?",
      options:{A:"One indicator can hide other dimensions of development, so a country may score well economically but poorly socially or environmentally",B:"All indicators measure exactly the same thing",C:"Indicators are only used for weather forecasting",D:"GDP per capita includes literacy, life expectancy and inequality automatically"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-DEV-15",
      scaffold:"Development is multi-dimensional. GDP per capita measures average income, but not distribution, health, education, freedom or sustainability. Strong answers compare indicators and explain what each includes and leaves out.",
      reforge:{stem:"Country A has high GDP per capita but low literacy and high inequality. What does this show?",options:{A:"It must be fully developed",B:"Literacy is not linked to development",C:"Economic data alone can hide social weaknesses, so multiple indicators are needed",D:"HDI would be useless because GDP is high"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-32",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"A student says a youthful population is always a development problem. What is the best evaluation?",
      options:{A:"It always causes immediate economic collapse",B:"It only matters in developed countries",C:"It can create pressure on schools and healthcare, but with education and jobs it can become a demographic dividend",D:"It means death rates must be rising"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-DEV-16",
      scaffold:"A youthful population raises dependency and service pressure in the short term. But if young people receive education, healthcare and employment, they can become a large productive workforce — a demographic dividend. Evaluation depends on investment and job creation.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which condition is most important if a youthful population is to support development?",options:{A:"No schooling so wages stay low",B:"Investment in education, health and employment opportunities",C:"A ban on migration to cities",D:"Keeping all work informal"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-33",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"How can debt relief help development in a low-income country?",
      options:{A:"It always removes the need for tax revenue",B:"It immediately makes every household rich",C:"It prevents trade from happening",D:"It can free government money previously used for debt repayments so it can be spent on health, education or infrastructure"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-DEV-17",
      scaffold:"Debt relief can reduce the burden of repayments, allowing public spending on services and infrastructure. But it is not automatically successful: benefits depend on governance, corruption levels, conditions attached and whether money reaches development priorities.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why might debt relief fail to reduce poverty even if repayments fall?",options:{A:"Because debt relief bans education spending",B:"Because all countries have identical governance",C:"Because infrastructure never affects poverty",D:"Because freed money may be poorly governed, lost to corruption or not targeted at services for the poorest"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEV-34",spec:"GCSE-DEV",stem:"Which statement best distinguishes aid from foreign direct investment (FDI)?",
      options:{A:"Aid and FDI are exactly the same",B:"FDI is always charity with no profit motive",C:"Aid can only be given by TNCs",D:"Aid is support such as money, goods or expertise, often from governments or NGOs; FDI is investment by a company or investor into another country's economy"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-DEV-18",
      scaffold:"Aid is intended to support development or emergency relief, though it may have conditions. FDI is investment into production, services or assets in another country, usually with a profit motive. Both can support development, but their aims and trade-offs differ.",
      reforge:{stem:"A car company builds a factory in an emerging country to produce vehicles for profit. What is this?",options:{A:"Foreign direct investment",B:"Emergency aid",C:"Debt relief",D:"Fairtrade certification"},correct:"A"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-GEO-INDIA"] = {
  label: "India — Emerging Country Case Study",
  color: "#0f766e",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-01",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"Which statement best describes India's global economic significance?",
      options:{A:"It has the smallest economy of the BRICS countries and is shrinking",B:"It is the world's largest economy and has been since 1990",C:"It is the world's fifth largest economy, having overtaken the UK in 2022, and is predicted to be second largest by 2050",D:"Its economy is entirely based on agriculture"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-IND-01",
      scaffold:"Key India stats worth memorising: seventh largest country by area; second largest population (1.3+ billion); fifth largest economy (overtook the UK, Sept 2022); GDP around US$3.18 trillion (2021) with GDP per capita only about US$2,256; roughly 500% GDP growth since 1990; annual growth around 8.7%. The gap between huge total GDP and low GDP per capita is the single most useful contrast in this case study.",
      reforge:{stem:"India has the world's fifth largest total GDP but a GDP per capita of only around US$2,256. What does this contrast show?",options:{A:"Its GDP figure must have been calculated incorrectly",B:"India is a developed country by every measure",C:"GDP per capita is always higher than GDP",D:"Its enormous population divides the national wealth thinly, so a large economy does not mean a wealthy population"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-02",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"How has the structure of India's economy changed since 1990?",
      options:{A:"Agriculture now contributes over 75% of GDP",B:"It has shifted from being dominated by agriculture to being dominated by services, though agriculture is still the largest employer",C:"Manufacturing has disappeared entirely",D:"It has shifted from services to agriculture"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-IND-02",
      scaffold:"The classic exam trap here is the split between GDP contribution and employment. Services now dominate GDP, but agriculture remains the largest single employer — falling from around 75% of workers to just over 50% by 2010 and about 43% by 2019. So a huge share of the workforce is still in the low-productivity sector, which is exactly why average incomes lag behind headline GDP growth.",
      reforge:{stem:"Agriculture employs around 43% of India's workers but contributes a far smaller share of GDP. What does this imply?",options:{A:"Agricultural productivity and incomes are low relative to services and manufacturing, contributing to rural poverty",B:"Agriculture is growing faster than any other sector",C:"India imports no food",D:"Agricultural workers are the highest paid in India"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-03",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"Why are TNCs attracted to invest in India?",
      options:{A:"India bans foreign investment in manufacturing",B:"A large, skilled, low-cost and largely English-speaking workforce, combined with special economic zones offering reduced taxes",C:"India has the highest labour costs in Asia",D:"India has a very small domestic market"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-IND-03",
      scaffold:"India's pull factors: skilled, well-educated, low-cost, largely English-speaking labour (crucial for call centres and IT outsourcing); economic liberalisation since 1991 (government took less control over what industries produce); reduced import tariffs; special economic zones with tax reductions. Evidence: FDI inflows of $45 billion in 2021, Google's $1bn investment in Bharti Airtel (2022), Facebook's $5.7bn in Reliance Jio (2020).",
      reforge:{stem:"Which government action since 1991 has most directly encouraged foreign investment in India?",options:{A:"Raising taxes on all foreign companies",B:"Banning the export of manufactured goods",C:"Nationalising all foreign-owned companies",D:"Economic liberalisation — reducing state control over production, cutting import tariffs and creating special economic zones with lower taxes"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-04",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"A student writes: 'India receives aid, so it must be a poor developing country.' Why is this misleading?",
      options:{A:"India both receives and gives aid — it is now a donor as well as a recipient, reflecting its emerging status",B:"Aid has no connection to a country's level of development",C:"Only developed countries can receive aid",D:"India has never received any aid"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-IND-04",
      scaffold:"India received US$4,240 million of Official Development Assistance in 2017 (over 50% from Japan), targeted at its poorest states such as Madhya Pradesh — but it is simultaneously an aid donor to other countries. It also changed policy in 2003 to refuse tied aid except from a few partners including the UK, USA and Germany. Being both recipient and donor is a hallmark of an emerging country.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why did India decide in 2003 to stop accepting most tied aid?",options:{A:"India no longer needed any external funding at all",B:"Tied aid is illegal under international law",C:"Tied aid requires the recipient to spend the money on goods and services from the donor country, limiting its value and independence",D:"Tied aid must be repaid with very high interest"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-05",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"Which pair of figures best illustrates the regional inequality between India's urban core and rural periphery?",
      options:{A:"Delhi and Bihar both have a literacy rate of 86%",B:"Delhi has a higher fertility rate than Bihar",C:"GDP per capita of US$7,500 in Delhi compared with US$630 in Bihar",D:"Bihar has a higher life expectancy than Delhi"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-IND-05",
      scaffold:"Delhi (urban core) vs Bihar (rural periphery): life expectancy 72.9 vs 68.7 years; literacy 86.21% vs 69.83%; GDP per capita US$7,500 vs US$630; death rate 4.1 vs 5.4 per 1,000; fertility 1.6 vs 3.0. Learn one figure from each row — a 4-mark 'explain two socio-economic differences between regions' question needs both the difference AND the reason.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why has Bihar remained poorer than Delhi?",options:{A:"Bihar has no agricultural land at all",B:"Recurring floods hamper development, poor infrastructure deters investment, and out-migration of working-age people has drained its labour force",C:"Bihar refuses to trade with the rest of India",D:"Bihar has a much larger population than the rest of India combined"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-06",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"Roughly what proportion of India's population lived in urban areas in 2021, and how fast is this changing?",
      options:{A:"About 34% (475 million people), increasing by around 2% a year",B:"About 80%, and falling",C:"About 60%, and falling rapidly",D:"About 5%, and static"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-IND-06",
      scaffold:"34% urban in 2021 = 475 million people, growing about 2% per year, with roughly 20 million people migrating from rural to urban areas annually. The significance: India is still majority rural, so it has decades of urbanisation ahead — which is why housing, water supply and air quality pressures in cities are projected to worsen, not ease.",
      reforge:{stem:"About 20 million Indians migrate from rural to urban areas each year. Which combination of push and pull factors best explains this?",options:{A:"Push: government orders. Pull: compulsory rural resettlement",B:"Push: overcrowded cities. Pull: empty countryside",C:"Push: high urban wages. Pull: subsistence farming opportunities",D:"Push: flooding, poor rural infrastructure and low farm incomes. Pull: urban employment, higher wages and better services"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-07",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"How has India's demographic profile changed as its economy has grown?",
      options:{A:"Life expectancy has fallen while fertility has risen",B:"There has been no measurable demographic change since 1960",C:"Life expectancy rose from 45 years in 1960 to 70 in 2021, while death rates, infant mortality and fertility rates have all fallen",D:"Birth and death rates have both risen sharply"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-IND-07",
      scaffold:"Economic development → demographic change. Life expectancy 45 (1960) → 70 (2021), driven by better diets, healthcare availability and water quality. Fertility falling because women are more educated, more women work, contraception is more available, and family planning education has spread. This is textbook DTM stage 3 — link the two topics in your answers.",
      reforge:{stem:"India's population pyramid has changed shape between 1960 and 2022. Which change would you expect to see?",options:{A:"A widening base as birth rates rise",B:"No change in shape, only in total size",C:"A complete inversion, with more over-80s than under-10s",D:"A narrowing base and a widening middle and top as birth rates fall and life expectancy rises"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-08",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"Which is a clear SOCIAL benefit of India's economic development?",
      options:{A:"An increase in car ownership in New Delhi",B:"Improved gender equality — more women have access to education, contraception and career opportunities",C:"A rise in carbon dioxide emissions",D:"An increase in the number of landfill sites"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-IND-08",
      scaffold:"Sort impacts carefully — exam questions specify social, economic or environmental. Social positives: improved gender equality (education, literacy, family planning, career opportunities — though female literacy still trails male), better health across all age groups, rising average incomes. B, C and D are all environmental impacts, and negative ones.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which is a social COST of India's rapid economic development?",options:{A:"Growth of illegal settlements around cities with poor housing, no clean water supply and increased spread of disease",B:"Improved access to family planning",C:"Increased literacy rates among women",D:"Rising average incomes"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-09",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"Why does rural-to-urban migration create problems for India's rural areas?",
      options:{A:"It is mainly younger, working-age people who leave, so rural areas are left with an ageing, economically inactive population and less investment",B:"It raises rural wages above urban wages",C:"It causes rural areas to become overcrowded",D:"It increases the number of schools needed in villages"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-IND-09",
      scaffold:"This is a favourite 4-mark 'impacts on different age groups' question. Chain it: rural–urban migration (1) → an ageing population left in rural areas with no-one to care for them and few economically active workers (1). Add the other side: children in rural areas are more likely to work rather than attend school, and rural investment is far lower than urban.",
      reforge:{stem:"A 4-mark question asks for two negative impacts of rapid development on different age groups. Which answer is structured correctly?",options:{A:"Air pollution is a problem in New Delhi because there are lots of cars and factories and it is very bad.",B:"Development is bad for everyone in India.",C:"Air pollution (1) causes breathing problems among the elderly and young children (1); a shortage of urban jobs (1) leaves young rural–urban migrants unemployed (1)",D:"Old people and young people are both affected by development in different ways."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-10",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"What are the main causes of India's severe urban air pollution?",
      options:{A:"Almost entirely from imported goods",B:"Only volcanic activity",C:"Solely the burning of stubble by farmers",D:"Vehicle emissions, fossil-fuel power stations, factories, stubble burning, construction dust and biomass cooking fuel"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-IND-10",
      scaffold:"Multi-causal — a good answer names several sources. New Delhi alone has over 10 million vehicles and 3,000–4,000 factories; over 100 million people nationally rely on biomass for cooking. Scale evidence: air pollution runs at 4× WHO guidelines, and 21 of the world's 30 most polluted cities were in India in 2019.",
      reforge:{stem:"What are the human effects of India's air pollution?",options:{A:"There are no measurable health effects",B:"Life expectancy is around 5 years lower on average, with over 1.5 million deaths in 2019 and rising rates of asthma, lung disease and cardiac problems",C:"It reduces crop yields but has no effect on human health",D:"It only affects people over 65"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-11",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"Approximately what proportion of India's sewage is treated, and what is the consequence?",
      options:{A:"About 95% is treated, so water pollution is minimal",B:"All sewage is treated but rivers are polluted by volcanic ash",C:"Only about 30% is treated, contributing to around 70% of surface water being unfit for human consumption",D:"None is treated, but this has no effect on water quality"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-IND-11",
      scaffold:"Water pollution causes: only 30% of sewage treated; industrial waste water carrying mercury, lead and arsenic; heavy pesticide use including DDT, banned elsewhere. Consequences: the Indus and Ganges are among the ten most polluted rivers in the world, 33% of remaining rivers moderately or severely polluted, ~70% of surface water unfit to drink. Effects: aquatic life damaged, liver and kidney disease, gastric cancers, birth defects, lower crop yields.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why is India's continued use of pesticides such as DDT an environmental concern?",options:{A:"DDT is too expensive for farmers to use",B:"DDT only affects the air, not water",C:"DDT improves water quality",D:"DDT is banned in many countries because it persists in the environment and accumulates in water, soil and food chains"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-12",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"What is the main environmental consequence of India sending around 90% of its waste to landfill?",
      options:{A:"A reduction in greenhouse gas emissions",B:"Increased methane emissions, leaching of toxins into ground and surface water, and landfill fires such as Ghazipur in March 2022",C:"A fall in the total volume of waste produced",D:"Improved groundwater quality"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-IND-12",
      scaffold:"India has over 3,000 landfills and generates around 2.01 billion tonnes of waste annually, ~90% landfilled. Ghazipur in Delhi is 65 m high and caught fire in March 2022, worsening air pollution — a useful example because it links land pollution to air pollution. India is also the third largest CO₂ emitter and fourth largest methane emitter, with over 60% of methane from agriculture.",
      reforge:{stem:"India is the world's fourth largest emitter of methane, with over 60% coming from agriculture. Why does this create a development dilemma?",options:{A:"Reducing emissions means constraining agriculture and energy use that millions depend on, so international pressure to cut emissions may hinder future development",B:"Methane emissions have no climate effect",C:"India could simply stop all farming without consequence",D:"Only developed countries are asked to reduce emissions"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-13",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"Which evidence best demonstrates India's growing geopolitical influence?",
      options:{A:"Its withdrawal from all international organisations",B:"Its refusal to trade with the EU or USA",C:"Membership of BRICS, the G20, the UN Security Council and the World Bank, and responsibility for around 15% of global growth according to the IMF",D:"Its declining population"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-IND-13",
      scaffold:"Geopolitical influence: growing role in ASEAN; membership of BRICS, G20, UN Security Council, World Bank, IMF, WTO, Asian Development Bank; the IMF attributes about 15% of global growth to India. Trade relationships: the EU is India's third largest trading partner (exports €46.2bn, imports €41.9bn — roughly balanced); with the USA India exports more (US$49.7bn) than it imports (US$26.6bn).",
      reforge:{stem:"How does the 1947 partition of India and Pakistan still affect India's development today?",options:{A:"It has had no effect since 1947",B:"It removed all border disputes in the region",C:"It made India a developed country",D:"It caused around one million deaths and displaced 15 million people, and continuing tensions with Pakistan affect regional stability and trade relations"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-14",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"Which is a COST to India of its changing international relations?",
      options:{A:"Low tax rates offered to attract TNCs reduce government revenue available for investment in public services",B:"India becoming able to provide aid to other countries",C:"Increased trade with countries around the world",D:"Wages rising and improving average quality of life"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-IND-14",
      scaffold:"Costs: pressure to cut greenhouse gas emissions may constrain development; low taxes for TNCs reduce revenue for services; increased reliance on TNCs; weak regulation causes environmental damage; rising military spending creates regional tension. Benefits: growth funds healthcare, education and infrastructure; wider trade; rising wages; India as aid donor; improved national security. B, C and D are all benefits.",
      reforge:{stem:"India's increased defence spending is listed as both a cost and a benefit. Why?",options:{A:"Because it has no effect either way",B:"It improves national security, but can also heighten tensions with neighbours such as Pakistan and China and diverts money from public services",C:"Because defence spending is not really a form of spending",D:"Because defence spending only affects developed countries"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-15",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"India is both a recipient and a source of foreign direct investment. Which fact demonstrates the outward flow?",
      options:{A:"Indian firms invested $17.24 billion abroad, and India is the second largest FDI contributor in the UK — including TVS Motors' £100 million in Norton Motorcycles",B:"Singapore provides 27% of investment into India",C:"India received $45 billion of FDI inflows in 2021",D:"Google invested US$1 billion in Bharti Airtel"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-IND-15",
      scaffold:"Inflows (into India): $45bn in 2021, mainly from Singapore 27%, USA 18%, Mauritius 16%, Netherlands 8%, targeting automobiles, electrical machinery and textiles. Outflows (from India): $17.24bn invested abroad; second largest FDI contributor in the UK; Mastek invested £79m creating 1,600 UK jobs; TVS Motors £100m in Norton. B, C and D are all inflows — read the question direction carefully.",
      reforge:{stem:"What does India's position as a major outward investor suggest about Frank's dependency theory?",options:{A:"It confirms the theory exactly as Frank described it",B:"It shows India is still purely a raw material exporter",C:"It proves core countries no longer exist",D:"It challenges the theory — a former periphery country is now investing capital into core economies such as the UK"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-16",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"How has improved education contributed to India's economic development?",
      options:{A:"Only private education exists in India",B:"Education has been reduced to lower government spending",C:"Free primary education since 2009 and the 2020 National Education Policy raise skills and employability, feeding the positive multiplier effect",D:"Education has no connection to economic development"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-IND-16",
      scaffold:"Primary education became free in 2009; the 2020 National Education Policy aimed to improve equality and overall standards. Literacy is around 77%. The chain to learn: education → skills → employability → higher wages → more consumer spending and tax revenue → more investment in services and infrastructure → multiplier effect. It also links to falling fertility, since educated women have fewer children.",
      reforge:{stem:"Improving female education in India affects both the economy and demography. How?",options:{A:"It raises fertility rates and lowers incomes",B:"It raises employability and incomes while also lowering fertility rates, as educated women tend to have smaller families and marry later",C:"It affects the economy but has no demographic effect",D:"It reduces the workforce because women stop working"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-FB-01",spec:"GCSE-IND",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about India's location and significance.",
      template:"India is located in Asia between the Arabian Sea and the Bay of ___. It is the ___ largest country in the world by area and has the ___ largest population.",
      blanks:["Bengal","seventh","second"],
      bank:["Bengal","seventh","second","Oman","first","third"],
      tag:"MC-IND-01",
      scaffold:"Site and situation: a peninsula with water on three sides, north of the equator, bordering six countries (Bangladesh, Nepal, Myanmar, Pakistan, China, Bhutan). Seventh largest by area, second largest by population. Its position between the Middle East and South East Asia gives access to both customers and labour."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-FB-02",spec:"GCSE-IND",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about India's economy.",
      template:"In 2021 India's GDP was about US$3.18 ___, but GDP per ___ was only about US$2,256. Since 1990 GDP has grown by around ___%.",
      blanks:["trillion","capita","500"],
      bank:["trillion","capita","500","billion","head","50"],
      tag:"MC-IND-01",
      scaffold:"The tension in this case study: enormous total output, modest income per person, because 1.3 billion people share it. Annual growth is around 8.7%, and India overtook the UK to become the fifth largest economy in September 2022."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-FB-03",spec:"GCSE-IND",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about India's economic sectors.",
      template:"India's GDP is now dominated by the ___ sector, but ___ remains the largest employer, falling from about 75% of workers to around ___% by 2019.",
      blanks:["service","agriculture","43"],
      bank:["service","agriculture","43","primary","manufacturing","75"],
      tag:"MC-IND-02",
      scaffold:"Separate GDP contribution from employment — this is where marks are lost. Services dominate output; agriculture still employs the largest share of people (75% → just over 50% by 2010 → about 43% by 2019). Low agricultural productivity is a key cause of rural poverty in states like Bihar."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-FB-04",spec:"GCSE-IND",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about regional inequality in India.",
      template:"Delhi, the urban core, has a GDP per capita of about US$7,500 and literacy of 86.21%, while ___, in the rural periphery, has GDP per capita of just US$___ and a much higher ___ rate of 3.0.",
      blanks:["Bihar","630","fertility"],
      bank:["Bihar","630","fertility","Mumbai","6300","death"],
      tag:"MC-IND-05",
      scaffold:"Delhi vs Bihar is the core–periphery contrast you must be able to quote. Life expectancy 72.9 vs 68.7; literacy 86.21% vs 69.83%; GDP per capita US$7,500 vs US$630; death rate 4.1 vs 5.4; fertility 1.6 vs 3.0. Bihar's recurring floods, poor infrastructure and out-migration explain the gap."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-FB-05",spec:"GCSE-IND",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about urbanisation in India.",
      template:"In 2021 about ___% of India's population lived in urban areas — around 475 million people. Urbanisation is rising by about ___% a year, with roughly ___ million people migrating from rural to urban areas annually.",
      blanks:["34","2","20"],
      bank:["34","2","20","64","12","5"],
      tag:"MC-IND-06",
      scaffold:"India is still majority rural at 34% urban, which is why its urbanisation pressures are projected to intensify for decades. 475 million urban residents, growing ~2% per year, fed by roughly 20 million rural–urban migrants annually."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-FB-06",spec:"GCSE-IND",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about demographic change in India.",
      template:"Life expectancy in India rose from ___ years in 1960 to ___ years in 2021, while ___ rates are falling as more women are educated and contraception is more available.",
      blanks:["45","70","fertility"],
      bank:["45","70","fertility","55","80","death"],
      tag:"MC-IND-07",
      scaffold:"Economic development drives demographic change: better diets, healthcare and water quality raised life expectancy from 45 to 70; education, female employment, contraception and family planning are lowering fertility. This places India in stage 3 of the DTM."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-FB-07",spec:"GCSE-IND",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about pollution in India.",
      template:"Only about ___% of India's sewage is treated, leaving roughly ___% of surface water unfit for human consumption. Air pollution is around ___ times the WHO recommended guidelines.",
      blanks:["30","70","4"],
      bank:["30","70","4","90","15","2"],
      tag:"MC-IND-11",
      scaffold:"Water: 30% of sewage treated, ~70% of surface water unfit to drink, the Indus and Ganges among the world's ten most polluted rivers. Air: 4× WHO guidelines, 21 of the world's 30 most polluted cities in 2019, over 1.5 million deaths in 2019, life expectancy about 5 years lower on average."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-FB-08",spec:"GCSE-IND",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about India's international position.",
      template:"India is a member of ___, a group that also includes Brazil, Russia, China and South Africa. According to the ___, India is responsible for about 15% of global growth. India is now both a recipient and a ___ of aid.",
      blanks:["BRICS","IMF","donor"],
      bank:["BRICS","IMF","donor","ASEAN","WTO","borrower"],
      tag:"MC-IND-13",
      scaffold:"India's memberships: UN (and Security Council), IMF, World Bank, WTO, Asian Development Bank, G20, BRICS, with growing influence in ASEAN. Being simultaneously an aid recipient (US$4,240m ODA in 2017) and an aid donor is a defining feature of an emerging country."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-17",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"Why has India's growth in IT and business services been important for economic development?",
      options:{A:"It has created skilled jobs, attracted investment and increased export earnings in services",B:"It has made agriculture disappear completely",C:"It proves manufacturing is no longer part of India's economy",D:"It only benefits foreign tourists"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-IND-17",
      scaffold:"India's service sector, including IT and business process outsourcing, has generated skilled employment, export income and global links. It also supports multiplier effects, although benefits are uneven and many workers remain in lower-paid informal sectors.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why is Bangalore often used as evidence of India's changing economy?",options:{A:"It is associated with high-tech and IT service growth, showing a shift toward higher-value services",B:"It proves India has no rural poverty",C:"It is a subsistence farming region only",D:"It shows that FDI has stopped entering India"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-18",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"A student says India's economic growth has benefited everyone equally. Which evidence challenges this?",
      options:{A:"All states have identical income and infrastructure",B:"Strong regional inequality remains between richer urban states and poorer rural areas",C:"No one has moved to cities",D:"India has no informal employment"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-IND-18",
      scaffold:"Emerging-country growth is uneven. India's major cities and some states have gained strongly from services, industry and FDI, while many rural areas still face poorer access to sanitation, healthcare, education and secure employment.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why should an answer about India's development include regional inequality?",options:{A:"Because every region has exactly the same development level",B:"Because inequality only exists between countries, never within them",C:"Because national averages can hide large differences between urban cores and rural peripheries",D:"Because India's development is entirely environmental"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-19",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"How can TNC investment create both benefits and costs for India?",
      options:{A:"It only creates costs and never jobs",B:"It only creates benefits and has no trade-offs",C:"It can create jobs and technology transfer, but profits may be repatriated and environmental pressures may increase",D:"It is the same as debt relief"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-IND-19",
      scaffold:"TNCs can bring FDI, jobs, skills, infrastructure and tax revenue. Evaluation matters: some profits leave the country, wages may be low, working conditions can be poor, and factories or offices can increase pressure on water, energy and transport.",
      reforge:{stem:"A foreign company opens a factory in India. Which is the most balanced judgement?",options:{A:"It is automatically harmful",B:"It is automatically perfect development",C:"It has no effect on local workers",D:"It may support growth through jobs and investment, but the net benefit depends on wages, working conditions, tax paid, environmental impact and whether profits stay in India"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-IND-20",spec:"GCSE-IND",stem:"Why is rapid urban growth in India linked to both opportunity and challenge?",
      options:{A:"Cities only create problems and never jobs",B:"Urban growth means rural areas instantly become richer",C:"Urban growth has no connection to infrastructure",D:"Cities offer jobs and services, but rapid growth can increase pressure on housing, transport, sanitation and air quality"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-IND-20",
      scaffold:"Urban growth is a core feature of India's development. It can concentrate investment, education and employment, but if infrastructure lags behind population growth it creates congestion, pollution, informal housing and pressure on services.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which answer best evaluates rural-to-urban migration in India?",options:{A:"It has no impact on either cities or rural areas",B:"It can improve access to work and services for migrants, but also increase pressure on urban infrastructure and leave some rural areas with labour shortages",C:"It only happens because cities force people to leave",D:"It removes all inequality immediately"},correct:"B"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-GEO-BIOSPHERE"] = {
  label: "People and the Biosphere",
  color: "#0f766e",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"GCSE-BIO-01",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"What is the main factor determining the global distribution of biomes?",
      options:{A:"The distance a country is from the equator has no effect on its biome",B:"Biome type is determined only by soil pH",C:"Climate — temperature and precipitation levels shape the length of the growing season and therefore plant and animal biodiversity",D:"Government policy decides which biome forms in a country"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-BIO-01",
      scaffold:"Climate (temperature and precipitation) is the primary control on biome distribution: it determines the growing season length, which drives plant growth, which in turn determines biodiversity — the hotter and wetter a place is, the more plant species it supports, providing more food and habitats and so more animal species too. Local factors (altitude, soil/rock type, drainage, continentality, ocean currents) then create variation within that broad climatic pattern.",
      reforge:{stem:"Two locations are at a similar latitude but one is far inland (continental) and one is on the coast. Why might their biomes differ?",options:{A:"Inland and coastal areas always share an identical biome",B:"Ocean currents only affect biomes at the equator",C:"Latitude is the only factor that ever affects climate",D:"Continentality means inland areas heat up and cool down faster, producing a more extreme temperature range than the moderated coastal climate"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-BIO-02",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"A tropical rainforest and a hot desert are both located close to the equator. Why is their biodiversity so different?",
      options:{A:"Deserts always have higher biodiversity than rainforests",B:"The rainforest's high year-round rainfall and warmth support far greater plant growth than the desert's low precipitation, providing more habitats and food for animals",C:"Biodiversity is unrelated to precipitation",D:"Latitude alone determines biodiversity regardless of rainfall"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-BIO-02",
      scaffold:"Tropical rainforest: over 2000mm rainfall annually, hot and wet year-round, no dormant season — supports roughly 50% of the world's plant and animal species. Hot desert: below 250mm rainfall, extreme temperature swings — low biodiversity (cacti, yucca, scorpions, camels). Both sit at similar latitudes, proving precipitation, not latitude alone, is doing the work here.",
      reforge:{stem:"The taiga and temperate forest are both found in the northern hemisphere, but the taiga has lower biodiversity. What is the main reason?",options:{A:"The taiga's short growing season (2–3 months) and harsh cold climate limit plant growth to mainly coniferous trees, compared with the temperate forest's 6–8 month growing season",B:"Biodiversity is identical in all forest biomes",C:"The taiga receives more rainfall than the temperate forest",D:"The taiga is located closer to the equator"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-BIO-03",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"Which local factor explains why biome type can change dramatically over a short horizontal distance in a mountainous region?",
      options:{A:"Latitude, since altitude and latitude are the same thing",B:"Continentality, which only applies to low-lying areas",C:"Altitude — temperature decreases with height, so vegetation zones change from forest at the base to tundra-like conditions near the summit",D:"Ocean currents, since mountains are never affected by the sea"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-BIO-03",
      scaffold:"Local factors that modify biome distribution within the broader climatic pattern: altitude (temperature falls as height increases, compressing multiple biome-like zones onto one mountainside), rock/soil type (pH and mineral content affect which plants grow), drainage (soil moisture affects plant type), continentality, and ocean currents (warm/cold currents heat or cool nearby land).",
      reforge:{stem:"A cold ocean current flows along a coastline that would otherwise be warm enough for tropical vegetation. What effect is this local factor likely to have?",options:{A:"It would increase biodiversity regardless of temperature",B:"It cools the adjacent land, potentially creating a cooler, drier biome than the latitude alone would predict",C:"Ocean currents have no measurable effect on nearby land climate",D:"It would make the coastline hotter than inland areas at the same latitude"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-BIO-04",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"In a tropical rainforest, vegetation is cleared. Explain the immediate impact on soil using the concept of interdependence.",
      options:{A:"With no leaf litter or roots being added, nutrient supply to the soil is disrupted, and the exposed soil is also more vulnerable to erosion and leaching",B:"Soil nutrients increase because there is no longer any competition from plant roots",C:"Removing vegetation has no effect on soil since they are separate, unconnected systems",D:"Soil quality automatically improves once vegetation is removed"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-BIO-04",
      scaffold:"Interdependence means biotic and abiotic components of a biome are interlinked, so a change in one affects the others. Soil (abiotic) depends on dead organic matter (biotic) for nutrients via decomposition. Removing vegetation cuts off that nutrient supply, exposes soil directly to sun and rain (increasing erosion), and reduces the biological weathering that builds new soil in the first place.",
      reforge:{stem:"Following the same clearance, what is the most likely knock-on effect on local rainfall patterns?",options:{A:"Soil erosion causes rainfall to increase",B:"Rainfall would increase because there is less vegetation to intercept it",C:"Rainfall patterns are entirely unrelated to vegetation cover",D:"Reduced transpiration from fewer plants can lead to less atmospheric moisture and therefore less frequent, less reliable precipitation"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-BIO-05",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"Which is an example of a 'regulating' ecosystem service, as opposed to a 'provisioning' one?",
      options:{A:"Fuel wood collected for cooking",B:"Timber harvested for construction",C:"Fruit and nuts collected for food",D:"Waste decomposition and maintenance of water quality"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-BIO-05",
      scaffold:"The four ecosystem service categories: provisioning (goods — timber, food, water, medicine, fuel), regulating (climate, water quality, waste decomposition, soil quality, disease/pest control, air quality), cultural (health/spiritual benefits, recreation, employment, education), and supporting (soil formation, nutrient cycling). B, C and D are all tangible goods, so they are provisioning services.",
      reforge:{stem:"A rainforest's role in absorbing CO2 through photosynthesis and helping maintain the balance of atmospheric gases is an example of which ecosystem service category?",options:{A:"Regulating — it controls a natural process (climate/atmospheric composition) rather than providing a tangible good",B:"Provisioning, since gases are a tangible good",C:"Cultural, since it benefits human wellbeing",D:"None of these categories apply to gases"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-BIO-06",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"The Awá people of the Amazon rely on the rainforest for shelter, rope, food and fuel. Why are indigenous communities like the Awá especially vulnerable to commercial exploitation of the biosphere?",
      options:{A:"Indigenous communities do not use any natural resources",B:"Commercial exploitation always benefits indigenous communities financially",C:"Their livelihoods depend directly on ecosystem goods and services, so deforestation or pollution removes the very resources they rely on for survival",D:"The Awá import all of their food and materials from cities"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-BIO-06",
      scaffold:"The Awá depend on the rainforest directly: branches and palm leaves for shelters (tapā í), palm fibres for rope and hammocks, wood for fires, and food from honey, fish, berries (açaí), tapir, deer and howler monkeys. Commercial exploitation (mining, agriculture, logging, HEP) removes exactly these resources, forcing communities to abandon traditional lifestyles when food sources disappear.",
      reforge:{stem:"A mining company clears forest to build access roads near an indigenous community's territory. Which secondary impact is most likely to follow?",options:{A:"No impact, since mining only affects the exact area being mined",B:"Toxic waste chemicals entering the river system, damaging both the aquatic ecosystem and the community's water and food supply",C:"An automatic increase in the community's traditional food sources",D:"Improved biodiversity as a direct result of road building"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-BIO-07",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"Why does the construction of a hydroelectric dam on a river affect ecosystems even far downstream?",
      options:{A:"The dam traps sediment and can block fish migration routes, altering the flow of nutrients and disrupting species like salmon that need to travel upstream",B:"Dams increase the amount of sediment reaching downstream ecosystems",C:"Dams only affect the exact location where they are built",D:"HEP generates no environmental impact once construction is finished"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-BIO-07",
      scaffold:"HEP impacts: forest clearance for the dam/reservoir, flooding of habitats, loss of food sources, a physical barrier to migratory fish (e.g. salmon), and sediment trapped behind the dam rather than replenishing the ecosystems and floodplains downstream. This is a good example of how one intervention creates a chain of effects across a whole river system, not just the dam site.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which statement best explains why cleared biomes are difficult to fully restore once exploited?",options:{A:"Ecosystem restoration has no relationship to how long the land was exploited",B:"Once cleared, biomes are legally prevented from ever regenerating",C:"Cleared land regenerates to its original state within a few months",D:"The interdependent relationships between soil, vegetation, and species take a very long time to re-establish, so the ecosystem cannot immediately return to providing the same goods and services"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-BIO-08",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"How does deforestation directly affect the global carbon cycle?",
      options:{A:"Deforestation has no effect on atmospheric CO2 levels",B:"It reduces the number of carbon sinks (plants and soils) able to absorb CO2 from the atmosphere, while burning cleared biomass releases stored carbon directly",C:"Deforestation removes CO2 from the atmosphere more effectively than intact forest",D:"Trees play no role in the carbon cycle"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-BIO-08",
      scaffold:"Plants and soils act as carbon sinks — absorbing CO2 during photosynthesis, storing it in biomass, and (over long timescales) in soil and eventually fossil fuels. Human activity increases atmospheric CO2 two ways: burning biomass/fossil fuels releases stored carbon directly, and deforestation simply removes the sinks that would otherwise keep absorbing it — a double impact.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why might felling trees for agriculture have a bigger long-term climate impact than the carbon released by the felling itself?",options:{A:"Agricultural land absorbs more CO2 than forest",B:"There is no long-term impact beyond the immediate release",C:"The loss of a carbon sink means future CO2 emissions are no longer absorbed as effectively, compounding the initial release",D:"Felling trees permanently removes CO2 from the atmosphere"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-BIO-09",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"Why is soil in a biome that has recently been deforested likely to become less fertile over time?",
      options:{A:"Nutrients are usually supplied through decomposition of leaf litter and dead organic matter; without vegetation, this input stops and existing nutrients are washed away by leaching and erosion",B:"Soil fertility is entirely unrelated to vegetation cover",C:"Deforestation adds nutrients to soil through increased sunlight exposure",D:"Soil fertility always increases once trees are removed"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-BIO-09",
      scaffold:"The nutrient cycle depends on continuous input from decomposing organic matter. Remove the vegetation and that supply stops; the exposed soil surface is also more prone to leaching (nutrients washed down through the soil) and surface erosion (topsoil washed away entirely) since there's no canopy or root system to protect and hold it in place.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why are tropical rainforest soils described as particularly vulnerable when vegetation is removed, even though rainforests have such high biodiversity?",options:{A:"Rainforest soils contain no organic matter at all",B:"Rainforest soils are naturally the most fertile soils in the world",C:"Most nutrients in a rainforest are stored in the living biomass rather than the soil itself, so once the biomass is removed there is very little nutrient reserve left in the ground",D:"Removing rainforest vegetation has no effect on soil nutrients"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-BIO-10",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"According to Malthus's theory (1798), what would eventually happen if population growth outpaced food supply?",
      options:{A:"Population growth would have no relationship to food supply",B:"A 'Malthusian catastrophe' — famine, disease or war acting as positive checks that increase the death rate and bring population back into balance with resources",C:"Food supply would automatically double whenever population doubled",D:"Technology would always increase food supply fast enough to prevent any problem"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-BIO-10",
      scaffold:"Malthus (1798): population grows faster than food supply, so eventually there won't be enough food, triggering 'positive checks' (famine, disease, war) that raise the death rate, or 'preventative checks' that lower the birth rate — either way, this brings population back in line with resources. Malthus's original predictions proved wrong because he didn't foresee later agricultural technology, but neo-Malthusians argue his logic still applies given finite land and rising food prices today.",
      reforge:{stem:"Neo-Malthusians point to modern famines as evidence supporting Malthus's original theory. What is their key argument?",options:{A:"Famines have never occurred since 1798, disproving Malthus entirely",B:"Neo-Malthusians agree completely with Boserup's theory instead",C:"Technology has permanently solved the problem Malthus described, so neo-Malthusian concerns are baseless",D:"Most available agricultural land has now been used, fertile land is declining, and food prices are rising while population keeps growing — so a Malthusian check may still occur"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-BIO-11",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"How does Boserup's theory (1965) directly contradict Malthus's view of population and resources?",
      options:{A:"Boserup argues population growth stimulates technological innovation that increases food production, rather than leading inevitably to famine",B:"Boserup argues population and food supply are completely unrelated",C:"Boserup agrees entirely with Malthus that population growth always outpaces resources",D:"Boserup predicts food supply will decline as population grows, just as Malthus did"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-BIO-11",
      scaffold:"Boserup (1965) is the optimistic counter to Malthus's pessimism: as population grows, necessity drives innovation — new farming technology, more efficient use of resources, and a shift from non-renewable to renewable resources — increasing the food/resource supply to match demand, rather than being outpaced by it.",
      reforge:{stem:"The Green Revolution of the 1960s–70s saw crop yields rise dramatically through new seed varieties, fertilisers and irrigation as global population grew. Which theory does this best support?",options:{A:"Neither theory, since crop yields are unrelated to population",B:"It disproves Boserup by showing technology cannot increase food supply",C:"Boserup's theory — population pressure stimulated technological innovation that increased food supply",D:"Malthus's theory — it demonstrates a Malthusian catastrophe occurring exactly as predicted"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-BIO-12",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"Why does increasing global wealth generally lead to greater pressure on the biosphere's resources?",
      options:{A:"Wealthier countries always use fewer natural resources than poorer countries",B:"Wealthier populations consume more food, water, energy and consumer goods, and live in larger homes — all of which increase resource extraction and waste",C:"Increased wealth automatically reduces a population's ecological footprint to zero",D:"Wealth has no relationship to resource consumption"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-BIO-12",
      scaffold:"Rising average wealth increases demand across the board: more spending on food, water (appliances like dishwashers), larger homes (more land), energy (production requires land for power stations/wind/solar farms), and consumer goods (more industrial production and waste). Combined with population growth reaching 8 billion in 2022 and rising urbanisation (55%), this compounds the pressure on biomes worldwide.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which combination of trends explains the sharpest recent rise in global resource demand?",options:{A:"A falling global population with static wealth levels",B:"Resource demand is unrelated to any demographic or economic trend",C:"Declining urbanisation with no change in wealth",D:"Population growth combined with rising average wealth and increasing urbanisation, all occurring simultaneously"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-BIO-FB-01",spec:"GCSE-BIO",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about factors affecting biome distribution.",
      template:"The main factor affecting biome distribution is ___, but local factors such as ___, rock and soil type, drainage and ___ also cause variation.",
      blanks:["climate","altitude","continentality"],
      bank:["climate","altitude","continentality","population","currency","transport"],
      tag:"MC-BIO-01",
      scaffold:"Climate (temperature and precipitation) sets the broad global pattern of biomes; altitude, rock/soil type, drainage, continentality and ocean currents then create local variation within and across that pattern."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-BIO-FB-02",spec:"GCSE-BIO",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about ecosystem services.",
      template:"___ services are tangible products like timber and food. ___ services include climate control and waste decomposition. ___ services include recreation and education.",
      blanks:["Provisioning","Regulating","Cultural"],
      bank:["Provisioning","Regulating","Cultural","Supporting","Extracting","Economic"],
      tag:"MC-BIO-05",
      scaffold:"The four ecosystem service categories: provisioning (goods — timber, food, water, medicine), regulating (climate, water/air quality, waste decomposition, pest control), cultural (health, recreation, employment, heritage, education), and supporting (soil formation, nutrient cycling)."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-BIO-FB-03",spec:"GCSE-BIO",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about deforestation and the carbon cycle.",
      template:"Plants and soils act as carbon ___, absorbing CO2 during ___. Deforestation removes this ability, while burning ___ releases stored carbon directly into the atmosphere.",
      blanks:["sinks","photosynthesis","biomass"],
      bank:["sinks","photosynthesis","biomass","sources","respiration","minerals"],
      tag:"MC-BIO-08",
      scaffold:"Plants absorb CO2 during photosynthesis, storing carbon in biomass and eventually soil — this makes vegetated biomes carbon sinks. Deforestation both removes this ongoing absorption and, if the cleared biomass is burned, releases the carbon that was already stored."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-BIO-FB-04",spec:"GCSE-BIO",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about population and resource theories.",
      template:"___ took a pessimistic view, arguing population growth would outpace food supply leading to a catastrophe. ___ took an optimistic view, arguing population growth stimulates ___ to increase food production.",
      blanks:["Malthus","Boserup","technology"],
      bank:["Malthus","Boserup","technology","government","migration","trade"],
      tag:"MC-BIO-11",
      scaffold:"Malthus (1798, pessimistic): population outpaces food, causing famine/disease/war. Boserup (1965, optimistic): population pressure drives technological innovation that raises food production to meet demand. Both theories focus on food but can be applied to other resources too."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-BIO-13",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"A student mixes up abiotic and biotic components of an ecosystem. What is the correct distinction?",
      options:{A:"Abiotic components are living organisms; biotic components are non-living factors",B:"Abiotic components only exist in deserts",C:"Abiotic components are non-living environmental factors (e.g. climate, light, soil type); biotic components are the living organisms that interact with the environment and each other",D:"They are two names for exactly the same thing"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-BIO-KT-01",
      scaffold:"A simple memory hook: 'a-' at the start of abiotic means 'without' — abiotic factors have no life. Biotic factors are the living parts of an ecosystem (plants, animals, decomposers) that depend on and interact with those non-living conditions.",
      reforge:{stem:"Soil type and sunlight exposure are examples of which type of ecosystem component?",options:{A:"Neither — soil and sunlight are not considered ecosystem components",B:"Both abiotic and biotic simultaneously",C:"Biotic components, since they directly involve living organisms",D:"Abiotic components, since they are non-living environmental factors"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-BIO-14",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"In a food chain, what is the correct order and role of producers, consumers and decomposers?",
      options:{A:"Consumers and producers are the same trophic level",B:"Producers convert sunlight into energy (glucose) via photosynthesis; consumers gain energy by eating other organisms; decomposers break down dead organic material and release nutrients back into the ecosystem",C:"Producers eat consumers, who then decompose to release energy",D:"Decomposers convert sunlight into glucose"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-BIO-KT-02",
      scaffold:"Energy flow: producers (plants) capture solar energy → consumers eat producers or other consumers → decomposers break down dead matter from any level, releasing nutrients back into the soil for producers to use again — this cycle is what nutrient cycling describes.",
      reforge:{stem:"A dead animal is broken down by fungi and bacteria, releasing nutrients back into the soil. What role are the fungi and bacteria playing in this food chain?",options:{A:"Decomposers",B:"Producers",C:"Abiotic components",D:"Primary consumers"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-BIO-15",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"What is altitudinal zonation?",
      options:{A:"The process of carbon dioxide being stored long-term in soil or vegetation",B:"A theory about population growth and food supply",C:"The presence of distinct bands of different vegetation types at different elevations on a mountain, caused by changing environmental conditions such as temperature with altitude",D:"The measurement of a mountain's total height above sea level"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-BIO-KT-03",
      scaffold:"As altitude increases, temperature falls (roughly 6.5°C per 1000m) and conditions become harsher, so vegetation changes in visible bands up a mountainside — from forest at the base, through coniferous and alpine vegetation, to bare rock and snow near the summit.",
      reforge:{stem:"Climbing a mountain, a hiker passes through forest, then coniferous vegetation, then bare rock near the summit, as temperature falls with height. What term describes this pattern?",options:{A:"The hydrological cycle",B:"Nutrient cycling",C:"Carbon sequestration",D:"Altitudinal zonation"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-BIO-16",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"How does carbon sequestration differ from simply 'not emitting greenhouse gases'?",
      options:{A:"Carbon sequestration is the active, long-term capture and storage of carbon dioxide already in the atmosphere (e.g. by forests or oceans), not just the avoidance of new emissions",B:"Carbon sequestration and emissions are the same process",C:"Only oceans, never forests, can sequester carbon",D:"Carbon sequestration means releasing carbon dioxide into the atmosphere"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-BIO-KT-04",
      scaffold:"A mature tropical rainforest is a major carbon sink because photosynthesis actively removes CO2 from the air and locks it into wood and soil for decades — this is why large-scale deforestation is doubly damaging: it releases stored carbon and removes future sequestration capacity.",
      reforge:{stem:"A mature forest is cleared and burned, releasing stored carbon dioxide and removing the trees that once absorbed it. What has been lost from this landscape?",options:{A:"A form of altitudinal zonation",B:"A carbon sink — a system that absorbed more carbon dioxide than it released",C:"An abiotic component with no relation to carbon",D:"A source of eutrophication"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-BIO-17",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"What causes eutrophication in an aquatic ecosystem, and why is it harmful?",
      options:{A:"Eutrophication increases oxygen levels in water, benefiting all aquatic species",B:"Eutrophication only occurs in desert biomes",C:"Eutrophication is caused by a lack of nutrients in the water",D:"Excess nutrients (often from agricultural fertiliser runoff) cause algae to grow rapidly; as the algae die and decompose, oxygen in the water is depleted, harming other aquatic life"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-BIO-KT-05",
      scaffold:"Eutrophication is a knock-on chain reaction: nutrient input (e.g. nitrates/phosphates from fertiliser) → algal bloom → algae blocks light for other plants and eventually dies → decomposition of dead algae consumes dissolved oxygen → fish and other aquatic organisms suffocate.",
      reforge:{stem:"Fertiliser runs off farmland into a nearby lake, causing a rapid increase in algae. What is the most likely eventual consequence for fish in the lake?",options:{A:"Eutrophication has no effect on any organisms living in the water",B:"Oxygen levels in the lake will rise sharply, benefiting all species",C:"As the algae die and decompose, oxygen levels in the water fall, potentially suffocating fish and other aquatic life",D:"Fish populations will increase immediately with no negative effects"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-BIO-18",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"Why are ecosystem services important when assessing the value of a biome?",
      options:{A:"They describe only the number of species present",B:"They are benefits ecosystems provide, such as food, flood regulation, carbon storage and recreation",C:"They prove every ecosystem has the same economic value",D:"They refer only to tourism income from national parks"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-BIO-KT-06",
      scaffold:"Ecosystem services are benefits people obtain from ecosystems. They include food and timber, water purification and flood regulation, recreation, nutrient cycling and soil formation.",
      reforge:{stem:"A wetland is drained for construction and the town later experiences worse flooding. Which ecosystem service was reduced?",options:{A:"Flood-water regulation and storage",B:"International trade in timber",C:"Mineral extraction from bedrock",D:"Altitudinal zonation of vegetation"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-BIO-19",spec:"GCSE-BIO",stem:"How does the precautionary principle apply to managing a threatened ecosystem?",
      options:{A:"Wait until damage is irreversible before taking action",B:"Assume any development is sustainable if it creates jobs",C:"Take preventive action when serious harm is possible, even if evidence is incomplete",D:"Ignore local communities because conservation is purely scientific"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-BIO-KT-07",
      scaffold:"The precautionary principle supports prevention where potential environmental damage is serious or irreversible, even when evidence is uncertain. It means assessing risk, monitoring impacts and avoiding permanent damage.",
      reforge:{stem:"A new road could fragment endangered-species habitat, but its full impact is uncertain. Which response best follows the precautionary principle?",options:{A:"Build immediately because the impact is not proven",B:"Assess alternatives and impacts before construction, then avoid or reduce habitat damage",C:"Ignore the species because roads never affect ecosystems",D:"Wait until the population collapses before monitoring it"},correct:"B"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-GEO-FORESTS"] = {
  label: "Forests Under Threat",
  color: "#0f766e",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"GCSE-FOR-01",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"In a tropical rainforest food web, the Zam tree relies on agouti to disperse its seeds, and leaf-cutter ants rely on Zam tree seedlings. If agouti are overhunted, what is the most likely knock-on effect?",
      options:{A:"The Zam tree population would increase because there is less competition for its seeds",B:"There would be no effect on any other species in the food web",C:"Fewer Zam trees germinate, reducing food for leaf-cutter ants and, in turn, the tree frogs and snakes further along the food chain",D:"Leaf-cutter ant numbers would rise because they are unaffected by tree numbers"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-FOR-01",
      scaffold:"This is a classic keystone-species/interdependence chain question. Agouti disperse Zam tree seeds → fewer agouti means fewer seeds spread and fewer new trees germinate → less food for leaf-cutter ants → less food for tree frogs that eat the ants → less food for snakes that eat the frogs. A single change ripples through the whole food web because rainforest components are interdependent.",
      reforge:{stem:"The Zam tree is pollinated by a specific butterfly species, and beetles lay eggs in that butterfly's faeces. If the Zam tree population declines, what happens to the beetle population?",options:{A:"Beetles would switch permanently to a completely different food source with no population change",B:"Beetle numbers would increase due to more available habitat",C:"Beetle numbers are entirely unrelated to tree or butterfly populations",D:"It is likely to decline too, since fewer Zam tree flowers mean fewer butterflies, and beetles depend indirectly on the butterflies for their egg-laying habitat"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-FOR-02",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"Which adaptation helps tropical rainforest trees such as those with buttress roots survive in the ecosystem?",
      options:{A:"Buttress roots allow trees to store water during droughts",B:"Buttress roots provide extra support above ground for very tall trees, since their below-ground roots are too shallow to anchor them alone",C:"Buttress roots protect trees from cold winter temperatures",D:"Buttress roots help trees avoid competition for sunlight"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-FOR-02",
      scaffold:"Rainforest plant adaptations: waxy leaves with drip tips (shed water fast, preventing mould/leaf damage), buttress roots (extra above-ground support since soil nutrients and therefore roots stay shallow), lianas (vines climbing tall trees to reach sunlight), epiphytes (grow on trunks/branches, absorbing nutrients from air/rain/debris), and smooth trunks (reduce epiphyte colonisation).",
      reforge:{stem:"A sloth has algae growing in its fur, and a toucan has an unusually large bill. What do these adaptations have in common?",options:{A:"Both help the animal survive specific challenges of rainforest life — camouflage from predators for the sloth, and reaching/cracking fruit and nuts for the toucan",B:"Both adaptations are found only in desert animals",C:"Neither adaptation provides any survival advantage",D:"Both adaptations evolved specifically to cope with cold temperatures"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-FOR-03",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"Why is nutrient cycling in a tropical rainforest described as 'rapid', despite the soil itself being relatively infertile?",
      options:{A:"Rainforest soils naturally contain the highest nutrient reserves of any biome",B:"Nutrient cycling in rainforests is actually the slowest of any biome",C:"High temperature and moisture cause rapid decomposition and plant uptake, so most nutrients are held in the living biomass rather than stored in the soil or litter",D:"Rainforest plants do not require nutrients from the soil"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-FOR-03",
      scaffold:"The heat and moisture of the rainforest accelerate decomposition (leaf litter breaks down fast) and plant growth (rapid nutrient uptake), so nutrients cycle quickly between biomass, litter and soil rather than accumulating in any one store. Because most nutrients end up locked in the standing biomass rather than the soil, the soil itself stays infertile — which is why cleared rainforest land quickly becomes unproductive for farming.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why does clearing tropical rainforest for agriculture typically produce only a few years of good crop yields before the soil becomes unproductive?",options:{A:"Nutrient levels in rainforest soil are unaffected by vegetation removal",B:"Rainforest soil is naturally the most fertile soil type and never loses productivity",C:"Crop failure in cleared rainforest is caused entirely by lack of rainfall",D:"Because most nutrients were stored in the felled biomass rather than the soil, that nutrient store is lost once the trees are removed and not replaced"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-FOR-04",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"In the taiga, salmon are described as a keystone species. Why does a decline in salmon numbers have a disproportionately large impact on the ecosystem?",
      options:{A:"Salmon are a critical food source for predators such as bears and wolves (up to 50% of a wolf's diet), so their decline directly threatens those populations too",B:"Wolves and bears do not rely on fish for any part of their diet",C:"Salmon numbers have no effect on any other taiga species",D:"Salmon have no predators in the taiga ecosystem"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-FOR-04",
      scaffold:"A keystone species has an outsized effect on its ecosystem relative to its numbers. Salmon migrate upstream and are a vital food source for bears and wolves — a barrier like a dam blocking migration, or disease affecting salmon, cuts that food supply and directly reduces predator populations. Scots Pine is the taiga's other named keystone species, supporting squirrels, voles, mice, insects, lichens and mosses through its bark and cones.",
      reforge:{stem:"A dam is built on a river that salmon use to migrate upstream in the taiga. What is the most likely ecological consequence?",options:{A:"Dams have no effect on any migratory species",B:"Salmon can no longer reach their spawning grounds, reducing their population and cutting off a major food source for bears and wolves further up the food chain",C:"Salmon numbers would increase because dams provide additional habitat",D:"Wolf populations would be entirely unaffected since they do not rely on salmon"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-FOR-05",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"Why do coniferous trees in the taiga have a conical shape and needle-like leaves?",
      options:{A:"These features help the trees absorb more sunlight during long summer days",B:"The conical shape sheds heavy snow to prevent branches breaking, and needle-shaped, waxy leaves reduce water loss and frost damage",C:"The conical shape helps trees survive drought conditions in hot climates",D:"These features are unrelated to survival and are purely decorative"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-FOR-05",
      scaffold:"Taiga tree adaptations: conical shape (sheds snow, preventing branch damage), needle-shaped waxy leaves (reduce transpiration/water loss and resist frost damage), flexible branches (bend rather than break under snow weight), wide shallow roots (avoid frozen ground/permafrost while anchoring against strong winds), and seeds protected inside cones.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why do many taiga animals such as the snowshoe hare change fur colour between summer (brown) and winter (white)?",options:{A:"Only taiga plants change colour seasonally, not animals",B:"Fur colour change has no survival benefit and occurs randomly",C:"It is purely a response to temperature with no camouflage function",D:"It provides camouflage appropriate to the season, helping the animal avoid predators (or, for predators, avoid detection by prey) against snow or bare ground"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-FOR-06",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"Why does the taiga have lower Net Primary Productivity (NPP) than the tropical rainforest?",
      options:{A:"The taiga receives more sunlight hours per year than the rainforest",B:"The taiga has a longer growing season than the rainforest",C:"Long cold winters, a short growing season and limited sunlight hours restrict plant growth, and slow decomposition means fewer nutrients are available",D:"NPP is identical across all forest biomes"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-FOR-06",
      scaffold:"NPP measures the rate of energy production by plants (photosynthesis minus respiration). The taiga's limiting factors: long cold winters, short 2–3 month growing season, low precipitation, slow chemical weathering, and nutrients stuck in a slowly-decomposing litter layer rather than the biomass. The rainforest has none of these limits, hence far higher NPP.",
      reforge:{stem:"A 4-mark exam question asks: 'Explain why the taiga forest biome has very low levels of productivity.' Which answer chain would score full marks?",options:{A:"Long cold winters (1) and limited sunlight hours (1) restrict growth, while nutrients are largely locked in the litter layer rather than available to plants (1), all acting as limiting factors on productivity (1)",B:"Productivity is low because there are lots of trees (1)",C:"The taiga has high productivity, not low (1)",D:"The taiga is cold (1)"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-FOR-07",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"Which countries experience the highest rates of tropical rainforest deforestation?",
      options:{A:"Only countries with no rainforest at all",B:"Canada, Russia and Scandinavia",C:"Malaysia, Brazil, India and Indonesia",D:"The UK, France and Germany"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-FOR-07",
      scaffold:"Malaysia, Brazil, India and Indonesia have the highest tropical deforestation rates. Note that deforestation is rarely caused by a single factor — settlement growth also drives agricultural clearance to feed the new population, so causes are usually linked together rather than isolated. Wildfires are a natural cause, but their frequency and severity have increased due to human-induced climate change.",
      reforge:{stem:"A student explains deforestation in Indonesia purely as 'logging for timber'. Why is this an incomplete answer?",options:{A:"Settlement growth never contributes to deforestation",B:"Logging is the only cause of deforestation anywhere in the world",C:"Timber logging has no connection to any other human activity",D:"Deforestation is usually the result of multiple linked causes — e.g. logging opens access roads that then enable agricultural clearance and settlement growth in the same area"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-FOR-08",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"How can drought act as an indirect threat to the tropical rainforest, beyond the direct loss of water?",
      options:{A:"Droughts always increase rainforest biodiversity",B:"Dry leaf litter kills decomposer organisms, reducing soil fertility, while drought-stressed trees drop more leaves, further reducing food supply and increasing wildfire risk",C:"Droughts have no effect on rainforest ecosystems beyond a temporary lack of water",D:"Drought conditions immediately restore rainforest nutrient levels"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-FOR-08",
      scaffold:"Climate change is the most significant indirect threat, mainly through altered rainfall. The 2015/16 drought and resulting wildfires destroyed around 2.5 billion trees and plants. Drought effects cascade: dead decomposers → lower soil fertility → disrupted nutrient cycle; excess leaf loss → less food for herbivores → food web impacts; reduced evapotranspiration → potentially even less future rainfall; and during drought the rainforest can flip to emitting CO2 rather than absorbing it.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why might a severe rainforest drought make future droughts more likely in the same region?",options:{A:"Stressed trees lose more leaves, reducing transpiration and evapotranspiration, which can decrease the moisture recycled into the atmosphere and lower future rainfall",B:"Fewer leaves always increase future rainfall",C:"Droughts permanently increase soil fertility, preventing future droughts",D:"Droughts have no relationship to future rainfall patterns"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-FOR-09",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"How does acid rain damage taiga forests, and where does it originate?",
      options:{A:"Acid rain has no measurable effect on coniferous trees",B:"Acid rain is caused directly by logging activity within the taiga itself",C:"Acid rain only affects tropical biomes, not the taiga",D:"Sulphur dioxide and nitrogen oxides from burning fossil fuels react with atmospheric water, and the resulting acidic rain damages needles and depletes soil nutrients like calcium"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-FOR-09",
      scaffold:"Acid rain forms when SO2 and NOx (released by burning fossil fuels, often far from the taiga itself) react with atmospheric water, lowering rainfall pH below 5.7. Impacts: damaged needles reduce photosynthesis, depleted soil calcium weakens trees, and weakened trees become more vulnerable to pests and disease — an important point because the source of acid rain often needs tackling far outside the taiga region where the damage appears.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why is tackling acid rain damage to the taiga particularly difficult compared with tackling a threat like logging?",options:{A:"Acid rain is not linked to any human industrial activity",B:"Acid rain can travel long distances from its industrial source, so the cause must be addressed outside the taiga region itself, not within it",C:"Acid rain has an identical, easily local solution to logging",D:"Acid rain is generated entirely within the taiga by the trees themselves"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-FOR-10",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"Between 2000 and 2013, how did the rate of taiga deforestation compare with deforestation in the Amazon over the same period?",
      options:{A:"Taiga deforestation occurred at one and a half times the rate of Amazon deforestation, mostly in Canada and Russia",B:"No deforestation occurred in the taiga during this period",C:"Taiga deforestation was far lower than in the Amazon",D:"Taiga and Amazon deforestation rates were identical"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-FOR-10",
      scaffold:"This statistic often surprises students, since the Amazon dominates media coverage of deforestation — but between 2000–2013, taiga loss (mainly in Canada and Russia) outpaced the Amazon by 1.5×. Main causes in the taiga: logging, mining, and oil/gas drilling, plus indirect impacts like air and water pollution from associated transport and road building.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why might taiga deforestation receive less international media attention than Amazon deforestation, despite occurring at a higher rate?",options:{A:"Taiga deforestation does not actually occur",B:"The Amazon has a lower deforestation rate, explaining the difference in coverage",C:"Public and media focus has historically centred on the Amazon's biodiversity and indigenous communities, even though taiga loss in Canada and Russia has been more extensive by rate",D:"Media coverage is always proportional to the actual scale of an environmental issue"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-FOR-11",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"What is the main aim of REDD (Reducing Emissions from Deforestation and Forest Degradation)?",
      options:{A:"To ban all logging activity worldwide immediately",B:"To provide financial incentives (cash or carbon credits) from developed countries and TNCs to encourage the protection and sustainable management of forests",C:"To fund the construction of new roads through protected forests",D:"To increase the rate of deforestation in developing countries"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-FOR-11",
      scaffold:"REDD is a UN strategy funded by developed countries and TNCs to offset their carbon emissions, paying to protect forests instead. Strengths: tackles climate change, involves local communities. Weaknesses: no clear definition of what counts as 'forest', corruption and fraud, and unclear forest ownership. Compare with CITES (184 countries, restricts trade in endangered species/timber, but hard to monitor and enforce, with weak sanctions).",
      reforge:{stem:"Why might REDD funding fail to achieve its intended protection of a forest area in practice?",options:{A:"REDD guarantees 100% success in every country that receives funding",B:"REDD has no known weaknesses or implementation challenges",C:"Corruption or unclear land ownership can mean money is misused or the wrong areas are 'protected' rather than the ones most at risk of deforestation",D:"REDD funding always goes directly and transparently to local communities"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-FOR-12",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"What is meant by 'sustainable forestry' as a management strategy for the taiga?",
      options:{A:"Sustainable forestry only applies to tropical rainforests, not the taiga",B:"Forests are cleared as quickly as possible to maximise short-term profit",C:"All logging is banned permanently across the entire taiga",D:"Logged areas are replanted with native species (afforestation) faster than they are used, rather than being permanently converted into plantations"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-FOR-12",
      scaffold:"Sustainable forestry balances the needs of current populations (industry, indigenous communities, wildlife) with future generations by ensuring replanting happens faster than logging removes trees, and natural forest isn't converted into monoculture plantations. The Boreal Forest Platform (industry, e.g. IKEA, working with NGOs like WWF) is a named example, alongside protected areas like Russia's proposed Dvinsky Reserve and Canada's Cape Breton Highlands National Park.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why might indigenous communities and oil/gas companies have conflicting views on taiga management, even if both groups say they want to protect the environment?",options:{A:"Indigenous communities depend on intact land for hunting and traditional life, while companies prioritise extracting resources for economic gain, even with mitigation efforts in place",B:"Oil and gas companies have no environmental impact on indigenous land",C:"Indigenous communities have no interest in how the taiga is managed",D:"Both groups always want exactly the same outcome with no disagreement"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-FOR-FB-01",spec:"GCSE-FOR",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about rainforest adaptations.",
      template:"Waxy leaves with drip tips allow rainwater to run off quickly, preventing ___ growth. ___ roots provide extra support for tall trees since below-ground roots are shallow. ___ are vines that climb trees to reach sunlight.",
      blanks:["mould","Buttress","Lianas"],
      bank:["mould","Buttress","Lianas","fungus","Epiphytes","Canopy"],
      tag:"MC-FOR-02",
      scaffold:"Key rainforest plant adaptations: waxy drip-tip leaves shed water fast (preventing mould and leaf damage), buttress roots give tall trees above-ground stability, and lianas use trees as scaffolding to reach the canopy's sunlight."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-FOR-FB-02",spec:"GCSE-FOR",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about keystone species in the taiga.",
      template:"___ are a keystone species that provide up to 50% of a wolf's diet, so a decline caused by disease or a ___ blocking migration reduces predator populations. ___ Pine is another taiga keystone species supporting squirrels, voles and insects.",
      blanks:["Salmon","dam","Scots"],
      bank:["Salmon","dam","Scots","Trout","bridge","Silver"],
      tag:"MC-FOR-04",
      scaffold:"Salmon and Scots Pine are the taiga's two named keystone species. Salmon migration blocked by a dam cuts a major food source for bears and wolves; a decline in Scots Pine removes food and habitat for squirrels, voles, mice, insects, lichens and mosses."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-FOR-FB-03",spec:"GCSE-FOR",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about global forest protection strategies.",
      template:"___ is a UN strategy that provides financial incentives to protect forests and offset carbon emissions. ___ restricts international trade in endangered species and timber. The ___ approves wood sourced from sustainably managed forests.",
      blanks:["REDD","CITES","FSC"],
      bank:["REDD","CITES","FSC","ITTA","UN","WWF"],
      tag:"MC-FOR-11",
      scaffold:"REDD (Reducing Emissions from Deforestation and Forest Degradation) funds forest protection via carbon credits. CITES (Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species) covers over 35,000 species across 184 countries. The FSC (Forest Stewardship Council) certifies sustainably sourced timber. ITTA restricts hardwood timber trade specifically."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-FOR-FB-04",spec:"GCSE-FOR",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about sustainable taiga management.",
      template:"Sustainable ___ involves replanting logged areas with native species faster than they are used, known as ___. The Boreal Forest ___ is an example of industry and NGOs working together to allow logging without losing biodiversity.",
      blanks:["forestry","afforestation","Platform"],
      bank:["forestry","afforestation","Platform","mining","deforestation","Council"],
      tag:"MC-FOR-12",
      scaffold:"Sustainable forestry replants logged areas (afforestation) at a faster rate than logging removes trees, avoiding conversion of natural forest into plantations. The Boreal Forest Platform pairs industry (e.g. IKEA) with NGOs (e.g. WWF) to balance logging with biodiversity protection."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-FOR-13",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"A student confuses clear felling and selective logging. What is the correct distinction?",
      options:{A:"Clear felling removes all trees in an area, while selective logging removes only certain trees, reducing the environmental impact of the harvest",B:"Selective logging always removes more trees than clear felling",C:"Clear felling and selective logging are two names for the same process",D:"Clear felling only happens in the taiga, selective logging only in the tropics"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-FOR-KT-01",
      scaffold:"Clear felling is fast and profitable but devastating — it destroys habitats completely and leaves soil exposed to erosion. Selective logging is slower and less profitable per trip but leaves the forest structure largely intact, making it a key part of sustainable forestry strategies.",
      reforge:{stem:"A logging company removes every tree in a section of forest in one operation. What is this method called, and what is its main environmental drawback compared to selective logging?",options:{A:"Sustainable forestry — it has no environmental drawbacks",B:"Clear felling — it destroys habitats completely and leaves soil exposed to erosion, unlike selective logging which leaves forest structure intact",C:"Afforestation — it involves planting new trees, not removing them",D:"Selective logging — it always removes more trees than clear felling"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-FOR-14",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"What are buttress roots, and why do tropical rainforest trees rely on them?",
      options:{A:"An adaptation only found in taiga conifer trees to survive cold winters",B:"Roots that grow underground to store water during droughts",C:"Large, thick roots that grow above the ground, providing extra stability for tall trees growing in the rainforest's thin, nutrient-poor topsoil",D:"Woody vines that climb from the forest floor up into the canopy"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-FOR-KT-02",
      scaffold:"Because rainforest soil is thin and nutrients cycle rapidly through the biomass rather than the ground, trees can't anchor deep roots the way temperate trees do — buttress roots spread wide above ground to give tall canopy trees the stability they need. Don't confuse them with lianas (climbing woody vines) or epiphytes (plants growing on other plants).",
      reforge:{stem:"Why do tall trees in a tropical rainforest rely on wide, above-ground buttress roots rather than deep roots?",options:{A:"Buttress roots allow trees to climb into the canopy like lianas",B:"Rainforest topsoil is thin and nutrient-poor, so wide-spreading roots above ground provide the stability that deep roots cannot in such shallow soil",C:"Buttress roots are an adaptation found only in taiga conifer trees",D:"Buttress roots are used to store water during droughts"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-FOR-15",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"What is a drip-tip, and what problem does it solve for rainforest plants?",
      options:{A:"A method used by loggers to identify which trees to fell",B:"A root adaptation that helps trees survive drought",C:"An animal adaptation for hibernating through winter",D:"A leaf adaptation with a tapered end that allows heavy rainwater to run off quickly, preventing fungal and bacterial growth on the leaf surface"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-FOR-KT-03",
      scaffold:"Tropical rainforests receive extremely high rainfall — if water pooled on leaves it would encourage the growth of mould, fungi and bacteria in the hot, humid climate. The tapered drip-tip lets water shed quickly, keeping the leaf surface drier.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why might a leaf with a drip-tip be less likely to develop fungal growth in a tropical rainforest than a leaf without one?",options:{A:"Its tapered end lets heavy rainwater run off quickly rather than pooling on the leaf surface in the hot, humid climate",B:"Drip-tips prevent all rainfall from reaching the leaf entirely",C:"Drip-tips are an adaptation for surviving cold winters",D:"Drip-tips have no effect on how water behaves on a leaf"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-FOR-16",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"What is the difference between commercial farming and subsistence farming, and why does this distinction matter for deforestation?",
      options:{A:"Commercial and subsistence farming are identical in scale and purpose",B:"Commercial farming produces surplus crops or livestock to sell for profit (often driving large-scale deforestation, e.g. cattle ranching); subsistence farming produces just enough food for a farmer's own family, generally on a smaller scale",C:"Subsistence farming always causes more deforestation than commercial farming",D:"Commercial farming only exists in the taiga, not the rainforest"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-FOR-KT-04",
      scaffold:"Scale is the key exam distinction: commercial agriculture (like cattle ranching or plantations) is a major cause of large-scale tropical deforestation because it's profit-driven and land-hungry, whereas subsistence farming tends to clear much smaller areas to meet a family's own needs.",
      reforge:{stem:"A farmer clears a small area of rainforest to grow just enough food to feed their own family. What type of farming is this, and how does its scale compare to cattle ranching?",options:{A:"Commercial farming — on the same scale as cattle ranching",B:"Plantation farming — always larger in scale than cattle ranching",C:"Subsistence farming — typically clearing much smaller areas than profit-driven commercial farming like cattle ranching",D:"Subsistence farming always clears more land than commercial farming"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-FOR-17",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"How does a debt-for-nature swap work as a forest conservation strategy?",
      options:{A:"A country's debt to another nation or organisation is reduced or cancelled in exchange for that country committing to conservation efforts, such as protecting forest",B:"A country sells its forests to pay off international debt",C:"It is a scheme where logging companies swap timber for cash payments",D:"It is another name for REDD+"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-FOR-KT-05",
      scaffold:"Debt-for-nature swaps give indebted countries a direct financial incentive to conserve forest rather than exploit it for short-term income — distinct from REDD, which is specifically a UN-run scheme funding emissions reduction from deforestation and forest degradation.",
      reforge:{stem:"A wealthy country agrees to cancel part of a developing country's debt in exchange for that country protecting an area of rainforest. What is this arrangement called?",options:{A:"Sustainable forestry",B:"CITES",C:"REDD",D:"A debt-for-nature swap"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-FOR-18",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"What is the correct definition of biodiversity, and how does deforestation affect it?",
      options:{A:"Biodiversity increases automatically after any deforestation",B:"Biodiversity is the large variety of animal and plant life living within the same ecosystem; deforestation destroys habitats and reduces this variety, often permanently",C:"Biodiversity refers only to the number of tree species, not animals",D:"Biodiversity is a measure of a forest's total biomass, unrelated to species variety"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-FOR-KT-06",
      scaffold:"Biodiversity loss from deforestation is especially severe in the tropical rainforest, since so many species depend on very specific, complex interdependent relationships within that ecosystem — remove one habitat layer (like the canopy) and multiple dependent species can be affected at once.",
      reforge:{stem:"An area of rainforest is cleared for cattle ranching, destroying habitat for thousands of species that lived there. What has been most directly lost as a result?",options:{A:"Carbon sequestration capacity has increased as a result",B:"Nutrient cycling, which always increases after deforestation",C:"Biodiversity — the large variety of animal and plant life in that ecosystem",D:"Nothing of ecological significance has been lost"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-FOR-19",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"What is leaching, and why does it make deforested rainforest soil less fertile?",
      options:{A:"Leaching only occurs in the taiga, not the tropical rainforest",B:"Leaching increases soil fertility over time",C:"Leaching is the process of trees absorbing nutrients from the soil",D:"Leaching is where minerals are washed out of the soil by rainfall; without tree cover to intercept rain and take up nutrients, exposed rainforest soil loses its nutrients quickly after deforestation"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-FOR-KT-07",
      scaffold:"In an intact rainforest, most nutrients are stored in the living biomass and cycled rapidly, not stored deep in the soil. Once trees are cleared, heavy tropical rainfall leaches remaining nutrients out of the exposed soil quickly, which is why deforested rainforest land is often unproductive within just a few years.",
      reforge:{stem:"Shortly after a rainforest is cleared, heavy rainfall washes most of the remaining soil nutrients away. What is this process called, and why does it happen so quickly?",options:{A:"Leaching — because most nutrients were stored in the vegetation rather than deep in the soil, they are lost fast once tree cover and root uptake are removed",B:"Nutrient cycling — a beneficial process that improves soil fertility after deforestation",C:"Leaching only occurs in the taiga, never in a rainforest",D:"Leaching increases soil fertility over time"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-FOR-20",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"What is CITES, and what is its purpose?",
      options:{A:"A private company that manages tropical plantations",B:"The Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna & Flora — an international agreement that works to protect threatened wildlife from exploitation through trade restrictions",C:"An agreement specifically about reducing carbon emissions from factories",D:"A United Nations scheme that pays countries to reduce deforestation"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-FOR-KT-08",
      scaffold:"CITES targets the trade side of species loss — regulating or banning international trade in endangered animals and plants (like ivory or rare timber) — which complements habitat-focused strategies like conservation areas and sustainable forestry.",
      reforge:{stem:"An international agreement restricts the global trade of ivory to protect endangered elephants from exploitation. Which agreement is this most likely to be?",options:{A:"CITES",B:"Sustainable forestry",C:"A debt-for-nature swap",D:"REDD"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-FOR-21",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"Why can selective logging be less damaging than clear-cutting, but still require careful management?",
      options:{A:"It removes every tree but leaves the soil untouched",B:"It removes selected trees and can retain canopy, but roads and repeated extraction can fragment the forest",C:"It prevents all carbon emissions from forestry",D:"It is the same as converting rainforest into a plantation"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-FOR-KT-09",
      scaffold:"Selective logging can retain more canopy, roots and biodiversity than clear-cutting if extraction is limited. However, roads fragment habitats, machinery damages remaining trees and repeated logging can degrade the forest, so monitoring matters.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which practice would make selective logging more sustainable?",options:{A:"Build as many access roads as possible",B:"Remove all mature trees in one operation",C:"Set limits, protect habitats and use planned routes with monitoring and replanting",D:"Burn the remaining vegetation after every harvest"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-FOR-22",spec:"GCSE-FOR",stem:"How can agroforestry reduce pressure on tropical rainforests?",
      options:{A:"It combines trees with crops or livestock, providing products while maintaining some canopy and soil protection",B:"It replaces all farming with mining",C:"It requires farmers to remove every tree before planting",D:"It makes soils fertile without any nutrient cycling"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-FOR-KT-10",
      scaffold:"Agroforestry integrates useful trees with crops or livestock. Trees can provide fruit, fuelwood, shade, habitat and carbon storage, while farmers earn income from several products.",
      reforge:{stem:"A farmer grows cocoa beneath retained shade trees and harvests fruit and timber as well. Which benefit does this illustrate?",options:{A:"A single-crop plantation with no biodiversity",B:"Agroforestry diversifying income while retaining some habitat and carbon storage",C:"Clear-cutting followed by permanent soil exposure",D:"Mining as a conservation strategy"},correct:"B"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-GEO-ENERGY"] = {
  label: "Consuming Energy Resources",
  color: "#0f766e",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENE-01",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"Which of these correctly classifies gas, wind and biofuel by energy type?",
      options:{A:"All three are renewable",B:"All three are non-renewable",C:"Gas is non-renewable (finite), wind is renewable (infinite), biofuel is recyclable (can be regrown/reused)",D:"Gas is renewable, wind is non-renewable, biofuel is finite"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-ENE-01",
      scaffold:"Non-renewable (finite, will run out): gas, oil, coal. Renewable (infinite, won't run out): hydroelectric, wave/tidal, wind, solar, geothermal. Recyclable (can be regrown or reused, e.g. biofuel and nuclear) sits as a distinct third category — don't lump it in with 'renewable' in an exam answer, since the distinction is often tested directly.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why is nuclear energy classed as 'recyclable' rather than 'renewable' or 'non-renewable'?",options:{A:"Its fuel (uranium) is a finite resource like fossil fuels, but nuclear material can be processed and partially reused, placing it in its own distinct category",B:"Nuclear energy is infinite and will never run out, making it fully renewable",C:"Nuclear energy cannot be classified using any of these three categories",D:"Nuclear energy comes from an inexhaustible natural process like sunlight"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENE-02",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"Why can renewable energy sources still have negative environmental impacts, even though they don't emit greenhouse gases once operational?",
      options:{A:"Renewable energy sources have zero environmental impact at every stage",B:"Renewable sources always improve biodiversity wherever they are built",C:"Only non-renewable sources have any environmental impact whatsoever",D:"Manufacturing and constructing the equipment (e.g. wind turbines, solar panels) produces greenhouse gas emissions, and the infrastructure itself can cause land use, visual, or wildlife impacts"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-ENE-02",
      scaffold:"Even 'clean' renewables carry costs: wind (visual/noise pollution, bird strikes, manufacturing emissions), solar (large land use, manufacturing emissions), HEP (flooding land, blocking fish migration, trapping sediment, deforestation in tropical areas), geothermal (emits sulphuric gases). A good exam answer avoids treating 'renewable' as automatically 'no impact'.",
      reforge:{stem:"A hydroelectric dam produces no direct greenhouse gas emissions during operation. Which impact makes it still environmentally costly?",options:{A:"HEP dams have no impact of any kind on the surrounding environment",B:"Large areas of land are flooded behind the dam, and the dam can block fish migration routes and trap sediment that downstream ecosystems depend on",C:"HEP dams eliminate flooding entirely with no other consequences",D:"HEP dams always increase biodiversity in the surrounding area"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENE-03",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"Why does the UK have large coal reserves that remain largely unused?",
      options:{A:"UK coal reserves have all already been fully extracted",B:"The UK has no coal reserves at all",C:"Accessibility has no bearing on whether a resource is used",D:"Accessibility — the coal lies too deep underground to be economically mined, even though the resource physically exists"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-ENE-03",
      scaffold:"Access to energy is shaped by: physical factors (geology determines whether coal/oil/gas formed; climate affects renewable efficiency — e.g. solar needs clear skies, wind needs steady but not excessive wind, HEP needs reliable precipitation), accessibility (depth underground or under oceans raises extraction cost), level of development (nuclear/geothermal need high technology and skilled staff), and politics (government investment decisions, and conflict disrupting supply, e.g. Russia's invasion of Ukraine affecting European gas).",
      reforge:{stem:"Why might a developing country struggle to develop nuclear power even if it has suitable uranium deposits?",options:{A:"Uranium deposits alone guarantee a country can build nuclear power stations",B:"Nuclear power requires no specialist technology or training of any kind",C:"Nuclear power requires high levels of technology and highly skilled staff, which may not be available in a country at an earlier stage of development",D:"Developing countries always have more nuclear capacity than developed countries"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENE-04",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"Why do emerging economies such as India and China have much higher energy demand growth than developed economies?",
      options:{A:"Energy demand is unrelated to a country's economic structure",B:"Their focus on secondary economic activities (manufacturing) requires large amounts of energy, whereas developed economies are seeing demand level off as manufacturing is increasingly imported",C:"Emerging economies use no energy at all",D:"Developed economies always have the highest energy demand growth"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENE-04",
      scaffold:"Energy demand by economic structure: developing (LIC) countries — primary activities (agriculture), low energy demand; emerging (NEE) countries like India and China — secondary activities (manufacturing), high and rising energy demand; developed (HIC) countries — demand levelling off as more goods are imported rather than manufactured domestically. The USA is a notable exception with very high per-capita consumption (4.25% of world population, 16% of world energy use).",
      reforge:{stem:"Why might a developed country's energy demand from industry decline even as its overall wealth and technology use increase?",options:{A:"As manufacturing is increasingly outsourced to emerging economies, domestic industrial energy demand falls even while consumer energy use (appliances, technology) may rise",B:"Wealth has no relationship whatsoever to energy demand",C:"Developed countries use no energy for consumer technology",D:"Manufacturing energy demand always rises in developed countries"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENE-05",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"Approximately what proportion of the world's primary energy currently comes from fossil fuels?",
      options:{A:"25%",B:"11%",C:"50%",D:"84%"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-ENE-05",
      scaffold:"Global energy mix: fossil fuels 84%, renewables 11%, nuclear 4%. Despite growing renewable investment, the overwhelming majority of global energy still comes from fossil fuels — a key statistic for explaining why 'switching to renewables' is not a quick or simple fix. Around 2.4 billion people still rely on traditional fuel sources for cooking/heating, and roughly 920 million people (13% of the world) have no access to electricity at all.",
      reforge:{stem:"Given that fossil fuels supply 84% of world energy and renewables only 11%, what does this suggest about the pace of the global energy transition?",options:{A:"The energy transition is already complete worldwide",B:"Despite growing investment, renewables would need a very large and sustained increase in capacity to significantly displace fossil fuels' current dominant share",C:"Renewables have already overtaken fossil fuels as the primary global energy source",D:"Fossil fuel use has no bearing on how quickly renewables can expand"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENE-06",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"Which factor caused global oil prices to rise sharply following Russia's invasion of Ukraine?",
      options:{A:"Political conflict disrupting supply and creating uncertainty about future availability",B:"Falling global demand for oil",C:"An international agreement to permanently fix oil prices",D:"A sudden global increase in oil reserves"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-ENE-06",
      scaffold:"Factors affecting oil supply and price: conflict (Russia/Ukraine raised prices due to supply concerns), diplomatic relations (Venezuela's 2010 threat to halt US sales), recession/boom (2008 financial crisis caused prices to crash), over/under-supply (Saudi Arabia increasing supply in 2013-14 to compete with Iran caused prices to fall; the 1979 Iranian Revolution cutting supply caused prices to rise), and customer preference shifts toward renewables reducing demand and therefore price.",
      reforge:{stem:"In 2013-14, Saudi Arabia significantly increased oil production during a rivalry with Iran. What effect did this have on global oil prices, and why?",options:{A:"Prices rose, because increased supply always raises prices",B:"Oil became a renewable resource as a direct result",C:"Prices fell, because the sudden increase in supply outpaced demand",D:"Prices were completely unaffected by the change in supply"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENE-07",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"What is meant by 'unconventional' oil and gas sources such as tar sands and shale gas?",
      options:{A:"They can only be found in the Arctic region",B:"They are extracted using methods other than traditional drilling/oil wells — for example, treating bitumen-rich sand or fracturing shale rock at high pressure",C:"They are sources that have never been used for energy production",D:"They are extracted using exactly the same method as conventional oil wells"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENE-07",
      scaffold:"Tar sands (e.g. Athabasca, Canada) are bitumen-rich reservoirs treated to extract heavy crude oil, using huge quantities of water — almost 60% of Canada's oil production comes from tar sands. Shale gas is extracted via hydraulic fracturing ('fracking'): sand, chemicals and water pumped at high pressure to fracture rock and release trapped gas — over 40% of US dry natural gas is shale gas. Both avoid traditional oil wells/platforms/drilling.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which environmental cost is specifically associated with fracking for shale gas, rather than tar sands extraction?",options:{A:"Decline of caribou, lynx and wolverine populations from surface habitat destruction",B:"Higher rates of air pollution linked to bitumen processing",C:"Small earthquakes and contamination of groundwater from the high-pressure injection process",D:"Higher greenhouse gas emissions than conventional oil extraction"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENE-08",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"Why is exploration for oil and gas in the Arctic increasing, despite the high costs involved?",
      options:{A:"Technological improvements have made extraction in cold/deep-sea areas easier, oil and gas company profits are high enough to fund the investment, and melting permafrost has increased accessibility",B:"Arctic extraction has become impossible due to permafrost melting",C:"The Arctic has no oil or gas reserves worth extracting",D:"There is no economic incentive to explore the Arctic region"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-ENE-08",
      scaffold:"The Arctic supplies 10% of world oil and 25% of world gas, holding an estimated 25-30% of remaining reserves. Drivers of increased exploration: high oil/gas company profits fund expensive exploration, better technology (finding reserves faster, drilling in previously inaccessible cold/deep areas, liquefying gas for transport), and ironically, melting permafrost from global warming making reserves more accessible. Costs include oil spill risk, damage to fisheries and indigenous livelihoods, and infrastructure-driven permafrost melt.",
      reforge:{stem:"How can Arctic oil and gas exploration create a feedback loop with climate change?",options:{A:"Extracting oil and gas from the Arctic actively cools the region",B:"Permafrost melt makes extraction completely impossible, halting all exploration",C:"Arctic exploration has no relationship whatsoever to climate change",D:"Melting permafrost (caused partly by global warming) makes reserves more accessible, encouraging further extraction and fossil fuel use, which contributes to further warming"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENE-09",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"What does it mean for a country to have an 'energy gap', and how does this relate to energy security?",
      options:{A:"An energy gap means a country produces far more energy than it needs",B:"An energy gap occurs when a country cannot meet its own energy demand from domestic resources, forcing it to import energy and making it less energy secure",C:"Energy gaps have no relationship to energy security",D:"Only developing countries can have an energy gap"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENE-09",
      scaffold:"Energy security requires an uninterrupted, affordable and accessible supply of energy. A country with an energy gap must import energy to make up the shortfall, making it dependent on other countries or a single source — reducing its security. Diversification (using a range of energy sources, including renewables and recyclables, rather than relying heavily on one) reduces this vulnerability.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why does relying heavily on a single imported energy source make a country less energy secure than diversifying its energy mix?",options:{A:"Relying on one source always guarantees a cheaper, more reliable supply",B:"Energy security depends only on a country's population size",C:"A disruption to that single source or supplier (e.g. conflict, political dispute) leaves the country with no alternative, whereas a diverse mix spreads the risk across multiple sources",D:"Diversification has no effect on a country's vulnerability to supply disruption"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENE-10",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"According to the examiner's tip in the SaveMyExams guide, why is 'just switching to renewables' not a simple solution to reducing fossil fuel reliance?",
      options:{A:"Renewable energy is often still more expensive and less efficient than fossil fuels, meaning far more capacity is needed to produce the same amount of electricity",B:"Renewable energy is always cheaper and more efficient than fossil fuels in every situation",C:"There is no cost or efficiency difference between renewable and fossil fuel energy",D:"Renewable energy produces more electricity per unit than fossil fuels in all cases"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-ENE-10",
      scaffold:"A concrete example: it takes 233 wind turbines to produce the same energy as one average coal-fired power station. Renewables also face reliability issues (wind needs the right wind speed, solar needs sunshine) and higher costs, even as those costs fall over time. This is why energy strategy tends to focus on diversification and efficiency rather than a single rapid switch.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why might a government promote both renewable energy investment AND energy efficiency measures (like insulation grants) rather than renewables alone?",options:{A:"Efficiency measures have no effect on total energy demand",B:"Governments only ever fund one energy strategy at a time",C:"Energy efficiency measures make renewable energy completely unnecessary",D:"Because renewables alone may not yet reliably or affordably replace all fossil fuel capacity, so reducing overall demand through efficiency also helps close the energy gap"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENE-11",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"What is meant by a person's 'ecological footprint', and why is it typically much higher in developed countries?",
      options:{A:"Ecological footprint measures only a person's carbon dioxide emissions",B:"It measures a person's or group's use of natural resources (in global hectares); developed countries consume more resources per person due to higher wealth and consumption",C:"Ecological footprints are always lower in developed countries than developing ones",D:"Ecological footprints are identical everywhere regardless of a country's wealth"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENE-11",
      scaffold:"Ecological footprint (measured in global hectares, gha) captures total natural resource use, not just carbon: USA averages 15.5 gha per person versus Haiti's 0.7 gha. Carbon footprint specifically (measured in tonnes CO2e) shows a similar gap: global average 7 tonnes, USA 21 tonnes, Malawi just 0.2 tonnes. Both metrics rise with wealth, driven by more cars, more imported/food-mile-heavy goods, and more energy-using technology.",
      reforge:{stem:"The USA's average carbon footprint (21 tonnes CO2e) is roughly 100 times Malawi's (0.2 tonnes CO2e). What is the main underlying cause of this gap?",options:{A:"Carbon footprint is unrelated to a country's wealth or consumption levels",B:"The USA has a smaller population than Malawi, explaining the difference",C:"Much greater wealth in the USA drives far higher consumption of cars, imported goods, technology and energy-intensive services",D:"Malawi has access to more advanced technology than the USA"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENE-12",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"Why might increasing wealth in a country eventually lead to a REDUCTION in its carbon footprint, despite wealth usually increasing consumption?",
      options:{A:"Once basic needs are met, wealthier, better-educated populations increasingly prioritise environmental concerns and can afford energy-efficient technology, insulation, and electric/hybrid vehicles",B:"Poorer countries always have lower carbon footprints regardless of any other factor",C:"Carbon footprint has no relationship to a country's environmental policy",D:"Wealth always increases carbon footprint with no possible reduction"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-ENE-12",
      scaffold:"This nuance is often missed: rising wealth first increases consumption (more cars, food miles, technology) but can eventually reduce carbon footprint per unit of activity, because wealthier populations can afford efficient appliances, home insulation, solar panels/heat pumps, and electric/hybrid cars, and because education and pressure groups (Friends of the Earth, Greenpeace) raise environmental awareness once basic needs like food and shelter are already secured.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which group is most likely to prioritise 'business as usual' (continued fossil fuel reliance) over rapid decarbonisation, according to typical stakeholder positions on energy?",options:{A:"Climate scientists, over 95% of whom oppose any continued fossil fuel use",B:"Environmental organisations such as Greenpeace and WWF",C:"All stakeholder groups have identical views on energy policy",D:"Oil and gas TNCs, who aim to maximise profits from existing fossil fuel operations even while making some sustainability commitments"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENE-FB-01",spec:"GCSE-ENE",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about classifying energy sources.",
      template:"___ energy sources such as gas, oil and coal are finite and will eventually run out. ___ energy sources such as wind and solar are infinite. ___ energy sources such as biofuel and nuclear can be reused or regrown.",
      blanks:["Non-renewable","Renewable","Recyclable"],
      bank:["Non-renewable","Renewable","Recyclable","Sustainable","Fossil","Clean"],
      tag:"MC-ENE-01",
      scaffold:"Three distinct categories, not two: non-renewable (finite — gas, oil, coal), renewable (infinite — HEP, wave/tidal, wind, solar, geothermal), and recyclable (can be regrown/reused — biofuel, nuclear). Keep 'recyclable' separate from 'renewable' in exam answers."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENE-FB-02",spec:"GCSE-ENE",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about factors affecting access to energy.",
      template:"Physical factors such as ___ determine whether coal, oil and gas have formed. ___ affects the cost of extracting deeply buried resources. The level of a country's ___ affects whether it has the technology for nuclear or geothermal power.",
      blanks:["geology","Accessibility","development"],
      bank:["geology","Accessibility","development","politics","climate","population"],
      tag:"MC-ENE-03",
      scaffold:"Access to energy depends on: physical factors (geology for fossil fuel formation, climate for renewable efficiency), accessibility (depth/location raising extraction cost — e.g. the UK's unmined deep coal), level of development (technology and skilled staff needed for nuclear/geothermal), and politics (investment decisions, conflict disrupting supply)."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENE-FB-03",spec:"GCSE-ENE",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about unconventional oil and gas.",
      template:"___ sands are bitumen-rich reservoirs found in Alberta, Canada, treated to extract heavy crude oil. ___ gas is extracted using hydraulic fracturing, known as ___.",
      blanks:["Tar","Shale","fracking"],
      bank:["Tar","Shale","fracking","Coal","Oil","drilling"],
      tag:"MC-ENE-07",
      scaffold:"Tar sands (Athabasca, Canada) require huge water use to treat bitumen into crude oil. Shale gas is released by fracking — pumping sand, chemicals and water at high pressure to fracture rock. Both are called 'unconventional' because they skip traditional oil wells and drilling rigs."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENE-FB-04",spec:"GCSE-ENE",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about energy security.",
      template:"An energy ___ occurs when a country cannot meet its energy demand from domestic resources and must ___ energy instead, making it less energy ___.",
      blanks:["gap","import","secure"],
      bank:["gap","import","secure","surplus","export","efficient"],
      tag:"MC-ENE-09",
      scaffold:"To be energy secure, a country needs an uninterrupted, affordable, accessible supply. An energy gap forces reliance on imports, and diversification (using a wide range of sources) is the main strategy for reducing this vulnerability."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENE-13",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"A student says 'renewable and recyclable energy mean the same thing.' What's the correct distinction?",
      options:{A:"Renewable and recyclable are interchangeable terms with no meaningful difference",B:"Both terms refer only to fossil fuels",C:"Renewable energy comes from a source that is effectively infinite or replenishes itself very quickly (e.g. wind, solar, hydroelectric); recyclable energy comes from a finite fuel supply that can be regrown or reused, such as biofuel",D:"Recyclable energy is infinite, renewable energy is finite"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-ENE-KT-01",
      scaffold:"This three-way classification is commonly tested: non-renewable (finite — coal, oil, gas), renewable (infinite/rapidly replenished — wind, solar, tidal, wave, hydroelectric, geothermal), and recyclable (a limited resource that can be regrown or reprocessed — biofuel, nuclear). Don't collapse recyclable into renewable in an exam answer.",
      reforge:{stem:"Uranium is a finite resource, yet nuclear energy is not classed as 'non-renewable' in the same way as coal or oil. Why?",options:{A:"Nuclear energy produces no waste of any kind",B:"Uranium is actually an infinite resource, making nuclear fully renewable",C:"Nuclear energy is identical to solar energy in classification",D:"Nuclear material can be processed and partially reused, placing nuclear energy in a distinct 'recyclable' category rather than a simple non-renewable one"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENE-14",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"What is the difference between primary and secondary energy?",
      options:{A:"Primary energy is a natural resource used in its raw form (e.g. coal, gas, sunlight); secondary energy is the product created from primary energy, most commonly electricity",B:"They are the same thing measured in different units",C:"Only renewable sources produce secondary energy",D:"Primary energy is always electricity; secondary energy is always a raw fuel"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-ENE-KT-02",
      scaffold:"Primary energy sources (coal, gas, wind, sunlight) are converted into secondary energy — usually electricity — at a power station or generation site, which is then distributed to homes and businesses via the grid.",
      reforge:{stem:"A coal-fired power station burns coal (a primary energy source) to generate electricity. What is the electricity produced correctly classified as?",options:{A:"Renewable energy, since it comes from a power station",B:"Primary energy, exactly the same as the coal itself",C:"Recyclable energy, since coal can be regrown",D:"Secondary energy — the product created from a primary energy source"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENE-15",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"A country has an energy surplus. What does this mean, and how does it differ from an energy deficit?",
      options:{A:"An energy surplus and energy deficit describe exactly the same situation",B:"An energy surplus means a country generates more energy than its population needs (supply exceeds demand); an energy deficit means it generates less than it needs (demand exceeds supply)",C:"A surplus means a country has run out of energy resources entirely",D:"A deficit means a country produces more energy than it can use"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENE-KT-03",
      scaffold:"A country with a surplus can export energy or build reserves; a country in deficit (an 'energy gap') must import to meet demand, which — as with the earlier energy-gap question — tends to reduce its energy security.",
      reforge:{stem:"A country generates 120 units of energy but its population only needs 100 units. What does this situation describe?",options:{A:"This situation cannot occur in reality",B:"An energy deficit — supply is less than demand",C:"An energy surplus — supply is greater than demand",D:"An energy gap that must be filled through imports"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENE-16",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"How does fracking release natural gas, and what environmental concern is most specifically linked to it?",
      options:{A:"Fracking involves burning coal to release trapped gas",B:"Fracking has no known environmental risks",C:"High-pressure liquids are pumped underground to crack shale rock and release trapped gas; this process has been linked to small earthquakes and the risk of groundwater contamination",D:"Fracking is a method of harnessing wind energy from underground caverns"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-ENE-KT-04",
      scaffold:"Fracking is a specific, named process (not a general term for 'gas extraction') — the fracturing of shale rock with high-pressure fluid injection is what creates the pathways for gas to escape, and it's this same high-pressure injection that has been linked to minor seismic activity and contamination risks.",
      reforge:{stem:"High-pressure liquid is pumped underground to fracture shale rock and release trapped gas. What is this process called, and what risk has it been linked to?",options:{A:"Fracking — linked to small earthquakes and possible groundwater contamination",B:"Geothermal extraction — linked to volcanic eruptions",C:"Tar sands extraction — linked to high water use only",D:"Open-cast mining — linked to surface habitat destruction only"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENE-17",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"How does geothermal energy generate electricity?",
      options:{A:"Solar panels absorb heat from the sun and convert it directly into electricity",B:"It uses the movement of tides to turn turbines",C:"It relies on burning geological deposits of coal and gas",D:"Water is pumped deep underground where it is heated by magma or radioactive rocks; the resulting steam rises and turns turbines to generate electricity"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-ENE-KT-05",
      scaffold:"Geothermal energy depends on accessible underground heat, which is why it's most viable in tectonically active regions (e.g. Iceland, sitting on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge) — don't confuse it with hydroelectric (moving river/dam water) or tidal (moving sea water) energy, which use very different mechanisms.",
      reforge:{stem:"Iceland generates significant geothermal energy due to its position on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. Why does this location make geothermal energy especially viable there?",options:{A:"Geothermal energy relies on tidal movement, unrelated to tectonic activity",B:"Tectonically active regions have accessible underground heat from magma, which can heat water pumped down to generate steam and electricity",C:"Iceland's location has no bearing on geothermal energy production",D:"Iceland's geothermal energy comes from burning underground fossil fuel deposits"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENE-18",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"A student mixes up tidal energy and wave energy. What is the correct distinction?",
      options:{A:"Tidal energy uses turbines on the seabed turned by water moving as tides rise and fall; wave energy uses turbines in floating buoys turned by the up-and-down motion of waves on the sea surface",B:"Tidal and wave energy are two names for exactly the same technology",C:"Wave energy relies on the gravitational pull of the moon, tidal energy does not",D:"Tidal energy is generated on the sea surface; wave energy is generated on the seabed"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-ENE-KT-06",
      scaffold:"Both are marine renewables but exploit different forces: tidal power depends on the predictable rise and fall of tides (driven by gravitational pull), captured by seabed turbines; wave power depends on surface wave motion, captured by floating buoy-based turbines — a useful pairing for a 'compare and contrast' exam question.",
      reforge:{stem:"A turbine on the seabed is turned by water as the tide rises and falls. What type of energy generation is this, and how does it differ from a buoy-mounted turbine turned by surface waves?",options:{A:"Hydroelectric energy — associated only with river dams, not the sea",B:"Geothermal energy — powered by underground heat, not water movement",C:"Wave energy — identical in mechanism to tidal energy",D:"Tidal energy — it relies on the predictable rise and fall of tides, unlike wave energy which relies on surface wave motion captured by floating buoys"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENE-19",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"What does decommissioning a power station involve, and why is it significant for nuclear power specifically?",
      options:{A:"Decommissioning has no associated costs",B:"Decommissioning is the process of closing a power station and safely disposing of its waste; for nuclear plants this is especially costly and can take over a year (often much longer) due to radioactive material",C:"Decommissioning only applies to renewable energy infrastructure like wind farms",D:"Decommissioning means building a new power station from scratch"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-ENE-KT-07",
      scaffold:"Decommissioning costs are a major hidden expense of nuclear power that's easy to overlook when comparing energy sources on 'cost per unit of electricity' alone — safely storing or disposing of radioactive waste can cost millions of pounds and take years, an issue not shared by renewable technologies like wind or solar.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why can safely closing a nuclear power station take significantly longer and cost more than closing a coal-fired power station?",options:{A:"Closing any power station always takes exactly the same time and cost",B:"Coal-fired power stations require more complex decommissioning than nuclear plants",C:"Decommissioning a nuclear plant involves safely disposing of radioactive waste, a costly and lengthy process not required at the same scale for other power stations",D:"Nuclear power stations have no waste to dispose of at all"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENE-20",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"Why is energy security a concern for a country that imports most of its natural gas?",
      options:{A:"Supply can be affected by international prices, political disputes or disruption to transport routes",B:"Imported gas is always cheaper and guaranteed",C:"Energy security means using only renewable electricity",D:"Importing fuel removes all environmental impacts"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-ENE-KT-08",
      scaffold:"Energy security means reliable, affordable access to energy. Dependence on imports can expose a country to price volatility, geopolitical conflict and disruption, so diversifying suppliers and sources can reduce vulnerability.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which strategy would most improve a country's energy security?",options:{A:"Depend on one overseas supplier",B:"Use a diverse mix of domestic and imported sources with storage and reliable infrastructure",C:"Close all power stations before alternatives are available",D:"Increase dependence on a single fuel"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ENE-21",spec:"GCSE-ENE",stem:"Why can renewable energy create a challenge for balancing electricity supply and demand?",
      options:{A:"Wind and solar output varies with weather and daylight, so backup, storage or grid management may be needed",B:"Renewables always produce maximum output at exactly the same time",C:"Renewable sources cannot connect to a national grid",D:"Electricity demand never changes during the day"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-ENE-KT-09",
      scaffold:"Wind and solar are intermittent: output varies with wind speed, daylight and cloud. Storage, interconnectors, flexible generation and demand management can help maintain a reliable grid.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which combination could help manage periods when wind and solar output are low?",options:{A:"Battery or pumped storage, interconnectors and flexible backup generation",B:"Removing all electricity storage",C:"Relying on one wind farm in one location",D:"Using electricity only during daylight hours"},correct:"A"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-GEO-DECISIONS"] = {
  label: "Making Geographical Decisions",
  color: "#0f766e",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEC-01",spec:"GCSE-DEC",stem:"In Paper 3, students are given a resource booklet on a geographical issue and asked to justify which of several options should be chosen. What makes something a 'geographical issue' suitable for this task?",
      options:{A:"It only ever involves a purely economic decision with no environmental or social dimension",B:"It involves a contested decision about the use of land or resources, connects to topics from across the specification, and has no single objectively 'correct' answer",C:"It is a simple factual question with one definitively correct answer",D:"It can only be about a hazard, never about development or resource use"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-DEC-01",
      scaffold:"A geographical issue is a genuine dilemma — e.g. whether to build a road through a rainforest, where to site a new reservoir, or how a coastline should be managed — that draws together physical and human geography and forces a trade-off between competing priorities (environmental, economic, social). There is no 'correct' answer; the exam rewards a well-evidenced, justified choice, not a particular option.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which of these is best described as a geographical issue rather than a simple factual question?",options:{A:"What the definition of GDP per capita is",B:"What the capital city of a named country is",C:"Whether a coastal town should use hard engineering or managed retreat to respond to erosion",D:"What year an earthquake happened"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEC-02",spec:"GCSE-DEC",stem:"A student's answer to the decision-making question only lists advantages of the option they chose and ignores every source that suggests problems with it. Why will this lose marks?",
      options:{A:"Top-level answers must use evidence from across the whole resource booklet, including sources that could support a different option, and explain why the chosen option still outweighs them",B:"The mark scheme only rewards the final decision, not the reasoning",C:"Using only supporting evidence is the correct approach and should gain full marks",D:"Sources that don't support the chosen option should be actively contradicted or dismissed as wrong"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-DEC-02",
      scaffold:"This question is marked using a levels-based mark scheme. The top level requires a balanced, well-evidenced argument that draws on multiple sources across the booklet — including ones that seem to favour a rival option — and explains why, on balance, the chosen option is still the strongest. A one-sided answer that cherry-picks only supportive evidence is capped at a lower level, however confident or detailed it is.",
      reforge:{stem:"What should a student do with a source in the resource booklet that provides evidence AGAINST the option they intend to choose?",options:{A:"Only use sources that agree with the chosen option",B:"State that the source must be factually incorrect",C:"Ignore it completely so the argument looks stronger",D:"Reference it, acknowledge the point it makes, and explain why the advantages of the chosen option still outweigh it"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEC-03",spec:"GCSE-DEC",stem:"Which three categories are commonly used to sort and weigh up evidence about a geographical issue?",
      options:{A:"Primary, secondary and tertiary industry",B:"Environmental, economic and social (sometimes with political factors added) — each option is judged against how it affects each of these dimensions",C:"Push, pull and intervening obstacles",D:"Weathering, erosion and deposition"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-DEC-03",
      scaffold:"Sorting evidence into environmental (impact on ecosystems, pollution, landscape), economic (costs, jobs, investment, growth) and social (quality of life, health, community, displacement) impacts — sometimes alongside political factors like governance or international relations — is the standard method for organising a decision-making answer and comparing options fairly.",
      reforge:{stem:"A source states that a proposed dam will displace 3,000 people from their homes. Which category of impact does this evidence belong to?",options:{A:"Purely economic, with no other dimension",B:"It cannot be categorised",C:"Environmental",D:"Social"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEC-04",spec:"GCSE-DEC",stem:"A student's decision-making answer states 'Option B is best because it is cheaper.' Why is this an incomplete justification?",
      options:{A:"It gives only one reason and doesn't reference specific evidence from the resource booklet or weigh it against the other factors and sources available",B:"It should instead focus only on environmental factors and ignore cost entirely",C:"Cost is never a valid factor in a geographical decision",D:"The answer is already sufficient for full marks"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-DEC-04",
      scaffold:"'Justify' requires more than stating a reason — it requires specific evidence (e.g. 'Source C shows Option B costs £2 million less than Option A') integrated into a wider argument that weighs multiple factors against each other, not just the single cheapest one. A strong answer explains why cost matters more than the trade-offs it involves in this particular case.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which version of a justification is strongest for a Making Geographical Decisions answer?",options:{A:"'I prefer Option B'",B:"'Option B is best because it is cheaper'",C:"'Source C shows Option B costs £2 million less than Option A, and although Source E shows it creates fewer jobs, the cost saving allows more to be invested in flood defences elsewhere, making it the stronger overall choice'",D:"'Option A and Option B are both fine'"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEC-05",spec:"GCSE-DEC",stem:"Why does a strong decision-making answer often consider both short-term and long-term impacts of an option?",
      options:{A:"Time frame has no bearing on how strong a decision is",B:"Only short-term impacts are ever relevant to a geographical decision",C:"Long-term impacts cannot be predicted, so they should never be mentioned",D:"An option can look favourable in the short term (e.g. quick job creation) but cause problems long-term (e.g. environmental degradation), or vice versa, and examiners reward answers that weigh both timeframes"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-DEC-05",
      scaffold:"A classic pattern in these resource booklets: an option like slash-and-burn farming or unrestricted logging may bring fast short-term economic benefit but undermine long-term sustainability, while an option like sustainable forestry or managed retreat may cost more upfront but protect a resource or community for longer. Recognising this tension across timeframes is a marker of top-level analysis.",
      reforge:{stem:"An option provides 500 jobs immediately but a source suggests the resource it exploits will be exhausted within 10 years. What does a strong answer do with this evidence?",options:{A:"Only mentions the immediate job creation",B:"Weighs the short-term economic benefit against the long-term sustainability concern raised by the source, rather than only mentioning one",C:"Assumes the resource will last forever regardless of the source",D:"Ignores the timeframe entirely"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEC-06",spec:"GCSE-DEC",stem:"What does it mean to consider 'scale' when evaluating a geographical decision?",
      options:{A:"Scale is irrelevant to justifying a geographical decision",B:"Scale only refers to the size of a map used in the resource booklet",C:"Recognising that an option's impact may differ at local, regional, national or global scale — e.g. benefiting a national economy while harming a local community",D:"Scale means whether the decision affects one person or many people, with no other meaning"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-DEC-06",
      scaffold:"A proposed airport expansion, for instance, might bring national economic benefits (more trade, more jobs across the country) while creating local costs (noise, displacement, local air pollution) for residents nearby. Strong answers explicitly name which scale each piece of evidence operates at, since a decision that's positive nationally can still be negative locally, and both matter to a full evaluation.",
      reforge:{stem:"A source shows a new mine will boost national GDP but increase water pollution in the immediate local river. What should a strong answer do with this?",options:{A:"Explicitly compare the national-scale economic benefit against the local-scale environmental cost, rather than treating the decision as good or bad overall without distinguishing scale",B:"Ignore scale since it isn't relevant to the mark scheme",C:"Only discuss the national benefit since GDP is more important",D:"Assume local and national impacts are always the same"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEC-07",spec:"GCSE-DEC",stem:"Why might different stakeholders (e.g. a local government, an environmental NGO, and a mining company) interpret the same resource booklet evidence differently?",
      options:{A:"Stakeholders always agree on the correct interpretation of geographical evidence",B:"Only government stakeholders' opinions are valid in a geographical decision",C:"Each stakeholder has different priorities and interests, so the same evidence can be weighed differently depending on whose perspective is being considered",D:"Evidence has only one possible interpretation regardless of who is reading it"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-DEC-07",
      scaffold:"This is exactly why 'there is no single correct answer' — a mining company will prioritise the economic evidence (jobs, profit, GDP growth), an environmental NGO will prioritise ecological evidence (habitat loss, pollution, biodiversity), and a local government may prioritise social evidence (housing, services, community impact). A sophisticated answer can acknowledge multiple viewpoints even while reaching its own justified conclusion.",
      reforge:{stem:"A geography student's essay considers how an environmental NGO and a logging company might each view the same deforestation evidence differently before reaching a personal conclusion. What skill is this demonstrating?",options:{A:"Evaluating evidence from multiple stakeholder perspectives before justifying a final decision",B:"Simply repeating the evidence without any interpretation",C:"Choosing a stakeholder at random and ignoring the others",D:"Avoiding a final decision altogether"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEC-08",spec:"GCSE-DEC",stem:"What is the key difference between the command words 'describe', 'explain' and 'justify' as used in Making Geographical Decisions questions?",
      options:{A:"All three command words require exactly the same type of answer",B:"'Describe' states what the evidence shows; 'explain' gives reasons why something happens; 'justify' requires a decision to be defended using evidence and reasoning to show why it is the strongest choice",C:"'Justify' only requires a one-word answer with no supporting evidence",D:"'Describe' requires more evidence than 'justify'"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-DEC-08",
      scaffold:"Command words set the assessment objective being tested. 'Describe' = state features/patterns from the evidence (AO1/AO3). 'Explain' = give reasons or causes (AO1/AO2). 'Justify' = make and defend a choice, weighing evidence for and against, reaching a supported conclusion (AO3/AO4) — the command word used in the final, highest-mark decision-making question.",
      reforge:{stem:"A question asks students to 'justify' which flood management option should be used. What must the answer include that a 'describe' question would not require?",options:{A:"A list of facts with no argument or decision",B:"Simply naming all three options with no further comment",C:"A description of the river's long profile",D:"A clear decision that is defended using weighed evidence, not just a description of what each option involves"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEC-09",spec:"GCSE-DEC",stem:"What does the concept of 'sustainability' typically require a decision to balance?",
      options:{A:"Maximising short-term profit regardless of future consequences",B:"Meeting present needs (economic, social, environmental) without compromising the ability to meet those needs in the future",C:"A decision that only considers the next twelve months",D:"Environmental protection only, with no consideration of economic or social factors"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-DEC-09",
      scaffold:"Sustainability sits at the intersection of environmental, economic and social factors — often visualised as three overlapping circles, with a truly sustainable option sitting where all three overlap. Resource booklet options are often deliberately designed so that no single choice is perfectly sustainable, forcing students to justify which trade-off is most acceptable.",
      reforge:{stem:"An option scores well on economic and social measures in a resource booklet, but a source shows it will damage a protected wetland. What must a strong answer do?",options:{A:"State that environmental damage is never relevant to sustainability",B:"Assume the option is automatically unsustainable and cannot be chosen",C:"Ignore the environmental source since two other categories score well",D:"Acknowledge the environmental cost from the source and directly compare it against the stated economic and social benefits before reaching a justified decision"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEC-10",spec:"GCSE-DEC",stem:"Why should a decision-making answer reference specific sources by name (e.g. 'Source B shows...') rather than making general, unreferenced claims?",
      options:{A:"Referencing specific sources shows the answer is grounded in the evidence provided, rather than relying on unsupported opinion or outside knowledge that isn't in the booklet",B:"Sources should only be referenced in the introduction, never in the main argument",C:"Referencing sources is optional and has no effect on the mark awarded",D:"General claims with no source reference always earn higher marks"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-DEC-10",
      scaffold:"The whole point of the resource-booklet-based question is to test whether a student can select, interpret and apply the evidence they've been given — not just write generically about the topic from memory. Explicitly citing sources (Figure 3, Source D, the table in Source A) is what distinguishes a top-level, evidence-based justification from a generic, unsupported essay.",
      reforge:{stem:"Two students write about the same geographical issue. One writes 'building the reservoir would harm the environment.' The other writes 'Source D shows the proposed reservoir would flood 40 hectares of ancient woodland, directly reducing local biodiversity.' Why does the second answer score more highly?",options:{A:"It is shorter than the first answer",B:"It avoids naming any source material",C:"It uses specific, referenced evidence from the resource booklet rather than a vague, unsupported general statement",D:"Both answers are equally strong"},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEC-11",spec:"GCSE-DEC",stem:"A student spends their entire answer describing what each option involves, then writes 'Overall, I think Option A is best' with no further explanation in the final line. What is missing?",
      options:{A:"The final line should introduce entirely new evidence not discussed anywhere else in the answer",B:"Nothing — describing each option in detail is sufficient for full marks",C:"A justified conclusion that explains, using evidence already discussed, why Option A outweighs the alternatives — a decision needs reasoning attached, not just a stated preference",D:"The student needs to describe the options in even more detail instead of reaching a conclusion"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-DEC-11",
      scaffold:"A common mid-level answer describes all the options accurately but fails to build an argument towards a conclusion, or states a preference without justifying it. Top-level answers integrate evaluation throughout the answer (weighing each source against the others as they go) so the final decision reads as the logical outcome of the evidence already discussed, not an unexplained final-line opinion.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which approach to structuring a Making Geographical Decisions answer is most likely to reach the top mark band?",options:{A:"Only describing the option that will eventually be chosen",B:"Weighing and evaluating evidence for each option throughout the answer, so the final justified decision follows logically from the argument already built",C:"Describing each option in full with no evaluation, then stating a preference in the last sentence",D:"Listing every source without connecting them to any argument"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEC-12",spec:"GCSE-DEC",stem:"Why might a resource booklet deliberately include sources that conflict with each other, such as one report claiming a scheme will create jobs and another claiming it will cause net job losses?",
      options:{A:"Conflicting evidence is realistic and tests whether a student can weigh contradictory sources, consider their reliability or bias, and still reach a reasoned decision",B:"Conflicting sources indicate an error in the exam paper that should be ignored",C:"Students should only ever use the source that supports their chosen option",D:"Contradictory evidence means no decision can be justified"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-DEC-12",
      scaffold:"Real geographical issues rarely have clean, one-sided evidence — a mining company's own report predicting job creation may be less impartial than an independent economic study predicting losses. Evaluating the source itself (who produced it, what interest do they have, how was the data collected) alongside its content is a mark of the highest-level answers, and shows the skill of critically evaluating evidence, not just summarising it.",
      reforge:{stem:"A resource booklet includes a report commissioned by the company proposing a new development, claiming it will benefit the local economy. What should a strong answer do with this source?",options:{A:"Treat the source as entirely objective with no need for scrutiny",B:"Assume all sources in a resource booklet are equally unbiased regardless of who produced them",C:"Ignore the source completely because it comes from a company",D:"Use the evidence but note the company has a vested interest in a positive outcome, which may affect the report's reliability"},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEC-13",spec:"GCSE-DEC",stem:"Why should a student rank the criteria before making a final geographical decision?",
      options:{A:"Ranking criteria helps show which impacts matter most and makes the final choice consistent with the evidence",B:"It means only the cheapest option can be selected",C:"It removes the need to use sources",D:"It guarantees every stakeholder will agree"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-DEC-13",
      scaffold:"Ranking criteria makes priorities explicit. A decision may value environmental protection, jobs, cost, risk or fairness differently, but the answer should explain those priorities and apply them consistently to the evidence.",
      reforge:{stem:"A student selects an option with lower environmental risk but higher cost. What should they explain?",options:{A:"Why environmental risk was given greater weight than the additional cost, using evidence from the booklet",B:"That cost is never relevant to geography",C:"That the cheapest option must always be best",D:"Nothing beyond naming the chosen option"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEC-14",spec:"GCSE-DEC",stem:"What is meant by a trade-off in a geographical decision?",
      options:{A:"Choosing an option that produces benefits in one area but involves a cost or disadvantage elsewhere",B:"Finding an option with no disadvantages at all",C:"Repeating the same evidence without evaluating it",D:"Ignoring social impacts in favour of economic growth"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-DEC-14",
      scaffold:"A trade-off occurs when improving one outcome requires accepting a cost elsewhere, such as jobs versus habitat loss or flood protection versus landscape change. Strong answers identify the trade-off and justify why one side should carry more weight in the particular case.",
      reforge:{stem:"A new airport creates jobs but increases noise for nearby residents. What is this an example of?",options:{A:"A trade-off between economic benefit and social cost",B:"A decision with only positive impacts",C:"Evidence that noise has no geographical importance",D:"A physical process rather than a human decision"},correct:"A"}
    }
    ,{
      id:"GCSE-DEC-FB-01",spec:"GCSE-DEC",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about geographical issues.",
      template:"A geographical issue involves a contested ___ about land or resource use, with no single ___ answer — geographers must weigh competing ___, economic and social priorities.",
      blanks:["decision","correct","environmental"],
      bank:["decision","correct","environmental","random","factual","historical"],
      tag:"MC-DEC-01",
      scaffold:"Geographical issues are genuine dilemmas that connect multiple topics and force a trade-off between environmental, economic and social priorities — there's no objectively right answer, only a better or worse justified one."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEC-FB-02",spec:"GCSE-DEC",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about using evidence in a justified decision.",
      template:"A top-level answer references specific ___ from the resource booklet, considers evidence both ___ and against the chosen option, and explains why the ___ outweigh the drawbacks.",
      blanks:["sources","for","advantages"],
      bank:["sources","for","advantages","opinions","against","disadvantages"],
      tag:"MC-DEC-02",
      scaffold:"A one-sided answer that only lists supporting evidence is capped at a lower mark band — top answers acknowledge counter-evidence and explain why, on balance, their chosen option still wins."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEC-FB-03",spec:"GCSE-DEC",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about categorising evidence.",
      template:"Evidence in a resource booklet is often sorted into ___, economic and ___ categories, sometimes alongside ___ factors, to help compare how each option performs.",
      blanks:["environmental","social","political"],
      bank:["environmental","social","political","physical","historical","biological"],
      tag:"MC-DEC-03",
      scaffold:"Sorting evidence into environmental, economic and social (plus sometimes political) categories is the standard method for comparing options fairly across every relevant dimension of impact."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEC-FB-04",spec:"GCSE-DEC",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about command words.",
      template:"'Describe' asks what the evidence shows; 'explain' asks for ___; 'justify' asks for a ___ to be defended using ___ evidence, weighed for and against.",
      blanks:["reasons","decision","referenced"],
      bank:["reasons","decision","referenced","opinions","guesses","random"],
      tag:"MC-DEC-08",
      scaffold:"'Justify' is the command word used for the final, highest-mark decision-making question — it demands a defended choice built from weighed, referenced evidence, not just a stated preference."
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-DEC-FB-05",spec:"GCSE-DEC",type:"fill_blank",
      stem:"Complete the sentence about evaluating sources.",
      template:"A strong answer considers who ___ a source and what ___ they might have, since this can affect the source's ___, alongside weighing what the evidence itself shows.",
      blanks:["produced","interest","reliability"],
      bank:["produced","interest","reliability","read","budget","length"],
      tag:"MC-DEC-12",
      scaffold:"Evaluating who produced a source and their possible bias (e.g. a company's own report on its own proposed development) is a top-level skill — it goes beyond just summarising what evidence says to critically assessing how much weight it should carry."
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-ECON-UK"] = {
  label: "Paper 2: The UK Economy Overview",
  color: "#166534",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"GCSE-ECON-01",spec:"GCSE-ECON",stem:"Which of the following is NOT one of the UK government's four main macroeconomic objectives?",
      options:{A:"Low and stable inflation",B:"Low unemployment",C:"Maximising the exchange rate at all times",D:"Balance of payments equilibrium"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-MACRO-01",
      scaffold:"The four main objectives are: economic growth, low unemployment, low and stable inflation, and balance of payments equilibrium. The exchange rate isn't a fixed target the government tries to maximise — a very strong pound hurts exporters, a very weak pound raises import costs, so governments generally want stability, not a 'maximum' rate.",
      reforge:{stem:"A government proudly announces the pound has reached its highest value against the dollar in a decade. Why might this NOT be entirely good news?",options:{A:"A stronger pound makes UK exports more expensive for foreign buyers, which can hurt export-dependent businesses and jobs",B:"A stronger pound always benefits every UK business equally",C:"Exchange rates have no effect on the balance of payments",D:"A strong pound automatically causes inflation to rise"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ECON-02",spec:"GCSE-ECON",stem:"UK CPI inflation falls from 6% to 3%. A student says: 'Prices are now falling.' Why is this wrong?",
      options:{A:"Prices are still rising — just more slowly. A lower positive inflation rate means the price level keeps increasing, only at a reduced pace",B:"The student is correct — a lower inflation figure always means prices have fallen",C:"CPI only measures wages, not prices, so the statement doesn't apply",D:"Inflation falling to 3% means prices have fallen by exactly 3%"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-INF-01",
      scaffold:"Inflation is the rate at which the price level is rising. As long as the inflation rate is positive, prices are still going up — 3% inflation means prices rose 3% over the year, not that they fell. Only 0% inflation (stable prices) or negative inflation (deflation) means prices themselves are falling.",
      reforge:{stem:"Inflation falls from 3% to 0.5% but stays positive. What is happening to the UK price level?",options:{A:"It continues to rise, just at a much slower rate than before",B:"It is falling",C:"It is completely unchanged",D:"It has fallen to zero"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ECON-03",spec:"GCSE-ECON",stem:"A worker is made redundant when a UK car factory closes because demand has permanently shifted towards electric vehicles made abroad. What type of unemployment is this?",
      options:{A:"Frictional unemployment — short-term, between jobs",B:"Structural unemployment — caused by a long-term decline in an industry as demand permanently shifts away from it",C:"Seasonal unemployment — tied to a particular time of year",D:"The worker isn't unemployed, they are economically inactive"},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-UNEMP-01",
      scaffold:"Structural unemployment happens when an industry declines permanently, often due to changing demand or technology, leaving workers' skills mismatched with available jobs. Frictional unemployment is brief, between-jobs search; seasonal unemployment is tied to the calendar (e.g. ski instructors in summer) — neither fits a permanent industry-wide shift like this one.",
      reforge:{stem:"A graduate spends three weeks applying for jobs before accepting a suitable offer. What type of unemployment does this best illustrate?",options:{A:"Frictional unemployment — short-term search between entering the job market and finding a role",B:"Structural unemployment",C:"Cyclical unemployment caused by a recession",D:"Seasonal unemployment"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ECON-04",spec:"GCSE-ECON",stem:"The government cuts income tax and increases spending on the NHS during an economic slowdown. What type of fiscal policy is this, and what is its main aim?",
      options:{A:"Expansionary fiscal policy — increasing aggregate demand to boost growth and reduce unemployment",B:"Contractionary fiscal policy — reducing aggregate demand to control inflation",C:"Monetary policy — using interest rates to influence spending",D:"Supply-side policy — improving the economy's long-run productive capacity"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-FISC-01",
      scaffold:"Cutting taxes and raising government spending both increase aggregate demand — this is expansionary fiscal policy, typically used to boost growth and employment during a slowdown. Contractionary fiscal policy does the opposite (raising taxes, cutting spending) to cool an overheating economy. Fiscal policy is spending/taxation, decided by the government — not interest rates, which is monetary policy set by the Bank of England.",
      reforge:{stem:"To bring down high inflation, the government raises income tax and cuts public spending. What type of fiscal policy is this?",options:{A:"Expansionary fiscal policy",B:"Contractionary fiscal policy — reducing aggregate demand to help control inflation",C:"Monetary policy",D:"Trade policy"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ECON-05",spec:"GCSE-ECON",stem:"The Bank of England raises the base interest rate. A student says: 'This will encourage people to spend more.' Why is this wrong?",
      options:{A:"Higher rates make borrowing dearer and saving more attractive — spending falls, not rises.",B:"The student is correct — higher interest rates always increase spending",C:"Interest rates only affect businesses, never consumers",D:"Interest rates have no effect on borrowing or saving decisions"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-MON-GCSE-01",
      scaffold:"Raising interest rates makes loans, mortgages and credit card debt more expensive, while making saving more rewarding — both effects tend to reduce spending. This is exactly why the Bank of England raises rates when it wants to bring down inflation: less spending means less pressure on prices.",
      reforge:{stem:"The Bank of England cuts the base interest rate to boost a slowing economy. What effect is this most likely to have on consumer spending?",options:{A:"It is likely to increase spending, since borrowing becomes cheaper and saving less rewarding",B:"It is likely to decrease spending",C:"Interest rate changes never affect consumer behaviour",D:"It will have no effect because banks ignore the base rate"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ECON-06",spec:"GCSE-ECON",stem:"A news headline says: 'UK government budget deficit widens.' A student says: 'This means the UK is importing more than it is exporting.' Why is this wrong?",
      options:{A:"The budget deficit and balance of payments measure different things.",B:"The student is correct — the two terms mean exactly the same thing",C:"A budget deficit can only be caused by a trade deficit",D:"The UK cannot have a budget deficit unless it also has a trade deficit"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-BOP-GCSE-01",
      scaffold:"These are easily confused but distinct: the government budget deficit is the gap between what government spends and what it raises in tax; the balance of payments (current account) measures the value of a country's exports against its imports. A country can run either deficit independently of the other.",
      reforge:{stem:"The UK's current account deficit widens because imports have risen faster than exports. Does this necessarily mean the government's budget deficit has also widened?",options:{A:"No — the two are separate measures; a current account deficit doesn't automatically mean the government is also overspending relative to tax revenue",B:"Yes, they always move together",C:"Yes, because imports are funded directly out of the government budget",D:"No such distinction exists between the two terms"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ECON-07",spec:"GCSE-ECON",stem:"The pound depreciates (falls in value) against the dollar. A student says: 'This is bad news for everyone in the UK.' Why is this an oversimplification?",
      options:{A:"A weaker pound benefits exporters but raises import costs for consumers.",B:"A weaker pound only ever benefits UK consumers",C:"Exchange rate changes have no effect on exporters or importers",D:"A weaker pound makes both exports and imports cheaper"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-EXR-01",
      scaffold:"Depreciation is a mixed picture: UK exporters benefit because their goods become cheaper for foreign buyers, but UK consumers and firms that rely on imported goods or raw materials face higher costs. Whether depreciation is 'good' or 'bad' for the UK overall depends on which groups you're asking about.",
      reforge:{stem:"The pound appreciates (rises in value) against the euro. Which UK group is most likely to benefit?",options:{A:"UK consumers buying imported goods, and UK tourists travelling to the eurozone, since their money now buys more",B:"UK exporters, since their goods become more competitive abroad",C:"Everyone in the UK equally, with no losers",D:"No one, since appreciation only ever causes harm"},correct:"A"}
    },
    {
      id:"GCSE-ECON-08",spec:"GCSE-ECON",stem:"The government imposes a tariff on imported steel to protect UK steel producers. A student says: 'This benefits everyone in the UK.' Why is this wrong?",
      options:{A:"Tariffs protect UK steel jobs but raise costs for other UK businesses and consumers.",B:"Tariffs benefit every group in the economy with no downside",C:"Tariffs only affect foreign countries, never UK consumers or businesses",D:"Tariffs automatically lower prices for UK consumers"},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-TRD-01",
      scaffold:"Protectionist measures like tariffs shield a specific domestic industry from foreign competition, which can save jobs in that industry — but the higher price of the protected good raises costs for UK businesses that use it as an input, and often for consumers too. It's a trade-off between groups, not a free win for everyone.",
      reforge:{stem:"A government scraps tariffs on imported steel as part of a new free trade agreement. Which UK group is most likely to lose out in the short term?",options:{A:"UK steel producers, who now face cheaper foreign competition",B:"UK car manufacturers, who now face higher costs",C:"UK consumers, who now face higher prices for steel-based goods",D:"No one loses out from removing tariffs"},correct:"A"}
    }
  ]
};

// ===== GCSE ECONOMICS — OCR J205 PAPER 2 =====

BANKS["GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL"] = {
  label: "Paper 2: The National Economy",
  color: "#166534",
  questions: [
    {id:"GCSE-P2-NAT-01",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL",stem:"Which is a macroeconomic objective of the UK government?",options:{A:"Keeping every firm profitable",B:"Maintaining low and stable inflation",C:"Maximising imports",D:"Fixing every wage at the same level"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-NAT-01",scaffold:"Macroeconomic objectives concern the performance of the whole economy, including growth, low unemployment, price stability and a sustainable balance of payments. They can conflict, so governments face trade-offs.",reforge:{stem:"Which outcome would generally be considered a national economic policy aim?",options:{A:"Falling productivity",B:"Persistent high unemployment",C:"Stable prices and sustainable economic growth",D:"A permanent trade deficit"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-NAT-02",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL",stem:"Why might the government remove VAT from a product such as a cycle helmet?",options:{A:"It makes supply perfectly inelastic",B:"It may be a merit good with wider benefits when consumed",C:"It is always a luxury good",D:"The policy guarantees a budget surplus"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-NAT-02",scaffold:"Merit goods create benefits for other people as well as the consumer. Reducing tax lowers the price and may encourage consumption, such as safer cycling or improved education, although the policy has an opportunity cost.",reforge:{stem:"A government subsidises school vaccination programmes because:",options:{A:"They always reduce government spending immediately",B:"They are examples of demerit goods",C:"They create positive external benefits beyond the individual receiving the vaccine",D:"They are supplied only by overseas firms"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-NAT-03",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL",stem:"Which tax system takes a larger percentage of income from higher earners?",options:{A:"A poll tax",B:"A proportional tax with one fixed amount",C:"Progressive taxation",D:"Regressive taxation"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-NAT-03",scaffold:"Under a progressive system, the average tax rate rises as income rises. This can reduce post-tax inequality and raise revenue, but higher rates may affect incentives depending on the circumstances.",reforge:{stem:"If a household on a low income pays 8% of its income in a tax while a high-income household pays 2%, the tax is best described as:",options:{A:"Regressive",B:"Deflationary",C:"Proportional",D:"Progressive"},correct:"D"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-NAT-04",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL",stem:"Which area is most likely to be included in government expenditure?",options:{A:"Public healthcare",B:"A firm's retained profit",C:"A consumer's private saving",D:"Household pocket money"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-NAT-04",scaffold:"Government spending includes public services such as health, education, transport, defence and social protection. Spending choices have opportunity costs because public funds are limited.",reforge:{stem:"Why might a government increase spending on education?",options:{A:"To improve human capital and potentially raise future productivity",B:"To guarantee that inflation becomes negative",C:"To reduce the size of the labour force",D:"To make all private firms state-owned"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-NAT-05",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL",stem:"A country has a labour force of 30 million and 1.5 million people are unemployed. What is the unemployment rate?",options:{A:"0.05%",B:"5%",C:"20%",D:"31.5%"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-NAT-05",scaffold:"The unemployment rate is unemployed people divided by the labour force, multiplied by 100: 1.5 million / 30 million x 100 = 5%.",reforge:{stem:"If 2 million people are unemployed in a labour force of 40 million, the unemployment rate is:",options:{A:"42%",B:"2%",C:"5%",D:"20%"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-NAT-06",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL",stem:"What does a government budget deficit mean?",options:{A:"Household saving is greater than borrowing",B:"Tax revenue is greater than government spending",C:"Government spending is greater than tax revenue in a period",D:"Exports are greater than imports"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-NAT-06",scaffold:"A budget deficit occurs when government expenditure exceeds its receipts, so the government may need to borrow. It is different from a current account deficit, which concerns international transactions.",reforge:{stem:"A government collects £720 billion in taxes and spends £760 billion. Before interest and other adjustments, it has:",options:{A:"A £1,480 billion trade deficit",B:"Balanced finances",C:"A £40 billion budget surplus",D:"A £40 billion budget deficit"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-NAT-07",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL",stem:"Why might an ageing population increase government spending?",options:{A:"Older people cannot consume public services",B:"Tax revenue must always rise faster than spending",C:"There may be more demand for pensions and healthcare",D:"The labour force automatically doubles"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-NAT-07",scaffold:"An ageing population can increase expenditure on pensions, social care and healthcare. The government may also face a smaller proportion of working-age taxpayers, creating pressure on public finances.",reforge:{stem:"Which demographic change is most likely to put pressure on social protection spending?",options:{A:"A fall in life expectancy",B:"A rise in the number of people in work",C:"A smaller need for healthcare",D:"A larger retired population relative to workers"},correct:"D"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-NAT-08",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-NATIONAL",stem:"Why might governments support a large infrastructure project?",options:{A:"It guarantees no opportunity cost",B:"It always reduces aggregate demand",C:"It can be funded without any resources",D:"It can provide long-term social benefits that private investors may not fully capture"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-NAT-08",scaffold:"Infrastructure such as rail links can create external benefits, connect regions and support productivity, but it may require very large upfront finance and careful cost-benefit evaluation.",reforge:{stem:"A new rail line reduces journey times for many firms and workers. This is one reason public support may be justified because it:",options:{A:"Creates wider benefits not captured in the ticket price alone",B:"Makes all private investment unnecessary",C:"Removes scarcity from the economy",D:"Guarantees every household receives the same income"},correct:"A"}}
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH"] = {
  label: "Paper 2: Economic Growth",
  color: "#15803d",
  questions: [
    {id:"GCSE-P2-GRO-01",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH",stem:"Real GDP rises from £500 billion to £515 billion. What is the approximate economic growth rate?",options:{A:"0.3%",B:"3%",C:"15%",D:"103%"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-GRO-01",scaffold:"Growth rate is the change in real GDP divided by the original GDP, multiplied by 100: £15 billion / £500 billion x 100 = 3%.",reforge:{stem:"Real GDP increases from £800 billion to £824 billion. The growth rate is:",options:{A:"103%",B:"2%",C:"3%",D:"24%"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-GRO-02",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH",stem:"Why is real GDP per capita useful when comparing living standards?",options:{A:"It ignores population changes completely",B:"It relates output to the size of the population and removes some price effects",C:"It measures only government spending",D:"It guarantees equal income distribution"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-GRO-02",scaffold:"Real GDP per capita divides inflation-adjusted output by population. It gives a rough average measure, but it does not show how income is distributed or capture every aspect of wellbeing.",reforge:{stem:"A country’s real GDP rises but its population rises even faster. What may happen to real GDP per capita?",options:{A:"It is unchanged by population",B:"It becomes a measure of inflation",C:"It may fall",D:"It must double"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-GRO-03",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH",stem:"Which description best fits a recession?",options:{A:"A rise in the price level only",B:"A permanent trade surplus",C:"A sustained fall in real GDP, commonly defined as two consecutive quarters",D:"A period of rapid growth and high employment"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-GRO-03",scaffold:"A recession is a period when economic activity contracts. Falling output can reduce business revenues, investment and employment, although definitions and severity can vary.",reforge:{stem:"During an economic boom, which combination is most likely?",options:{A:"Falling output and falling demand",B:"Zero investment and zero inflation",C:"A guaranteed current account surplus",D:"Rising output and strong employment"},correct:"D"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-GRO-04",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH",stem:"Which change is most likely to increase long-run economic growth?",options:{A:"A permanent fall in labour productivity",B:"Less training for workers",C:"Destroying transport infrastructure",D:"Investment in productive technology"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-GRO-04",scaffold:"Investment can increase the capital stock, capacity and productivity of an economy. Education, training, innovation and improved infrastructure can also shift productive potential outward.",reforge:{stem:"A firm installs more efficient machinery and workers receive training. The most likely long-run effect is:",options:{A:"Higher potential output through improved productivity",B:"Lower productive capacity",C:"A guaranteed fall in employment in every sector",D:"No change to the economy’s supply"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-GRO-05",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH",stem:"Which is a possible benefit of economic growth?",options:{A:"Higher average living standards and more employment",B:"Automatic equality of wealth",C:"Guaranteed lower pollution",D:"The end of scarcity"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-GRO-05",scaffold:"Growth can create jobs, raise incomes and provide more resources for public services. Its benefits depend on distribution and whether growth is environmentally sustainable.",reforge:{stem:"Why might economic growth reduce poverty?",options:{A:"It prevents every recession",B:"Higher output can create jobs and incomes, especially if the gains reach lower-income households",C:"Growth makes resources unlimited",D:"It automatically removes all taxes"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-GRO-06",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH",stem:"Why might rapid economic growth damage the environment?",options:{A:"A higher GDP makes pollution impossible",B:"Higher production may increase resource use, emissions and waste",C:"Growth always reduces energy demand",D:"Environmental quality is unrelated to production"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-GRO-06",scaffold:"If growth relies on fossil fuels or intensive resource use, it may create pollution, congestion and climate damage. Sustainable growth seeks to raise living standards without exhausting environmental resources.",reforge:{stem:"What does sustainable economic growth aim to do?",options:{A:"Maximise output regardless of future costs",B:"Keep real GDP permanently unchanged",C:"Increase living standards while limiting long-term environmental damage",D:"Stop all economic activity"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-GRO-07",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH",stem:"A country’s nominal GDP rises by 6% while prices rise by 8%. What is likely to have happened to real GDP?",options:{A:"It was unchanged because nominal GDP rose",B:"It cannot be affected by prices",C:"It fell by approximately 2%",D:"It rose by 14%"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-GRO-07",scaffold:"Nominal GDP is measured at current prices. If prices rise faster than nominal output, the volume of production may have fallen; approximately 6% - 8% = -2% before more precise adjustment.",reforge:{stem:"Why do economists use real rather than nominal GDP to assess changes in output over time?",options:{A:"Nominal GDP includes population",B:"Real GDP measures only exports",C:"Nominal GDP cannot be calculated",D:"Real GDP adjusts for price changes"},correct:"D"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-GRO-08",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-GROWTH",stem:"Why can a rise in GDP be an imperfect measure of wellbeing?",options:{A:"GDP measures only happiness",B:"GDP automatically records unpaid care",C:"Every person receives the same share of GDP",D:"It may hide inequality and excludes some non-market and environmental effects"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-GRO-08",scaffold:"GDP records the market value of output, not how income is shared, unpaid work, leisure, health or environmental quality. GDP per capita is useful but should be interpreted alongside other indicators.",reforge:{stem:"Two countries have the same GDP per capita, but one has much greater inequality and pollution. What does this show?",options:{A:"GDP per capita alone cannot fully compare wellbeing",B:"The countries have identical living standards in every respect",C:"GDP per capita measures pollution directly",D:"Inequality has no economic relevance"},correct:"A"}}
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP"] = {
  label: "Paper 2: Low Unemployment",
  color: "#16a34a",
  questions: [
    {id:"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-01",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP",stem:"A labour force contains 28 million people, of whom 1.4 million are unemployed. What is the unemployment rate?",options:{A:"0.5%",B:"5%",C:"20%",D:"29.4%"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-UNEMP-01",scaffold:"Unemployment rate = unemployed / labour force x 100. Therefore 1.4 / 28 x 100 = 5%.",reforge:{stem:"If 900,000 people are unemployed in a labour force of 30 million, the unemployment rate is:",options:{A:"33.3%",B:"0.3%",C:"3%",D:"30%"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-02",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP",stem:"What is meant by full employment?",options:{A:"Only highly skilled workers have jobs",B:"Every adult works for the government",C:"Most willing and able workers can find jobs, although some frictional unemployment remains",D:"There is no unemployment of any kind"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-UNEMP-02",scaffold:"Full employment does not usually mean zero unemployment. People may be temporarily between jobs or entering the labour market, so some frictional unemployment can remain.",reforge:{stem:"Why can an economy have full employment while its unemployment rate is above zero?",options:{A:"Full employment means firms stop recruiting",B:"Unemployment is counted as employment",C:"Some people are changing jobs or searching for their first suitable job",D:"The unemployment rate excludes all workers"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-03",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP",stem:"A worker loses a job because demand permanently shifts away from the industry. What type of unemployment is most likely?",options:{A:"Seasonal",B:"Voluntary retirement",C:"Structural",D:"Frictional"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-UNEMP-03",scaffold:"Structural unemployment occurs when the pattern of demand or production changes and workers’ skills no longer match available jobs. Retraining and mobility can reduce it.",reforge:{stem:"A ski instructor is unemployed during the summer but expects work next winter. This is:",options:{A:"Structural unemployment",B:"Cyclical unemployment",C:"Technological unemployment only",D:"Seasonal unemployment"},correct:"D"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-04",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP",stem:"What is cyclical unemployment most closely associated with?",options:{A:"Workers moving between jobs",B:"The school holiday calendar only",C:"A shortage of relevant skills in one industry",D:"A downturn reducing demand for goods and labour"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-UNEMP-04",scaffold:"Cyclical unemployment rises during recessions when aggregate demand and firms’ derived demand for labour fall. It may reduce as the economy recovers.",reforge:{stem:"A recession causes restaurants to reduce staff because customers are spending less. This is best described as:",options:{A:"Cyclical unemployment",B:"Frictional unemployment",C:"Seasonal unemployment",D:"Occupational mobility"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-05",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP",stem:"Which is a cost of unemployment to the government?",options:{A:"Higher income tax receipts from the unemployed",B:"More benefit spending and lower tax revenue",C:"An automatic rise in exports",D:"Lower demand for welfare payments"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-UNEMP-05",scaffold:"Unemployment can reduce income tax and national insurance receipts while increasing benefit payments. The government also faces the opportunity cost of using funds to support people out of work.",reforge:{stem:"How can high unemployment affect public finances?",options:{A:"It has no effect on government finances",B:"Tax receipts may fall while spending on benefits rises",C:"Tax receipts must rise because fewer people work",D:"Benefit spending automatically falls to zero"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-06",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP",stem:"Why can job losses damage a local economy?",options:{A:"Local firms are unaffected by household income",B:"Lower household income can reduce spending at local businesses",C:"Unemployment increases local demand in every shop",D:"Job losses automatically attract investment"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-UNEMP-06",scaffold:"Redundancies reduce incomes and spending, which can lower sales for other local firms. Persistent unemployment may also reduce skills, health and investment in the area.",reforge:{stem:"A factory closes and nearby shops lose customers. This illustrates how unemployment can:",options:{A:"Raise productivity immediately",B:"Have effects only on the closed factory",C:"Create a multiplier effect of lower spending in the local economy",D:"Increase local disposable income"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-07",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP",stem:"Which policy could reduce structural unemployment?",options:{A:"Making job information harder to access",B:"Banning labour mobility",C:"Retraining workers for occupations with growing demand",D:"Reducing all education spending"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-UNEMP-07",scaffold:"Retraining and better education can reduce skills mismatch. Relocation support, improved transport and information can also help workers move towards available vacancies.",reforge:{stem:"Why might subsidised adult training reduce unemployment?",options:{A:"It guarantees every firm makes a profit",B:"It reduces the number of available jobs",C:"It increases skills mismatch",D:"It can give displaced workers skills that match vacancies in expanding sectors"},correct:"D"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-UNEMP-08",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-UNEMP",stem:"Which person is economically inactive rather than unemployed?",options:{A:"Someone retired and not looking for paid work",B:"Someone who has applied for three jobs this week",C:"Someone temporarily between jobs and searching",D:"Someone actively seeking work and available to start"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-UNEMP-08",scaffold:"Unemployed people are without work but available for work and actively seeking it. People who are not working and not seeking or available for work may be economically inactive, such as some retirees or full-time carers.",reforge:{stem:"Which person should not normally be included in the unemployment rate?",options:{A:"A student who is not seeking or available for a job",B:"A laid-off worker applying for vacancies",C:"A graduate attending interviews",D:"A worker waiting to start a confirmed job"},correct:"A"}}
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME"] = {
  label: "Paper 2: Fair Distribution of Income",
  color: "#22c55e",
  questions: [
    {id:"GCSE-P2-INCOME-01",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME",stem:"What is the difference between income and wealth?",options:{A:"Income is a flow over time; wealth is the stock of assets owned",B:"Income and wealth are exactly the same",C:"Wealth is only wages; income is only property",D:"Income is always a stock"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-INCOME-01",scaffold:"Income is money received over a period, such as wages or rent. Wealth is the value of accumulated assets minus liabilities, such as property, savings and shares.",reforge:{stem:"Which is an example of wealth rather than income?",options:{A:"Interest received this year",B:"The salary paid this month",C:"A house owned by a household",D:"A weekly benefit payment"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-INCOME-02",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME",stem:"What does the median income represent?",options:{A:"The income of the government",B:"The middle value when incomes are ordered",C:"The highest income in the economy",D:"The total of all incomes"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-INCOME-02",scaffold:"The median is the middle observation in an ordered list. It can represent a typical household better than the mean when a few very high incomes pull the average upwards.",reforge:{stem:"Why might analysts prefer median household income to mean income when describing a typical household?",options:{A:"The median is the highest value",B:"It ignores every low income",C:"A few very high incomes have less influence on the median",D:"The median always includes wealth"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-INCOME-03",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME",stem:"Why might a professional sportsperson earn a very high wage?",options:{A:"Their labour supply is unlimited",B:"Wages are unrelated to demand",C:"Their scarce skills may face high demand from a large market",D:"High wages always reflect low productivity"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-INCOME-03",scaffold:"Wage differentials can reflect skill, training, scarcity, demand for the output, responsibility and bargaining power. High earnings may arise where a worker’s talent is scarce and the market is large.",reforge:{stem:"Which combination is most likely to raise a worker’s wage?",options:{A:"Low demand and many identical workers",B:"No training and no responsibility",C:"A complete absence of employers",D:"High demand for the worker’s output and a scarce skill"},correct:"D"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-INCOME-04",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME",stem:"Which is a possible consequence of a large gap between high and low incomes?",options:{A:"Identical housing for all households",B:"No differences in health",C:"An automatic rise in social mobility",D:"Greater poverty and unequal access to opportunities"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-INCOME-04",scaffold:"Large income differences may be associated with relative poverty, unequal housing, education and healthcare outcomes, and differences in life chances. The size of these effects depends on public services and redistribution.",reforge:{stem:"How can income inequality affect opportunity?",options:{A:"Low-income families may have less access to education, housing or healthcare",B:"It guarantees that all children start with the same resources",C:"It removes differences in living standards",D:"It makes poverty impossible to measure"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-INCOME-05",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME",stem:"What does a progressive income tax do?",options:{A:"Raises the average tax rate as income rises",B:"Charges every household the same cash amount",C:"Takes a higher proportion from low earners",D:"Only taxes wealth, never income"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-INCOME-05",scaffold:"Progressive taxation can redistribute income because higher earners pay a larger proportion. Disposable income becomes more equal, although tax design can affect incentives and administrative costs.",reforge:{stem:"Which policy is most directly redistributive?",options:{A:"Removing public education",B:"Using higher tax rates on higher incomes to fund benefits for lower-income households",C:"Charging the same flat amount to every household",D:"Reducing all welfare payments"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-INCOME-06",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME",stem:"If the richest 20% receive five times the income of the poorest 20%, what does this indicate?",options:{A:"The economy has no poverty",B:"Income is unequally distributed",C:"The two groups have equal incomes",D:"Wealth is measured exactly"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-INCOME-06",scaffold:"Comparing income shares between groups shows distribution and inequality. It does not by itself reveal the absolute living standards, wealth or public services available to each group.",reforge:{stem:"What does an income-share comparison tell an economist?",options:{A:"Only the inflation rate",B:"The number of firms in an industry",C:"How total income is divided between groups",D:"The exact wealth of every household"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-INCOME-07",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME",stem:"Why may wealth inequality be greater than income inequality?",options:{A:"Income cannot be saved",B:"All households own identical assets",C:"Assets can accumulate and generate further returns over time",D:"Wealth is paid only once a week"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-INCOME-07",scaffold:"People who already own property, shares or businesses may receive capital gains and investment income, allowing wealth to compound. Inheritance can also pass assets between generations.",reforge:{stem:"Which mechanism can widen wealth differences between households?",options:{A:"All households receiving identical wages",B:"A fall in every asset price",C:"Removing the ability to inherit assets",D:"House price rises benefiting existing owners more than non-owners"},correct:"D"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-INCOME-08",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-INCOME",stem:"Which is a likely aim of welfare benefits?",options:{A:"To make all workers earn the same wage",B:"To increase absolute poverty",C:"To eliminate the need for taxation",D:"To support household income and reduce hardship"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-INCOME-08",scaffold:"Benefits can provide a minimum income, insure against events such as unemployment and reduce poverty. They have a fiscal cost and may affect incentives depending on their design.",reforge:{stem:"A benefit paid to households below a minimum income is intended primarily to:",options:{A:"Reduce poverty and support basic living standards",B:"Raise the price level directly",C:"Increase the trade deficit",D:"Reward only high-income households"},correct:"A"}}
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE"] = {
  label: "Paper 2: Price Stability",
  color: "#16a34a",
  questions: [
    {id:"GCSE-P2-PRICE-01",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE",stem:"What does a positive inflation rate of 3% mean?",options:{A:"The average price level is rising by about 3% over the period",B:"Every price rises by exactly 3%",C:"Prices are falling by 3%",D:"Real output is rising by 3%"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-PRICE-01",scaffold:"Inflation is the rate of increase in the general price level. A positive rate means prices are still rising, even if some individual prices fall.",reforge:{stem:"Inflation falls from 6% to 2%, but remains positive. What happens to the general price level?",options:{A:"It falls to zero",B:"It continues to rise, but more slowly",C:"It must fall by 4%",D:"It is unchanged"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-PRICE-02",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE",stem:"A basket costs £50 in the base year and £60 in the next year. What is the CPI in the next year?",options:{A:"120",B:"83",C:"100",D:"110"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-PRICE-02",scaffold:"CPI = cost of basket in current period divided by cost in base period x 100: £60 / £50 x 100 = 120. This indicates prices are 20% higher than in the base year.",reforge:{stem:"A representative basket costs £80 in the base year and £88 later. Its CPI later is:",options:{A:"110",B:"180",C:"90",D:"100"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-PRICE-03",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE",stem:"Why are goods given different weights when calculating the CPI?",options:{A:"Weights measure unemployment",B:"Only luxury goods are included",C:"Households spend different proportions of their budgets on them",D:"All goods have identical importance"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-PRICE-03",scaffold:"The CPI uses a representative basket and weights items according to their share of household spending. A change in the price of a frequently purchased necessity can have a larger effect on household budgets.",reforge:{stem:"Why might food receive a larger CPI weight than a rare leisure purchase?",options:{A:"Food prices never change",B:"Leisure goods are excluded from inflation",C:"Weights are based on the producer’s profit",D:"Food usually represents a larger and more regular share of household spending"},correct:"D"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-PRICE-04",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE",stem:"Who is most likely to lose from unexpectedly high inflation?",options:{A:"A borrower whose debt is fixed in nominal terms",B:"A firm that raises its prices with its costs",C:"A worker whose wages rise faster than prices",D:"A person whose cash savings earn interest below inflation"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-PRICE-04",scaffold:"If inflation exceeds the interest rate on savings, the real value and purchasing power of those savings fall. Borrowers may gain if nominal debts become easier to repay in real terms, though lenders lose.",reforge:{stem:"If savings earn 2% interest while inflation is 5%, the real return is approximately:",options:{A:"+7%",B:"+3%",C:"-3%",D:"-7%"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-PRICE-05",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE",stem:"What is demand-pull inflation?",options:{A:"Prices rising because aggregate demand grows faster than productive capacity",B:"Prices falling because demand collapses",C:"Prices rising only because imported materials cost more",D:"A reduction in the money supply"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-PRICE-05",scaffold:"Demand-pull inflation occurs when spending pressure exceeds the economy’s ability to increase output, especially near capacity. Cost-push inflation instead originates from rising production costs.",reforge:{stem:"Households increase spending rapidly while factories are already near full capacity. This may cause:",options:{A:"A fall in aggregate demand",B:"Demand-pull inflation",C:"Deflation caused by excess supply",D:"Structural unemployment only"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-PRICE-06",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE",stem:"What is deflation?",options:{A:"An increase in real GDP",B:"A sustained fall in the general price level",C:"A slower rate of positive inflation",D:"A rise in the price level"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-PRICE-06",scaffold:"Deflation means the general price level falls. It can encourage consumers to delay purchases and increase the real burden of debt, although small price falls for particular goods are not necessarily deflation.",reforge:{stem:"Prices fall across the economy for a sustained period. This is known as:",options:{A:"Demand-pull inflation",B:"Hyper-employment",C:"Deflation",D:"Disinflation"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-PRICE-07",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE",stem:"Which group may benefit from unanticipated inflation?",options:{A:"A lender receiving fixed repayments",B:"A worker whose wage is fixed forever",C:"A borrower with a fixed-rate loan",D:"A saver with a fixed nominal return"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-PRICE-07",scaffold:"Unexpected inflation reduces the real value of fixed nominal repayments, which can benefit borrowers and disadvantage lenders. The effect depends on contracts and whether incomes adjust.",reforge:{stem:"Why might inflation help a household with a fixed-rate mortgage?",options:{A:"Its nominal repayments automatically become zero",B:"The lender receives more purchasing power",C:"Inflation guarantees a lower interest rate",D:"The real value of its outstanding debt can fall as prices and incomes rise"},correct:"D"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-PRICE-08",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-PRICE",stem:"Why is price stability an economic policy objective?",options:{A:"It guarantees high growth every year",B:"It removes all unemployment",C:"It ensures exports always exceed imports",D:"It makes planning, saving and investment easier"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-PRICE-08",scaffold:"Stable prices reduce uncertainty for households and firms. Very high or volatile inflation can distort decisions, redistribute income unpredictably and damage international competitiveness.",reforge:{stem:"Why can volatile inflation make business planning more difficult?",options:{A:"Firms cannot predict future costs, prices and returns as confidently",B:"It fixes every input cost",C:"It makes demand perfectly predictable",D:"It removes the need for investment decisions"},correct:"A"}}
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL"] = {
  label: "Paper 2: Fiscal Policy",
  color: "#15803d",
  questions: [
    {id:"GCSE-P2-FISC-01",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL",stem:"Which pair of actions is expansionary fiscal policy?",options:{A:"Higher taxes and lower government spending",B:"Lower taxes and higher government spending",C:"Higher interest rates and lower money supply",D:"A tariff and an import quota"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-FISC-01",scaffold:"Expansionary fiscal policy increases aggregate demand through lower taxation, higher spending or both. It may support growth and employment during a downturn but can increase inflation or borrowing.",reforge:{stem:"To stimulate demand during a recession, the government could:",options:{A:"Raise the central bank interest rate",B:"Increase infrastructure spending or reduce income tax",C:"Cut all public spending",D:"Raise taxes and reduce benefits"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-FISC-02",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL",stem:"Which is an example of an automatic stabiliser?",options:{A:"A firm changing its advertising budget",B:"Income tax receipts falling when incomes fall in a recession",C:"A one-off decision to build a new airport",D:"The central bank changing interest rates"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-FISC-02",scaffold:"Automatic stabilisers change the government’s budget without a new policy decision. In a downturn, tax receipts tend to fall and benefit payments rise, supporting aggregate demand.",reforge:{stem:"During a recession, unemployment benefits rise automatically. This can:",options:{A:"Make fiscal policy contractionary",B:"Have no effect on households",C:"Support household incomes and partially stabilise demand",D:"Reduce all government spending"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-FISC-03",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL",stem:"What is the likely effect of contractionary fiscal policy?",options:{A:"A direct fall in the exchange rate only",B:"An automatic increase in productive capacity",C:"Lower aggregate demand and reduced inflationary pressure",D:"Higher aggregate demand and more inflation"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-FISC-03",scaffold:"Higher taxes or lower government spending can reduce disposable income and spending, lowering aggregate demand. This may help control inflation but can weaken growth and employment.",reforge:{stem:"Why might a government cut spending when inflation is persistently high?",options:{A:"To guarantee faster economic growth",B:"To increase household disposable income",C:"To raise imports immediately",D:"To reduce demand pressure on scarce resources"},correct:"D"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-FISC-04",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL",stem:"What is public debt?",options:{A:"The value of all household mortgages",B:"A single year’s trade deficit",C:"Total private sector profits",D:"The accumulated borrowing of the government"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-FISC-04",scaffold:"Public debt is the stock of government borrowing built up over time. A budget deficit is a flow measured over a period; deficits can add to the debt, while surpluses can reduce it.",reforge:{stem:"Which statement correctly distinguishes a deficit from debt?",options:{A:"A deficit is a yearly shortfall; debt is the accumulated stock of borrowing",B:"Debt is a yearly flow and deficit is an accumulated stock",C:"They always mean the same thing",D:"Neither relates to government finances"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-FISC-05",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL",stem:"How can government spending reduce unemployment?",options:{A:"It can directly employ workers and raise demand for firms’ output",B:"It always reduces the labour force",C:"It makes all vacancies disappear",D:"It lowers demand for goods"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-FISC-05",scaffold:"Public-sector employment and spending on goods and services can raise demand, encouraging private firms to produce more and hire workers. The final effect depends on spare capacity and crowding out.",reforge:{stem:"A government funds a new hospital and buys equipment from local firms. This may reduce unemployment through:",options:{A:"Lower investment by every firm",B:"Direct jobs and higher derived demand for workers in supplying firms",C:"A fall in total demand",D:"A reduction in the labour force"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-FISC-06",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL",stem:"What is crowding out?",options:{A:"A fall in the price level from stronger competition",B:"Government borrowing contributing to higher interest rates and less private investment",C:"Households replacing imports with exports",D:"Firms hiring more workers after a tax cut"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-FISC-06",scaffold:"If government borrowing raises demand for loanable funds, interest rates may rise and private investment may be discouraged. Crowding out is less likely when the economy has spare capacity and interest rates are low.",reforge:{stem:"Why could heavy government borrowing reduce private investment?",options:{A:"It makes capital free",B:"It removes the need for finance",C:"It may push up interest rates, making business loans more expensive",D:"It guarantees that firms receive more profits"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-FISC-07",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL",stem:"Why may fiscal policy take time to affect the economy?",options:{A:"Households never respond to income",B:"Fiscal policy affects only foreign economies",C:"Budgets must be designed, approved and implemented before spending changes",D:"Taxes change every minute automatically"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-FISC-07",scaffold:"Recognition, decision and implementation lags mean policy may affect demand after economic conditions have changed. This can make timing difficult.",reforge:{stem:"A government announces a school-building programme, but construction starts a year later. This illustrates:",options:{A:"Perfectly instant policy transmission",B:"A fall in productive capacity caused by tax",C:"A current account surplus",D:"A time lag between fiscal decision and its economic effect"},correct:"D"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-FISC-08",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-FISCAL",stem:"Why might a government raise spending on education even if it creates a short-run deficit?",options:{A:"Education always reduces potential output",B:"A deficit can never finance investment",C:"The policy guarantees lower wages",D:"Human capital may raise future productivity and growth"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-FISC-08",scaffold:"Spending on education can be investment in human capital. Better skills may increase productivity, employment and tax receipts over time, although the benefits are uncertain and the spending has an opportunity cost.",reforge:{stem:"Which is an example of fiscal spending intended to improve long-run supply?",options:{A:"Funding vocational training and skills development",B:"Reducing all maintenance of public infrastructure",C:"Paying firms not to produce",D:"Cutting school access during a skills shortage"},correct:"A"}}
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY"] = {
  label: "Paper 2: Monetary Policy",
  color: "#16a34a",
  questions: [
    {id:"GCSE-P2-MON-01",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY",stem:"Which institution sets the UK base interest rate?",options:{A:"The Bank of England’s Monetary Policy Committee",B:"Every commercial bank separately",C:"The Office for National Statistics",D:"The World Trade Organization"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-MON-01",scaffold:"The Monetary Policy Committee sets Bank Rate to help achieve the inflation target. Commercial banks then decide their own rates, influenced by the base rate and risk.",reforge:{stem:"The UK base rate is changed by the:",options:{A:"International Monetary Fund only",B:"Bank of England Monetary Policy Committee",C:"Department for Transport",D:"Competition and Markets Authority only"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-MON-02",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY",stem:"What is the likely effect of a rise in interest rates on household borrowing?",options:{A:"Mortgage repayments must fall",B:"Borrowing becomes more expensive and spending may fall",C:"Borrowing becomes free",D:"Saving becomes less attractive"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-MON-02",scaffold:"Higher interest rates raise the cost of new and variable-rate borrowing. Households may cut consumption or delay purchases, reducing aggregate demand.",reforge:{stem:"Why might a higher interest rate reduce demand for new cars bought on credit?",options:{A:"Households receive more disposable income",B:"Credit becomes unlimited",C:"The finance repayments become more expensive",D:"The car becomes a merit good"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-MON-03",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY",stem:"Why might a rise in interest rates reduce inflation?",options:{A:"It makes all imports more expensive",B:"It guarantees a fall in wages",C:"It can reduce borrowing and spending, lowering demand pressure on prices",D:"It directly increases every firm’s capacity"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-MON-03",scaffold:"The interest-rate transmission mechanism works through borrowing, saving, asset prices and exchange rates. Lower aggregate demand can reduce inflation, although supply shocks may remain.",reforge:{stem:"If higher rates encourage saving and discourage consumption, the immediate effect on aggregate demand is likely to be:",options:{A:"A rise",B:"No possible change",C:"A shift in long-run supply only",D:"A fall"},correct:"D"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-MON-04",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY",stem:"Which group may lose when interest rates rise?",options:{A:"A saver receiving a higher return",B:"A lender making new loans",C:"A person with no borrowing or saving",D:"A household with a variable-rate mortgage"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-MON-04",scaffold:"Higher rates increase repayments for variable-rate borrowers, reducing disposable income. Savers may benefit from higher returns, while the effect on firms depends on their borrowing.",reforge:{stem:"A rise in mortgage rates is most likely to reduce the disposable income of:",options:{A:"A household with a variable-rate mortgage",B:"A household with no debt and no savings",C:"A bank receiving interest",D:"A saver whose rate rises by more than inflation"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-MON-05",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY",stem:"How might lower interest rates affect house prices?",options:{A:"Cheaper mortgages can increase demand for houses",B:"They always reduce demand for property",C:"They make borrowing impossible",D:"They affect only exports"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-MON-05",scaffold:"Lower rates can reduce mortgage costs and make borrowing more attractive, increasing housing demand. The effect on prices also depends on housing supply, incomes and expectations.",reforge:{stem:"If mortgage rates fall while the supply of homes is fixed in the short run, house prices may:",options:{A:"Become a measure of GDP",B:"Rise as demand increases",C:"Fall to zero",D:"Be unaffected in every case"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-MON-06",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY",stem:"Why might a rise in UK interest rates strengthen the pound?",options:{A:"It prevents all imports",B:"UK financial assets may become more attractive to overseas investors",C:"It makes UK exports cheaper automatically",D:"It reduces demand for UK assets"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-MON-06",scaffold:"Higher UK interest rates may increase demand for sterling-denominated savings and investments, raising demand for the pound. The exchange rate also depends on expectations and rates elsewhere.",reforge:{stem:"A stronger pound following higher UK interest rates may make:",options:{A:"Foreign holidays more expensive for UK residents",B:"All UK wages fall immediately",C:"Imports cheaper for UK buyers",D:"UK exports cheaper for foreign buyers"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-MON-07",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY",stem:"Why can a rise in interest rates create a trade-off for the economy?",options:{A:"It raises demand while reducing borrowing costs",B:"It affects only the financial sector",C:"It may reduce inflation but also weaken growth and employment",D:"It always increases growth and lowers unemployment"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-MON-07",scaffold:"Monetary policy can stabilise prices but weaker spending may reduce output and jobs. The size of the trade-off depends on the economy’s starting position and the cause of inflation.",reforge:{stem:"If inflation is high but economic growth is already weak, a rate rise could:",options:{A:"Guarantee strong growth",B:"Raise household borrowing",C:"Have no effect on demand",D:"Lower price pressure while adding to the risk of recession"},correct:"D"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-MON-08",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-MONETARY",stem:"What is the main purpose of an inflation target?",options:{A:"To guarantee that every individual price is fixed",B:"To set the exchange rate permanently",C:"To eliminate all unemployment",D:"To provide a clear measure that guides monetary policy and expectations"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-MON-08",scaffold:"An inflation target helps anchor expectations and gives the central bank a framework for deciding interest rates. It does not mean every price changes at the target rate.",reforge:{stem:"Why can a credible inflation target help households and firms?",options:{A:"It gives them a clearer basis for planning future prices and wages",B:"It guarantees prices never change",C:"It fixes every mortgage rate",D:"It removes supply shocks"},correct:"A"}}
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY"] = {
  label: "Paper 2: Supply-side Policies",
  color: "#15803d",
  questions: [
    {id:"GCSE-P2-SUP-01",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY",stem:"What is the main aim of supply-side policy?",options:{A:"To increase productive capacity and productivity",B:"To reduce the economy’s ability to produce",C:"To set one price for every product",D:"To make all demand disappear"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-SUP-01",scaffold:"Supply-side policies aim to improve the quantity or quality of factors of production and the efficiency of markets. They can shift long-run aggregate supply to the right.",reforge:{stem:"A successful supply-side policy should generally:",options:{A:"Prevent firms entering markets",B:"Allow the economy to produce more at a given price level",C:"Reduce skills and investment",D:"Lower potential output"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-SUP-02",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY",stem:"How can education and training improve supply?",options:{A:"They always reduce employment",B:"They can raise labour productivity and human capital",C:"They reduce workers’ skills",D:"They make capital unusable"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-SUP-02",scaffold:"Education and training can improve skills, productivity and occupational mobility. This can raise productive capacity, although benefits may take time and public funding has an opportunity cost.",reforge:{stem:"Why might vocational training reduce structural unemployment as well as raise supply?",options:{A:"It reduces the quality of the workforce",B:"It makes firms less productive",C:"It gives workers skills that match vacancies in expanding industries",D:"It removes all demand for labour"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-SUP-03",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY",stem:"Why might lower income tax encourage labour supply?",options:{A:"It reduces the reward from working",B:"It always lowers employment",C:"People may keep more of each extra pound earned",D:"It makes work illegal"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-SUP-03",scaffold:"Lower marginal tax rates may strengthen incentives to work more hours, gain skills or enter employment. The final effect depends on income effects, substitution effects and how tax revenue is used.",reforge:{stem:"A supply-side argument for reducing corporation tax is that it may:",options:{A:"Stop firms investing",B:"Make exports impossible",C:"Reduce the capital stock automatically",D:"Attract investment and increase firms’ productive capacity"},correct:"D"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-SUP-04",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY",stem:"How can improved broadband infrastructure increase productive capacity?",options:{A:"It prevents firms trading online",B:"It reduces access to information",C:"It makes every worker unemployed",D:"It can improve communication, remote working and business efficiency"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-SUP-04",scaffold:"Infrastructure can reduce business costs, improve connectivity and allow new firms to enter markets. This can raise productivity and competition, although installation is expensive.",reforge:{stem:"A reliable high-speed network allows firms to coordinate production more cheaply. This is likely to:",options:{A:"Raise productivity and shift productive capacity right",B:"Reduce efficiency",C:"Lower access to markets",D:"Make technology irrelevant"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-SUP-05",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY",stem:"Why might privatisation be considered a supply-side policy?",options:{A:"Private ownership may increase competition and efficiency in some industries",B:"It always makes markets less competitive",C:"It removes the profit motive",D:"It guarantees no firms enter"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-SUP-05",scaffold:"Privatisation can introduce competition, stronger incentives and private investment, but outcomes depend on regulation and market structure. A natural monopoly may still require controls.",reforge:{stem:"A possible risk of privatisation in a natural monopoly is that:",options:{A:"Investment becomes impossible",B:"A private firm may exploit market power without effective regulation",C:"Competition is guaranteed to be perfect",D:"Prices must fall to zero"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-SUP-06",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY",stem:"How could state-funded childcare increase potential output?",options:{A:"It prevents workers changing jobs",B:"It may allow more parents to enter or increase hours in paid work",C:"It reduces labour force participation",D:"It makes caring costs higher for every family"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-SUP-06",scaffold:"Affordable childcare can reduce a barrier to labour market participation, increasing the available workforce and potential output. It may also raise tax receipts and reduce benefit spending.",reforge:{stem:"A childcare subsidy may increase labour supply because it:",options:{A:"Reduces the number of working-age people",B:"Prevents firms recruiting",C:"Lowers the cost of taking a job for some parents",D:"Makes employment less accessible"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-SUP-07",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY",stem:"Why could a higher minimum wage have both positive and negative effects on supply?",options:{A:"It has no effect on workers or firms",B:"It prevents productivity improvements",C:"It may improve work incentives but raise firms’ labour costs",D:"It always reduces costs and raises unemployment"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-SUP-07",scaffold:"Higher wages can attract workers, reduce turnover and encourage productivity. If set above the equilibrium wage, they can also raise costs and reduce employment or output in some firms.",reforge:{stem:"A higher minimum wage may improve productivity when it:",options:{A:"Makes every firm close",B:"Reduces the reward from working",C:"Prevents training",D:"Motivates workers and reduces costly staff turnover"},correct:"D"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-SUP-08",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-SUPPLY",stem:"Why may supply-side policy take a long time to work?",options:{A:"All policies change capacity instantly",B:"Workers never need retraining",C:"Capital can be created without investment",D:"Education, infrastructure and investment need time before productivity rises"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-SUP-08",scaffold:"Long-run policies often require planning, construction, training and changes in behaviour. Their benefits may be substantial but are uncertain and delayed.",reforge:{stem:"Which policy is most likely to have a delayed effect on productive capacity?",options:{A:"Building a new rail network and training engineers",B:"Changing a shop’s opening sign for one day",C:"Reducing a single consumer’s spending",D:"Printing a menu"},correct:"A"}}
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL"] = {
  label: "Paper 2: Limitations of Markets",
  color: "#16a34a",
  questions: [
    {id:"GCSE-P2-MF-01",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL",stem:"What is a negative externality of consumption?",options:{A:"A cost imposed on third parties by consumption",B:"A private benefit received by the consumer",C:"A tax paid by a producer only",D:"A benefit received by everyone"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-MF-01",scaffold:"A negative consumption externality occurs when consumption harms people not involved in the transaction, such as passive smoking or pollution. The social cost exceeds the private cost.",reforge:{stem:"Second-hand smoke affecting people near a smoker is an example of:",options:{A:"A merit good",B:"A negative externality of consumption",C:"A positive externality of production",D:"A private benefit only"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-MF-02",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL",stem:"Which is a positive externality of consumption?",options:{A:"A tax on imports",B:"Benefits to others from an individual’s consumption",C:"A cost paid directly by a firm",D:"A fall in producer revenue"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-MF-02",scaffold:"Education and vaccination can create benefits for third parties. Because consumers may consider only their private benefit, the market may provide less than the socially efficient quantity.",reforge:{stem:"Why can education be under-consumed in a free market?",options:{A:"The government cannot fund schools",B:"Education is always a demerit good",C:"Individuals may ignore benefits such as a more skilled and productive society",D:"Education has no private benefit"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-MF-03",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL",stem:"What is the likely effect of a tax on sugary drinks?",options:{A:"It shifts demand right automatically",B:"It removes all external costs",C:"It raises firms’ costs and can reduce quantity demanded",D:"It makes sugary drinks free"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-MF-03",scaffold:"An indirect tax shifts supply up or left, raising the market price and reducing equilibrium quantity if demand responds. It may internalise some health costs but can be regressive.",reforge:{stem:"An indirect tax on a polluting product is intended partly to:",options:{A:"Increase consumption of the product",B:"Remove the product’s private benefits",C:"Guarantee a budget deficit",D:"Make producers and consumers face more of the social cost"},correct:"D"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-MF-04",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL",stem:"Why may a subsidy be used for a good with positive externalities?",options:{A:"To make the good more expensive",B:"To reduce all production",C:"To punish third parties",D:"To lower the price and encourage consumption towards the socially desirable level"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-MF-04",scaffold:"A subsidy lowers producers’ costs and can shift supply right, reducing price and increasing quantity. It may help goods such as education or healthcare, but it uses public funds.",reforge:{stem:"A subsidy for vaccinations is most likely to:",options:{A:"Increase uptake by reducing the effective price",B:"Reduce the positive external benefits",C:"Raise the private price",D:"Prevent anyone being vaccinated"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-MF-05",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL",stem:"Why can property rights sometimes help reduce pollution?",options:{A:"An owner has an incentive to protect and enforce the value of the resource",B:"Property rights make pollution costless",C:"No one can monitor a resource",D:"They guarantee every polluter is identified"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-MF-05",scaffold:"Clearly defined and enforceable property rights can give owners an incentive to prevent damage and seek compensation. Monitoring, legal costs and unclear boundaries may limit effectiveness.",reforge:{stem:"A weakness of using property rights to control river pollution is that:",options:{A:"The policy removes all legal costs",B:"Monitoring a large shared area and identifying polluters may be difficult",C:"Owners always have unlimited enforcement resources",D:"Pollution becomes a positive externality"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-MF-06",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL",stem:"Which is a merit good?",options:{A:"A good supplied only by monopolies",B:"A good that may be under-consumed because consumers underestimate its benefits",C:"A good with only negative externalities",D:"A good that must be banned"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-MF-06",scaffold:"Merit goods such as education may be under-consumed because of imperfect information or short-term decision-making. Governments may subsidise or provide them.",reforge:{stem:"Why might the government provide information about the benefits of healthy eating?",options:{A:"Healthy eating is a public bad",B:"It guarantees no food is purchased",C:"Consumers may otherwise underestimate private and social benefits",D:"Information always reduces demand"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-MF-07",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL",stem:"What does market failure mean?",options:{A:"The government has no revenue",B:"All markets have stopped operating",C:"The market allocation of resources is inefficient or does not maximise social welfare",D:"Every firm makes a loss"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-MF-07",scaffold:"Market failure occurs when private decisions lead to an inefficient outcome, often because of externalities, information gaps, public goods or market power. Government intervention can also fail.",reforge:{stem:"A market producing too much pollution at the unregulated equilibrium is an example of:",options:{A:"Perfect efficiency",B:"A budget surplus",C:"A rise in productive capacity",D:"Market failure because social costs are not fully reflected in the price"},correct:"D"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-MF-08",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-MARKETFAIL",stem:"Why can government intervention in markets be unsuccessful?",options:{A:"Governments always have perfect information",B:"Taxes never change behaviour",C:"Regulation has no administrative cost",D:"Policies may be poorly designed, costly or create unintended effects"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-MF-08",scaffold:"Government failure can arise from information gaps, implementation costs, unintended incentives and political pressures. Intervention should be judged against both the original market failure and its own costs.",reforge:{stem:"A pollution tax is set too high and causes a large fall in employment with little environmental gain. This illustrates:",options:{A:"A possible unintended cost of intervention",B:"Perfect policy targeting",C:"The end of scarcity",D:"A positive externality only"},correct:"A"}}
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE"] = {
  label: "Paper 2: International Trade",
  color: "#15803d",
  questions: [
    {id:"GCSE-P2-TRADE-01",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE",stem:"What is an import?",options:{A:"A good or service bought from another country",B:"A good sold to another country",C:"A domestic tax",D:"A government benefit"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-TRADE-01",scaffold:"Imports are goods and services purchased from abroad. Exports are domestic goods and services sold to other countries.",reforge:{stem:"A UK firm buys computer equipment made in South Korea. For the UK this purchase is:",options:{A:"A current transfer",B:"An import",C:"An export",D:"A domestic subsidy"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-TRADE-02",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE",stem:"Why can specialisation increase international trade?",options:{A:"It prevents exchange",B:"Countries concentrate resources on goods they can produce relatively efficiently",C:"It requires every country to produce everything",D:"It removes all opportunity costs"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-TRADE-02",scaffold:"Specialisation allows economies to focus on activities where they have an advantage, potentially raising productivity and output. Trade lets them exchange surplus production for other goods.",reforge:{stem:"A country specialises in renewable-energy equipment and imports food. The main economic rationale is that:",options:{A:"Imports are always free",B:"Trade eliminates all risks",C:"Resources are focused where the country is relatively productive, then exchanged",D:"The country no longer faces scarcity"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-TRADE-03",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE",stem:"Which is a possible benefit of free trade?",options:{A:"No exposure to overseas shocks",B:"An end to imports",C:"More choice and lower prices from competition and specialisation",D:"Guaranteed employment in every domestic industry"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-TRADE-03",scaffold:"Free trade can increase competition, reduce prices, expand choice, provide access to inputs and support specialisation. Some workers and industries may face adjustment costs.",reforge:{stem:"Why might consumers support fewer trade barriers?",options:{A:"Domestic firms face no competition",B:"All imports are banned",C:"Every domestic wage is guaranteed",D:"Imported goods may become cheaper and more varied"},correct:"D"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-TRADE-04",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE",stem:"What is a tariff?",options:{A:"A subsidy for exports only",B:"A limit on domestic wages",C:"A rise in interest rates",D:"A tax on imports"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-TRADE-04",scaffold:"A tariff raises the domestic price of an imported good and protects domestic producers, but consumers may pay more and other firms may face higher input costs.",reforge:{stem:"A tariff on imported steel is most likely to:",options:{A:"Raise the domestic price of steel and protect some domestic producers",B:"Make imported steel free",C:"Lower the cost for every steel user",D:"Guarantee a trade surplus"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-TRADE-05",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE",stem:"Why can a country import goods that it could produce itself?",options:{A:"Other countries may produce them at a lower opportunity cost",B:"Domestic resources are always unlimited",C:"Imports never require payment",D:"Trade is possible only when domestic production is impossible"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-TRADE-05",scaffold:"Comparative advantage is based on opportunity cost, not simply whether a country can produce a good. Trade can benefit both sides when each specialises relatively.",reforge:{stem:"Comparative advantage means a country:",options:{A:"Cannot import from less productive countries",B:"Can produce a good at a lower opportunity cost than another country",C:"Has the highest absolute output in every industry",D:"Must export every good it produces"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-TRADE-06",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE",stem:"Why might a country join a trading bloc using a common currency?",options:{A:"It prevents any country leaving",B:"It can reduce transaction costs and make price comparisons easier",C:"It removes all differences between economies",D:"It guarantees equal wages"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-TRADE-06",scaffold:"A common currency removes the need to exchange money between members and can reduce exchange-rate uncertainty. Members may lose independent monetary policy and face adjustment challenges.",reforge:{stem:"A common currency may encourage trade because firms:",options:{A:"Can ignore prices",B:"No longer need to produce exports",C:"Do not need to pay currency-conversion costs for transactions within the currency area",D:"Are protected from all competition"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-TRADE-07",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE",stem:"Why might a UK clothing firm move production abroad?",options:{A:"It wants to eliminate all exports",B:"Globalisation makes production impossible",C:"It may seek lower labour or production costs",D:"It must increase its UK costs"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-TRADE-07",scaffold:"Outsourcing can lower costs and prices or raise profits, but it may reduce domestic manufacturing employment and create dependence on overseas suppliers.",reforge:{stem:"A UK retailer buys clothes from a lower-cost overseas factory. One possible effect is:",options:{A:"Higher prices and more UK textile jobs in every case",B:"No change for workers or consumers",C:"An automatic fall in all imports",D:"Lower prices for consumers but pressure on some domestic textile jobs"},correct:"D"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-TRADE-08",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-TRADE",stem:"Which is a risk of relying heavily on international trade?",options:{A:"The economy becomes completely independent",B:"Consumers lose all choice",C:"Domestic firms face no uncertainty",D:"A foreign recession or supply disruption can reduce available exports or imports"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-TRADE-08",scaffold:"Trade creates interdependence. Overseas recessions, wars, transport disruption or exchange-rate changes can affect domestic output, prices and employment.",reforge:{stem:"A disruption closes a major overseas supplier of components. This shows that trade can:",options:{A:"Expose firms to international supply-chain risks",B:"Guarantee uninterrupted production",C:"Remove the need for inventories",D:"Make all domestic inputs cheaper"},correct:"A"}}
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP"] = {
  label: "Paper 2: Balance of Payments",
  color: "#16a34a",
  questions: [
    {id:"GCSE-P2-BOP-01",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP",stem:"Which transaction is a credit on the UK current account?",options:{A:"A UK firm exports software to a French customer",B:"A UK household buys an imported car",C:"A UK resident pays for a foreign holiday",D:"A UK firm imports machinery"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-BOP-01",scaffold:"Exports earn income from abroad and are recorded as credits. Imports and UK spending overseas are debits.",reforge:{stem:"A UK consultancy is paid by a business in Canada for its services. This is:",options:{A:"A government budget deficit",B:"A current-account credit for the UK",C:"A current-account debit for the UK",D:"A UK import of goods"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-BOP-02",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP",stem:"Which item belongs in trade in services?",options:{A:"A UK resident buys a foreign share",B:"A UK accountancy firm advises an overseas client",C:"A UK supermarket imports fruit",D:"A UK factory exports cars"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-BOP-02",scaffold:"Trade in services records services bought and sold internationally, such as tourism, finance, transport and professional advice. Goods are physical products.",reforge:{stem:"A foreign visitor pays a UK hotel. For the UK this is:",options:{A:"An import of goods",B:"A capital transfer only",C:"An export of a service",D:"An import of a service"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-BOP-03",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP",stem:"Which combination makes up the current account?",options:{A:"Only foreign direct investment",B:"Household mortgages and bank deposits",C:"Trade in goods and services, primary income and secondary income",D:"Only government borrowing"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-BOP-03",scaffold:"The current account includes trade in goods, trade in services, primary income such as profits and interest, and secondary income such as transfers and aid.",reforge:{stem:"Profits sent to the UK from a UK-owned company abroad are recorded as:",options:{A:"Trade in goods",B:"A tariff",C:"A household tax payment",D:"Primary income received"},correct:"D"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-BOP-04",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP",stem:"A country has goods exports of £200bn, goods imports of £250bn, and a services surplus of £30bn. Ignoring other items, it has a:",options:{A:"£20bn current account surplus",B:"£80bn surplus",C:"Balanced current account",D:"£20bn current account deficit"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-BOP-04",scaffold:"The goods balance is -£50bn and the services balance is +£30bn, giving a combined deficit of £20bn before primary and secondary income.",reforge:{stem:"Goods trade is -£70bn and services trade is +£50bn, with no other current-account items. The balance is:",options:{A:"A £20bn deficit",B:"A £20bn surplus",C:"A £120bn deficit",D:"Zero"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-BOP-05",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP",stem:"Why might a current account deficit be less concerning during an investment boom?",options:{A:"Imports may include capital goods that increase future productive capacity",B:"A deficit always means no investment",C:"Capital goods are never imported",D:"It proves exports are rising faster than imports"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-BOP-05",scaffold:"A deficit can reflect strong domestic demand or imports of machinery and technology that support future exports. Its sustainability depends on how it is financed and the economy’s competitiveness.",reforge:{stem:"A country imports advanced machinery to expand factories. This may initially worsen the current account but could later:",options:{A:"Remove the need for workers",B:"Raise productive capacity and export potential",C:"Make all future exports impossible",D:"Reduce investment"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-BOP-06",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP",stem:"Which policy could help reduce a current account deficit in the long run?",options:{A:"Increasing demand for imports only",B:"Improving productivity so domestic firms become more competitive",C:"Making exports more expensive without improving quality",D:"Reducing investment in education"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-BOP-06",scaffold:"Supply-side policies can reduce unit costs and improve quality, supporting exports and reducing reliance on imports. Demand management and exchange-rate policies may also affect the deficit.",reforge:{stem:"Why might research and development support improve the current account?",options:{A:"It makes imports mandatory",B:"It directly removes all transfers",C:"Innovative firms may produce more attractive exports",D:"It guarantees a fall in domestic productivity"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-BOP-07",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP",stem:"What may happen to a current account deficit after a currency depreciation?",options:{A:"Trade values cannot change",B:"The current account becomes a budget account",C:"Exports may become cheaper and imports dearer, potentially improving the balance",D:"Exports become dearer and imports cheaper in every case"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-BOP-07",scaffold:"Depreciation can improve price competitiveness, but the final effect depends on elasticities, contracts and whether the value of exports and imports changes enough.",reforge:{stem:"A weaker domestic currency may reduce a current account deficit if:",options:{A:"No one changes quantities purchased",B:"Imports become cheaper",C:"Exports become more expensive to overseas buyers",D:"Foreign demand for exports and domestic demand for imports respond sufficiently to prices"},correct:"D"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-BOP-08",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-BOP",stem:"Why might a government not immediately try to eliminate a current account deficit?",options:{A:"Deficits can never be financed",B:"A deficit always means the economy is in recession",C:"The current account measures only tax revenue",D:"It may reflect strong consumer demand or imports for productive investment"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-BOP-08",scaffold:"A current account deficit is not automatically harmful. Policymakers consider its size, persistence, financing, composition and interaction with growth, inflation and employment.",reforge:{stem:"Which question is most relevant when evaluating a current account deficit?",options:{A:"Is it temporary and financing productive investment, or persistent and difficult to finance?",B:"Does it prove every household is poor?",C:"Does it mean the budget is balanced?",D:"Are imports always undesirable?"},correct:"A"}}
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR"] = {
  label: "Paper 2: Exchange Rates",
  color: "#15803d",
  questions: [
    {id:"GCSE-P2-EXR-01",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR",stem:"What is an appreciation of the pound?",options:{A:"A rise in the value of the pound against another currency",B:"A fall in the value of the pound",C:"A rise in UK unemployment",D:"A tax on foreign goods"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-EXR-01",scaffold:"Appreciation means sterling buys more foreign currency. Depreciation means it buys less. Floating exchange rates change through demand and supply in the foreign exchange market.",reforge:{stem:"If £1 buys €1.20 instead of €1.10, sterling has:",options:{A:"Had no change in value",B:"Appreciated against the euro",C:"Depreciated against the euro",D:"Become inconvertible"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-EXR-02",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR",stem:"Which change would increase demand for sterling?",options:{A:"Foreign investors selling UK assets",B:"Overseas buyers purchasing more UK exports",C:"UK residents buying more imports",D:"UK tourists buying foreign holidays"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-EXR-02",scaffold:"Foreign buyers need sterling to purchase UK exports, increasing demand for the currency. UK demand for imports increases supply of sterling as residents exchange it for foreign currency.",reforge:{stem:"A rise in foreign demand for UK financial assets is likely to:",options:{A:"Have no exchange-rate effect",B:"Reduce the demand for UK assets",C:"Increase demand for sterling and put upward pressure on its value",D:"Increase supply of sterling and lower its value"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-EXR-03",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR",stem:"What is a likely effect of pound depreciation on UK import prices?",options:{A:"Import prices cannot change",B:"UK exports become more expensive to UK buyers",C:"Imported goods and raw materials become more expensive in pounds",D:"All imports become cheaper"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-EXR-03",scaffold:"When sterling falls, more pounds are needed to buy foreign currency. Importers may pass higher costs to consumers, creating cost-push inflation.",reforge:{stem:"A weaker pound raises the cost of imported energy. This may:",options:{A:"Reduce every firm’s costs",B:"Make imported energy free",C:"Lower demand for all exports",D:"Increase production costs and add to inflationary pressure"},correct:"D"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-EXR-04",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR",stem:"How can a stronger pound affect UK exporters?",options:{A:"Their goods become cheaper for overseas buyers",B:"Their imported inputs become more expensive",C:"Exports are automatically banned",D:"Their goods become more expensive for overseas buyers"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-EXR-04",scaffold:"Appreciation raises the foreign-currency price of UK exports, potentially reducing demand. It can also lower the sterling cost of imported inputs, benefiting some firms.",reforge:{stem:"A UK holiday abroad becomes cheaper after sterling appreciates because:",options:{A:"Each pound buys more foreign currency",B:"Foreign hotels reduce every price",C:"UK wages automatically rise",D:"The pound has depreciated"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-EXR-05",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR",stem:"A UK company must pay €1,000 when £1 = €1.25. Approximately how many pounds are needed?",options:{A:"£800",B:"£1,000",C:"£1,250",D:"£80"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-EXR-05",scaffold:"Pounds needed = euros / euros per pound: €1,000 / 1.25 = £800.",reforge:{stem:"If £1 = $1.25, how many dollars would £400 buy?",options:{A:"$1,600",B:"$320",C:"$500",D:"$1,250"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-EXR-06",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR",stem:"Why might a current account deficit put downward pressure on a currency?",options:{A:"A deficit means foreign currency is not used",B:"Residents need to sell the domestic currency to buy imports",C:"Exports always increase demand for the currency",D:"Imports create unlimited demand for domestic currency"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-EXR-06",scaffold:"When residents purchase imports, they supply domestic currency in exchange for foreign currency. If this pressure exceeds demand from exports and capital inflows, the currency may depreciate.",reforge:{stem:"If a country persistently imports much more than it exports, one possible exchange-rate pressure is:",options:{A:"No need for foreign currency",B:"A fixed exchange rate automatically",C:"Downward pressure on its currency from greater supply",D:"Guaranteed appreciation"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-EXR-07",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR",stem:"What is a possible benefit of a currency appreciation?",options:{A:"Guaranteed higher export demand",B:"A rise in import prices",C:"Cheaper imports and lower costs for firms using imported inputs",D:"More expensive imports"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-EXR-07",scaffold:"Appreciation makes foreign goods and inputs cheaper in domestic currency, which can reduce inflation and help consumers. Exporters may lose competitiveness.",reforge:{stem:"A stronger pound may help UK manufacturers that:",options:{A:"Sell only to overseas customers and face no competition",B:"Depend entirely on expensive exports",C:"Have no imported inputs",D:"Buy imported machinery or raw materials"},correct:"D"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-EXR-08",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-EXR",stem:"Why may a depreciation fail to improve the current account quickly?",options:{A:"Exports become impossible to sell",B:"Imports always become cheaper",C:"Exchange rates never affect trade prices",D:"Contracts and inelastic demand may prevent quantities responding immediately"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-EXR-08",scaffold:"The value of trade depends on price elasticities and time. In the short run, contracts and inelastic demand can cause the balance to worsen before quantities adjust.",reforge:{stem:"The J-curve effect suggests a depreciation may initially:",options:{A:"Worsen the current account before improving it later",B:"Improve the current account instantly in every case",C:"Have no effect on imports",D:"Make exports more expensive"},correct:"A"}}
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL"] = {
  label: "Paper 2: Globalisation",
  color: "#16a34a",
  questions: [
    {id:"GCSE-P2-GLOB-01",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL",stem:"What is globalisation?",options:{A:"Growing economic integration and interdependence between countries",B:"A ban on all international trade",C:"An economy becoming completely self-sufficient",D:"A fall in communication between countries"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-GLOB-01",scaffold:"Globalisation involves increasing flows of goods, services, capital, technology, information and sometimes labour across borders. Transport and communication have helped accelerate it.",reforge:{stem:"Which development is evidence of globalisation?",options:{A:"International communication becomes impossible",B:"A firm designs a product in one country, sources components globally and sells worldwide",C:"A country bans all foreign investment",D:"Consumers can buy only local products"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-GLOB-02",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL",stem:"Why can multinational corporations increase globalisation?",options:{A:"They remove international supply chains",B:"They operate, invest and organise production across several countries",C:"They produce only for one local market",D:"They prevent foreign direct investment"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-GLOB-02",scaffold:"Multinational corporations can move capital, technology, management and production between countries. They may create jobs and investment, but their power and working conditions can be controversial.",reforge:{stem:"A multinational builds a factory in a developing country. This is an example of:",options:{A:"A fall in capital flows",B:"Economic isolation",C:"Foreign direct investment",D:"A domestic import tariff"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-GLOB-03",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL",stem:"How has the internet supported globalisation?",options:{A:"It removes access to overseas suppliers",B:"It makes information less available",C:"It allows firms and consumers to communicate, compare prices and trade internationally",D:"It prevents firms advertising abroad"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-GLOB-03",scaffold:"Digital communication lowers information and transaction costs. Firms can coordinate supply chains and market products globally, while consumers can compare and buy from overseas.",reforge:{stem:"Online platforms can increase globalisation because they:",options:{A:"Prevent international sales",B:"Make all products non-tradable",C:"Reduce access to price information",D:"Connect businesses and customers across national borders"},correct:"D"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-GLOB-04",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL",stem:"Which is a possible benefit of globalisation for a developing economy?",options:{A:"It guarantees equal income for all citizens",B:"It removes all environmental costs",C:"It prevents domestic firms exporting",D:"Foreign investment may create jobs, skills and access to technology"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-GLOB-04",scaffold:"Globalisation can bring capital, employment, technology transfer and export opportunities. Benefits depend on regulation, local skills, infrastructure and how gains are distributed.",reforge:{stem:"Why might a developing country welcome a multinational investment?",options:{A:"It may add capital and jobs that support growth",B:"It guarantees no profits leave the country",C:"It eliminates all dependence on trade",D:"It prevents workers gaining skills"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-GLOB-05",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL",stem:"Which is a possible cost of globalisation for workers in a high-income country?",options:{A:"Some jobs may be outsourced or face greater international competition",B:"Every worker receives a higher wage",C:"No industries change",D:"Competition always disappears"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-GLOB-05",scaffold:"Trade and mobile production can create efficiency and lower prices, but workers in industries facing import competition or relocation may lose jobs or bargaining power.",reforge:{stem:"A factory moves production overseas to lower costs. Workers left in the original country may face:",options:{A:"A fall in international competition",B:"Structural unemployment and the need to retrain",C:"Guaranteed higher wages",D:"No change in employment"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-GLOB-06",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL",stem:"What is one concern about multinational corporations?",options:{A:"They operate only in their home country",B:"They may shift profits, exploit weak regulation or create environmental damage",C:"They can never create employment",D:"They are unable to transfer technology"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-GLOB-06",scaffold:"MNCs can bring benefits, but governments may worry about tax avoidance, bargaining power, labour standards, environmental costs and profit repatriation.",reforge:{stem:"Why might a host government regulate a multinational factory?",options:{A:"To stop all technology transfer",B:"Because foreign firms cannot produce goods",C:"To protect workers and the environment while retaining investment benefits",D:"To prevent any jobs being created"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-GLOB-07",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL",stem:"Why is GDP per capita not a complete measure of development?",options:{A:"It includes every aspect of wellbeing",B:"It cannot be calculated from GDP",C:"It does not fully show inequality, health, education or environmental quality",D:"It measures only population"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-GLOB-07",scaffold:"GDP per capita is an average output or income measure. Development also involves health, education, life expectancy, equality, security and environmental sustainability.",reforge:{stem:"Which indicator adds information that GDP per capita may miss?",options:{A:"The exchange rate alone",B:"The number of imports only",C:"A firm’s advertising budget",D:"Life expectancy or educational attainment"},correct:"D"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P2-GLOB-08",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P2-GLOBAL",stem:"What is a likely effect of greater global supply-chain interdependence?",options:{A:"All countries become immune to shocks",B:"Firms no longer need suppliers",C:"Trade becomes impossible",D:"A disruption in one country can affect production and prices elsewhere"},correct:"D",tag:"MC-GCSE-P2-GLOB-08",scaffold:"Global supply chains can lower costs but also transmit shocks quickly. Firms may respond by holding inventories, diversifying suppliers or reshoring some production.",reforge:{stem:"A port closure delays components used by factories worldwide. This demonstrates that globalisation can:",options:{A:"Spread supply disruptions across countries",B:"Prevent international effects",C:"Remove the need for logistics",D:"Guarantee stable prices"},correct:"A"}}
  ]
};

// ===== GCSE ECONOMICS — OCR J205 PAPER 1 =====

BANKS["GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND"] = {
  label: "Paper 1: Economic Foundations",
  color: "#166534",
  questions: [
    {id:"GCSE-P1-FND-01",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND",stem:"A town has enough land to build either a health centre or 120 new homes, but not both. Which economic idea is illustrated when the council chooses the health centre?",options:{A:"Scarcity and opportunity cost",B:"Unlimited resources",C:"A fall in productivity",D:"A decrease in demand"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-FND-01",scaffold:"Resources are limited relative to wants, so a choice must be made. The opportunity cost is the next best alternative given up - here, the homes - rather than every possible alternative.",reforge:{stem:"A student spends Saturday revising rather than working a paid shift. What is the student's opportunity cost?",options:{A:"The revision notes produced",B:"The wages from the next best work shift given up",C:"All future earnings from employment",D:"The cost of the textbook"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-FND-02",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND",stem:"Which item is a factor of production classified as capital?",options:{A:"A delivery van used by a bakery",B:"The owner who organises the bakery",C:"The flour used to make bread",D:"A baker's time and skill"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-FND-02",scaffold:"Capital means man-made resources used to produce other goods and services, such as machinery, tools, buildings and vehicles. It does not mean money itself. Land is natural resources, labour is human effort and enterprise organises production and takes risk.",reforge:{stem:"A farmer buys a new irrigation system for the fields. Which factor of production has increased?",options:{A:"Land",B:"Labour",C:"Capital",D:"Enterprise"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-FND-03",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND",stem:"Why can a country experience scarcity even when its average income is high?",options:{A:"High-income countries have no wants",B:"Resources remain finite while wants continue to expand",C:"Only low-income countries use resources",D:"Scarcity means a country has low GDP"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-FND-03",scaffold:"Scarcity is universal: wealth can increase the range of goods people can buy, but it does not make land, skilled workers, time or raw materials unlimited. Scarcity is therefore different from poverty.",reforge:{stem:"Which statement best distinguishes scarcity from poverty?",options:{A:"Scarcity affects only households; poverty affects only firms",B:"Scarcity is limited resources relative to wants; poverty is low income or resources for a person or group",C:"They are two names for exactly the same problem",D:"Poverty exists in rich countries but scarcity does not"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-FND-04",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND",stem:"A country moves workers and machines from producing food to producing medical equipment. What is the main trade-off?",options:{A:"The opportunity cost is the medical equipment produced",B:"The opportunity cost is the food no longer produced",C:"There is no opportunity cost because both are useful",D:"The opportunity cost is the country's population"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-FND-04",scaffold:"Opportunity cost is the benefit of the next best alternative forgone. When resources are switched into medical equipment, the relevant sacrificed benefit is the food those resources would have produced.",reforge:{stem:"A bakery uses its only oven to make celebration cakes instead of bread for one afternoon. The opportunity cost is:",options:{A:"The cakes sold",B:"The bread that could have been produced and sold",C:"The price of the oven",D:"The bakery's total profit for the year"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-FND-05",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND",stem:"A firm separates production into small tasks so each worker repeats one specialised job. Which is a likely benefit?",options:{A:"Workers become less familiar with their task",B:"Workers may become faster and more skilled through repetition",C:"The firm must produce fewer units",D:"Every worker must buy their own machinery"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-FND-05",scaffold:"Division of labour can raise productivity because repetition improves skill and saves time moving between tasks. It may also allow specialised machinery, although boredom and dependence on a production line are possible drawbacks.",reforge:{stem:"Which is a possible disadvantage of extensive division of labour?",options:{A:"Higher output per worker is impossible",B:"Workers may become bored doing a narrow, repetitive task",C:"Training can never be focused",D:"The firm loses all economies of scale"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-FND-06",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND",stem:"Which example is an economic service rather than a physical product?",options:{A:"A packet of rice",B:"A bicycle",C:"A train journey",D:"A refrigerator"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-FND-06",scaffold:"A service is an intangible activity provided to a consumer, such as transport, insurance or a cinema visit. Products are physical goods that can be handled, although some purchases combine both goods and services.",reforge:{stem:"Which activity is part of the tertiary sector?",options:{A:"Mining copper",B:"Assembling smartphones",C:"Providing car insurance",D:"Growing wheat"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-FND-07",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND",stem:"An entrepreneur notices demand for low-cost school lunches, organises workers and invests savings to open a cafe. Which role is being described?",options:{A:"Enterprise - organising resources and taking the risk of running the business",B:"Land - providing the natural resources only",C:"Labour - supplying physical effort only",D:"Capital - supplying a machine only"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-FND-07",scaffold:"Enterprise brings the other factors of production together, makes decisions and accepts the risk that the business may not succeed. The entrepreneur may also provide labour or capital, but the organising and risk-taking role is enterprise.",reforge:{stem:"Which action best shows enterprise?",options:{A:"A worker follows instructions on a production line",B:"A bank lends money for a new machine",C:"A founder identifies an opportunity, hires staff and accepts the risk of loss",D:"A field provides space for a crop"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-FND-08",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-FOUND",stem:"A country is producing inside its production possibility frontier. What does this suggest?",options:{A:"It is using all resources at maximum efficiency",B:"It has unused or inefficiently used resources",C:"It has no opportunity costs",D:"Its resources are unlimited"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-FND-08",scaffold:"A PPF shows the maximum combinations of two goods possible with current resources and technology. A point inside it indicates spare capacity or inefficiency; a point on the frontier is productively efficient. Moving along the frontier still involves opportunity cost.",reforge:{stem:"What could shift a country's PPF outward?",options:{A:"A rise in consumer confidence only",B:"A fall in demand for its exports",C:"Investment in better technology or worker training",D:"A movement from unemployment into an existing job"},correct:"C"}}
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS"] = {
  label: "Paper 1: The Role of Markets",
  color: "#15803d",
  questions: [
    {id:"GCSE-P1-MKT-01",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS",stem:"What is a market?",options:{A:"Only a physical shop where goods are sold",B:"Any arrangement that allows buyers and sellers to exchange goods, services or resources",C:"A government department that sets all prices",D:"A business that has no competitors"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-MKT-01",scaffold:"A market can be physical, such as a high street, or online and financial. Its essential feature is interaction between buyers and sellers, allowing exchange and helping determine price and quantity.",reforge:{stem:"Which is an example of a factor market?",options:{A:"Consumers buying cinema tickets",B:"Firms hiring software engineers",C:"Households buying washing machines",D:"Tourists booking hotel rooms"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-MKT-02",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS",stem:"A price rises because demand for a product has increased while supply is unchanged. Which function of the price mechanism is most directly shown?",options:{A:"Signalling that the product is in greater demand",B:"Guaranteeing every consumer can buy the product",C:"Removing all competition",D:"Making resources unlimited"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-MKT-02",scaffold:"Prices signal information to buyers and sellers. A higher price can communicate scarcity or stronger demand. It may also ration the product and create an incentive for firms to supply more, but the information role is the clearest here.",reforge:{stem:"A rise in the price of scarce concert tickets prevents some people from buying them. Which price function is this?",options:{A:"Rationing - price allocates the limited tickets among competing buyers",B:"Specialisation - workers repeat a task",C:"Production - price creates more venues immediately",D:"Redistribution - income is transferred to every buyer"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-MKT-03",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS",stem:"How can a higher market price act as an incentive to producers?",options:{A:"It may increase potential profit, encouraging firms to supply more",B:"It forces consumers to buy more",C:"It removes the firm's production costs",D:"It guarantees a profit whatever the firm produces"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-MKT-03",scaffold:"The price mechanism can encourage suppliers when higher prices make production more profitable. The incentive is not a guarantee: costs, competition and the eventual response of other firms still matter.",reforge:{stem:"The price of strawberries rises after a poor harvest. What incentive might this create for growers next season?",options:{A:"To reduce all investment in strawberries",B:"To consider planting more strawberries if expected returns justify the cost",C:"To stop responding to market information",D:"To make consumers demand fewer strawberries automatically"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-MKT-04",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS",stem:"Which statement correctly compares factor and product markets?",options:{A:"Firms sell labour in product markets",B:"Households buy final goods in product markets, while resources such as labour are exchanged in factor markets",C:"Factor markets sell only food",D:"Product markets exist only online"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-MKT-04",scaffold:"Product markets involve final goods and services. Factor markets involve the resources used to produce them, including labour, land and capital. Households can supply factors and demand products; firms do the opposite.",reforge:{stem:"A company pays rent for a warehouse and wages to its employees. These payments are made in:",options:{A:"A product market only",B:"Factor markets, because property and labour are productive resources",C:"A market with no economic cost",D:"A consumer-only market"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-MKT-05",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS",stem:"What is a likely strength of allowing prices to be determined by demand and supply?",options:{A:"Prices can communicate changing shortages and surpluses",B:"No consumer ever faces a price rise",C:"The government knows every consumer's preference",D:"The market always produces a socially fair outcome"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-MKT-05",scaffold:"The price mechanism coordinates dispersed information and can respond to changing conditions. It does not guarantee fairness or prevent price rises, and it may fail where external costs or benefits are ignored.",reforge:{stem:"Why might a free market outcome be criticised as unfair?",options:{A:"Prices may ration goods according to ability to pay, so low-income households may be excluded",B:"Prices never change when demand changes",C:"Free markets always provide too much public transport",D:"Firms cannot respond to incentives"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-MKT-06",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS",stem:"A government charges 10p for each single-use carrier bag. If the main purpose is to encourage reusable bags, the charge is acting mainly as:",options:{A:"A signal and incentive to change behaviour",B:"A subsidy to plastic producers",C:"A guarantee of free bags",D:"A fixed cost of every retailer"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-MKT-06",scaffold:"A small charge can signal the environmental cost of the choice and create an incentive to avoid it. The policy may also raise revenue, but its behavioural purpose is central.",reforge:{stem:"A congestion charge makes driving into a city centre more expensive. Its intended price-mechanism effect is to:",options:{A:"Signal and discourage the use of scarce road space at busy times",B:"Make road space unlimited",C:"Increase demand for driving at every price",D:"Remove the need for public transport"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-MKT-07",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS",stem:"At a price below equilibrium, what is likely to occur in a competitive market?",options:{A:"A surplus because sellers offer more than buyers want",B:"A shortage because quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied",C:"No change because price has no role",D:"A fall in demand caused by lower price"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-MKT-07",scaffold:"Below equilibrium, more buyers want the product than firms are willing to supply. This excess demand creates upward pressure on price, moving the market towards equilibrium unless a control prevents adjustment.",reforge:{stem:"At a price above equilibrium, which outcome is most likely?",options:{A:"Excess demand and queues",B:"A surplus, with quantity supplied greater than quantity demanded",C:"A shortage of inputs only",D:"An automatic increase in consumer incomes"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-MKT-08",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-MARKETS",stem:"Why may a government intervene in a market instead of relying entirely on the price mechanism?",options:{A:"Markets can create external costs or fail to provide socially important goods",B:"Prices never convey information",C:"Every market has exactly one seller",D:"Consumers have no influence on demand"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-MKT-08",scaffold:"Market failure occurs when private decisions do not produce the best outcome for society, for example pollution from production or under-provision of merit goods. Intervention may help, although government failure is also possible.",reforge:{stem:"Pollution from a factory harms nearby residents who are not involved in the sale. This is an example of:",options:{A:"A positive externality",B:"A negative externality and possible market failure",C:"A factor market clearing",D:"A benefit received only by the producer"},correct:"B"}}
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-ECON-P1-DS"] = {
  label: "Paper 1: Demand, Supply & Price",
  color: "#16a34a",
  questions: [
    {id:"GCSE-P1-DS-01",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS",stem:"The price of coffee rises and consumers buy less coffee, with no other change. What is this called?",options:{A:"A decrease in demand",B:"A contraction in quantity demanded - movement along the existing demand curve",C:"An increase in supply",D:"A shift of the demand curve right"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-DS-01",scaffold:"A price change causes a movement along a demand curve, changing quantity demanded. A decrease in demand means the whole curve shifts left because a non-price determinant changes, such as income, tastes or the price of a related good.",reforge:{stem:"Household incomes fall and fewer restaurant meals are wanted at every possible price. This is:",options:{A:"A contraction caused by a higher meal price",B:"A decrease in demand - the demand curve shifts left",C:"An extension of demand",D:"A shift of supply right"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-DS-02",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS",stem:"Which change would shift the demand curve for bus travel to the right?",options:{A:"A fall in the price of bus tickets",B:"A rise in the price of train travel, a substitute",C:"A rise in fuel costs for bus operators",D:"A fall in the number of bus routes"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-DS-02",scaffold:"A rise in the price of a substitute makes bus travel relatively more attractive, increasing demand for buses at each price. Ticket price itself causes movement along the curve; operator costs affect supply.",reforge:{stem:"If cars and petrol are complements, what is likely to happen to demand for petrol when the price of cars rises sharply?",options:{A:"Demand for petrol rises",B:"Demand for petrol falls because fewer cars are bought or used",C:"Supply of petrol shifts right",D:"There is no relationship"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-DS-03",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS",stem:"Which change is most likely to shift the supply curve for bread to the left?",options:{A:"Cheaper flour",B:"Improved oven technology",C:"A rise in baker wages and energy costs",D:"More bakeries entering the market"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-DS-03",scaffold:"Higher costs reduce the quantity firms are willing and able to supply at each price, shifting supply left. Lower input costs, better technology or more firms would tend to shift supply right.",reforge:{stem:"A government subsidy lowers the cost of producing solar panels. What happens to market supply, other things equal?",options:{A:"Supply shifts right",B:"Supply shifts left",C:"Quantity demanded falls along demand",D:"Demand becomes perfectly inelastic"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-DS-04",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS",stem:"The equilibrium price in a market is the price at which:",options:{A:"Quantity demanded equals quantity supplied",B:"Every consumer receives the same quantity",C:"Firms make the highest possible profit",D:"The government has fixed the price"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-DS-04",scaffold:"Equilibrium is the market-clearing position where planned purchases equal planned sales. It does not necessarily maximise profit or produce a fair distribution.",reforge:{stem:"If demand increases while supply is unchanged, what is the usual new equilibrium effect?",options:{A:"Lower price and lower quantity",B:"Higher price and higher quantity",C:"Higher price and lower quantity",D:"No change in either price or quantity"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-DS-05",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS",stem:"A shop sells 2,000 notebooks at £4 each. Its total revenue is:",options:{A:"£500",B:"£2,000",C:"£8,000",D:"£8"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-DS-05",scaffold:"Total revenue equals price multiplied by quantity sold: £4 x 2,000 = £8,000. Revenue is not the same as profit because costs have not yet been subtracted.",reforge:{stem:"A price falls from £5 to £4 and quantity sold rises from 1,000 to 1,400 units. What happens to total revenue?",options:{A:"It falls from £5,000 to £4,000",B:"It rises from £5,000 to £5,600",C:"It stays at £5,000",D:"It cannot be calculated"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-DS-06",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS",stem:"The price of a product rises by 10% and quantity demanded falls by 2%. The demand is:",options:{A:"Price elastic, with PED of -5",B:"Price inelastic, with PED of -0.2",C:"Unit elastic, with PED of 1",D:"Perfectly elastic"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-DS-06",scaffold:"PED = percentage change in quantity demanded divided by percentage change in price: -2% / 10% = -0.2. The absolute value is below 1, so demand is price inelastic. Because quantity changes proportionally less than price, revenue would tend to rise after the price increase.",reforge:{stem:"Demand has a PED of -2. Which description is correct?",options:{A:"It is price inelastic",B:"It is price elastic: quantity demanded responds proportionally more than price",C:"Demand does not respond to price",D:"Price and quantity must rise together"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-DS-07",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS",stem:"Which factor is likely to make the supply of new houses price inelastic in the short run?",options:{A:"Houses can be built instantly",B:"Planning restrictions and long construction times",C:"Large stocks of completed houses",D:"Perfectly mobile construction resources"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-DS-07",scaffold:"Low PES means quantity supplied changes by a smaller percentage than price. Construction takes time, land is limited and planning rules can restrict rapid expansion, so supply is relatively inelastic in the short run.",reforge:{stem:"A theatre has only 500 seats for tonight's performance. Why is seat supply likely to be perfectly inelastic for tonight?",options:{A:"The theatre can add unlimited seats when price rises",B:"The number of seats is fixed, so a higher price cannot increase tonight's capacity",C:"Demand is always inelastic",D:"The seats are a substitute for tickets"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-DS-08",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-DS",stem:"A maximum price is set below the equilibrium price for rented homes. What is the likely result if it is effective?",options:{A:"A surplus of homes",B:"A shortage because quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied",C:"Higher supply and lower demand",D:"No effect on the market"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-DS-08",scaffold:"A binding maximum price keeps rent below the market-clearing level. More people want homes, while landlords may supply fewer, creating excess demand. Non-price rationing such as queues may follow.",reforge:{stem:"A minimum price for farm produce is set above equilibrium and enforced. What is most likely?",options:{A:"A surplus because quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded",B:"A shortage of produce",C:"No change because the price is below equilibrium",D:"A shift in demand left only"},correct:"A"}}
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP"] = {
  label: "Paper 1: Competition",
  color: "#22c55e",
  questions: [
    {id:"GCSE-P1-CMP-01",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP",stem:"Which is a barrier to entry into the pharmaceutical industry?",options:{A:"A patent protecting a new medicine",B:"A large number of potential customers",C:"Low start-up costs",D:"Easy access to identical technology"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-CMP-01",scaffold:"A patent gives the holder legal protection from direct copying for a period, making entry harder. Other barriers include large capital requirements, economies of scale, strong brands and access to specialist skills.",reforge:{stem:"Why can economies of scale be a barrier to entry for a new supermarket?",options:{A:"An established large chain may have lower average costs and can price aggressively",B:"Small firms automatically receive bigger discounts",C:"Economies of scale increase every entrant's costs equally",D:"They prevent consumers from comparing prices"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-CMP-02",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP",stem:"Which is an example of non-price competition?",options:{A:"Cutting the selling price",B:"Offering a stronger after-sales service",C:"Reducing a product's tax",D:"Lowering a firm's wage bill"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-CMP-02",scaffold:"Non-price competition tries to attract customers through quality, branding, advertising, loyalty schemes, design or service rather than a lower price. Firms may use it to avoid a damaging price war.",reforge:{stem:"A supermarket launches a loyalty app with personalised offers but leaves shelf prices unchanged. This is mainly:",options:{A:"Non-price competition",B:"A price ceiling",C:"A fall in supply",D:"A barrier caused by a patent"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-CMP-03",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP",stem:"What is a likely benefit to consumers of more competition between firms?",options:{A:"Less choice and weaker service",B:"Greater pressure on firms to keep prices low or improve quality",C:"Guaranteed identical products",D:"No need for firms to innovate"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-CMP-03",scaffold:"Competition can benefit consumers through lower prices, more choice, improved quality and innovation. The size of these benefits depends on how easily consumers can switch and how many effective rivals exist.",reforge:{stem:"If several firms compete to supply broadband, which consumer benefit is most likely?",options:{A:"Firms face less pressure to improve speeds",B:"Consumers may gain from better service, choice or lower prices",C:"All firms must charge the same price",D:"The market becomes a monopoly immediately"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-CMP-04",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP",stem:"A market has one dominant seller and very high barriers to entry. Which concern is most relevant?",options:{A:"The firm may have less pressure to control prices or improve quality",B:"Consumers will always receive too much choice",C:"The firm cannot earn revenue",D:"Supply must be perfectly elastic"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-CMP-04",scaffold:"A monopoly or dominant firm may gain market power. It could exploit consumers through higher prices, restrict output or become X-inefficient, although large scale and investment may create some benefits.",reforge:{stem:"Which is a possible benefit of a natural monopoly in a network industry such as water?",options:{A:"Duplicating pipes by many firms may be wasteful, so one network can reduce average cost",B:"A monopoly can never invest",C:"Consumers automatically pay the lowest possible price",D:"There are no fixed costs"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-CMP-05",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP",stem:"A firm has 40% of sales in a market. What does this figure measure?",options:{A:"Its market share",B:"Its total revenue",C:"Its price elasticity",D:"Its average cost"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-CMP-05",scaffold:"Market share is a firm's sales as a percentage of total market sales, measured by value or volume. A high share may indicate market power but does not by itself prove that competition is weak.",reforge:{stem:"A market's four largest firms account for 80% of sales. This suggests:",options:{A:"A highly concentrated market with a small number of major firms",B:"A market with no barriers to entry",C:"Perfect competition",D:"That every firm has the same market share"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-CMP-06",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP",stem:"Why might firms use a loss-leading strategy?",options:{A:"To attract customers who may also buy profitable products",B:"To ensure every product is sold below cost forever",C:"To remove all fixed costs",D:"To reduce the size of their market"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-CMP-06",scaffold:"A loss leader is sold at a very low price, sometimes below cost, to attract customers and increase sales of other items. Its success depends on the extra purchases and whether competitors respond.",reforge:{stem:"A cinema offers very cheap tickets but charges high prices for drinks. The ticket is most likely acting as:",options:{A:"A loss leader to attract customers who may buy other products",B:"A fixed cost",C:"A barrier that prevents entry",D:"A supply-side tax"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-CMP-07",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP",stem:"Which is most likely to increase competition in a market?",options:{A:"Removing unnecessary restrictions that stop new firms entering",B:"Granting one firm an exclusive licence",C:"Making patents permanent",D:"Increasing switching costs for consumers"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-CMP-07",scaffold:"Lowering avoidable barriers can make entry easier and increase the number of rivals. Competition policy may also prevent anti-competitive mergers or abuse of market power.",reforge:{stem:"Why might a regulator block a merger between the two largest firms in a market?",options:{A:"It could reduce rivalry and allow the merged firm to raise prices or restrict choice",B:"It would guarantee lower prices",C:"It would make entry easier for every small firm",D:"Mergers cannot affect market power"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-CMP-08",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-COMP",stem:"A firm spends heavily on branding while keeping its price unchanged. What might the branding achieve?",options:{A:"It can differentiate the product and make demand less sensitive to price",B:"It makes the product identical to every rival",C:"It removes the firm's variable costs",D:"It prevents all consumer choice"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-CMP-08",scaffold:"Branding and product differentiation can create loyalty and make substitutes seem less close. This may give the firm some pricing power, although advertising is costly and competitors can imitate it.",reforge:{stem:"Which outcome is a possible drawback of intense advertising competition?",options:{A:"It can raise firms' costs and may persuade consumers without improving the product",B:"It always lowers average costs",C:"It guarantees perfect information",D:"It removes the need for product quality"},correct:"A"}}
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD"] = {
  label: "Paper 1: Production",
  color: "#4ade80",
  questions: [
    {id:"GCSE-P1-PRD-01",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD",stem:"Which cost is most likely to be fixed for a small furniture manufacturer in the short run?",options:{A:"Timber used for each table",B:"Electricity used by each machine",C:"Rent for the factory building",D:"Packaging for each item sold"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-PRD-01",scaffold:"Fixed costs do not change directly with output in the short run, such as rent or some insurance. Variable costs change as output changes, such as materials, packaging and power used in production.",reforge:{stem:"Which is a variable cost for a bakery?",options:{A:"Annual building insurance",B:"Flour used in each batch of bread",C:"Rent for the shop",D:"A fixed monthly licence fee"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-PRD-02",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD",stem:"A firm has fixed costs of £6,000 and variable costs of £4,000 at an output of 2,000 units. What is its average cost per unit?",options:{A:"£2",B:"£3",C:"£5",D:"£10"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-PRD-02",scaffold:"Total cost is fixed cost plus variable cost: £6,000 + £4,000 = £10,000. Average cost is total cost divided by output: £10,000 / 2,000 = £5 per unit.",reforge:{stem:"Total cost is £18,000 at an output of 3,000 units. What is average cost?",options:{A:"£5 per unit",B:"£6 per unit",C:"£15 per unit",D:"£54,000 per unit"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-PRD-03",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD",stem:"A business sells 500 units at £12 each and has total costs of £4,500. What is its profit?",options:{A:"£1,500",B:"£4,500",C:"£6,000",D:"£10,500"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-PRD-03",scaffold:"Revenue is price multiplied by quantity: £12 x 500 = £6,000. Profit is total revenue minus total cost: £6,000 - £4,500 = £1,500.",reforge:{stem:"A firm's revenue is £24,000 and total cost is £27,000. Which statement is correct?",options:{A:"It makes a £3,000 profit",B:"It makes a £3,000 loss",C:"It breaks even",D:"Its average cost is zero"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-PRD-04",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD",stem:"A factory produces 1,200 units using 6 workers. What is labour productivity?",options:{A:"6 units per worker",B:"200 units per worker",C:"1,206 units per worker",D:"7,200 units per worker"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-PRD-04",scaffold:"Labour productivity is output per worker: 1,200 / 6 = 200 units per worker. Higher productivity means more output is produced for each unit of labour input.",reforge:{stem:"Output rises from 900 to 1,200 units while the number of workers stays at 6. By how much does output per worker rise?",options:{A:"25 units",B:"50 units",C:"150 units",D:"300 units"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-PRD-05",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD",stem:"Which change is most likely to raise labour productivity?",options:{A:"Less training and older equipment",B:"Better worker training and improved technology",C:"More machine breakdowns",D:"Reducing output while keeping workers unchanged"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-PRD-05",scaffold:"Training raises human capital and technology can allow each worker to produce more. Productivity may also improve through specialisation, motivation, better management or investment.",reforge:{stem:"A firm introduces a safer, faster machine but keeps its workforce unchanged. The most likely immediate effect is:",options:{A:"Higher output per worker",B:"Lower labour productivity by definition",C:"No possible change in output",D:"A shift in consumer demand only"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-PRD-06",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD",stem:"Which is a purchasing economy of scale?",options:{A:"A large firm buys raw materials in bulk at a lower price per unit",B:"A firm hires a specialist manager",C:"A firm spreads risk across several products",D:"A firm borrows from a bank at a lower interest rate"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-PRD-06",scaffold:"Purchasing economies arise because large firms can negotiate bulk discounts. Other economies include technical, managerial, financial, marketing and risk-bearing economies.",reforge:{stem:"A large company can borrow at a lower interest rate than a small start-up because lenders see it as safer. This is a:",options:{A:"Financial economy of scale",B:"Diseconomy of scale",C:"Variable cost",D:"Negative externality"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-PRD-07",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD",stem:"What is a likely diseconomy of scale?",options:{A:"Bulk buying lowers input prices",B:"Communication problems make decisions slower as the organisation grows",C:"A bank offers a lower loan rate",D:"Specialist managers improve efficiency"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-PRD-07",scaffold:"Diseconomies of scale occur when average costs rise as a firm becomes too large. Coordination, communication, motivation and control can become more difficult.",reforge:{stem:"A company's average cost rises after it expands rapidly and managers struggle to coordinate many sites. This suggests:",options:{A:"Diseconomies of scale",B:"A purchasing economy",C:"A fall in fixed costs to zero",D:"Perfect competition"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-PRD-08",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-PROD",stem:"Why might a firm invest in employee wellbeing facilities even though they cost money?",options:{A:"They may improve morale, reduce absence and raise productivity",B:"They guarantee workers will never leave",C:"They turn all costs into revenue",D:"They reduce the need for any management"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-PRD-08",scaffold:"Wellbeing can motivate workers and reduce sickness or staff turnover. The benefit is not automatic: the effect depends on whether the productivity gain is greater than the cost.",reforge:{stem:"Which chain best explains how better working conditions could improve a firm's performance?",options:{A:"Improved morale -> lower absence or higher effort -> higher output per worker",B:"Higher morale -> unlimited resources -> no costs",C:"Better conditions -> lower demand -> higher revenue",D:"Higher wages -> no need for training ever"},correct:"A"}}
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR"] = {
  label: "Paper 1: The Labour Market",
  color: "#86efac",
  questions: [
    {id:"GCSE-P1-LAB-01",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR",stem:"Why is the demand for labour called derived demand?",options:{A:"It is derived from the demand for the goods and services labour helps produce",B:"It is set only by workers' leisure choices",C:"It is unrelated to a firm's output",D:"It is always higher than labour supply"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-LAB-01",scaffold:"Firms demand workers because workers help produce output that can be sold. If demand for the final product rises, the demand for the labour used to make it may rise too, though productivity and wage costs also matter.",reforge:{stem:"Demand for delivery drivers increases after online shopping becomes more popular. This is because:",options:{A:"Demand for labour is linked to demand for the delivery service",B:"Labour demand never depends on product demand",C:"Drivers are a factor of land",D:"Wages automatically fall when demand rises"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-LAB-02",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR",stem:"Which factor is likely to increase the supply of qualified nurses?",options:{A:"More funded training places and improved qualification opportunities",B:"Fewer people of working age",C:"Lower wages with no other change",D:"A ban on workers moving region"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-LAB-02",scaffold:"Labour supply depends on population, participation, skills, qualifications, wages, working conditions and mobility. Training can increase the number of workers able to enter a skilled occupation.",reforge:{stem:"Which change would most likely reduce the supply of construction workers to a particular city?",options:{A:"Better public transport into the city",B:"Higher wages and improved conditions",C:"A shortage of affordable housing near the city",D:"More local training courses"},correct:"C"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-LAB-03",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR",stem:"At the equilibrium wage in a labour market:",options:{A:"The quantity of labour demanded equals the quantity supplied",B:"Every worker earns the same wage",C:"There is no demand for the final product",D:"The government must employ every worker"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-LAB-03",scaffold:"The labour market uses demand and supply to determine an equilibrium wage and employment level. Different occupations can have different equilibria because their skills, demand and working conditions differ.",reforge:{stem:"If demand for software developers rises while supply is unchanged, the usual short-run effect is:",options:{A:"A lower wage and lower employment",B:"A higher equilibrium wage and employment",C:"No change because labour is not a market",D:"A fall in demand for software"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-LAB-04",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR",stem:"Why might specialist surgeons earn more than workers in occupations requiring fewer qualifications?",options:{A:"Training and qualification requirements restrict supply while demand for the skill is high",B:"Surgeons have no opportunity cost",C:"All high wages are set by law",D:"The supply of surgeons is unlimited"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-LAB-04",scaffold:"Wage differentials can reflect skill and qualification requirements, scarcity, demand, responsibility, risk, union strength and non-monetary advantages. A restricted supply with strong demand can raise wages.",reforge:{stem:"Why may a night-shift worker receive a wage premium?",options:{A:"Unsocial hours can reduce labour supply, so employers may pay more to attract workers",B:"Night work always requires no skill",C:"The premium proves supply is unlimited",D:"Demand for labour is zero at night"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-LAB-05",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR",stem:"A legal minimum wage is set below the equilibrium wage. What is its likely effect?",options:{A:"It is non-binding and may have little effect on the market wage",B:"It creates a guaranteed surplus of labour",C:"It makes employment illegal",D:"It always cuts employment"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-LAB-05",scaffold:"A minimum wage only changes the market if it is set above equilibrium, where it is binding. A lower-than-equilibrium minimum does not constrain the wage, although the law may still reassure workers.",reforge:{stem:"A binding minimum wage is set above the equilibrium wage. Which outcome is possible?",options:{A:"More people want to work than firms want to employ, creating excess labour supply",B:"Labour demand and supply both fall to zero",C:"Every firm hires more workers",D:"The equilibrium wage is forced lower"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-LAB-06",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR",stem:"Which is an example of geographical labour immobility?",options:{A:"A qualified worker cannot afford to move to a region with vacancies",B:"A worker learns a new skill",C:"A student changes their career preference",D:"A firm advertises a vacancy nationally"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-LAB-06",scaffold:"Geographical mobility is the ability to move location for work. Housing costs, family commitments, transport and regional information can prevent workers moving even when vacancies exist elsewhere.",reforge:{stem:"A worker is unwilling to move from retail into engineering because they lack the required qualifications. This is:",options:{A:"Occupational immobility",B:"Geographical mobility",C:"Derived demand",D:"A fixed cost"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-LAB-07",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR",stem:"Gross pay is £2,400 and deductions are £500. What is net pay?",options:{A:"£1,900",B:"£2,900",C:"£500",D:"£1,200"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-LAB-07",scaffold:"Net pay is what the worker receives after deductions such as income tax, National Insurance and pension contributions: £2,400 - £500 = £1,900. Gross pay is before deductions.",reforge:{stem:"A worker's gross monthly pay is £2,800. Tax and other deductions total £650. Net pay is:",options:{A:"£3,450",B:"£2,150",C:"£650",D:"£2,800"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-LAB-08",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-LABOUR",stem:"A professional footballer's international call-up makes the player's skills more valuable to clubs. In a labour-market diagram, this is most likely to:",options:{A:"Shift demand for the player's labour to the right",B:"Shift supply of all labour to the left",C:"Reduce the player's productivity",D:"Make wages unrelated to demand"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-LAB-08",scaffold:"If a worker becomes more valuable to employers, demand for that type of labour rises. With supply unchanged, the equilibrium wage and employment may increase. Demand for labour is affected by the value of marginal product and the demand for the final output.",reforge:{stem:"A new qualification makes a group of technicians more productive, while the number of qualified technicians is unchanged. What is likely?",options:{A:"Demand for their labour may rise because each worker can add more output",B:"Demand must fall because productivity is higher",C:"Supply becomes zero",D:"Wages cannot change"},correct:"A"}}
  ]
};

BANKS["GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY"] = {
  label: "Paper 1: Money & Financial Markets",
  color: "#bbf7d0",
  questions: [
    {id:"GCSE-P1-MNY-01",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY",stem:"Which function of money allows people to compare the prices of different goods?",options:{A:"Medium of exchange",B:"Unit of account",C:"Store of value only",D:"Method of taxation"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-MNY-01",scaffold:"A unit of account provides a common measure of value, making prices comparable. Money also acts as a medium of exchange, a store of value and a standard for deferred payment.",reforge:{stem:"When a shopper uses pounds to pay for a meal, money is acting mainly as:",options:{A:"A medium of exchange",B:"A barrier to trade",C:"A factor of production",D:"A production possibility frontier"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-MNY-02",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY",stem:"Why is money generally more convenient than barter?",options:{A:"It avoids the need for a double coincidence of wants",B:"It has no value to anyone",C:"It cannot be stored",D:"It must be exchanged only online"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-MNY-02",scaffold:"Barter requires each person to want exactly what the other offers at the same time. Money acts as an accepted medium of exchange, making transactions easier and supporting specialisation.",reforge:{stem:"Which feature is most important for money to work as a medium of exchange?",options:{A:"It is widely accepted by people in the economy",B:"It is impossible to divide",C:"Its value changes unpredictably every minute",D:"It can only be owned by banks"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-MNY-03",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY",stem:"A saver deposits £3,000 for one year at 4% simple interest. How much interest is earned?",options:{A:"£12",B:"£120",C:"£3,120",D:"£7,500"},correct:"B",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-MNY-03",scaffold:"Interest is 4% of £3,000: 0.04 x £3,000 = £120. The final balance would be £3,120 if no withdrawals or fees apply.",reforge:{stem:"A saver deposits £5,000 at 3% simple interest for one year. What is the final balance?",options:{A:"£5,015",B:"£5,150",C:"£5,300",D:"£6,500"},correct:"B"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-MNY-04",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY",stem:"What is a likely reason for borrowing money?",options:{A:"To pay for a house or business investment before enough income has been saved",B:"To guarantee that no interest is charged",C:"To make saving impossible",D:"To remove every financial risk"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-MNY-04",scaffold:"Borrowing allows households and firms to bring spending or investment forward, but interest and repayment create a cost. Whether borrowing is sensible depends on the purpose, affordability and risk.",reforge:{stem:"Which is a potential disadvantage of borrowing?",options:{A:"Interest and repayments reduce future disposable income",B:"Borrowing always increases future income",C:"Loans have no effect on financial risk",D:"Borrowing removes the need for a budget"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-MNY-05",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY",stem:"Why might a household save money?",options:{A:"To build a fund for future spending or protect against unexpected costs",B:"Because saving always gives a higher return than every investment",C:"To increase current spending immediately",D:"Because savings cannot earn interest"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-MNY-05",scaffold:"Saving postpones consumption and can provide security or fund a planned purchase. The reward may be interest, but inflation, tax and account conditions affect the real return.",reforge:{stem:"If the interest rate on savings rises, what is one likely effect?",options:{A:"Saving may become more attractive relative to immediate consumption",B:"Saving becomes impossible",C:"All households must save the same amount",D:"The value of money is guaranteed to rise faster than prices"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-MNY-06",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY",stem:"What is the main purpose of insurance?",options:{A:"To pool risk so an individual can receive compensation after a covered loss",B:"To guarantee that accidents never happen",C:"To make every investment profitable",D:"To remove all uncertainty from the economy"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-MNY-06",scaffold:"Insurance transfers and pools risk. Policyholders pay premiums, and the insurer pays valid claims under the policy terms. It reduces the financial impact of covered events but does not prevent them or cover every loss.",reforge:{stem:"Why can insurance encourage a person to buy a car?",options:{A:"Cover for certain losses reduces the financial risk of ownership",B:"Insurance makes fuel free",C:"Insurance guarantees the car will never be damaged",D:"Insurance removes the need to follow the law"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-MNY-07",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY",stem:"Which is a likely role of a bank in financial markets?",options:{A:"Taking deposits and lending some funds to borrowers",B:"Producing all physical goods in the economy",C:"Setting every worker's wage",D:"Removing the need for money"},correct:"A",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-MNY-07",scaffold:"Commercial banks help channel funds from savers to borrowers, provide payment services and assess credit risk. Lending is not free and banks must manage the risk that loans are not repaid.",reforge:{stem:"Why might a bank charge a higher interest rate to a borrower with a poor credit history?",options:{A:"The bank may judge the loan more risky and require compensation for the greater chance of default",B:"The borrower has no opportunity cost",C:"Banks do not consider risk",D:"Interest rates are unrelated to lending"},correct:"A"}},
    {id:"GCSE-P1-MNY-08",spec:"GCSE-ECON-P1-MONEY",stem:"Which characteristic would make an item a poor form of money?",options:{A:"It is durable and divisible",B:"It is widely accepted",C:"It spoils quickly and is difficult to divide",D:"It can be used to store value"},correct:"C",tag:"MC-GCSE-P1-MNY-08",scaffold:"Good money should be durable, portable, divisible, recognisable, limited in supply and generally accepted. Perishable or indivisible items are inconvenient for transactions and poor stores of value.",reforge:{stem:"Why are grains of rice an imperfect form of money?",options:{A:"They can be durable and portable in some conditions",B:"They may spoil, be difficult to standardise and be too plentiful",C:"They are accepted by every bank",D:"They have no possible value to the people who grow them"},correct:"B"}}
  ]
};

// ===== BUSINESS, CHEMISTRY, CRIMINOLOGY =====

BANKS["BUS-1"] = {
  label: "Marketing & People",
  color: "#b45309",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"BUS-01",stem:"A business moves from a product-orientated approach to a market-orientated approach. Which best describes the difference?",
      options:{A:"Product orientation focuses on price; market orientation focuses on quality.",B:"Product orientation develops goods based on what the business can make",C:"Market orientation only applies to services, not manufactured goods.",D:"There is no practical difference between the two approaches."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-BUS-01",
      scaffold:"Product orientation = 'we make it, they'll buy it' (Ford Model T — any colour as long as it's black). Market orientation = research what customers want, then produce it. Market orientation requires continuous market research, is more customer-led, more responsive to change but costlier to maintain. Edexcel examiners flag students who describe these as the same or confuse them with niche vs mass market.",
      reforge:{stem:"Apple's development of the iPhone involved extensive consumer research into what people wanted from a handheld device before designing the product. This is an example of:",options:{A:"Product orientation — Apple designed what they thought was best.",B:"Market orientation — Apple identified consumer needs and desires first, then developed the product around them.",C:"Niche marketing — Apple only targeted one segment.",D:"Mass marketing — Apple sold to everyone."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"BUS-02",stem:"A business has fixed costs of £200,000, a selling price of £50 per unit, and variable costs of £30 per unit. What is the break-even output?",
      options:{A:"4,000 units",B:"6,667 units",C:"10,000 units",D:"40,000 units"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-BUS-02",
      scaffold:"Break-even = Fixed Costs ÷ Contribution per unit. Contribution = Selling price − Variable cost = £50 − £30 = £20. Break-even = £200,000 ÷ £20 = 10,000 units. Common errors: dividing by price instead of contribution, or forgetting to subtract variable costs first. Always show the contribution step.",
      reforge:{stem:"Fixed costs rise to £240,000. Selling price and variable costs stay the same. What is the new break-even output?",options:{A:"10,000 units",B:"12,000 units (£240,000 ÷ £20 contribution)",C:"8,000 units",D:"4,800 units"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"BUS-03",stem:"A student writes: 'A price skimming strategy means the business sets the lowest possible price.' Why is this wrong?",
      options:{A:"It isn't — price skimming does mean setting the lowest price.",B:"Price skimming only applies to supermarkets.",C:"Price skimming and penetration pricing are the same strategy.",D:"Price skimming sets a HIGH initial price to target early adopters."},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-BUS-03",
      scaffold:"Skimming = high price initially → targets early adopters willing to pay a premium (e.g. new iPhone launches) → price falls as competition increases. Penetration = low price initially → gains market share quickly → price rises once established. Students frequently swap these. Remember: 'skim the cream off the top' = start high.",
      reforge:{stem:"Netflix entered the UK market in 2012 with a low monthly subscription price to attract subscribers quickly. This is an example of:",options:{A:"Price skimming — high initial price for early adopters.",B:"Penetration pricing — low initial price to rapidly gain market share before raising prices later.",C:"Predatory pricing — illegally undercutting competitors.",D:"Price discrimination — charging different customers different prices."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"BUS-04",stem:"Which of the following is a difference between a sole trader and a private limited company (Ltd)?",
      options:{A:"A private limited company has limited liability",B:"Sole traders pay more tax than Ltd companies.",C:"Only Ltd companies can make a profit.",D:"Sole traders must have at least two owners."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-BUS-04",
      scaffold:"Key distinctions: Sole trader — unlimited liability (personal assets at risk), simple to set up, all profit kept, no public accounts. Ltd — limited liability (shareholders protected), must file accounts at Companies House, shares can only be sold privately (not on stock exchange), more complex admin. Unlimited vs limited liability is the most-tested distinction.",
      reforge:{stem:"A sole trader's business fails with £80,000 in debts. The owner has personal savings of £50,000 and a car worth £15,000. What is the maximum the creditors can claim?",options:{A:"£80,000 — but only from business assets.",B:"£80,000 — from both business and personal assets, because unlimited liability means personal assets are not protected.",C:"£50,000 — only from personal savings.",D:"Nothing — the debt is written off when a business fails."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"BUS-05",stem:"Herzberg's two-factor theory distinguishes between hygiene factors and motivators. Which correctly identifies each?",
      options:{A:"Hygiene factors motivate workers and motivators prevent dissatisfaction (this is Herzberg reversed).",B:"Hygiene factors prevent dissatisfaction; motivators actively drive performance.",C:"Hygiene factors and motivators are the same concept in different words.",D:"Motivators are only relevant to managers, not shop floor workers."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-BUS-05",
      scaffold:"Herzberg: hygiene factors (salary, job security, working conditions, company policy) — adequate = no dissatisfaction, inadequate = dissatisfaction. They do NOT motivate. Motivators (achievement, recognition, the work itself, responsibility, advancement) — presence = motivation and job satisfaction. Implication: paying workers more stops them being unhappy but doesn't make them work harder. Contrast with Taylor (money motivates) and Maslow (hierarchy of needs).",
      reforge:{stem:"A company improves its canteen and increases salaries. According to Herzberg, what is the likely effect on staff motivation?",options:{A:"Motivation will increase significantly — better pay and facilities make staff work harder.",B:"Dissatisfaction may fall (hygiene factors improved) but motivation will not necessarily increase — the company needs to also address motivators like responsibility and recognition.",C:"No effect — Herzberg said nothing about pay.",D:"Motivation will fall because staff become complacent."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"BUS-06",stem:"Ansoff's Matrix shows four growth strategies. A business selling its existing product to a new market is pursuing:",
      options:{A:"Market penetration",B:"Product development",C:"Market development",D:"Diversification"},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-BUS-06",
      scaffold:"Ansoff Matrix: Market penetration = existing product, existing market (lowest risk). Market development = existing product, NEW market (e.g. expanding abroad). Product development = NEW product, existing market. Diversification = new product, new market (highest risk). Students frequently swap market development and product development. The key question: is the product new or existing?",
      reforge:{stem:"McDonald's launches a new plant-based burger to sell to its existing customers in its existing restaurants. This is an example of:",options:{A:"Market development — new market targeted.",B:"Product development — new product sold to the existing market.",C:"Diversification — new product and new market.",D:"Market penetration — same product, same market."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"BUS-07",stem:"A business has revenue of £500,000 and total costs of £420,000. What is the net profit margin?",
      options:{A:"84%",B:"80,000%",C:"19%",D:"16%"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-BUS-07",
      scaffold:"Net profit margin = (Net profit ÷ Revenue) × 100 = (£80,000 ÷ £500,000) × 100 = 16%. Net profit = Revenue − Total costs = £500,000 − £420,000 = £80,000. Common errors: dividing profit by costs instead of revenue, or confusing gross profit margin (which uses gross profit, not net profit).",
      reforge:{stem:"Revenue = £800,000. Cost of sales = £300,000. Other costs = £200,000. What is the gross profit margin?",options:{A:"37.5%",B:"62.5% (gross profit = £800k − £300k = £500k; margin = £500k ÷ £800k × 100)",C:"50%",D:"25%"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"BUS-08",stem:"Evaluate whether price is always the most important element of the marketing mix for a business. (12 marks)",
      type:"extended_answer",
      model_answer_outline:{
        KAA_strand_1:"Price affects demand directly via PED — for price-elastic goods (close substitutes, luxury items), competitive pricing is critical. A price above competitors loses sales quickly. Apply: budget airlines (Ryanair) where price dominates buying decisions.",
        KAA_strand_2:"However, for price-inelastic goods (necessities, strong brands), product quality and brand image (promotion) may matter more. Apply: Apple — customers pay premium prices because of perceived product superiority and brand loyalty, not because Apple has the lowest price.",
        EV_strand:"Depends on: (1) market type — commodity markets (petrol, wheat) compete mainly on price; differentiated markets compete on brand, quality, service; (2) target segment — price-sensitive consumers (budget shoppers) vs quality-conscious consumers; (3) product life cycle — price more important at maturity (competition peaks) than at launch (skimming strategy). Judgement: price is one of four interdependent elements — for most businesses, the optimal marketing mix balances all four, with price's relative importance varying by context."
      },
      tag:["MC-BUS-03","MC-TECH-02"],
      scaffold:"12-mark Business essays: AO1 (knowledge), AO2 (application to context/business), AO3 (evaluation — weigh the argument, consider counter-evidence, reach a judgement). Use specific businesses as examples, not generic descriptions."
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["CHEM-1"] = {
  label: "Atomic Structure & Bonding",
  color: "#7c3aed",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"CHEM-01",stem:"The relative atomic mass of chlorine is 35.5. A student says: 'This means chlorine atoms have 35.5 protons.' Why is this wrong?",
      options:{A:"Weighted mean mass of all naturally occurring isotopes relative to 1/12 of carbon-12.",B:"It isn't wrong — atoms can legitimately have fractional numbers of protons.",C:"Chlorine has a relative atomic mass of 35.5 because it has 35.5 neutrons.",D:"Relative atomic mass equals the number of electrons in the neutral atom."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-CHEM-01",
      scaffold:"Relative atomic mass (Ar) = weighted mean mass of naturally occurring isotopes relative to 1/12 of carbon-12. Chlorine: 75% 35Cl + 25% 37Cl → Ar = (0.75×35) + (0.25×37) = 35.5. Every individual chlorine ATOM has exactly 17 protons (atomic number = proton number = always whole). The fractional Ar reflects the isotopic mixture in a sample — not any individual atom.",
      reforge:{stem:"Bromine has two isotopes: 79Br (50.7%) and 81Br (49.3%). Calculate the relative atomic mass of bromine to 1 decimal place.",options:{A:"80.0",B:"79.5 (= (0.507×79) + (0.493×81) = 40.05 + 39.93 = 79.98 ≈ 80.0)",C:"80.5",D:"79.0"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CHEM-02",stem:"Which of the following correctly explains why the second ionisation energy of sodium is much higher than the first?",
      options:{A:"The second electron is removed from a higher energy level, further from the nucleus.",B:"The second electron is removed from a full inner shell (2p)",C:"Sodium has fewer protons after losing the first electron.",D:"The second ionisation energy is higher because the atom becomes negatively charged."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-CHEM-02",
      scaffold:"Na: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s¹. First IE removes the lone 3s¹ electron — outer shell, well-shielded, low energy. Second IE removes a 2p electron from the 2p⁶ sub-shell — this is an INNER shell, less shielded, much closer to the nucleus (higher nuclear attraction). The large jump between 1st and 2nd IE identifies sodium as Group 1 (one outer electron). This pattern of IE jumps is key to identifying group number.",
      reforge:{stem:"An element has successive ionisation energies (kJ/mol): 738, 1451, 7733, 10,540. Which group is this element in?",options:{A:"Group 1",B:"Group 2 — large jump occurs between 2nd and 3rd IE, indicating two outer-shell electrons are removed relatively easily before the inner shell is breached.",C:"Group 3",D:"Group 7"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CHEM-03",stem:"A student draws a dot-and-cross diagram for water (H₂O) with no lone pairs on the oxygen. What is wrong?",
      options:{A:"oxygen has no lone pairs in water.",B:"Water has three bonds, not two.",C:"Oxygen has 6 valence electrons: 2 are used in bonds with hydrogen",D:"Lone pairs are optional in dot-and-cross diagrams."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-CHEM-03",
      scaffold:"Water: O has 6 valence electrons. Shares 1 with each H (2 bonding pairs). Remaining 4 electrons = 2 lone pairs on O. VSEPR: 4 electron pairs around O (tetrahedral electron geometry) but 2 are lone pairs → bent/V-shaped molecule. Bond angle ≈ 104.5° (less than 109.5° because lone pairs repel more than bonding pairs). Without lone pairs, students predict 180° (linear) — wrong.",
      reforge:{stem:"Using VSEPR theory, predict the shape of NH₃ (ammonia). Nitrogen has 5 valence electrons.",options:{A:"Trigonal planar — three bonds, no lone pairs.",B:"Trigonal pyramidal — nitrogen forms 3 bonds (using 3 electrons) and has 1 lone pair remaining. Four electron pairs (3 bonding + 1 lone) → tetrahedral electron geometry → trigonal pyramidal molecular shape, bond angle ≈ 107°.",C:"Linear — like CO₂.",D:"Tetrahedral — same as methane."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CHEM-04",stem:"Which type of bonding explains why diamond has a very high melting point?",
      options:{A:"Metallic bonding — delocalised electrons hold the structure together.",B:"Ionic bonding — alternating positive and negative ions.",C:"Van der Waals forces — strongest when molecular mass is high.",D:"Covalent bonding in a giant covalent lattice"},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-CHEM-04",
      scaffold:"Giant covalent structures: diamond (C), silicon (Si), silicon dioxide (SiO₂). High MP because: MANY strong covalent bonds must be broken throughout the lattice — requires enormous energy. Contrast with simple molecular covalent (e.g. CO₂, H₂O) — LOW MP because only weak intermolecular forces (London dispersion, dipole-dipole, H-bonding) between molecules are broken. The bonds WITHIN molecules are not broken on melting.",
      reforge:{stem:"Iodine (I₂) has a much lower melting point than silicon dioxide (SiO₂), despite both being covalently bonded. The reason is:",options:{A:"I₂ has weaker covalent bonds within its molecules.",B:"I₂ is a simple molecular structure — melting only requires overcoming weak London dispersion forces between molecules. SiO₂ is a giant covalent lattice requiring thousands of strong covalent bonds to be broken.",C:"SiO₂ has ionic bonding that is stronger than covalent.",D:"Iodine atoms are lighter so the structure melts more easily."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CHEM-05",stem:"A student confuses the terms 'empirical formula' and 'molecular formula.' Which correctly distinguishes them?",
      options:{A:"Empirical formula shows the simplest whole-number ratio of atoms in a compound",B:"They are the same thing — different names for the same concept.",C:"Molecular formula is only used for gases.",D:"Empirical formula always has the same subscript numbers as the molecular formula."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-CHEM-05",
      scaffold:"Empirical formula = simplest ratio. Molecular formula = actual. They can be identical (e.g. H₂O, HCl) or different (e.g. ethene: molecular C₂H₄, empirical CH₂). To find molecular from empirical: divide the molar mass by the empirical formula mass, then multiply all subscripts. This is tested in every moles/calculation paper.",
      reforge:{stem:"A compound has an empirical formula of CH₂ and a molar mass of 56 g/mol. The empirical formula mass is 14 g/mol. What is the molecular formula?",options:{A:"CH₂",B:"C₄H₈ — (56 ÷ 14 = 4), so multiply CH₂ by 4.",C:"C₂H₄",D:"C₃H₆"},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CHEM-06",stem:"Why does fluorine have a higher electronegativity than chlorine, despite chlorine having more protons?",
      options:{A:"Fluorine has more electrons than chlorine.",B:"Fluorine has a smaller atomic radius",C:"Fluorine has a higher boiling point than chlorine.",D:"Chlorine's extra protons are cancelled out by its extra electrons."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-CHEM-06",
      scaffold:"Electronegativity increases across a period (more protons, same shielding) and decreases down a group (more shielding, larger radius). F is most electronegative element: small atom + low shielding + high effective nuclear charge → very strong pull on bonding electrons. Cl has 10 inner electrons shielding its nucleus; F only has 2. The shielding effect overwhelms the extra proton count.",
      reforge:{stem:"Down Group 7 (F → Cl → Br → I), electronegativity decreases because:",options:{A:"The number of protons decreases.",B:"Atomic radius increases and shielding from more inner electron shells increases — bonding electrons are further from the nucleus and less strongly attracted, reducing electronegativity.",C:"The halogens become less reactive.",D:"The number of valence electrons changes."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CHEM-07",stem:"In a mass spectrometer, what is detected to produce the mass spectrum?",
      options:{A:"The mass of neutral atoms.",B:"The colour of light emitted when atoms are heated.",C:"The mass-to-charge ratio (m/z) of positive ions",D:"The electrical charge of electrons in each atom."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-CHEM-07",
      scaffold:"Mass spectrometer steps: (1) Vaporise sample. (2) Ionise — high-energy electrons knock electrons off atoms → positive ions. (3) Accelerate through electric field. (4) Deflect in magnetic field — lighter/more charged ions deflect more. (5) Detect — ions hit detector, signal proportional to abundance. x-axis = m/z (mass-to-charge ratio), y-axis = relative abundance. Used to find Ar and identify isotopes.",
      reforge:{stem:"In a mass spectrum of a sample of neon, two peaks appear at m/z = 20 and m/z = 22. What does this tell us?",options:{A:"Neon exists as two different molecules.",B:"Neon has two naturally occurring isotopes — ²⁰Ne and ²²Ne — differing in the number of neutrons. The height of each peak indicates the relative abundance of each isotope.",C:"Neon has lost two electrons in the ionisation stage.",D:"The mass spectrometer is incorrectly calibrated."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CHEM-08",stem:"A student writes: 'Ionic compounds conduct electricity because they contain free electrons.' Why is this wrong?",
      options:{A:"It isn't — ionic compounds do contain free electrons.",B:"Ionic compounds never conduct electricity.",C:"Ionic compounds conduct through electron movement in the solid state only.",D:"Ionic compounds conduct when MOLTEN or in SOLUTION because the ions become free to move and carry charge — not because of free electrons."},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-CHEM-08",
      scaffold:"Conduction in ionic compounds: solid = no (ions fixed in lattice). Molten or dissolved = yes (ions free to move). Charge carriers = ions (not electrons). Contrast with metals: conduct via delocalised electrons in all states. Contrast with graphite: conducts via delocalised electrons within layers. This distinction — ions vs electrons as charge carriers — is tested frequently.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which correctly explains why metals conduct electricity in the solid state but ionic compounds do not?",options:{A:"Metals have stronger bonds than ionic compounds.",B:"Metals contain a 'sea' of delocalised electrons that can move freely through the lattice in all states. Ionic compounds have no free electrons — in the solid state, ions are fixed and cannot carry charge.",C:"Metals have a higher melting point.",D:"Ionic compounds have more electrons than metals."},correct:"B"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["CRIM-1"] = {
  label: "Changing Awareness of Crime",
  color: "#374151",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"CRIM-01",stem:"A student defines 'crime' as 'anything that is morally wrong.' Why is this sociologically imprecise?",
      options:{A:"Crime is an act that violates criminal law and carries formal state sanctions.",B:"crime and immorality are the same thing.",C:"Crime is defined by victims, not the state.",D:"Morality is irrelevant to criminology."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-CRIM-01",
      scaffold:"Crime = behaviour that violates criminal law + attracts formal state sanctions. Key points: (1) relative — what counts as crime changes over time (homosexuality was criminalised in the UK until 1967) and across place (cannabis is legal in some US states, illegal in others). (2) Not the same as deviance (violating norms, may be informal) or immorality (violating ethical standards). WJEC expects precise definitional distinctions.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which of the following illustrates that crime is a socially constructed concept?",options:{A:"All societies agree that murder is a crime.",B:"The same act (e.g. possession of cannabis) is a criminal offence in one country but legal and regulated in another — showing that what counts as crime is determined by social and legal context, not by any universal standard.",C:"Crime rates are measured using official statistics.",D:"Criminals are born with genetic predispositions to offend."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CRIM-02",stem:"What does the 'dark figure of crime' refer to?",
      options:{A:"Crime committed during night-time hours that goes unwitnessed.",B:"The dark figure: the gap between actual crime and crime officially recorded.",C:"Crime committed by offenders whose identity is unknown to police.",D:"Violent crime that is especially severe or causes lasting harm."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-CRIM-02",
      scaffold:"Dark figure = crime that doesn't appear in official statistics because: victim doesn't report (embarrassment, fear, distrust of police), police don't record it (discretion, administrative decisions), no victim to report (drug offences, white-collar crime). This is why official statistics undercount crime. Victim surveys (e.g. Crime Survey for England and Wales) try to measure the dark figure by asking people directly about their experiences.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which type of crime is MOST likely to contribute to the dark figure, and why?",options:{A:"Murder — because bodies are hard to hide.",B:"Domestic abuse — victims often don't report due to fear of the perpetrator, shame, economic dependence, or lack of confidence that the police will help, meaning much of it never enters official statistics.",C:"Speeding — cameras don't catch everyone.",D:"Shoplifting — easy to detect on CCTV."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CRIM-03",stem:"Which of the following is a limitation of official crime statistics as a measure of crime?",
      options:{A:"They include too much data from victim surveys.",B:"They are updated too frequently to be reliable.",C:"They only measure crimes that are reported to and recorded by the police",D:"They overcount crime because police are motivated to record everything."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-CRIM-03",
      scaffold:"OCS limitations: (1) dark figure — much crime unreported. (2) Police discretion — not all reported crimes are recorded (counting rules, targets). (3) Social construction — what gets counted reflects police priorities, not reality. (4) Exclude victimless crimes. Strengths: consistent methodology over time, allow comparisons, free, cover all recorded crimes. WJEC expects balanced evaluation of sources.",
      reforge:{stem:"The Crime Survey for England and Wales (CSEW) is considered to give a more complete picture of crime than official police statistics because:",options:{A:"It only measures violent crime accurately.",B:"It asks a representative sample of the population directly about crimes they have experienced, including those not reported to the police — capturing part of the dark figure that OCS miss.",C:"It is produced by the government so must be accurate.",D:"It includes data from court convictions."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CRIM-04",stem:"A criminologist argues that media representations of crime create a 'moral panic.' By this they mean:",
      options:{A:"The media accurately reflects the true level and distribution of crime in society.",B:"The public feel morally responsible and guilty when they read about crime.",C:"Crime only increases at times when media coverage of crime intensifies.",D:"The media overrepresents certain crimes, creating exaggerated public fear and distorted perception."},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-CRIM-04",
      scaffold:"Moral panic (Cohen, 1972 — Mods and Rockers): media exaggerates threat → public fear disproportionate to actual risk → demands for action → police/courts respond more harshly → potentially amplifies the problem (deviancy amplification spiral). 'Folk devils' = the group labelled as the threat (e.g. youth gangs, asylum seekers, benefit claimants). Relevant to WJEC Unit 2 on media and crime.",
      reforge:{stem:"Following a moral panic about knife crime, police stop-and-search operations increase significantly, leading to more knife-crime arrests. A criminologist argues this data doesn't prove knife crime increased. Why?",options:{A:"Stop and search is always ineffective.",B:"More searches = more chances to find knives = more arrests. The rise in recorded offences may reflect increased police activity rather than an actual rise in knife crime — this is deviancy amplification, where the response to a moral panic creates evidence that appears to confirm the original panic.",C:"The statistics are deliberately falsified.",D:"Knife crime only occurs in urban areas."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CRIM-05",stem:"Which of the following best describes 'white-collar crime'?",
      options:{A:"Non-violent crime by high-status individuals in the course of their occupation.",B:"Crime committed by people in professional dress or formal business attire.",C:"Any crime that takes place in an office building or corporate environment.",D:"Any crime that does not involve direct physical violence, regardless of status."},
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-CRIM-05",
      scaffold:"Sutherland (1949) coined 'white-collar crime' — crime by the powerful in their professional roles. Examples: financial fraud (Bernie Madoff), corporate manslaughter (Grenfell), tax avoidance/evasion, bribery, insider trading. Under-represented in OCS because: complex, harder to detect, powerful offenders have resources to avoid prosecution, treated more leniently. WJEC links this to inequality and selective law enforcement.",
      reforge:{stem:"White-collar crime is under-represented in official statistics compared to street crime. Which factor MOST explains this?",options:{A:"White-collar crime causes less harm than street crime.",B:"White-collar crime is harder to detect, victims may not know they've been defrauded, perpetrators have legal resources to avoid prosecution, and agencies may lack expertise or motivation to pursue powerful offenders — reflecting selective enforcement.",C:"Police deliberately protect rich offenders.",D:"White-collar crime is legal in most cases."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CRIM-06",stem:"According to strain theory (Merton), crime occurs because:",
      options:{A:"Some people are biologically predisposed to criminality.",B:"Merton's strain theory — approved goals with no legitimate means to reach them.",C:"Criminal values are learned through socialisation in communities.",D:"Inadequate policing allows crime to flourish."},
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-CRIM-06",
      scaffold:"Merton's strain theory: American Dream = everyone can succeed (meritocracy). Reality = working-class and minority groups lack access to education/jobs. Strain = gap between cultural goals and structural means. Adaptations: Conformity (accept both), Innovation (adopt goals, reject means → crime), Ritualism, Retreatism (reject both), Rebellion. Innovation explains property crime among the poor. Evaluation: ignores white-collar crime, gender, doesn't explain why only some 'innovate'.",
      reforge:{stem:"A young person from a deprived area cannot access university due to low income and poor schooling but still wants the material success they see advertised. According to Merton, if they turn to drug dealing, they are adopting which adaptation?",options:{A:"Ritualism — they keep trying through legitimate means.",B:"Innovation — they accept the cultural goal of material success but replace legitimate means (education, employment) with illegitimate ones (drug dealing) due to blocked opportunities.",C:"Retreatism — they give up on both goals and means.",D:"Rebellion — they reject society's goals entirely."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CRIM-07",stem:"A student answers a WJEC Criminology question about causes of crime using only one theory. What is the examiner likely to penalise?",
      options:{A:"one theory is sufficient for any answer.",B:"The student for using a theory at all.",C:"Lack of range and evaluation",D:"Answers that mention sociological theories."},
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-TECH-02",
      scaffold:"WJEC Criminology: assessment requires AO1 (knowledge), AO2 (application), AO3 (analysis/evaluation). Higher bands require considering competing explanations, weighing evidence, and reaching a reasoned conclusion. Single-theory answers are capped. A strong answer might compare strain theory with subcultural theory and biological approaches, noting what each explains and what each fails to account for.",
      reforge:{stem:"'Evaluate biological explanations for criminal behaviour.' A top-band answer would:",options:{A:"Describe Lombroso's theory in great detail.",B:"Explain biological theories (genetics, brain structure, hormones), apply evidence (twin studies, XYY research), then evaluate by comparing with sociological alternatives — noting biological determinism ignores social context and that correlation is not causation.",C:"Argue that biology proves criminals are born, not made.",D:"List all possible causes of crime without evaluating any."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CRIM-08",stem:"Situational crime prevention (SCP) aims to reduce crime by:",
      options:{A:"Rehabilitating offenders through prison education.",B:"Addressing the root social causes of crime such as poverty and inequality.",C:"Increasing police patrol numbers in high-crime areas.",D:"Altering the environment to increase the effort and risk of offending."},
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-CRIM-08",
      scaffold:"SCP (Clarke, 1980s) based on rational choice theory — offenders make cost-benefit calculations. Reduce opportunity → reduce crime. Techniques: target hardening (locks, safes), increase surveillance (CCTV), reduce anonymity (name badges), deflect offenders (youth clubs). Evaluation: displacement (crime moves elsewhere, not reduced overall), ignores causes, class-based (protects wealthy areas better). Contrast with social/community crime prevention which addresses underlying causes.",
      reforge:{stem:"A criticism of situational crime prevention is that it may cause 'displacement.' By this, critics mean:",options:{A:"CCTV cameras are moved to other locations.",B:"Crime is not eliminated but merely shifted to other locations, times, or types of offence where prevention measures are weaker — overall levels of crime may not fall.",C:"Offenders are displaced into prison.",D:"Victims move away from high-crime areas."},correct:"B"}
    }
  ]
};

