// ===== MEDIA STUDIES BANK 1 — Theoretical Framework =====

BANKS["MEDIA-1"] = {
  label: "Theoretical Framework",
  color: "#7c2d12",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"MED-01",
      stem:"Stuart Hall's encoding/decoding model argues that audiences:",
      options:{
        A:"Passively receive the single meaning intended by the producer.",
        B:"Actively decode media texts, and the meaning created depends on the viewer's cultural background — producers encode a preferred reading, but audiences may adopt a negotiated or oppositional reading.",
        C:"Always reject the preferred reading of media texts.",
        D:"Decode messages identically regardless of their social position."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MED-01",
      scaffold:"Hall (1980) encoding/decoding: producers encode a preferred reading using dominant ideology. Audiences decode from their own cultural position: (1) Dominant/hegemonic — accepts preferred meaning fully; (2) Negotiated — partially accepts, modifies from own position; (3) Oppositional — rejects preferred reading, substitutes their own. Key point: meaning is not fixed in the text but created in the interaction between text and reader. Exam: apply this when analysing audience response, not just representation.",
      reforge:{stem:"A news broadcast frames immigration negatively. A viewer who is themselves an immigrant rejects this framing and sees the report as biased. Hall would describe this as:",options:{A:"A dominant reading — they accepted the preferred meaning.",B:"An oppositional reading — the viewer's social position (as an immigrant) leads them to reject the preferred meaning and substitute an alternative interpretation based on their own experience.",C:"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.",D:"Evidence that media texts have no fixed meaning at all."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"MED-02",
      stem:"Curran and Seaton's power without responsibility model argues that:",
      options:{
        A:"Media diversity increases as ownership concentrates.",
        B:"The concentration of media ownership in the hands of a few large conglomerates limits the range of ideas and viewpoints in circulation, serving the interests of powerful owners rather than the public.",
        C:"Public service broadcasting is the greatest threat to media diversity.",
        D:"Media owners have no influence over editorial content."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MED-02",
      scaffold:"Curran & Seaton: media ownership concentrated in a few powerful companies (News Corp, Disney, Comcast). This concentration: reduces diversity of voices; creates commercial pressure prioritising profit over quality/public interest; enables owners to influence editorial line (Murdoch and The Sun's political positions). BUT: Curran also notes digital media has created some diversity (YouTube, podcasting). Hesmondhalgh critique: market logic dominates cultural industries regardless of ownership structure. Exam: evaluate — does concentration always limit diversity?",
      reforge:{stem:"A media conglomerate that owns a news channel, a film studio and several newspapers is an example of:",options:{A:"Vertical integration — owning multiple stages of the same production chain only.",B:"Conglomeration — ownership across multiple different media sectors, creating cross-promotional synergy and market dominance. Curran & Seaton argue this limits diversity by concentrating power in fewer hands.",C:"Horizontal integration — acquiring competitors at the same level of production only.",D:"Public service broadcasting — a regulatory obligation rather than an ownership model."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"MED-03",
      stem:"Gerbner's cultivation theory argues that:",
      options:{
        A:"Television audiences are entirely immune to media influence.",
        B:"Long-term, heavy exposure to television cultivates a shared worldview — heavy viewers develop a distorted perception of social reality that more closely matches the TV world than actual social statistics, particularly regarding crime and danger (the 'mean world syndrome').",
        C:"Media effects are immediate and direct — one violent programme causes violent behaviour.",
        D:"Cultivation only affects children, not adult viewers."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MED-03",
      scaffold:"Gerbner (1970s+) cultivation theory: TV is the dominant storytelling medium. Heavy viewers (4+ hours/day) develop TV-cultivated perceptions: overestimate crime rates, police violence prevalence, and physical danger. 'Mean world syndrome' — heavy viewers more fearful, more conservative about crime. First-order effects (facts) and second-order effects (values/attitudes). Mainstreaming — heavy viewing homogenises diverse groups toward TV's dominant values. Criticisms: correlation not causation; people choose programmes (reinforcement not cultivation); ignores active audience; pre-digital TV environment.",
      reforge:{stem:"Research finds heavy TV viewers consistently overestimate the percentage of the population who are criminals. Gerbner would explain this as:",options:{A:"Heavy viewers being less educated and therefore more fearful.",B:"Cultivation — heavy TV exposure leads viewers to perceive social reality as matching TV's overrepresentation of crime, producing first-order cultivation effects on factual beliefs about the world.",C:"Evidence that media has no effect on attitudes, only on factual knowledge.",D:"The result of watching news rather than drama."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"MED-04",
      stem:"In media studies, 'representation' means:",
      options:{
        A:"How accurately media texts depict real-world statistics about social groups.",
        B:"The process by which media construct versions of reality — selecting, combining and presenting elements to produce meanings about social groups, events and ideas. Representations are constructions, not reflections, of reality.",
        C:"The legal obligation for broadcasters to include diverse characters.",
        D:"The number of minority groups shown in a media text."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MED-04",
      scaffold:"Representation: media don't reflect reality — they RE-present it through selection and construction. Key ideas: (1) Re-presentation — same reality can be constructed differently (tabloid vs broadsheet coverage of same event). (2) Stereotyping — shortcut representations that can reinforce or challenge ideological assumptions. (3) Power — who controls representation? Whose perspective is centred? (4) Stuart Hall's 'the spectacle of the other' — difference is marked, exoticised, othered. Exam always asks for application to specific texts — don't just define, analyse HOW the text constructs representation.",
      reforge:{stem:"Two newspapers cover the same protest: one uses the headline 'Passionate campaigners demand action' and another uses 'Troublemakers disrupt city'. This illustrates which concept?",options:{A:"Cultivation — both papers are shaping long-term attitudes about protest.",B:"Re-presentation — the same event is constructed differently through language choices, encoding different preferred readings about protesters and their legitimacy. Neither is a neutral 'reflection' of the event.",C:"Hypodermic needle theory — both headlines directly cause readers to hold fixed views.",D:"Conglomeration — both papers are owned by the same company."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"MED-05",
      stem:"Livingstone and Lunt's critique of regulation argues that:",
      options:{
        A:"Media regulation is unnecessary in a digital age.",
        B:"Traditional media regulation models — designed for broadcast media with clear national gatekeepers — are inadequate for converged, digital, global media environments where audiences are also producers and national boundaries are meaningless.",
        C:"Ofcom is effective at regulating all forms of digital media.",
        D:"Self-regulation by media companies always protects the public interest."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MED-05",
      scaffold:"Livingstone & Lunt (2011): traditional regulation framework built on broadcast model (few producers, many passive consumers, national transmission). Digital convergence undermines this: (1) Audiences become producers (UGC — YouTube, TikTok); (2) Global platforms (Netflix, Google) operate across jurisdictions; (3) Algorithmic curation not 'editorial' in traditional sense; (4) Speed of content creation outpaces regulatory response. Regulatory bodies: Ofcom (UK broadcasting + telecoms), IPSO (press), BBFC (film classification), ASA (advertising). OSA 2023 — new attempt to regulate online harms. Exam: evaluate whether any regulatory model can address these challenges.",
      reforge:{stem:"A teenager is exposed to harmful content via a recommendation algorithm on a social media platform. This situation most clearly illustrates:",options:{A:"The continuing effectiveness of the hypodermic needle model for digital platforms.",B:"Livingstone and Lunt's argument that traditional national regulation cannot govern global digital platforms operating across jurisdictions.",C:"The need to return to public service broadcasting as the dominant media model.",D:"Cultivation theory — long-term algorithmic exposure shapes users' worldview."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"MED-06",
      stem:"Van Zoonen's feminist media theory argues that:",
      options:{
        A:"Women are never represented positively in media texts.",
        B:"Gender is a cultural construction performed and perpetuated through representation — media texts produce and reproduce gendered identities by consistently presenting femininity and masculinity in particular ways, often through the 'male gaze'.",
        C:"Media representation has no impact on real-world gender inequality.",
        D:"Only texts produced by women can represent gender fairly."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MED-06",
      scaffold:"Van Zoonen (1994) feminist media theory: gender is not natural but constructed through cultural practices including media representation. The 'male gaze' (Mulvey — applied to film originally): camera positions women as spectacle for male viewer's pleasure. Women coded as objects rather than subjects. BUT: Van Zoonen acknowledges female audiences actively resist and renegotiate representations. Contemporary relevance: online content creation, OnlyFans debate — exploitation or empowerment? Intersectionality (Crenshaw) — gender intersects with race, class in representations. Exam: avoid stating all representation is negative — evaluate complexity.",
      reforge:{stem:"A music video features a female artist singing from a stationary position while the camera repeatedly focuses on her body in fragmented close-ups. Van Zoonen and Mulvey would analyse this as:",options:{A:"Empowering representation — the female artist is the centre of attention.",B:"An example of the male gaze — the camera's fragmentation of the female body positions her as visual spectacle for a presumed male viewer's pleasure rather than as a subject with agency, reproducing a patriarchal representation of femininity.",C:"Negotiated representation — audiences will interpret this differently.",D:"Evidence of increasing female representation in the music industry."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"MED-07",
      stem:"The 'hypodermic needle' model of media effects assumes:",
      options:{
        A:"Media effects are complex, mediated by audience factors.",
        B:"Media messages are injected directly into passive audiences who absorb them uncritically, producing immediate, uniform behavioural effects — a simple, direct cause-and-effect relationship.",
        C:"Audiences actively resist media influence.",
        D:"Long-term exposure to media gradually shapes worldview."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MED-07",
      scaffold:"Hypodermic needle (magic bullet) model: early effects theory (1920s-40s). Assumes: audiences are passive, homogeneous, isolated from social ties; media has direct, powerful, immediate effects. Associated with: Bandura's Bobo doll experiments (children imitated modelled aggression); moral panics about film violence. Criticisms: (1) audiences are NOT passive — two-step flow (Katz & Lazarsfeld), active audience theory; (2) social context mediates effects; (3) laboratory conditions are artificial; (4) correlation ≠ causation. Modern relevance: still cited in political debates (video games/violence) despite weak evidence. Exam: most useful as a model to critique rather than apply.",
      reforge:{stem:"Research attempting to prove that violent video games cause violent behaviour repeatedly fails to establish a causal link. This most directly undermines:",options:{A:"Cultivation theory — which also relies on long-term effects.",B:"The hypodermic needle model — which predicts direct, measurable behavioural effects from media exposure. The absence of clear causal evidence suggests audiences are not the passive receptacles this model assumes.",C:"Uses and gratifications theory — which predicts people choose violent games to meet aggressive needs.",D:"Reception theory — which predicts all audiences read games the same way."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"MED-08",
      stem:"Blumler and Katz's uses and gratifications theory argues that:",
      options:{
        A:"Media producers control how audiences use and interpret texts.",
        B:"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.",
        C:"Audiences are passive and their gratifications are manufactured by the media industry.",
        D:"Uses and gratifications only applies to news media, not entertainment."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MED-08",
      scaffold:"Blumler & Katz (1974) uses and gratifications: shifts focus from 'what does media DO to audiences?' to 'what do audiences DO with media?' Four needs: (1) Diversion/escape — entertainment, relaxation; (2) Personal relationships — companionship, social interaction via media (parasocial relationships); (3) Personal identity — finding values and role models in texts; (4) Surveillance/information — understanding the world. Modern examples: binge-watching (escape), following influencers (parasocial), using TikTok for news. Criticism: assumes audiences are always rational and purposeful; ignores ideological effects; ignores how industry shapes 'choice'.",
      reforge:{stem:"A student watches a reality TV show featuring young people from a similar background and identifies with a contestant who overcomes adversity. Uses and gratifications would explain this as:",options:{A:"Cultivation — long-term viewing has shaped the student's worldview.",B:"The personal identity function — the student is actively using the text to explore and reinforce their own values and sense of self by identifying with a character whose experiences resonate with their own.",C:"Diversion — the student is using the text purely for escapism.",D:"Evidence of the hypodermic needle effect — the show is modelling behaviour."},correct:"B"}
    }
  ]
};

// ===== MEDIA STUDIES BANK 2 — Industry & Audience =====

BANKS["MEDIA-2"] = {
  label: "Industry & Audience",
  color: "#7c2d12",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"MED-09",
      stem:"In media industry terms, 'vertical integration' means:",
      options:{
        A:"A company expanding into the same level of production as its competitors.",
        B:"A media company owning businesses at multiple stages of the same production and distribution chain — for example, a studio that produces films, owns the distribution network and operates the cinemas that screen them.",
        C:"A company merging with a rival to reduce competition at the same production level.",
        D:"The process of distributing content vertically down from producers to passive audiences."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MED-09",
      scaffold:"Vertical integration: ownership across different stages of one supply chain (production → distribution → exhibition). Example: Disney produces content (studio), distributes it (Disney+, theatrical), and licenses merchandise — controlling the whole chain. Benefits: cost efficiencies, control over release windows and pricing, guaranteed outlets. Risks: regulatory scrutiny (anti-competition). Contrast with horizontal integration: buying competitors at the SAME level (Disney acquiring 21st Century Fox — both studios). Exam: always specify which type and why it matters for diversity/power.",
      reforge:{stem:"A record label that records artists, distributes their music through its own streaming platform, and books them into venues it owns is an example of:",options:{A:"Horizontal integration — it operates at multiple levels of the same market.",B:"Vertical integration — the label controls production (recording), distribution (streaming) and exhibition (venues), owning the entire supply chain from creation to audience delivery.",C:"Conglomeration — it owns businesses in unrelated industries.",D:"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"MED-10",
      stem:"The concept of 'synergy' in the media industry refers to:",
      options:{
        A:"The tension between public service and commercial broadcasting models.",
        B:"The mutual promotion of products across a conglomerate's holdings — where one product markets another across different media platforms, multiplying revenue from a single IP.",
        C:"The process of audiences creating their own media content in response to industry products.",
        D:"The regulatory requirement for balanced coverage across different media outlets."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MED-10",
      scaffold:"Synergy: when a conglomerate's various arms reinforce each other commercially. Example: Marvel Cinematic Universe — Disney produces the film (studio), releases on Disney+ (streaming), sells merchandise through Disney stores, promotes via ABC News (TV), covers in Marvel comics (publishing). Each outlet promotes the others — the whole is worth more than the sum of its parts. This is why conglomerates acquire across media types, not just depth. Exam link: synergy benefits large conglomerates, making it harder for independent producers to compete — connects to Curran & Seaton's diversity concerns.",
      reforge:{stem:"Disney releases a new superhero film, simultaneously launching a Disney+ tie-in series, a video game, and a toy line. Which media industry concept best describes this strategy?",options:{A:"Convergence — different technologies delivering the same content.",B:"Synergy — the conglomerate uses its multiple holdings (film studio, streaming platform, games, retail) to cross-promote a single IP, maximising revenue while each arm markets the others.",C:"Vertical integration — Disney controls production and distribution of all these products.",D:"Public service broadcasting — the content is made available across platforms."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"MED-11",
      stem:"Which statement best describes the concept of 'media convergence'?",
      options:{
        A:"The trend for media companies to produce content exclusively for a single platform.",
        B:"The merging of previously distinct media technologies, industries and content forms — so that a single device (smartphone) can deliver television, radio, newspapers, social media and interactive games, collapsing former boundaries between media types.",
        C:"The process by which audiences become more homogeneous in their media consumption habits.",
        D:"The regulatory requirement for broadcasters to serve all sections of the audience equally."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MED-11",
      scaffold:"Convergence (Jenkins): technological — devices deliver multiple media types (phone = TV + radio + newspaper + camera). Industrial — companies merge across formerly separate sectors (telecoms + media + tech: Apple, Google, Amazon all now media companies). Content — formats blend (podcast + live event + YouTube series). Cultural — audiences move fluidly between platforms for the same story (transmedia storytelling). Consequences: disrupts traditional regulatory boundaries; enables new forms of audience participation; challenges business models of legacy media (print, linear TV). Livingstone & Lunt: regulation designed for distinct sectors can't keep up with converged landscape.",
      reforge:{stem:"A viewer watches a live football match on their phone, simultaneously discussing it on social media and accessing statistics via a club app. This scenario illustrates:",options:{A:"Vertical integration — multiple platforms owned by one company.",B:"Media convergence — a single device delivers formerly separate media experiences (television, social media, data journalism) simultaneously, and the viewer participates rather than passively consuming.",C:"Cultivation theory — heavy media use shaping perception of football culture.",D:"Uses and gratifications — the viewer is passive and absorbing the content."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"MED-12",
      stem:"Public service broadcasting (PSB) obligations in the UK require broadcasters to:",
      options:{
        A:"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.",
        B:"Serve the public interest by providing informative, educational and culturally diverse content — including programmes that would not be commercially viable — and ensuring universal access regardless of ability to pay.",
        C:"Remain entirely funded by advertising and subscription revenue.",
        D:"Broadcast only content produced in the United Kingdom."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MED-12",
      scaffold:"PSB in the UK: BBC (licence fee funded), ITV/Channel 4/Channel 5 (commercial but with PSB obligations). Core PSB requirements: universality of access; range and diversity of content; information, education and entertainment; regional programming; content that would be commercially unviable (minority languages, disability programming, arts). Regulatory body: Ofcom. BBC Charter sets obligations. Debates: is PSB still relevant in a streaming era? Netflix/Amazon/YouTube provide choice without PSB obligations. Charter renewal debates (BBC 2027): should licence fee model continue? Criticism from the right: BBC has political bias. Criticism from the left: cuts threaten diverse programming.",
      reforge:{stem:"A Channel 4 documentary about life in a deprived Yorkshire town receives low ratings but wins a BAFTA and is praised for giving voice to underrepresented communities. This is best understood as:",options:{A:"Evidence that Channel 4 is poorly managed — low-rated content should be cancelled.",B:"Channel 4 fulfilling its PSB remit — producing content of cultural value that serves underrepresented audiences and would be unlikely to be made by a purely commercial broadcaster focused solely on ratings and profit.",C:"An example of synergy — the BAFTA award drives subscriptions.",D:"Uses and gratifications — the small audience chose this content to satisfy specific needs."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"MED-13",
      stem:"In Hesmondhalgh's analysis of cultural industries, the 'cultural discount' refers to:",
      options:{
        A:"The reduced price at which media companies sell content to developing nations.",
        B:"The reduction in value or appeal a cultural product suffers when exported to a different cultural context — local references, humour and cultural specificity make content less appealing to overseas audiences, explaining why US content often dominates global markets (it is made for a large domestic market and engineered for broad appeal).",
        C:"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost.",
        D:"The financial discount applied to public service content that receives subsidy."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MED-13",
      scaffold:"Cultural discount (Hoskins & Mirus, developed by Hesmondhalgh): when media crosses cultural boundaries, local specificity reduces its value to foreign audiences. US media minimises cultural discount by: producing for large domestic market (economies of scale), using universal genres (action, romance), English language advantage, minimal cultural specificity. This gives US content a competitive advantage globally — other nations' content faces higher discount barriers. Counter-examples: Korean Wave (Hallyu) — K-drama, K-pop have reduced their cultural discount through universal genre conventions + distinctive aesthetics. Exam: connect to global media dominance debates.",
      reforge:{stem:"A British sitcom with jokes about specific UK institutions (NHS, the BBC, British politics) struggles to attract audiences in the United States. This illustrates:",options:{A:"Conglomeration — US media companies block foreign content.",B:"The cultural discount — the British sitcom's specific cultural references reduce its appeal to US audiences who lack the contextual knowledge to appreciate the humour, making it less commercially viable in the US market compared to content engineered for broad international appeal.",C:"Regulation — US broadcasters are not permitted to air British content.",D:"Cultivation — US audiences have been cultivated to prefer domestic content."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"MED-14",
      stem:"The 'two-step flow' model of media influence (Katz and Lazarsfeld) argues that:",
      options:{
        A:"Media influence operates in two stages: first affecting children, then adults.",
        B:"Media messages reach audiences primarily through opinion leaders — influential individuals who consume media, interpret it, and then pass on filtered versions to their social networks — rather than flowing directly and uniformly to a passive mass audience.",
        C:"Audiences process media effects in two cognitive stages: conscious and unconscious.",
        D:"Broadcast media and social media form two separate flows of influence on public opinion."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MED-14",
      scaffold:"Two-step flow (Katz & Lazarsfeld, 1955 — 'Personal Influence'): challenged hypodermic needle model using empirical research on voting behaviour and fashion. Step 1: opinion leaders (politically engaged, media-heavy consumers) receive and interpret media messages. Step 2: opinion leaders pass interpreted messages to their social networks through conversation, recommendation and discussion. Implications: (1) social relationships mediate media effects; (2) audiences are not isolated or uniform; (3) personal influence is often more persuasive than direct media. Modern relevance: social media influencers operate as digital opinion leaders — the model maps well onto Instagram/TikTok recommendation culture. Limitation: oversimplifies; ignores multi-directional flows in social media era.",
      reforge:{stem:"A beauty influencer with 2 million Instagram followers reviews a new skincare product. Their followers buy it in large numbers, despite no traditional advertising. Which media concept best explains this?",options:{A:"Hypodermic needle — the influencer injects the message directly into passive followers.",B:"Two-step flow — the influencer acts as an opinion leader, having consumed and evaluated the product and then passing a filtered, personalised recommendation to their social network, whose trust in the influencer makes the message more persuasive than a direct advertisement.",C:"Cultivation — long-term exposure to beauty content cultivates a desire for skincare products.",D:"Uses and gratifications — followers use the influencer's content to satisfy a need for consumer information."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"MED-15",
      stem:"In the context of digital media, 'user-generated content' (UGC) challenges traditional media industry models because:",
      options:{
        A:"UGC is always higher quality than professionally produced media content.",
        B:"It blurs the distinction between producer and audience — anyone with a smartphone can create and distribute content globally, undermining the gatekeeping power of traditional media institutions and creating a competitive content environment that legacy media cannot easily replicate.",
        C:"Regulators require platforms to promote UGC over professional content.",
        D:"UGC content is exempt from copyright law, allowing free distribution."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MED-15",
      scaffold:"UGC and the 'prosumer' (Toffler): audiences as producers. Platforms enabling UGC: YouTube, TikTok, Instagram, Twitch, podcasting. Challenges to legacy media: (1) Gatekeeping disrupted — publishers no longer control what reaches audiences; (2) Revenue diverted — advertising follows attention, which increasingly goes to UGC platforms; (3) Speed — UGC often breaks news faster than journalists; (4) Scale — YouTube alone uploads 500 hours of video per minute. BUT: quality control, misinformation, algorithmic curation create new problems (Livingstone & Lunt). Gauntlett's 'making is connecting' — cultural participation is democratised. Critical view: platform companies (not creators) capture most of the value; precarious 'creator economy'.",
      reforge:{stem:"A teenager's home video of a local flood goes viral on TikTok, reaching 10 million viewers before any news broadcaster has covered the story. This illustrates:",options:{A:"Cultivation theory — audiences are being cultivated to seek dramatic content.",B:"How UGC challenges traditional media gatekeeping — the teenager acts as both producer and distributor, bypassing editorial processes that would normally control what reaches a mass audience, and the platform's algorithm distributes the content at a scale and speed legacy media cannot match.",C:"The two-step flow — the teenager is acting as an opinion leader.",D:"Synergy — TikTok and news broadcasters benefit from sharing the same content."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"MED-16",
      stem:"Ofcom's duty to maintain 'due impartiality' in broadcast news requires that:",
      options:{
        A:"All news programmes must give exactly equal time to all political parties.",
        B:"Broadcasters must present controversial matters with sufficient balance — not favouring any particular viewpoint — though this does not mean every perspective receives identical airtime, but that no significant strand of opinion is unreasonably excluded or misrepresented.",
        C:"Broadcasters are prohibited from reporting on government policy during election campaigns.",
        D:"News anchors must declare their political views at the start of each broadcast."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-MED-16",
      scaffold:"Due impartiality (Ofcom Broadcasting Code Section 5): applies to ALL broadcasters (not print — IPSO). Not equal airtime but 'adequate' balance over time. Distinction: news (impartiality strictly required) vs. factual programmes vs. comment/opinion. Broadcast vs press: press (The Sun, Guardian) can be politically partisan — not regulated for impartiality. Broadcast must be impartial because: public ownership of airwaves; scarcity of broadcast spectrum historically (though this is less relevant in digital age). Debates: does social media undermine the concept of regulated impartiality? Foreign news channels (Russia Today, Al Jazeera) and UK regulatory jurisdiction. Exam: compare broadcast regulation with press self-regulation (IPSO) — very different frameworks.",
      reforge:{stem:"The Sun newspaper endorses a political party the day before a general election. A television news channel does the same. Which of these actions would breach UK media regulation?",options:{A:"Both — print and broadcast media are held to the same impartiality standard.",B:"Only the television news channel — broadcast media are regulated by Ofcom's due impartiality rules, which prohibit political endorsements. The Sun, as a print publication, is not subject to broadcast impartiality requirements and may express political opinions freely.",C:"Neither — freedom of expression protects both from regulation.",D:"Only the Sun — print media faces stricter regulation than broadcast."},correct:"B"}
    }
  ]
};

BANKS["PE-1"] = {
  label: "Applied Anatomy & Physiology",
  color: "#14532d",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"PE-01",
      stem:"During maximal exercise, cardiac output increases dramatically. Which combination of factors is primarily responsible?",
      options:{
        A:"Decreased heart rate and increased stroke volume.",
        B:"Increased heart rate (via sympathetic nervous system) and increased stroke volume (via Starling's law — greater venous return stretches the ventricles, causing a more forceful contraction).",
        C:"Increased heart rate only — stroke volume remains constant during exercise.",
        D:"Decreased stroke volume compensated by a very large increase in heart rate."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-PE-01",
      scaffold:"Cardiac output (Q) = heart rate (HR) × stroke volume (SV). At rest: ~5L/min (70bpm × 70ml). During maximal exercise: up to 20-25L/min in trained athletes. HR increases via sympathetic NS (adrenaline, reduced parasympathetic tone). SV increases via: (1) Starling's law — greater venous return (muscle pump, venoconstriction) → ventricle fills more → stronger contraction; (2) Increased contractility (adrenaline); (3) Reduced afterload (vasodilation in working muscles). Trained athletes: higher SV due to cardiac hypertrophy (eccentric) — HR lower at rest and submaximal exercise.",
      reforge:{stem:"A trained endurance athlete has a resting heart rate of 40bpm compared to an untrained person's 70bpm, but their cardiac output at rest is similar. What explains this?",options:{A:"The athlete's heart beats faster so it needs fewer beats per minute at rest.",B:"The athlete has a higher stroke volume from cardiac hypertrophy — more blood per beat means fewer beats per minute are needed to deliver the same cardiac output.",C:"The athlete's muscles require less oxygen at rest, so the heart slows down.",D:"The athlete has a lower total blood volume than an untrained person."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"PE-02",
      stem:"The oxygen dissociation curve shifts to the right during intense exercise. What causes this and what is its effect?",
      options:{
        A:"Lower CO₂ and higher pH — haemoglobin becomes better at absorbing oxygen.",
        B:"Higher CO₂, lower pH (acidosis), higher temperature and higher 2,3-BPG — haemoglobin's affinity for oxygen decreases, so oxygen is offloaded more readily to working muscles (the Bohr effect).",
        C:"Lower temperature in working muscles increases haemoglobin's oxygen affinity.",
        D:"The curve shift is caused by increased heart rate, not blood chemistry."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-PE-02",
      scaffold:"Bohr effect / oxygen dissociation curve: at rest, curve sits in mid-position. During exercise: (1) CO₂↑ (cellular respiration) → carbonic acid → H⁺↑ → pH↓ (acidosis); (2) Temperature↑ in working muscles; (3) 2,3-BPG↑ (produced by red blood cells during high demand). All three shift curve RIGHT: haemoglobin releases O₂ more readily at the same partial pressure. Practical effect: more O₂ unloaded to active muscle fibres. At the lungs: relatively low CO₂, lower temp → curve shifts left → HbO₂ formed more readily. This is a beautiful auto-regulatory mechanism matching O₂ delivery to metabolic demand.",
      reforge:{stem:"A sprinter's blood becomes more acidic during a 400m race. What is the immediate physiological consequence of this for oxygen delivery?",options:{A:"Haemoglobin binds more tightly to oxygen, reducing delivery to working muscles.",B:"The Bohr effect shifts the ODC right — lower pH reduces haemoglobin's oxygen affinity, causing more O₂ to be released at the tissues where CO₂ and lactate are highest.",C:"Oxygen delivery stops completely until blood pH returns to the normal range.",D:"The lungs compensate by absorbing extra oxygen to counteract the increased acidity."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"PE-03",
      stem:"Which correctly describes the sliding filament theory of muscle contraction?",
      options:{
        A:"Actin and myosin filaments shorten during contraction, pulling the Z-lines together.",
        B:"Myosin heads attach to actin binding sites, forming cross-bridges, then pivot (power stroke), pulling actin towards the centre of the sarcomere — shortening the sarcomere without the filaments themselves changing length.",
        C:"Myosin filaments slide over actin filaments and both increase in length during contraction.",
        D:"Contraction occurs when calcium ions cause actin filaments to push against each other."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-PE-03",
      scaffold:"Sliding filament theory (Huxley): actin and myosin filaments don't shorten — they slide past each other. Sequence: (1) Action potential → T-tubules → sarcoplasmic reticulum releases Ca²⁺; (2) Ca²⁺ binds troponin → tropomyosin moves → exposes actin binding sites; (3) Myosin head (loaded with ADP+Pi) attaches → cross-bridge formed; (4) Power stroke — myosin pivots, pulls actin towards M-line; (5) ATP binds myosin head → detaches from actin; (6) ATP hydrolysis re-cocks myosin head. Z-lines pulled together → sarcomere shortens → muscle contracts. Without ATP: cross-bridges remain attached (rigor mortis).",
      reforge:{stem:"A student states 'muscles contract because the actin and myosin filaments get shorter.' Why is this incorrect?",options:{A:"It is correct — both filaments shorten equally during contraction.",B:"The filaments themselves don't change length — the sarcomere shortens because actin filaments slide towards the centre (M-line) due to myosin cross-bridge power strokes, reducing the H-zone and I-band widths while the A-band stays the same length.",C:"Only myosin shortens; actin stays the same length.",D:"It is incorrect because it is the Z-lines, not the filaments, that shorten."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"PE-04",
      stem:"A marathon runner at 80% VO₂ max is primarily using which energy system, and why?",
      options:{
        A:"ATP-PC system — it is the most efficient for sustained effort.",
        B:"Aerobic glycolysis and fat oxidation via the aerobic system — at this intensity, oxygen supply is sufficient to sustain aerobic metabolism. Fat and carbohydrate are broken down through the Krebs cycle and electron transport chain to produce large amounts of ATP, with CO₂ and water as by-products.",
        C:"Lactic acid system — all running above walking pace uses anaerobic glycolysis.",
        D:"ATP-PC system transitioning to lactic acid — the aerobic system is too slow for running."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-PE-04",
      scaffold:"Energy systems: (1) ATP-PC (phosphocreatine): immediate, anaerobic, 0-10 seconds, high power (sprinting). (2) Lactic acid (anaerobic glycolysis): fast, 10 seconds–2 minutes, moderate-high intensity (800m), produces lactate. (3) Aerobic: slower onset, unlimited duration, low-moderate intensity — uses glycolysis + Krebs + ETC. At 80% VO₂max a marathon runner is below their lactate threshold — aerobic system dominant. Fat provides ~50% of fuel at marathon pace; carbohydrate the rest. VO₂max: maximum rate of O₂ consumption — a key indicator of aerobic capacity. Lactate threshold: intensity at which lactate accumulates faster than it's cleared.",
      reforge:{stem:"A 400m runner starts fast (ATP-PC), continues through lactic acid threshold, then slows in the final 100m due to lactate accumulation. Which energy system sequence is correct?",options:{A:"Aerobic system first, then lactic acid, then ATP-PC as fatigue accumulates.",B:"All three systems overlap throughout — ATP-PC dominates the first ~10 seconds, lactic acid takes over for the main effort, and the aerobic contribution grows progressively.",C:"The three energy systems activate sequentially with no overlap at any stage.",D:"Only ATP-PC and aerobic systems operate — the lactic acid system does not function at 400m race pace."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"PE-05",
      stem:"What is VO₂ max and why does it matter for endurance performance?",
      options:{
        A:"The maximum heart rate achievable — higher means better endurance.",
        B:"The maximum rate at which an individual can consume and utilise oxygen during maximal exercise — a key indicator of aerobic capacity. Higher VO₂ max allows athletes to sustain higher absolute workloads aerobically, delaying reliance on anaerobic pathways and lactate accumulation.",
        C:"The volume of oxygen in the lungs at maximum inspiration.",
        D:"The oxygen deficit incurred during the first minutes of exercise before aerobic systems activate."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-PE-05",
      scaffold:"VO₂ max (ml/kg/min): gold standard measure of cardiorespiratory fitness. Elite male endurance athletes: 70-90 ml/kg/min (Kipchoge ~90); untrained males ~35-40. Determined by: (1) Cardiac output (central limitation — heart's ability to pump oxygenated blood); (2) Oxygen-carrying capacity (haemoglobin, blood volume); (3) Peripheral extraction (muscle capillary density, mitochondrial density, oxidative enzyme activity). Training adaptations increase VO₂ max by: increasing stroke volume, plasma volume, mitochondrial density. Limitation as predictor: lactate threshold and running economy better predict elite marathon performance among athletes with similar VO₂ max.",
      reforge:{stem:"Two athletes have the same VO₂ max of 70 ml/kg/min, but athlete A consistently beats athlete B in marathons. Which factor most likely explains this?",options:{A:"Athlete A has a higher maximum heart rate, allowing greater oxygen delivery.",B:"Athlete A has a higher lactate threshold — they sustain a greater % of VO₂ max before lactate accumulates, so they maintain race pace longer.",C:"Athlete A actually has a higher VO₂ max when the test is conducted correctly.",D:"Athlete A uses fat more efficiently below 50% VO₂ max, sparing glycogen."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"PE-06",
      stem:"During the recovery period after intense exercise, oxygen consumption remains elevated above resting levels. This is called:",
      options:{
        A:"Oxygen deficit — the oxygen that was lacking during exercise.",
        B:"Excess Post-Exercise Oxygen Consumption (EPOC) — elevated oxygen uptake after exercise restores the body to its pre-exercise state by replenishing ATP-PC stores, removing lactate (converting to glucose or CO₂/water), lowering elevated body temperature and hormone levels, and restoring oxygen stores in myoglobin and haemoglobin.",
        C:"The oxygen debt — the body pays back exactly the oxygen it borrowed during the exercise.",
        D:"Aerobic glycolysis continuing as the body cools down."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-PE-06",
      scaffold:"EPOC (formerly 'oxygen debt' — now recognised as more complex): Fast component (alactacid, 2-3 min): replenish PC stores (ATP-PC resynthesis), restore O₂ bound to myoglobin/haemoglobin. Slow component (lactacid, 30-60+ min): lactate removal (converted to glucose in liver via Cori cycle, oxidised in heart/slow-twitch fibres, small amount excreted in sweat/urine); elevated metabolic rate (elevated temperature, catecholamines); protein repair and synthesis. Practical implications: active recovery (light exercise) speeds lactate clearance vs passive recovery. Magnitude of EPOC related to exercise intensity and duration.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why does active recovery (light jogging) after intense exercise reduce recovery time compared to passive rest?",options:{A:"Jogging replenishes PC stores faster than complete rest because the aerobic system runs at full capacity.",B:"Light exercise maintains elevated blood flow, speeding lactate clearance, oxygen delivery for PC resynthesis, and CO₂ removal compared with passive recovery.",C:"Active recovery stops EPOC entirely by returning heart rate to resting level more quickly.",D:"Light jogging lets the aerobic system keep producing ATP so the PC system doesn't need to work at all."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"PE-07",
      stem:"Type I (slow-twitch) and Type IIx (fast-twitch) muscle fibres differ primarily in:",
      options:{
        A:"Type I fibres are larger and produce more force; Type IIx fibres are smaller and fatigue-resistant.",
        B:"Type I fibres are smaller, fatigue-resistant, aerobic, high in mitochondria and myoglobin — suited to endurance. Type IIx fibres are larger, fatigue rapidly, anaerobic, rely on glycolysis — suited to short, high-power activities.",
        C:"Type IIx fibres are used for all moderate-intensity activity; Type I only activate during maximal effort.",
        D:"The only difference is contraction speed — both types fatigue at the same rate."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-PE-07",
      scaffold:"Fibre type comparison: Type I (slow oxidative): small, many mitochondria, high myoglobin (red colour), dense capillary network, fatigue-resistant, low force output, aerobic — endurance athletes (marathon runners). Type IIa (fast oxidative-glycolytic): intermediate — moderate force, moderate fatigue resistance, both aerobic and anaerobic capacity — 400-800m runners. Type IIx (fast glycolytic): large, few mitochondria, low myoglobin (pale), glycolytic, high force output, fatigue rapidly — sprinters, power athletes. Recruitment order (Henneman's size principle): Type I → IIa → IIx as intensity increases. Genetics largely determines fibre ratio; training causes some Type IIx → IIa shift.",
      reforge:{stem:"An elite 100m sprinter stops after 10 seconds of maximal effort and their muscles fatigue extremely quickly. A marathon runner can maintain effort for 2+ hours. This difference is primarily explained by:",options:{A:"Sprinters have larger hearts and greater cardiac output, benefiting explosive efforts.",B:"Fibre type composition — elite sprinters have more Type IIx fast-twitch fibres with higher force production and ATP-PC reliance; marathoners have more Type I slow-twitch fibres.",C:"Sprinters use the ATP-PC system, which is actually less efficient than the aerobic system.",D:"Marathon runners have lower VO₂ max but superior lactate threshold and fat oxidation."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"PE-08",
      stem:"Which correctly describes the mechanics of breathing during maximal exercise?",
      options:{
        A:"Inspiration is always passive (elastic recoil); only expiration becomes active during exercise.",
        B:"During maximal exercise, both inspiration and expiration become active processes — additional inspiratory muscles (scalenes, sternocleidomastoid, pectoralis minor) are recruited, and expiration uses the internal intercostals and abdominals to forcibly compress the thorax, maximising tidal volume and breathing rate.",
        C:"Breathing rate increases but tidal volume stays constant during intense exercise.",
        D:"The diaphragm relaxes during exercise, and breathing is entirely managed by the intercostal muscles."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-PE-08",
      scaffold:"Mechanics of breathing: rest — inspiration: diaphragm + external intercostals (active); expiration: passive (elastic recoil). Exercise — increasing demand: additional inspiratory muscles recruited (scalenes, sternocleidomastoid). At maximal intensity: expiration becomes ACTIVE — internal intercostals and abdominals forcibly compress thorax. Result: tidal volume (volume per breath) increases (from ~0.5L at rest to ~3L in trained athletes during max exercise) AND breathing rate increases (from ~12-15 to 40-60 breaths/min). Minute ventilation = tidal volume × breathing rate. Neural control: medullary respiratory centre (rhythmicity); chemoreceptors detect CO₂/H⁺/O₂ changes and adjust ventilation.",
      reforge:{stem:"During a maximal 1-minute effort, a cyclist's breathing rate increases from 15 to 50 breaths per minute and their tidal volume from 0.5L to 2.5L. What is the approximate increase in minute ventilation?",options:{A:"From 7.5L/min to 12.5L/min — a modest increase in breathing rate only.",B:"From 7.5L/min to 125L/min — a ~16-fold increase, achieved by increasing both breathing rate and tidal volume.",C:"Minute ventilation stays constant — only the ratio of rate to tidal volume changes.",D:"From 7.5L/min to 50L/min — tidal volume does not change during exercise."},correct:"B"}
    }
  ]
};

// ===== PE BANK 2 — Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics =====

BANKS["PE-2"] = {
  label: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics",
  color: "#14532d",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"PE-09",
      stem:"A sprinter's muscles produce ATP primarily via the phosphocreatine (ATP-PC) system in the first 10 seconds of a race. Which statement correctly describes a limitation of this system?",
      options:{
        A:"It requires oxygen and therefore cannot activate instantly at race start.",
        B:"Phosphocreatine stores are very limited — they are depleted within approximately 10 seconds of maximal effort, after which the body must rely on anaerobic glycolysis and then the aerobic system to continue ATP resynthesis.",
        C:"The ATP-PC system produces lactic acid, causing the characteristic burn felt in sprinting.",
        D:"It is the slowest of the three energy systems to activate, so it is only used after 30 seconds."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-PE-09",
      scaffold:"ATP-PC system: ATP + phosphocreatine (stored in muscle) → ADP + energy + creatine. Advantages: immediate (no oxygen needed, no by-products), fastest ATP resynthesis rate. Limitations: very small PC stores (depleted in ~8-10 seconds of maximal effort); cannot sustain high-intensity exercise beyond this. Recovery: PC stores ~50% replenished in 30 seconds, ~100% in 3-4 minutes (requires oxygen — hence active recovery helps). Creatine supplementation: can increase PC stores by ~20%, giving marginal gains in repeated sprint efforts. Exam: link to sport specificity — which sports rely on this system and for how long.",
      reforge:{stem:"A tennis player hits a maximal serve, then has 20 seconds before serving again. Why is this recovery interval important physiologically?",options:{A:"It allows lactic acid from the serve to be fully removed.",B:"It allows partial replenishment of phosphocreatine stores via the aerobic system — PC is ~50% restored after 30 seconds, so 20 seconds allows enough recovery for another near-maximal serve without significant performance decrement. Full PC restoration takes 3-4 minutes.",C:"It allows the aerobic system to build up ATP stores for the next serve.",D:"It reduces heart rate to resting levels before the next maximal effort."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"PE-10",
      stem:"During the lactic acid (anaerobic glycolysis) energy system, glucose is broken down to produce ATP. What is the main physiological consequence of this process?",
      options:{
        A:"Large amounts of CO₂ are produced, which accumulates in the muscles and causes fatigue.",
        B:"Pyruvate is converted to lactate (lactic acid) when oxygen is insufficient — hydrogen ions (H⁺) accumulate, lowering muscle pH (acidosis), which inhibits enzyme activity and cross-bridge formation, causing the muscular fatigue and burning sensation associated with high-intensity exercise.",
        C:"Glucose is only partially broken down, so the energy yield is identical to the aerobic system.",
        D:"Fat is broken down alongside glucose, providing additional ATP at high intensities."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-PE-10",
      scaffold:"Anaerobic glycolysis (lactic acid system): glucose → pyruvate → lactate. ATP yield: 2 ATP per glucose (compared to ~36-38 aerobic). Duration: ~10 seconds to ~2 minutes of high intensity (800m, 400m swimming). Fatigue mechanism: it is NOT lactic acid itself — it's H⁺ ion accumulation (acidosis) that: (1) inhibits phosphofructokinase (PFK — key glycolytic enzyme); (2) interferes with calcium's role in cross-bridge cycling; (3) reduces muscle contractility. Lactate is actually a useful fuel (heart, slow-twitch fibres oxidise it). Lactate threshold: intensity above which lactate accumulates faster than cleared — key training marker. Blood lactate testing: used to prescribe training zones.",
      reforge:{stem:"An 800m runner 'ties up' in the final 100m — legs feel heavy and pace drops dramatically despite effort. Which physiological process is primarily responsible?",options:{A:"Depletion of phosphocreatine stores causing the ATP-PC system to fail.",B:"Accumulation of hydrogen ions from anaerobic glycolysis, reducing intramuscular pH — the resulting acidosis inhibits key glycolytic enzymes and impairs cross-bridge cycling, reducing the muscle's ability to generate force despite maintained neural drive.",C:"Oxygen running out — the aerobic system switches off at high intensities.",D:"Lactic acid physically blocking muscle fibre contraction."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"PE-11",
      stem:"Which of the following best describes the principle of 'progressive overload' in training?",
      options:{
        A:"Performing the same exercise at the same intensity every session to build consistency.",
        B:"Systematically and gradually increasing the training stimulus — through frequency, intensity, time or type (FITT) — beyond what the body is currently adapted to, triggering physiological adaptations that improve performance over time.",
        C:"Performing the most intense possible session in every training block to maximise gains.",
        D:"Overloading the body once per month with a very long session to stimulate adaptation."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-PE-11",
      scaffold:"Principles of training — SPORT: Specificity, Progression, Overload, Reversibility, Tedium/Variation. Progressive overload: the body adapts to current stress, so stress must increase for further adaptation. FITT framework: Frequency (sessions per week), Intensity (% of max HR or 1RM), Time (session duration/volume), Type (modality). Adaptation lag: 4-6 weeks before measurable physiological adaptation. Overtraining syndrome: applying overload without sufficient recovery — increased injury risk, performance decline, immune suppression. Periodisation: planned variation of overload across a training cycle (macrocycle → mesocycle → microcycle) to peak for competition and prevent overtraining.",
      reforge:{stem:"A swimmer has trained consistently at the same distance and speed for six months. Their performance has plateaued. Which training principle is being violated, and what is the solution?",options:{A:"Specificity — they should switch to a different sport to stimulate new physical adaptations.",B:"Progressive overload — the stimulus is no longer sufficient; the swimmer must increase frequency, intensity, or volume to trigger further physiological adaptation.",C:"Reversibility — training for too long causes fitness to decline through overuse.",D:"Tedium — the swimmer is bored, which is a psychological rather than physiological issue."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"PE-12",
      stem:"In biomechanics, Newton's third law of motion states that for every action there is an equal and opposite reaction. Which sporting example best illustrates this?",
      options:{
        A:"A swimmer accelerating when the resistance of the water decreases.",
        B:"A sprinter driving their foot backwards and downwards into the track — the track exerts an equal and opposite ground reaction force (GRF) forwards and upwards on the sprinter, propelling them forward.",
        C:"A high jumper jumping higher on a trampoline than on solid ground because the surface is softer.",
        D:"A basketball player decelerating when they stop running — the equal and opposite reaction causes them to fall forward."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-PE-12",
      scaffold:"Newton's Third Law: for every force, an equal and opposite force acts. Application in sport: ground reaction force (GRF) — measured by force plates. Sprinting: foot pushes back → ground pushes forward (propulsion). Jumping: foot pushes down → ground pushes up (takeoff force). Swimming: hand/arm pushes water back → water pushes swimmer forward. Newton's First Law (inertia): an object remains at rest or constant velocity unless acted on by a net force. Newton's Second Law: F = ma — force equals mass × acceleration. Application: heavier athletes require more force to accelerate; lighter athletes accelerate more easily with same force. Exam: always link the law to a specific mechanism in the movement, not just the definition.",
      reforge:{stem:"A long jumper plants their take-off foot and leaps forward. Identify the Newton's Third Law interaction at take-off.",options:{A:"The jumper's momentum carries them forward — no reaction force is involved.",B:"The jumper's foot exerts a force downward and backward into the board — the board exerts an equal and opposite ground reaction force upward and forward on the jumper, providing the propulsive and vertical forces needed to launch them into the jump.",C:"The jumper's muscles contract, pulling the body upward without requiring contact force.",D:"Air resistance acts backward on the jumper, so the Newton's Third Law pair is between the jumper and the air."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"PE-13",
      stem:"Which correctly describes the role of levers in the musculoskeletal system, using the bicep curl as an example?",
      options:{
        A:"The bicep curl uses a first-class lever, giving a mechanical advantage greater than 1.",
        B:"The bicep curl is a third-class lever — the effort (bicep force at insertion on the radius) acts between the fulcrum (elbow joint) and the load (weight in the hand). This gives a mechanical disadvantage (effort > load) but produces a large range of motion and speed of movement at the hand.",
        C:"Levers in the human body always produce mechanical advantage — muscles exert less force than the load they move.",
        D:"The bicep curl is a second-class lever because the load (weight) is between the fulcrum and the effort."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-PE-13",
      scaffold:"Levers: fulcrum (F), effort (E), load (L). Classes: First class: F between E and L (seesaw; neck extension — head is load, atlas joint is fulcrum, posterior neck muscles are effort). Second class: L between F and E (mechanical advantage >1 — wheelbarrow; calf raise — ball of foot is fulcrum, body weight is load, calf muscles are effort). Third class: E between F and L (mechanical disadvantage — most joints in the human body; bicep curl, knee extension). Most human levers are third-class — muscles insert close to joints, so small muscle movement = large end-limb movement (speed advantage despite force disadvantage). Exam: identify F, E, L and class, then comment on mechanical advantage/disadvantage.",
      reforge:{stem:"A goalkeeper dives to save a penalty, extending their arm rapidly to reach the ball. Why does the third-class lever arrangement of most arm joints benefit this action despite its mechanical disadvantage?",options:{A:"Third-class levers provide the greatest mechanical advantage, allowing the goalkeeper to exert more force than the load.",B:"Third-class levers (effort between fulcrum and load) sacrifice mechanical advantage — the muscle exerts more force than the load — but produce a large range of motion and high velocity at the end of the limb (hand). For a goalkeeper, the speed of the hand reaching the ball matters far more than the magnitude of force, so this lever arrangement is functionally advantageous.",C:"The goalkeeper's muscles are strongest when the lever is in third class configuration.",D:"Third-class levers reduce energy expenditure, allowing repeated dives without fatigue."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"PE-14",
      stem:"In sport, fluid mechanics affects performance through drag forces. Which factor has the GREATEST effect on drag experienced by a cyclist?",
      options:{
        A:"The mass of the cyclist — heavier riders always experience more drag.",
        B:"The frontal area presented to airflow — drag increases approximately with the square of velocity and is directly proportional to frontal cross-sectional area, so a cyclist in an aerodynamic tuck (reduced frontal area) experiences dramatically less drag than one sitting upright.",
        C:"The colour of the cycling kit — darker colours create more drag.",
        D:"The temperature of the air — warmer air creates more drag than cooler air."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-PE-14",
      scaffold:"Drag equation: F_drag = ½ × ρ × v² × C_d × A. ρ = air density, v = velocity (squared — so doubling speed quadruples drag), C_d = drag coefficient (shape/surface), A = frontal area. In cycling: frontal area is the dominant variable athletes can control. Aerodynamic tuck (Tour de France time trial position) can reduce frontal area by ~25%, reducing drag by ~25%. Surface texture matters: smooth (lycra) vs. rough; dimples on golf balls create turbulent boundary layer — reduces drag at high speeds. Altitude effects: lower air density at altitude → less drag (but less oxygen too). Training application: marginal gains in professional cycling — wheels, helmets, position all optimised for drag reduction.",
      reforge:{stem:"A time-trial cyclist switches from an upright position to a full aerodynamic tuck at 50km/h. What is the primary biomechanical benefit?",options:{A:"The tuck position increases muscle activation, producing more power.",B:"The tuck dramatically reduces the cyclist's frontal cross-sectional area — since drag is directly proportional to frontal area and increases with the square of velocity, even a modest reduction in frontal area at 50km/h produces a large reduction in drag force, allowing the same power output to produce greater speed.",C:"The tuck lowers the cyclist's centre of mass, improving stability on climbs.",D:"The tuck position reduces the length of the levers, making pedalling more efficient."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"PE-15",
      stem:"The term 'angular momentum' in sport refers to:",
      options:{
        A:"The speed at which an athlete rotates, measured in degrees per second.",
        B:"The product of moment of inertia and angular velocity — a conserved quantity in the absence of external torques, which explains why a figure skater pulling their arms in spins faster (reduced moment of inertia → increased angular velocity to conserve angular momentum).",
        C:"The force applied at the edge of a rotating body, causing it to spin.",
        D:"The distance from the axis of rotation to the athlete's centre of mass."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-PE-15",
      scaffold:"Angular momentum (L) = moment of inertia (I) × angular velocity (ω). Moment of inertia (I) = mass × radius² — depends on how mass is distributed relative to the axis. Conservation: in the absence of external torques, angular momentum is conserved. Figure skater: arms out → large I → small ω (slow spin). Arms pulled in → small I → large ω (fast spin). Same L. Diver: tuck position (small I) → fast rotation. Pike/straight (large I) → slow rotation. Gymnast on bars: hollow body (large I) → slow swing; compressed (small I) → fast spin. Application: coaches teach athletes to manipulate body position to control rotation speed. Exam: apply the I × ω = constant relationship to a named sport.",
      reforge:{stem:"A diver leaves the board in a straight position performing slow rotations, then pulls into a tight tuck and rotates rapidly before straightening again to enter the water. Explain this sequence using angular momentum.",options:{A:"The diver generates more muscular force in the tuck position, increasing angular momentum.",B:"Angular momentum is conserved (no external torques in flight) — tucking reduces moment of inertia, so angular velocity increases to compensate; straightening before entry restores slow rotation.",C:"The diver's muscles contract more forcefully in the tuck, creating the extra spin.",D:"Air resistance is reduced in the tuck, which allows the body to rotate more quickly."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"PE-16",
      stem:"Which best describes the psychological concept of 'arousal' and its relationship to sports performance, as described by the inverted-U theory?",
      options:{
        A:"Higher arousal always leads to better performance — elite athletes should aim to maximise arousal before competition.",
        B:"There is an optimal level of arousal for peak performance — too little (under-arousal) leads to inattention and poor effort, while too much (over-arousal) causes anxiety, muscle tension and breakdown of fine motor control. The optimal level varies by task complexity and individual.",
        C:"Arousal only affects performance in fine motor skill tasks — gross motor tasks are unaffected.",
        D:"The inverted-U theory shows that arousal and performance are linearly related up to a maximum, after which performance drops to zero instantly."
      },
      correct:"B",tag:"MC-PE-16",
      scaffold:"Arousal-performance relationship: Drive theory (Hull): linear — more arousal → better performance (for well-learned skills). Inverted-U (Yerkes-Dodson): optimal arousal zone — performance peaks at moderate arousal, declines at extremes. Task complexity modifies optimal zone: complex/fine motor (golf putting, snooker) → optimal arousal is LOW (over-arousal disrupts fine control). Gross motor (weightlifting, rugby tackle) → optimal arousal can be HIGH. Individual differences: extroverts seek higher arousal; introverts peak at lower arousal. Catastrophe theory (Hardy): arousal + cognitive anxiety → performance drops catastrophically, not gradually (unlike inverted-U). Zones of Optimal Functioning (ZOF — Hanin): optimal arousal is individual, not a universal moderate level. Pre-competition routines aim to achieve optimal arousal state.",
      reforge:{stem:"A golfer who plays brilliantly in practice rounds has a crucial 3-foot putt to win a major championship. Their hands shake and they miss the putt completely. Which psychological concept best explains this?",options:{A:"Drive theory — high arousal should produce the golfer's best performance.",B:"Over-arousal beyond the optimal zone (inverted-U) disrupts fine motor control — putting requires low optimal arousal, so the high-pressure context causes the yips.",C:"The ZOF — the golfer has entered their personal ideal performance zone.",D:"Progressive overload — the golfer has peaked too early through excessive training."},correct:"B"}
    }
  ]
};

