BANKS["CS-2"] = {
  label: "Data Structures & Networks",
  color: "#1e3a5f",
  questions: [
    {
      id:"CS-09",
      stem:"A stack data structure operates on which principle?",
      options:{
        A:"Last In, First Out — the most recently added item is removed first.",
        B:"First In, First Out — the first item added is the first removed.",
        C:"Random access — any element can be accessed directly by index.",
        D:"Priority-based — items are removed in order of their assigned priority."
      },
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-CS-05",
      scaffold:"Stack: LIFO (Last In, First Out). Think of a stack of plates — you add (push) and remove (pop) from the top only. Operations: push (add to top), pop (remove from top), peek (view top without removing), isEmpty. Used in: call stack (function calls), undo functionality, expression evaluation, depth-first search. Contrasted with a queue (FIFO — first in, first out), which adds at the back and removes from the front.",
      reforge:{stem:"Which real-world scenario is best modelled by a stack?",options:{A:"A printer queue — documents print in the order they were submitted.",B:"A browser's back button — the most recently visited page is the first returned to when you press back.",C:"A hospital waiting list — patients are seen in order of arrival.",D:"A playlist — songs play in the order they were added."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CS-10",
      stem:"In a binary tree, what is the maximum number of nodes at depth d (where the root is at depth 0)?",
      options:{
        A:"d",
        B:"2d",
        C:"d²",
        D:"2^d"
      },
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-CS-06",
      scaffold:"Binary tree: each node has at most 2 children (left and right). At depth 0: 1 node (root = 2⁰). At depth 1: up to 2 nodes (2¹). At depth 2: up to 4 nodes (2²). At depth d: up to 2^d nodes. A perfect binary tree of height h has 2^(h+1) − 1 total nodes. This exponential growth is what makes binary search efficient — each level doubles the number of nodes.",
      reforge:{stem:"A binary search tree has height 4 (root at height 0). What is the maximum total number of nodes it can contain?",options:{A:"16 nodes — 2⁴ = 16 (height 4, leaf nodes only).",B:"31 nodes — using 2^(h+1) − 1 where h = 4 gives 2⁵ − 1 = 31 total nodes.",C:"20 nodes — intermediate guess between 16 and 31.",D:"8 nodes — 2³, which would be height 3."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CS-11",
      stem:"The OSI model layer responsible for logical addressing (IP addresses) and routing packets between networks is:",
      options:{
        A:"Layer 1 — Physical",
        B:"Layer 2 — Data Link",
        C:"Layer 4 — Transport",
        D:"Layer 3 — Network"
      },
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-CS-07",
      scaffold:"OSI model (7 layers, bottom to top): Physical (bits/cables), Data Link (MAC addresses, frames, switches), Network (IP addresses, routing, routers), Transport (TCP/UDP, port numbers, end-to-end delivery), Session, Presentation, Application. The Network layer (Layer 3) handles logical addressing — IP addresses — and routing between different networks. Data Link (Layer 2) handles physical addressing within the same network using MAC addresses.",
      reforge:{stem:"A packet travels from a computer in London to a server in Tokyo. Which OSI layer handles the routing decisions along the way?",options:{A:"Layer 2 — Data Link, using MAC addresses.",B:"Layer 4 — Transport, using port numbers.",C:"Layer 7 — Application, using domain names.",D:"Layer 3 — Network, using IP addresses to make routing decisions at each router hop."},correct:"D"}
    },
    {
      id:"CS-12",
      stem:"TCP differs from UDP primarily in that TCP:",
      options:{
        A:"Provides reliable, ordered delivery through acknowledgements, retransmission of lost packets, and flow control — at the cost of greater overhead and latency.",
        B:"Is faster because it sends data without waiting for acknowledgement.",
        C:"Can only be used for video streaming applications.",
        D:"Operates at the Network layer rather than the Transport layer."
      },
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-CS-08",
      scaffold:"TCP (Transmission Control Protocol): connection-oriented (three-way handshake: SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK), reliable (acknowledgements + retransmission), ordered (sequence numbers), flow controlled. Used for: web (HTTP/HTTPS), email, file transfer. UDP (User Datagram Protocol): connectionless, no acknowledgements, no ordering, no retransmission — faster but unreliable. Used for: video streaming, online gaming, DNS, VoIP — where speed matters more than perfect delivery.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why is UDP preferred over TCP for live video streaming?",options:{A:"UDP provides better data security than TCP for video streams.",B:"UDP is faster — it sends packets without acknowledgements or retransmission, accepting some data loss. Streaming tolerates this; file transfers cannot.",C:"TCP cannot handle the video file formats used in live streaming.",D:"UDP is more reliable than TCP for large file transfers where accuracy matters most."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CS-13",
      stem:"A queue implemented using an array has a 'rear' pointer at index 7 in an array of size 8. What problem does a circular queue solve?",
      options:{
        A:"It allows the queue to hold more than 8 elements.",
        B:"It sorts elements as they are added.",
        C:"It enables priority-based removal of elements.",
        D:"It reuses vacated front positions by wrapping the rear pointer back to index 0 — preventing false 'full' conditions when space exists at the front of the array."
      },
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-CS-09",
      scaffold:"Linear queue problem: after dequeue operations, front pointer advances but those array positions cannot be reused without shifting all elements (O(n)). When rear reaches the end, the array appears full even if spaces exist at the front. Circular queue: uses modular arithmetic ((rear + 1) % size) to wrap around — rear pointer goes 7 → 0 → 1, reusing freed positions. Full condition: (rear + 1) % size == front. More efficient for fixed-size queues.",
      reforge:{stem:"How does a circular queue detect whether it is full?",options:{A:"When the rear pointer equals the array size.",B:"When all elements have the same value.",C:"When (rear + 1) % size == front — the next position rear would advance to is where front currently is, meaning no more space without overwriting existing elements.",D:"When the front pointer reaches index 0."},correct:"C"}
    },
    {
      id:"CS-14",
      stem:"Public key cryptography (asymmetric encryption) works because:",
      options:{
        A:"The receiver's public key encrypts the message, but only the receiver's mathematically linked private key can decrypt it — the public key cannot reverse its own encryption.",
        B:"Both sender and receiver use the same secret key, shared securely in advance.",
        C:"Messages are compressed before transmission, making them unreadable without the decompression algorithm.",
        D:"A trusted third party holds the key and decrypts messages on both sides."
      },
      correct:"A",tag:"MC-CS-10",
      scaffold:"Asymmetric encryption: key pair — public key (shared openly) and private key (kept secret). Anyone can encrypt with the public key; only the private key holder can decrypt. Based on mathematical problems that are easy to compute in one direction but infeasible to reverse (e.g. multiplying two large primes is easy; factoring their product is computationally infeasible). Used in HTTPS (TLS), SSH, digital signatures. Solves the key distribution problem of symmetric encryption.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why does public key cryptography solve a problem that symmetric encryption cannot?",options:{A:"Asymmetric encryption is mathematically unbreakable; symmetric encryption is not.",B:"Symmetric encryption requires sharing a secret key in advance — a problem over public networks. Asymmetric uses a public/private key pair, avoiding this key-exchange vulnerability.",C:"Public keys are longer and therefore provide stronger encryption by default.",D:"Symmetric encryption cannot physically be used for internet-based communication."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CS-15",
      stem:"In a graph, a depth-first search (DFS) differs from breadth-first search (BFS) in that DFS:",
      options:{
        A:"Always finds the shortest path between two nodes.",
        B:"Uses a queue to track which nodes to visit next.",
        C:"Visits nodes in order of their distance from the starting node.",
        D:"Explores as far as possible along each branch before backtracking, using a stack (or recursion)."
      },
      correct:"D",tag:"MC-CS-11",
      scaffold:"DFS: goes deep first — follows a path until it reaches a dead end, then backtracks. Uses a stack (LIFO) or recursion. Suitable for: maze solving, topological sort, cycle detection. BFS: explores all neighbours at the current depth before going deeper. Uses a queue (FIFO). Finds shortest path in unweighted graphs. Memory: DFS uses O(depth) space; BFS uses O(width) space — BFS can use much more memory on wide graphs.",
      reforge:{stem:"Why is BFS preferred over DFS for finding the shortest path in an unweighted graph?",options:{A:"BFS is always faster than DFS for finding any path in a graph.",B:"DFS finds a path but not necessarily the shortest — it goes deep first. BFS explores level by level and guarantees the shortest path in an unweighted graph.",C:"DFS cannot traverse graphs that contain cycles.",D:"BFS uses less memory than DFS in all cases because it doesn't use a stack."},correct:"B"}
    },
    {
      id:"CS-16",
      stem:"Normalisation in relational databases aims to:",
      options:{
        A:"Speed up database queries by duplicating frequently accessed data.",
        B:"Encrypt sensitive data to prevent unauthorised access.",
        C:"Reduce data redundancy and update anomalies by organising data into well-structured tables with clear dependencies.",
        D:"Convert the database from SQL to NoSQL format."
      },
      correct:"C",tag:"MC-CS-12",
      scaffold:"Normalisation: process of structuring a relational database to reduce redundancy and dependency. Key normal forms: 1NF (atomic values, no repeating groups); 2NF (no partial dependencies — non-key attributes depend on the whole primary key); 3NF (no transitive dependencies — non-key attributes depend only on the primary key). Benefits: eliminates update anomalies (changing one row inconsistently), insertion anomalies, deletion anomalies. Trade-off: more joins required for queries.",
      reforge:{stem:"What is an update anomaly, and how does normalisation prevent it?",options:{A:"An error caused by using incorrect SQL syntax in a query.",B:"An update anomaly — duplicated data in a non-normalised table means one fact must be changed in multiple rows; missing one creates inconsistency.",C:"A security vulnerability created by storing unencrypted sensitive data.",D:"Slow query performance caused by joining too many tables simultaneously."},correct:"B"}
    }
  ]
};

